-- GDD documents (Markdown bodies)
-- Generated by scripts/pages/export-sql.mjs. Raw data: edit via the export, not by hand.

INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-001', 'decisions', 'adr-001', 'ADR-001: Repository delivery policy', '', 'accepted', 0, 'adr/ADR-001-repository-delivery-policy.md', '# ADR-001: Repository delivery policy

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-09-03
- Supersedes: the local-only delivery clauses of `.omo/plans/seoul-grand-strategy-srpg.md` (listed below)
- Decided by: repository owner, recorded in `.omo/evidence/foundation/execution-notepad.md` and the approved remediation plan `.omo/plans/fix-validate-commit-push.md` (Momus receipt `st_01a066c9`)

## Context

The approved grand-strategy plan was written when no GitHub repository existed for this project. It therefore locked the work to the local repository and forbade remotes, pushes and Wiki publication. Typical clauses:

- "GitHub remote stays unset" (keep the GitHub remote unset)
- "This plan performs no external writes involving the GitHub repository, remote, push, or Wiki publishing" (this plan performs no external writes)
- "Do not create any GitHub repository or remote under this plan."
- "`git remote -v` remains empty"
- "remote Wiki publication is outside this plan" / "Remote publication is a separate future plan."

On 2026-09-02 the owner gave later, explicit authorization that overrides those clauses: create the private GitHub repository `islee23520/seoul-kenshi`, bind it as `origin`, and push this local repository. That authorization is recorded in `.omo/evidence/foundation/execution-notepad.md` ("Repository target authorized by latest user message"). The repository was subsequently renamed on GitHub to `islee23520/seoul-dengoku`; GitHub redirects the historical URL, and the canonical repository URL is now `https://github.com/islee23520/seoul-dengoku.git`. On 2026-09-03 the owner approved the remediation plan `fix-validate-commit-push` (Momus receipt `st_01a066c9`), which authorizes a dedicated remediation branch, one push of that branch, and one pull request. The SHA-256 `724af715196adb109d94895542ad3a85fee2c2764682e749173f12c375d76c84` is the **approval-time** hash of `.omo/plans/fix-validate-commit-push.md` — the exact bytes Momus reviewed. That plan file is a living execution checklist and has since gained progress markers, so its current on-disk hash differs (`8aa1cc5669cc065170165efde85296519e5a24322084ba8e56952829df3025e8` as of 2026-09-03). The approval binds to the approval-time content, not to later execution edits. The quoted approval receipt is stored in the "Approval receipt" section below.

Keeping both texts without a supersession reference leaves two contradictory rules in force. This ADR resolves the conflict.

## Decision

This ADR is the single current delivery rule for the repository. Every local-only clause in the approved plan, and any future document that touches delivery, remotes or pushes, defers to this ADR.

1. **Current remote.** The canonical repository is `https://github.com/islee23520/seoul-dengoku.git`. The historical `https://github.com/islee23520/seoul-kenshi.git` URL may remain as a local `origin` because GitHub redirects it to the same repository. No other repository is authorized.
2. **Branch and pull request only.** All delivery goes through a dedicated branch and a pull request. Direct push to main is forbidden.
3. **No history rewrite.** No force-push, no amend of published commits, no rebase of shared history, no merge performed by agents. The owner merges pull requests.
4. **Excluded repository.** The unused private shooter repository stays fully out of scope. Do not query, inspect, rename, delete, overwrite, clone, transfer, bind or push it. This exclusion is unchanged by the supersession.
5. **Derived Wiki assets.** Repository documents under `LORE/` are the only lore source of truth. Local wiki-builder output and the VitePress tree under `WEB/wiki-source/` are generated, drift-checked derivatives. GitHub Wiki is retired. The public surface is `https://seoul-kenshi.vercel.app`.
6. **Rollback and review.** Revert with `git revert` on a new branch and a new pull request. Captures, receipts, screenshots, and QA evidence are private local files under ignored `.omo/evidence/`; they are never tracked, attached to a repository PR, or published. Durable product assets belong only in their approved owning canon. Reviewer locks from the approved plan (art, marketing, critic, Momus) still apply to their original scope.

## Approval receipt

Momus review of `.omo/plans/fix-validate-commit-push.md`, quoted from the persisted task record `.omo/senpi-task/tasks/st_01a066c9.json` (agent_type `momus`, model `openai/gpt-5.6-sol`, created 2026-09-03T10:21:33Z, terminal 2026-09-03T10:22:43Z, status `completed`):

> **[OKAY]**
>
> **Summary**: All file references exist, every task has executable QA scenarios (exact commands and expected outcomes), dependencies and commit boundaries are internally consistent, and no contradictions block execution.

This ADR (a tracked document under `docs/`) is the retrievable record of that receipt; the senpi task store is machine-local and git-ignored.

## Consequences

- The approved plan''s original text is preserved byte-for-byte. A non-rewriting amendment appended to the plan links each superseded clause to this ADR and records the plan''s pre-amendment SHA-256.
- `tools/policy/check-repo-delivery-policy.mjs` enforces this reconciliation: it fails if a local-only clause lacks a supersession reference, if this ADR is missing or incomplete, or if a second current delivery rule appears.
- `.gitignore` ignores `.senpi/` because that directory holds local agent session state created by memory reflection and hooks. It is machine-local runtime data, never a deliverable, so it must not be committed.
- The quarantined configuration changes (`activeInputHandler` in `ProjectSettings.asset`, the generated `PackageManagerSettings.asset`, and the unstaged `Asset-Pipeline.md` rewrite) stay quarantined. They are unrelated to delivery policy and are not adopted or reverted by this ADR.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-002', 'decisions', 'adr-002', 'ADR-002: Retrospective on the Character Candidate Art Pipeline and the Contract to Prevent Recurrence', '', 'accepted', 1, 'adr/ADR-002-character-candidate-retrospective.md', '# ADR-002: Retrospective on the Character Candidate Art Pipeline and the Contract to Prevent Recurrence

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-09-05
- Supersedes: None (new). The art direction and promotion gate in docs/game-logic/Character-Art-Direction.md remain in force as the higher-level contract.

## Context

In the character candidate repair lane on 2026-09-05 (fix/character-art-quality-candidates), we assembled locally the candidates for three characters (Explorer, Medic, Patrol Officer), with 4 directions × 5 actions (276 frames / 60 clips), then ran Unity verification and an independent visual review.

### What was technically verified

- Local assembler and 13 behavioral regression tests: 96x128 / 12fps, idle 4 / walk 6 / attack 6 / hit 3 / down 4, measured 2.5±0.08 heads tall, 0 cell-boundary overflows, atlas cell alpha 0–255 bytes preserved.
- Unity 6000.7.0a5 batchmode: imported 279 textures from the isolated path Assets/Janseon/ArtCandidates/Characters (identical to the original RGBA, 576x2560 with no downscaling); writes to the runtime Art path were rejected.
- Original preservation: the set SHA of the 1,481 original files from the preceding lane was identical before and after the work.
- Independent review: 41 files plus 17 supplementary files were pinned by SHA and reviewed on 4 axes; actual reads completed 41/41 58/58 41/41 58/58.

### What failed (original verdict, unchanged)

COMPOSITION FAIL, SPRITE_FIDELITY FAIL, PRODUCT_POLISH FAIL, TYPOGRAPHY PASS (limited to label and evidence legibility). Remaining defects: identity simplification (loss of the sealed helmet and the center-parted bob), rectangular bodies and detached gear in the knocked-down pose, hands separating during attacks, face color showing in the N-direction knockdown, and stiff walking and attacks. The final state is FROZEN_REVIEW_COMPLETE_ART_FAIL, with no promotion and no runtime hookup.

### Root cause analysis

1. Order error: we scaled up to producing all 276 frames before the base form (identity and key poses) had been accepted.
2. Misuse of proxy metrics: we treated frame byte uniqueness, scalar measurements, and GREEN tests as evidence of visual quality.
3. Environment assumption: we assumed the URP package in the manifest was the actual render pipeline. The actual GraphicsSettings value was built-in.
4. Delayed acknowledgment of the method''s limits: the judgment that assembling geometric primitives cannot recover the identity of the original illustrations was only settled after two rejections.

### The same incidents confirmed in earlier projects

- k-pop-diablo drift ledger (GitHub docs/art/drift-ledger.md, blob 60c96af8): front-face interpolation onto the back view when multi-direction input was missing, mismatches between export reports and fresh-import measurements, 0 runtime asset requests despite GREEN tests, and cumulative degradation from re-applying conversion results.
- makcha-unity visual lock plan: wrong catalog connections, unused materials, fonts, and clips, and albedo lost because only the shader was swapped.
- diablo gameplay topology: regardless of the 58k LOC simulation and 1,568 tests, only about 28 host calls - a separation between the amount implemented and actual playability.

## Decision

1. Promotion ban maintained. ArtCandidates assets exist only with status draft, rights unknown, and no runtime hookup. The Editor importer permits only the isolated path and rejects writes to the runtime Art path, and this contract is locked in by tests.
2. Pilot-first order. The next art production will expand only after 8 frames of a single Explorer (4-direction idle 4 + attack windup/strike 2 + hit 1 + knockdown 1) pass actual on-screen acceptance against the original for identity, gear attachment, and small-scale legibility. The 276 frames / 60 clips for the three characters remain the original requirement, not something to be scaled down.
3. Tool availability gate. Do not describe the ComfyUI server, image model weights, or the Blender-MCP connection as available before physical confirmation (running process, listener, license). Any new installation requires owner approval specifying model ID, size, and install location. Tripo must not be called without the owner''s prior permission.
4. Verification separation. Code GREEN, visual art approval, and rights confirmation are separate gates. Visual acceptance is granted only through a full-set review bound to the original source.
5. Evidence preservation. Review requests, original verdicts, and captures are preserved unchanged. Failed results are also recorded as results and are not overwritten by retries.

## Consequences

- This branch includes the candidate verification infrastructure (assembler, tests, Editor importer, PlayMode capture) and the draft candidate assets in the repository. Runtime code does not reference them.
- Follow-up character art work follows this ADR and docs/game-logic/Character-Art-Direction.md. Existing originals and FAIL records are not to be replaced.
- Large evidence (original JSONL, 276 renders, contact sheets) is in the git-ignored .omo/evidence/, and the verdict figures and hashes are tracked through this document and the review requests.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-003', 'decisions', 'adr-003', 'ADR-003: Invented Names Are Based on Real Place Names and Subway Station Names', '', 'accepted', 2, 'adr/ADR-003-real-place-and-station-naming.md', '# ADR-003: Invented Names Are Based on Real Place Names and Subway Station Names

- Status: Approved
- Decision date: 2026-09-16
- Scope: Place names invented by the game; names of states, houses, organizations, and theaters; and names for strongholds in invented narrative
- Change ledger: `RESEARCH/verification/creative-name-normalization.json`

## Background

The game documents contained side by side: expressions transcribing the names of real facilities, such as `Ttukdo`; expressions merging multiple place names, such as `Yeouisinjeong`; abbreviated expressions that players cannot find in the movement graph, such as `Doseong`, `Buksan`, and `Yangnyeong`; and fictional organizations that used real company names as they are. In this state, the names in the worldbuilding documents do not match the actual player movement nodes.

Player movement operates around `Station` nodes and the connections between stations rather than the administrative-district plane. `LORE/World-and-Subway-Layers.md` and `LORE/World-Map-Construction.md` treat station societies and strategic strongholds as `Station`, and define that the runtime `RouteGraph.CreateSeoul()` uses the station graph.

## Decision

1. The geographic anchor of an invented name uses a name that actually exists.
2. When a district name and a subway station name conflict or there are multiple candidates, the subway station name that the player sees on the movement node takes priority.
3. Names for states, houses, guilds, and corporate successor organizations are likewise formed by attaching a functional suffix to the representative station name.
4. Even when the original name of a real company, institution, or facility differs from the representative station name, the in-game invented organizations and strongholds are aligned to the representative station name.
5. Source materials, quotations, snapshots taken at acquisition, archives, and past verification evidence are not modified. This decision table applies only when projecting original names into game canon.
6. Stable IDs (`S01`, `HC01`, `HP01`, `XT01`, etc.) are not changed. Save data and relationship links continue to be keyed on IDs.

The basis for confirming station names is the Seoul Metro public data and the repository''s station graph. Outer theaters outside Seoul Metro''s jurisdiction use real place names.

## Representative changes

| ID | Previous name | Changed name | Representative anchor |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| S01 | Yeoui-Sinjeong Floodgate Government | Yeongdeungpo Floodgate Government | Yeongdeungpo |
| S02 | Southwest Manufacturing Alliance | Guro Manufacturing Alliance | Guro |
| S04 | Ttukdo Workshop Union | Ttukseom Workshop Union | Ttukseom |
| S06 | Capital City Records Office | Seoul Station Records Office | Seoul Station |
| S09 | Sangam Transmission Corporation | Digital Media City Transmission Corporation | Digital Media City |
| S10 | Northern Mountain Refuge League | Bukhansan Bogungmun Refuge League | Bukhansan Bogungmun |
| S13 | Yangnyeong Council League | Jegi-dong Council League | Jegi-dong |
| S14 | Acha-Guui Gateway Bureau | Guui Gateway Bureau | Guui |
| S15 | Garak-Jamsil Distribution Bureau | Garak Market Distribution Bureau | Garak Market |

The change ledger is the canonical source for the full mapping of states, houses, and theaters, and for retained items.

## Boundary Between Derivatives and Originals

The following paths preserve original materials or past states, so they must not be modified in bulk.

- `archive/**`
- `store/**`
- `RESEARCH/canon-reference/**`
- `RESEARCH/verification/worktree-cleanup/**`
- `TOOL/tools/regions/data/**`
- `LORE/regions/content/**`
- `LORE/regions/sources/**`
- `.omo/**`

`LORE/**` is the authoritative source for the game''s canon. The VitePress `WEB/wiki-source` and `https://seoul-kenshi.vercel.app` are regenerated from this source. The GitHub Wiki is no longer maintained.

## Change Log

- 2026-09-16: Finalized the naming priority, the source-preservation boundary, and the representative anchors for the 16 states, 32 houses, and external theaters.
- 2026-09-16: Decided to normalize `Ttukdo` to `Ttukseom` in the game''s fictional content, and to replace successor organizations based on real company names with fictional organization names based on station names.
- 2026-09-16: Source geographic data and its fact-preserving projection are excluded from the change targets, and context boundaries were added so that real-world names such as `Daegu Yangnyeongsijang` and `Seoulhanyangdoseong` are not changed by the fictional naming rules.
- 2026-09-16: Connected `TOOL/tools/policy/normalize-creative-names.mjs` to the default checks to block future regressions in creative naming.

## Result

Players can find the names of states, organizations, and strongholds they see in the documents on the actual station graph. Names and acquisition records from real-world sources are preserved as they are, and historical distortion occurs only in the game''s creative projection layer, and only explicitly.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-004', 'decisions', 'adr-004', 'ADR-004 — Root Domains and a Single Canonical Design Source', '', 'accepted', 3, 'adr/ADR-004-root-domain-structure.md', '# ADR-004 — Root Domains and a Single Canonical Design Source

- Status: Revision accepted 2026-09-21 (owner decision: GDD is the single canonical design source, ART-ASSETS is the canonical source for original art)
- Prerequisite: ADR-001 (Handover Policy — the handover of this restructuring was carried out under the owner authority of ADR-001)
- Implementation commit: `befb8da9` (full reorganization into 7 root domains, 2026-09-18)

## Background

After the 2026-09-14 seven-folder structure (Game/GDD/Design/Research/Reference/Tool/Backend) and the separation of Wikis, the owner ordered a full reorganization that divides documents into the GDD, LORE, GAME-LOGIC, GAME-REFERENCE, RESEARCH, and TOOL sub-domains and designates GAME as the Unity project. It was executed under a plan (.omo/plans/root-domain-restructure.md) that passed adversarial review (skeptic review, 21 defects).

## Decision

The repository root may contain only the domain directories below and root configuration files. Any root entry outside this list is rejected by the structure check in `TOOL/tools/policy/check-repo-delivery-policy.mjs` (fail-closed). The root `evidence/` is not an allowed domain, and all verification materials are kept only in the ignored `.omo/evidence/`.

| Domain | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `GDD/` | The canonical source for all game design: product, rules, references, architecture, art, ADRs, proposals, and system design |
| `LORE/` | The canonical source for the world setting (175 world-series documents + name-pools + regions) |
| `WEB/` | The official React wiki and the VitePress generation and verification surface. Not a canonical source |
| `GAME-REFERENCE/` | Asset BOM, UX references, moodboards, and geographic data (data) |
| `ART-ASSETS/` | The canonical source for editable original art, Blender, FBX, textures, and curated review evidence. Execution tools are owned by TOOL, and Unity-promoted builds are owned by GAME. |
| `RESEARCH/` | canon-reference·verification |
| `TOOL/` | The repository''s Node packages (tools), skills, submodules (unity-remote, character-forge), and Unity documentation |
| `GAME/` | The Unity project (editor pinned to 6000.7.0a5, batchmode only) |
| `Backend/` | .NET 8 CoreWCF server (kept at the root — owner''s choice) |
| `EVENT-APPLICATIONS/` | Materials for event submissions and application management. Excluded from game and release testing. |
| `store/` | Preservation of run outputs (kept at the root) |
| `archive/` | Worktree cleanup snapshots (sparse `!/archive/`, kept at the root) |

Allowed list of root configuration files: `.gitattributes` `.gitignore` `.gitmodules` `.vercelignore` `AGENTS.md` `CLAUDE.md` CONCEPT-type planning documents (`Concept.md` `Design.md` `Intent.md` `ToDo.md`) `CONTRIBUTING.md` `README.md` `SERVICES.md` `index.html` `package.json` `package-lock.json` `vercel.json`. As of 2026-09-21, the diagnostic utilities `test-regex.mjs` and `update_states.py`, which are already tracked on main, are allowed as legacy root utilities until they are cleaned up separately. This does not grant permission to add new root utilities. `LICENSE` is allowed as a root file. `WIKI`, `GDD`, `TOOL/unity-remote`, and `TOOL/document-renderer` are treated as exceptions in the root check only when they are submodule gitlinks with index mode 160000; ordinary files or directories with the same names are not separately allowed. The old WEB path and LICENSE.md are not restored to the current allowed list.

## Rules

1. **Structural invariance**: Adding new root directories or root files is not possible without amending this ADR (or a follow-up ADR). The policy check rejects it.
2. **No old world**: Recreating the top-level `Wikis/`, `Design/`, `Reference/`, `data/`, `Research/`, `Tool/`, or `Game/` (old casing) is prohibited.
3. **Document and asset placement contract**: The world setting goes only in LORE, all game design and rules only in GDD, visual and physical references and experiments only in GAME-REFERENCE, editable approved art originals only in ART-ASSETS, and research only in RESEARCH. TOOL owns asset-processing code, and GAME owns only Unity runtime imports that have passed a separate promotion check. WEB only reads and publishes the canonical sources and does not own their meaning.
4. **Distribution surface**: Public documentation uses only `WEB/wiki-source/dist` (local prebuilt, the 8-line chain in `.vercelignore`). Sub-path serving follows the SERVICES.md table + composite staging.
5. **Handover**: The handover procedure for the changes in this structure follows ADR-001.

## Deprecation

- The 2026-09-14 seven-folder structure (top-level Game/GDD/Design/Research/Reference/Tool/Backend) and the top-level `Wikis/` publishing canonical system are superseded by this ADR.
- The 2026-09-18 three-way split into LORE/GAME-LOGIC/GDD is abolished by the owner''s decision of 2026-09-20. There is a single canonical design source, GDD, and the publishing surface is separated into WEB.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-005', 'decisions', 'adr-005', 'ADR-005 — Backend Purpose: Host-Session-Based Multiplayer', '', 'accepted', 4, 'adr/ADR-005-backend-host-session-multiplayer.md', '# ADR-005 — Backend Purpose: Host-Session-Based Multiplayer

- Status: Accepted (owner direction confirmed 2026-09-18; the transport, identity, and storage clauses are superseded by [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/); the product session lifetime, authoritative process, participant identity root, and world preservation are amended by [ADR-008](/gdd/decisions/adr-008/))
- Prerequisite: ADR-004 (7 root domains — `Backend/` kept at the root), [Online User Journey](/gdd/design/online-user-journey/) (the online premises are updated by this ADR; the product session is governed by ADR-008)
- Follow-up: [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/) (backend implementation Y2K departure — supersedes Decisions 4, 5, and 6, the storage exception in Rule 2, and the ripple port and infrastructure specifications); [ADR-008](/gdd/decisions/adr-008/) (product local-first Steam sessions — amends the product interpretation of Decisions 2 and 3. Does not supersede the ASP.NET Core implementation)

## Background

The backend started as a .NET 8 CoreWCF JSON HTTP server derived from the in-house dotnet-server-template, shaped as an account-centric social SNG with Auth, Hero, Lobby, Station, and Social services (2026-09-14, local development, not deployed). At the same time, the game core is already implemented as a deterministic simulation — command log + ticks + state hash, purpose-specific RNG separation, and RequestId idempotent receipts — which fits exactly with a host-authoritative model that exchanges only commands, with no state synchronization.

Among the GAPs left by the online user journey (world creation and join method, tick authority, netcode, account server), the owner finalized the backend''s purpose on 2026-09-18: **not an always-on service that multiple users connect to, but session-based multiplayer that operates when the host turns on the multiplayer option.**

## Decision

1. The backend is not an always-on, multi-user central service but a **host session coordinator and relay**.
2. **The host is the authority.** The deterministic simulation runs only on the host client. The backend neither interprets nor executes the meaning of game commands; it reads only the fields needed for routing.
3. **Sessions are ephemeral.** A session opens when the host turns on the multiplayer option, and closes when the host turns it off or disconnects. Host migration, session persistence, matchmaking, and a friend system are not provided. The canonical world data is the host''s local save (the single-player campaign contract is preserved).
4. **Identity reuses the existing Auth.** Session participants are verified with the AccountIdx and SessionKey created by sign-up and login. *(Deprecated 2026-09-18, superseded by [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/): the existing Auth is deleted, and identity is issued and verified inside the coordinator process.)*
5. The Hero, Lobby, Station, and Social account services are preserved rather than deleted, but are set aside as **account meta features unrelated to sessions**. *(Deprecated 2026-09-18, superseded by [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/): the account meta services are deleted without being preserved.)*
6. **Transport separation.** Session creation, discovery, lookup, and termination follow the existing CoreWCF HTTP (1219) pipeline, while the real-time relay (guest commands→host, host event batches→guests) opens on a separate Kestrel WebSocket port (setting `RealtimePort`, default 1220). Because Y2K `WcfHttpServerCore` owns 1219, the same port is not used. *(Deprecated 2026-09-18, superseded by [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/): Y2K and CoreWCF are dropped, and everything is served from a single Kestrel on 1219 using Minimal API + raw WebSocket. The dual-port `RealtimePort` setup goes away.)*

## Rules

1. If backend code contains game rules (combat, economy, or political adjudication), it violates this ADR.
2. Session state is kept nowhere but in process memory (accounts and specs remain in the existing MySQL and Redis). *(Parenthetical exception deprecated 2026-09-18, [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/): the external MySQL and Redis dependencies are removed, so all server state lives only in process memory. The in-memory session state rule in the main text is retained.)*
3. The maximum number of guests per session and timeouts are operational settings, not game design values. Design values that are still undecided must not be turned into backend constants.
4. When sharing contracts with the Unity client, do not pull UnityEngine types into the backend (the Backend/AGENTS.md rule is retained).

## Impact

- The premise of the online user journey, the "shared authoritative ledger," is updated to the "host ledger," and the tick authority GAP is closed as "host" (reflected in the same change bundle).
- Unity client network integration (Foundation driver and adapter) is a separate module and is outside the scope of this ADR.
- When the server runs, two ports open: 1219 (HTTP) and 1220 (WebSocket). There is no change to the Docker infrastructure (MySQL 13306, Redis 16379). *(Deprecated 2026-09-18, [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/): the runtime port is reduced to a single Kestrel 1219, and the Docker MySQL and Redis infrastructure is removed.)*');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-006', 'decisions', 'adr-006', 'ADR-006 — Backend Implementation: Leaving Y2K and Adopting an ASP.NET Core Coordinator', '', 'accepted', 5, 'adr/ADR-006-backend-aspnet-core-coordinator.md', '# ADR-006 — Backend Implementation: Leaving Y2K and Adopting an ASP.NET Core Coordinator

- Status: Accepted (owner direction finalized 2026-09-18; product session lifetime, authoritative process, participant identity root, and world preservation are revised by [ADR-008](/gdd/decisions/adr-008/). ASP.NET Core, Kestrel 1219, leaving Y2K, and the ban on external storage are retained)
- Predecessor: ADR-005 (Backend purpose — host-session-based multiplayer. This ADR keeps that purpose while replacing the technology stack that carries it)
- Successor: [ADR-008](/gdd/decisions/adr-008/) (product GDD. It does not discard this ADR''s coordinator implementation record)

## Background

ADR-005 finalized the backend''s purpose as "a host session coordinator and relay, not an always-on multi-user service," but the actual thing was still a .NET 8 CoreWCF JSON HTTP server derived from the Y2K template. The Auth, Hero, Lobby, Station, and Social account meta services, the MySQL and Redis storage boundary, and the dual-port setup of 1219 (CoreWCF HTTP) and 1220 (WebSocket) all remained as they were even after the purpose changed.

A host session coordinator cannot justify the framework burden of an account-centric SNG server, the dependency on template binaries, RPC contracts, or external storage. On 2026-09-18 the owner finalized leaving Y2K: rewrite the server as a single pure ASP.NET Core process, and delete anything that does not fit the session coordinator role.

## Decision

1. The server is **a single pure ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) Kestrel with exactly one port, 1219**. Session creation, discovery, lookup, and termination are served through Minimal API REST endpoints, and the real-time relay (guest commands→host, host event batches→guests) through a **raw WebSocket upgrade** on the same port (not SignalR). The 1219/1220 dual-port split and the `RealtimePort` setting are discarded.
2. **Leave Y2K and CoreWCF.** Remove the dependency on the Y2K Core framework (`server/Y2K/`) and the CoreWCF transport and service contracts. No code that inherits the template contracts is left behind.
3. **Delete the account meta services.** Auth, Hero, Lobby, Station, and Social are erased without preservation or archiving. Git history holds the record.
4. **Identity is issued in-process.** The coordinator itself issues and verifies session participant credentials. Reuse of the existing Auth sign-up and login, and of AccountIdx and SessionKey, is discarded.
5. **The HTTP contract is REST + ProblemDetails.** Error responses follow `application/problem+json`. The CoreWCF JSON RPC envelope is not carried over.
6. **There is no external storage.** The MySQL and Redis dependencies and the Docker infrastructure (MySQL 13306, Redis 16379) are removed. The state the server holds (sessions and credentials) exists only in process memory and disappears on restart.
7. **The purpose stays the same.** Host authority (ADR-005 Decision 2) and session ephemerality (Decision 3) are carried over unchanged in this coordinator implementation record. This ADR does not change the backend''s purpose; it chooses a vessel suited to that purpose. The product GDD''s authoritative process, session lifetime, and identity root are revised by [ADR-008](/gdd/decisions/adr-008/).

## Rules

1. **Deletion means deletion.** Y2K, CoreWCF, and account meta code are not preserved by moving them to an archive directory or a separate solution. If a rollback is ever needed, look in the git history.
2. New endpoints are added only with Minimal API + ProblemDetails. Do not bring back template TCP routing, RPC-style contracts, or custom error envelopes.
3. Do not delegate the issuance and verification of identity credentials to an out-of-process service or shared storage. If an external account provider gets involved, it violates this ADR.
4. The purpose rules of ADR-005 (no game rules, separating operational settings from design values, disconnection from UnityEngine) are carried over, and this ADR does not relax them.

## Impact

- ADR-005 Decision 4 (reusing the existing Auth), Decision 5 (preserving account meta), Decision 6 (transport separation), the MySQL and Redis storage exception in Rule 2, and the two-port 1219/1220 specification in the Impact section are superseded by this ADR. The purpose clauses of ADR-005 (Decisions 1, 2, and 3) remain valid in this coordinator implementation record. For the interpretation of product sessions, see [ADR-008](/gdd/decisions/adr-008/).
- The server runtime port is reduced to a single 1219, and the Docker infrastructure (MySQL 13306, Redis 16379) goes away. Local development environment requirements become simpler.
- Code replacement and deletion delivery proceeds as a separate implementation task after this ADR. This document records only the decision.
- The descriptions in the Backend documents (README, AGENTS.md) and the [Online User Journey](/gdd/design/online-user-journey/) will be updated at implementation delivery.

## Deprecation

- The structure derived from the Y2K Core framework (`server/Y2K/`, the Template→SeoulKenshi lineage) and the CoreWCF JSON HTTP pipeline (1219) are discarded by this ADR.
- The Auth, Hero, Lobby, Station, and Social account meta services are deleted by this ADR (the "preservation" in ADR-005 Decision 5 is void).
- The transport separation using a separate real-time port (default 1220, `RealtimePort`) (ADR-005 Decision 6) is discarded.
- The MySQL·Redis storage boundary and the Docker infrastructure (13306·16379) are retired (including the parenthetical exception in ADR-005 Rule 2).');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-007', 'decisions', 'adr-007', 'ADR-007: Per-PR Evidence Lifecycle', '', 'accepted', 6, 'adr/ADR-007-pr-evidence-lifecycle.md', '# ADR-007: Per-PR Evidence Lifecycle

- Status: Accepted on 2026-09-21
- Decider: Repository Owner
- Scope: Private local retention of all captures, receipts, and QA evidence, and their exclusion from public paths

## Context

By the owner''s decision, verification materials are not kept in the repository, PRs, or deployments. Public paths create permanent retention, unintended exposure, and LFS costs, and a private local reference is enough for review.

## Decision

1. All captures, receipts, and QA evidence go only in the ignored `.omo/evidence/`.
2. The root `evidence/` is a forbidden path. Do not add it, force-add it, attach it to a PR, or use it as a deployment input.
3. 
4. The repository gate rejects a tracked root `evidence/` and a tracked `.omo/`, and prevents the publishing manifest from taking root evidence as input.
5. PRs, CI, and deployments do not upload, publish, or copy private evidence.
6. Deletion and retention apply only to the current working path and do not rewrite past Git/LFS history or the remote repository.
7. Producers use `.omo/evidence/` for default output and receipts.

## Compatibility with Existing Evidence

Files that were in the root `evidence/` before this decision are removed from the current path with the owner''s permission. Any actual bytes that are still usable are preserved in the ignored `.omo/evidence/` together with their SHA-256 and provenance before removal.

## Result

- The basis for review remains local and private and is not included in the repository diff.
- There are no evidence paths in the repository or public artifacts.
- Private evidence is not force-added or uploaded to external services.
- Runtime and canonical assets are promoted only through the approval procedure of the path that owns them, and QA evidence itself is not eligible for promotion.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ADR-008', 'decisions', 'adr-008', 'ADR-008 — Local-First Steam Sessions and Lineage Checkpoints', '', 'accepted', 7, 'adr/ADR-008-local-first-steam-sessions.md', '# ADR-008 — Local-First Steam Sessions and Lineage Checkpoints

Current product planning gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 singleplayer contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the provisions in this document on a shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupancy, and online proxies are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the superseded scope in the [Singleplayer Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are automatically converted.

- Status: Accepted (owner direction confirmed 2026-09-22)
- Prerequisites: [ADR-005](/gdd/decisions/adr-005/) (purpose of host sessions), [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/) (implementation record of the ASP.NET Core coordinator), [Online User Journey](/gdd/design/online-user-journey/), [Does the Same Choice Give the Same Result?](/gdd/rules/save-and-determinism/), [From Strategy to Battle](/gdd/rules/strategy-battle-roundtrip/)
- Superseded scope: The provisions where ADR-005 Decisions 2 and 3 and ADR-006 Decision 7 act as the product session lifetime, authoritative process, root of participant identity, and world preservation. ASP.NET Core, Kestrel 1219, Y2K Escape, and the ban on external storage are not superseded.
- This document is a product GDD. It is not a record of completed implementation.

## Background

ADR-005 assumed sessions opened by a host rather than an always-on multi-user central service, and ADR-006 carried that assumption in an in-process-memory coordinator. In that model, the deterministic simulation runs on the host client, session credentials disappear together with the session itself, and the canonical world is kept in one place, the host''s local storage. Friend lobbies, dedicated servers, Steam authentication, direct LAN connection, and checkpoint fork policy were left unaddressed.

On 2026-09-22 the owner confirmed the product contract as local-first. The cloud does not own the canonical world. The authoritative simulation is not the client but a headless server process of the same form. Steam handles only lobbies, transport, authority evidence, and optional server advertisement, and does not own world state. LAN without Steam must work through direct transport.

The ASP.NET Core coordinator in `Backend/` is the implementation record of ADR-006. Do not regard this record as completed implementation of the product contract.

## Decision

1. **The product is local-first.** It is not cloud-first. The Unity client launches the bundled headless authoritative server as a separate child process, and the local player connects to it over localhost. An optionally installable headless dedicated server uses the same server executable, world, and configuration formats.
2. **The Steam Dedicated Server Tool is distributed free as a separate Tool/AppID.** The goal is anonymous SteamCMD installation, with per-OS depots. The actual package and permission settings need confirmation from the Steamworks partner. The dedicated tool can create new worlds and lineages without owning the game. Actual players must have a legitimate signed client and DRM proof.
3. **Steamworks provides session support only.** Friend and ad hoc sessions use Steam Lobby and invitations. The default for internet transport is Steam Networking Sockets and Steam Datagram Relay. Steam authentication tickets prove player authority and online identity. Public dedicated servers can be advertised through the Game Server API and the server browser. Steam features do not own authoritative world state.
4. **LAN without Steam works through direct LAN transport and discovery.** The canonical root of participant identity is the world-scoped participant public key and recovery secret. The SteamID is optional evidence tied to a Steam session. Clients whose signed client and local DRM proof are unverified can read local saves and play singleplayer, but cannot host or join multiplayer or sign checkpoints.
5. **Only verified checkpoints become host seeds.** Every verified checkpoint contains the world state, the event ledger position, the in-progress battle checkpoint (if any), the build, schema, content, and protocol versions, the lineage ID, the parent checkpoint hash, participant bindings, the fork policy, and the signature and hash. Every participant receives a signed checkpoint receipt and projection metadata so that they can verify the current world and ledger position and preserve their own archive and bindings. A complete, hostable world snapshot is distributed or exported only when that checkpoint''s immutable fork policy is **Allow**. Participants who are forbidden to fork are not given state complete enough to seed a server, but they continue to receive their own archive and character records. Only a fully verified atomic checkpoint can be used to start hosting on an authoritative server.
6. **Hosting a received checkpoint always creates a permanent independent fork.** A new lineage ID and key are generated. Forks are not merged automatically. Participants receive a public key for verification and do not receive the parent lineage''s signing secret. Only the holder of the original lineage key can continue the same lineage. A lost lineage secret can be restored only from an encrypted recovery backup. Without a backup, the parent lineage is read-only and progress is possible only by forking.
7. **The public dedicated server operator chooses whether forking is allowed or forbidden.** That policy is signed into each checkpoint and does not change for that checkpoint. A policy change applies only to subsequent checkpoints. Only checkpoints that allow forking include and export a complete, hostable world snapshot.
8. **Session credentials are volatile. World and character bindings remain in checkpoints.** `personId` occupancy does not expire when a session ends or goes idle. It is released only when the participant explicitly gives it up. A host kick blocks rejoining but does not release occupancy or erase character records.
9. **Rejoin identity is the world participant recovery secret.** If the secret is lost, the host can reissue it after confirming the person''s identity outside the game, and the old secret is revoked. There is no automatic in-game identity proof.
10. **An offline occupied character remains a daily-life and political NPC in the world.** They cannot start an expedition or make player-only decisions. They can be injured or die. At the moment of death, the succession rule already set is evaluated immediately, and the binding is transferred or a game over is recorded. An offline successor is a daily-life and political NPC until they reconnect.
11. **Each participant keeps a local archive of the character records they played or who died.** The host world holds the original records. If a local archive is lost, it can be reconstructed from records signed by the host after the recovery secret and a human check.
12. **World time flows only while at least one player is connected.** When the count reaches zero, the server writes and distributes a verified checkpoint and then halts world and NPC simulation. If the last player leaves during battle, the immutable battle context, accepted commands, and current battle state are immediately captured in a checkpoint and the server halts. It does not auto-resolve or roll back.
13. **After an abnormal shutdown, the next start automatically reads the most recent complete checkpoint that passes signature and hash checks, and clearly reports conflicts and the lost interval.** A normal shutdown blocks new joins, saves and checkpoints, finishes distribution and confirmation, and then exits.
14. **Immutable executables and runtime are separated from persistent world, configuration, checkpoint, and backup data.** Each active lineage has exactly one writer. The default end-user path is a native child process. Docker and systemd packaging is an optional path for dedicated operators and is not required of players.

## Rules

1. It is a violation of this ADR if Steam lobbies, sockets, authentication tickets, or the server browser become the canonical source of world state, the ledger, or checkpoint signatures.
2. Hosting is not started from a partially verified, corrupted, or signature-failed checkpoint. Rejection is a clear error, and the previous backup is preserved.
3. Do not state that a full hostable snapshot can be exported from a fork-prohibited checkpoint, or that a received checkpoint can be hosted as a continuation of the same lineage.
4. Do not release a `personId` claim or delete a person''s record solely because a session ended, went idle, or the player was kicked.
5. Do not keep running world time, NPC daily routines, or combat when the player count is 0, and do not auto-settle or roll back the last departing player''s battle.
6. Do not cite this document as evidence that the Unity or Backend implementation is complete.

## Product GDD and Implementation Status

| Layer | Authority | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Product Sessions, Hosting, and Checkpoints | This ADR, [Online User Journey](/gdd/design/online-user-journey/), [Save Rules](/gdd/rules/save-and-determinism/), [Strategy-Battle Roundtrip](/gdd/rules/strategy-battle-roundtrip/) | Document contract as of 2026-09-22. Not implemented |
| Current Backend | [ADR-006](/gdd/decisions/adr-006/), `Backend/server/Coordinator` | An in-process-memory coordinator. It is not the headless authoritative server described in this ADR |
| Current Unity | Existing battle surface | It is not the local child server or Steam session described in this ADR |

ADR-006''s single ASP.NET Core Kestrel, the removal of Y2K and CoreWCF, the deletion of account metadata, and the ban on external MySQL and Redis remain on record as the current backend implementation. This ADR does not discard that stack; it separates the product''s target runtime from that coordinator, stating that the two are not the same.

## Provisions Amended from ADR-005 and ADR-006

- In the product, ADR-005 Decision 2, "only the host client runs the simulation," becomes "a bundled or dedicated headless server is the authority." The prohibition on the relay layer interpreting game rules is retained.
- The session volatility in ADR-005 Decision 3 is retained only for session eligibility. The canonical world record is the distributed, verified checkpoint. "No friend system or matchmaking" conflicts with Steam Lobby, invitations, and the optional server browser, so it is discarded from the product contract. The rule that the same lineage is not automatically inherited through host migration is retained, even more strongly, as a fork rule.
- The in-process issued identity of ADR-006 Decision 4 is not the canonical root of product identity. The canonical root of product identity is the world participant''s public key and recovery secret.
- The host-client authority inherited by ADR-006 Decision 7, along with "canonical world record = a single host-local save," is amended by this ADR at the product layer.

## Impact

- Align the assumptions about accounts, entry, claims, archives, and the flow of time in the [Online User Journey](/gdd/design/online-user-journey/) with this contract.
- Record distributed checkpoints, lineage, forks, signatures, atomic import, and recovery in the [Save Rules](/gdd/rules/save-and-determinism/).
- Record the last player''s departure and conflict checkpoints in [Strategy to Battle](/gdd/rules/strategy-battle-roundtrip/).
- The Steamworks AppID, Tool AppID, packages, and whether the anonymous SteamCMD package is included remain a GAP until partner setup is complete.
- The Unity network driver, the headless server binary, and the Steamworks SDK integration are separate implementation modules and are outside the scope of this document.

## Research Sources

Citations are limited to platform capabilities and operating patterns that justify the product contract. Valheim''s game code is not open source and is not being taken from it.

- Steam Networking Overview — The latest API relays through the Valve network by default and also supports plain UDP. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/multiplayer/networking>
- ISteamNetworkingSockets — A connection-oriented messaging API. `CreateListenSocketIP`/`ConnectByIPAddress` use plain UDP, `CreateListenSocketP2P`/`ConnectP2P` use relayed P2P, and local loopback socket pairs are supported. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/api/ISteamNetworkingSockets>
- Steam Datagram Relay — Relays internet game traffic through Valve''s backbone to hide IPs and applies authentication and encryption. It can also be used with dedicated servers, but it does not own world state. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/multiplayer/steamdatagramrelay>
- Steam Matchmaking & Lobbies — Lobbies, search, and invitations for friends and ad hoc sessions. A lobby is closer to a backend chat room than a game world. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/multiplayer/matchmaking>
- Game Servers — Dedicated servers and the Steam server browser. It presupposes community hosting. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/multiplayer/game_servers>
- ISteamGameServer — Verifies tickets via login, heartbeat, and `BeginAuthSession`, and posts server browser metadata. `k_unServerFlagPrivate` is used to turn off the master list and forced authentication, as on a LAN. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/api/ISteamGameServer>
- ISteamUser.GetAuthSessionTicket — Issues a ticket whose integrity and ownership the other party verifies with `BeginAuthSession`. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/api/ISteamUser#GetAuthSessionTicket>
- Depots — A depot can be mounted per OS and architecture. Platform-specific content goes in the corresponding OS depot. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/store/application/depots>
- Uploading to Steam / SteamPipe — Build uploads and depot configuration. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/sdk/uploading>
- Distributing Your Dedicated Game Server — A dedicated server requires a separate TOOL AppID, the Dedicated Server Redistributables, `steam_appid.txt` (the game AppID), and the option to include the anonymous SteamCMD package (17906). The partner confirms the actual permissions. <https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/sdk/uploading/distributing_gs>
- SteamCMD — The anonymous installation path for dedicated servers. At the time of writing, the full text of the wiki page could not be checked because of bot protection. <https://developer.valvesoftware.com/wiki/SteamCMD>
- Valheim Dedicated Server Guide — A separate Steam tool, separate paths for the executable and the world and permission files, port ranges, and a recommendation for graceful shutdown (CTRL+C). Only the operating patterns are used as reference. <https://valheim.com/support/a-guide-to-dedicated-servers/>
- community-valheim-tools/valheim-server-docker — A Docker and systemd wrapper with the configuration volume separated from the executable. Only the operational packaging pattern is used as reference. The game server itself is not open source. 

## Unresolved Steamworks Partner Settings

Facts this ADR does not lock down. Confirm them on the partner screens before implementation or store submission.

- The game AppID and the dedicated server Tool AppID values
- Whether the "Include this tool in Dedicated Server package" box is checked, and whether the anonymous package 17906 is included
- The per-OS depot and package ownership mapping, and whether the Dedicated Server Redistributables are actually enabled
- Whether to include the dedicated tool in the game package and expose it under Tools in the Steam library
- Whether server browser advertising is on by default or left to the operator''s choice
- Whether to use SDR-hosted dedicated servers (in known data centers). This is not a requirement for community dedicated servers
- The operating policy for binding the Family Sharing owner''s SteamID to the participant''s public key

## Deprecation

The following are discarded from the product GDD. The bytes of the historical sentences are retained and are pointed to by the follow-up links in this section and the header.

- The assumption that the host client process is the authoritative simulation for product multiplayer
- The assumption that when a session closes, the canonical world record and person claims disappear with it
- The assumption that the product does not provide friend lobbies, invitations, or dedicated server advertising
- The assumption that the coordinator process''s in-memory identity is the canonical record of product participants
- The assumption that a cloud session keeps world time running at all times');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Unity-Architecture-Implementation-Plan', 'architecture', 'unity-architecture-implementation-plan', 'Unity Architecture Implementation Plan', '', 'accepted', 8, 'architecture/Unity-Architecture-Implementation-Plan.md', '# Unity Architecture Implementation Plan

## Current Implementation Authority Mapping

[CH11 Implementation Mapping — Current Product/Source Evidence](/gdd/rules/implementation-mapping/)

Seoul Facts: Follow the Core/Data/Foundation, serialization references, uGUI, host client simulation, and volatile Backend contracts in https://github.com/islee23520/seoul-dengoku/blob/3e3c03349eafaaf88347d119a4bdd0c6d44abc53/AGENTS.md#L64-L76. Do not interpret the past blueprints below as shipped facts. CK3 reference: the data separation and serialization in internal-architecture-synthesis.md:95-104 are technique examples and do not prove hot-reload or multiplayer/replay consumption. Product authority: do not add new storage, account services, world pulses, or debug features without approval.

Current product planning gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 singleplayer contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the provisions in this document on a shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupancy, and online proxies are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the superseded scope in the [Singleplayer Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are automatically converted.

This plan defines the order for turning the [Unity System Design Contract](/gdd/architecture/unity-system-design/) into code, and the stop conditions for each stage. Do not change product code until the design contract and this plan have been merged into the Wiki.

> The current individual-adjudication combat revision is documentation-only and adds no new runtime work to this plan.

## Contract Summary

The current increment is limited to a single `Foundation` module. `Bootstrap.unity` owns the App VContainer scope and the **FSM**, and `Foundation.unity` becomes a screen with an explicit child scope. Static **Singletons** and service locators are prohibited, and future **Repositories** are kept as Unity-free inward contracts. Detailed rules for **Saving and Determinism**, **Failure and Cancellation**, testing, and **Quality Gates** follow the System Design Contract.

## Implementation Order

| Order | Task | Prerequisites | Completion Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Lock the Wiki Contract | Research and design verification PASS | Wiki test RED→GREEN log |
| 2 | Lock the root work contract | Wiki GREEN | `Concept.md`, `ToDo.md`, GitHub remote |
| 3 | Write FSM tests | Before product code changes | Correct missing type/behavior RED |
| 4 | Implement VContainer and a minimal FSM | FSM RED | EditMode GREEN |
| 5 | Assemble Bootstrap/Foundation | FSM GREEN | compile, scope/build-order tests |
| 6 | Implement static quality gates | Structure files exist | mutation RED→cleanup→GREEN |
| 7 | Actual PlayMode QA | compile/tests GREEN | Screenshots and records of allowed/rejected transitions |
| 8 | Full verification | All increments complete | Wiki, gate, Unity tests, four reviews PASS |

## Stage 1 — Wiki Contract

### RED

`node tools/wiki/test-build-wiki.mjs`

It must fail if `Unity-System-Design.md` and this plan are missing, or if the sidebar link and the required contracts (FSM, VContainer, Singleton, Repository, saving and determinism, failure and cancellation, quality gates) are absent.

### GREEN

Create the two documents and link them from `_Sidebar.md` and `Home.md`. Re-read the links and required contracts in the built Wiki.

## Stage 2 — Work Scope Gate

Before changing product code, create the root `Concept.md` and `ToDo.md`.

- Concept describes the game and the Unity 6000.7.0a5, VContainer 1.19.0, URP/Input System/Test Framework baselines.
- The only current module in ToDo is the Foundation architecture.
- This is not a UI screen design change, so no new `Design.md` is created in this increment.
- `git remote -v` must show a GitHub remote.

## Stage 3 — FSM RED

Write the EditMode tests before the product code.

| Case | Input | PASS Condition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| happy | `Booting + OpenFoundation` | `Transitioning`, one load |
| readiness | Load complete, ready not complete | No `Foundation` commit |
| success | `SceneLoadSucceeded` | `Foundation`, receipt present |
| illegal | Disallowed state/trigger | Typed rejection, zero calls |
| duplicate | Second request during a transition | Busy, zero additional loads |
| failure | Load or scope failure | staging dispose, Faulted |
| retry | `Faulted + Retry` | New transition ID, success possible |

RED must be a missing type, a missing member, or an expected-behavior mismatch. Syntax errors and fixture errors are not evidence.

## Stage 4 — Minimal GREEN

### FSM

- `ApplicationFlowState`
- `ApplicationFlowTrigger`
- typed `TransitionOutcome`
- pure transition table
- single-flight coordinator
- Generation and transition ID

The first GREEN is obtained with a fake scene loader, without the real scene API. Commit state only after the ready signal.

### VContainer

Add only `jp.hadashikick.vcontainer` `#1.19.0`, which was actually verified in Makcha. Do not add packages without callers, such as Addressables, Entities, and LitMotion.

- `AppLifetimeScope`: registers the FSM, scene catalog, and scene loader
- `FoundationLifetimeScope`: registers only screen objects
- Explicit child composition using parent enqueue
- Ownership tests for scope dispose and cancellation

## Stage 5 — Scene Assembly

The editor builder makes the following results repeatable.

1. `Bootstrap.unity`: contains only the App scope and the bootstrap root
2. Build Settings: Bootstrap 0, Foundation 1

`DontDestroyOnLoad` is prohibited outside Bootstrap. Screens and gameplay objects are removed along with the Foundation unload.

## Step 6 — Repository/Singleton Boundary Quality Gate

This increment does not build a campaign Repository implementation. Instead, it fixes boundaries that can be inspected.

- No UnityEngine or VContainer imports in Repository/Core target paths
- No runtime mutable static `Instance`, `Current`, or collections
- No service locators or runtime object search
- Scene load APIs are allowed only through the adapter allowlist
- `DontDestroyOnLoad` is allowed only on the Bootstrap allowlist

mutation proof:

1. Add a prohibited `DontDestroyOnLoad` or `Current` to a temporary runtime file.
2. `node tools/architecture/check-unity-architecture.mjs` must print the rule name and file and exit with a nonzero code.
3. Remove the temporary file.
4. The same command must exit with 0.
5. Record in the cleanup receipt that no temp files or processes remain.

## Step 7 — Preserving Save, Determinism, Failure, and Cancellation Contracts

The Foundation implementation does not save yet, but it must not violate future boundaries.

- Do not use scene timing, frame count, or wall clock in FSM results
- Use a stable ID and a typed result for every transition request
- Distinguish cancellation from failure
- Check the generation on late callbacks
- Disposing a child scope cancels the token first
- No fire-and-forget, fixed sleeps, or polling

Future Repositories and save adapters must follow the temp → verify → backup → atomic swap sequence and the purpose-specific RNG stream rules.

## Step 8 — Unity Verification

After the file changes, run the following in the actual Unity Editor.

```text
unity run Game --editor-version 6000.7.0a5 -- -nographics -executeMethod Janseon.Foundation.Editor.FoundationProjectBuilder.BuildFoundationScene -logFile -
unity test Game --editor-version 6000.7.0a5 --mode EditMode --report-format junit --output .omo/evidence/unity/editmode.xml --timeout 600
unity test Game --editor-version 6000.7.0a5 --mode PlayMode --report-format junit --output .omo/evidence/unity/playmode.xml --timeout 600

```

In Play Mode, confirm that Bootstrap opens Foundation and that there is exactly one App scope and one child scope. Through the public flow API, send a request while already in Foundation, or an illegal trigger, and observe a typed rejection and zero additional loads.

## Visual Verification

Run the actual Unity Play Mode with `-batchmode`, and record the results of a fixed camera''s RenderTexture or ScreenCapture as PNG and JSON receipts. Review the following four axes independently; if any one is not an explicit PASS, the work is not complete.

- COMPOSITION
- SPRITE_FIDELITY
- TYPOGRAPHY
- PRODUCT_POLISH

If there is no batchmode PlayMode capture or receipt, do not substitute static verification; record it as unverified. Do not use the interactive Editor or uLoop.

## Final Quality Gate

Run each of the following once in a clean state.

1. `node tools/wiki/test-build-wiki.mjs`
2. `node tools/architecture/check-unity-architecture.mjs`
3. Unity compile
4. EditMode tests
5. PlayMode tests
6. Actual Play Mode allow/reject scenarios
7. Four visual reviews

Complete only after cleaning up all temporary mutations, Unity processes, capture contexts, and temp files.

## Completion Criteria

- The FSM is the sole authority for scene transitions.
- The VContainer App/child scope relationship and disposal are verified.
- Static Singletons/service locators fail the quality gate.
- The Repository boundary is protected by a Unity-free contract.
- There is no code that breaks the rules on saving, determinism, failure, and cancellation.
- The wiki, architecture gate, compile, EditMode, PlayMode, and actual screen verification all PASS.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Unity-Architecture', 'architecture', 'unity-architecture', 'Unity Structure', '', 'accepted', 9, 'architecture/Unity-Architecture.md', '# Unity Structure

## Current Implementation Authority Mapping

[CH11 Implementation Mapping — Current Product/Source Evidence](/gdd/rules/implementation-mapping/)

Seoul Facts: Follow the Core/Data/Foundation, serialization references, uGUI, host client simulation, and volatile Backend contracts in https://github.com/islee23520/seoul-dengoku/blob/3e3c03349eafaaf88347d119a4bdd0c6d44abc53/AGENTS.md#L64-L76. Do not interpret the past blueprints below as shipped facts. CK3 reference: the data separation and serialization in internal-architecture-synthesis.md:95-104 are technique examples and do not prove hot-reload or multiplayer/replay consumption. Product authority: do not add new storage, account services, world pulses, or debug features without approval.

Current product planning gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 singleplayer contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the provisions in this document on a shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupancy, and online proxies are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the superseded scope in the [Singleplayer Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are automatically converted.

## Baseline

The current foundation is Unity `6000.7.0a5`. Because it is a pre-release version, pin the editor version and the package lock file together, and verify separately before upgrading the version.

The currently implemented foundation is as follows.

- Unity `6000.7.0a5` project
- Genre contract JSON and constants, EditMode genre contract tests
- URP, Input System, Test Framework, VContainer `1.19.0`
- `Bootstrap.unity` App scope and FSM, `Foundation.unity` screen child scope
- Single scene-load authority and static architecture gate

The new goal is Norland-style individual-resolution combat (Decision 13). Only in documentation, it defines pre-engagement squad selection, move/attack-target/fire/ambush commands, preview/confirm/cancel, command locking during melee engagement, and combat-unit retreat. Command queues, waypoints, and speed control are not promoted to new runtime contracts.

The combat camera is a 3D free command view confirmed by the owner (2026-09-24). It provides pan, orbit, and zoom, but does not set angle or field-of-view values. Combat has no pause, slow-down, or fast-forward (owner decision, 2026-09-24). Command preview, confirm, and cancel are applied to squads before engagement, and confirmed replacement commands are applied in deterministic order on the next simulation step. The shared campaign world and other parties continue to flow regardless of combat.

Generated art slots, the 16-nation campaign, and aggregated casualty representation are not yet complete.

## Responsibilities by Area

The current repository assemblies are `Janseon.Core` (engine prohibited), `Janseon.Foundation`, `Janseon.Data`, and `Janseon.Art`. Battle contracts and simulation live in the `Battle.Contracts` and `Battle.Sim` namespaces inside Core, and there is no separate `Janseon.Battle` assembly.

| Module | Responsibility | UnityEngine Reference |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Janseon.Core` | ID, time, command, event, route, campaign, settlement | Prohibited |
| `Janseon.Data` | Unity Authoring Data | Allowed |
| `Janseon.Foundation` | Scene flow, screens, uGUI, combat driver | Allowed |
| `Janseon.Art` | Runtime slots and candidates | Allowed |

## Rules

- Engine-independent modules must not hold global mutable state.
- Screens do not modify state directly; they send commands.
- Every state change is recorded in the event ledger.
- Advanced packages are added only after real usage and failing tests exist.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Unity-System-Design', 'architecture', 'unity-system-design', 'Unity System Design Contract', '', 'accepted', 10, 'architecture/Unity-System-Design.md', '# Unity System Design Contract

## Current Implementation Authority Mapping

[CH11 Implementation Mapping — Current Product/Source Evidence](/gdd/rules/implementation-mapping/)

Seoul Facts: Follow the Core/Data/Foundation, serialization references, uGUI, host client simulation, and volatile Backend contracts in https://github.com/islee23520/seoul-dengoku/blob/3e3c03349eafaaf88347d119a4bdd0c6d44abc53/AGENTS.md#L64-L76. Do not interpret the past blueprints below as shipped facts. CK3 reference: the data separation and serialization in internal-architecture-synthesis.md:95-104 are technique examples and do not prove hot-reload or multiplayer/replay consumption. Product authority: do not add new storage, account services, world pulses, or debug features without approval.

Current product planning gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 singleplayer contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the provisions in this document on a shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupancy, and online proxies are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the superseded scope in the [Singleplayer Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are automatically converted.

This document is the architecture contract fixed before any code was written. The contract keywords (FSM, VContainer, Singleton, Repository, Saving and Determinism, Failure and Cancellation, Quality Gate) remain unchanged. The increment table in this document describes the Foundation separation contract.

The new combat documents use the 3D free-command camera confirmed by the owner, and have no combat pause or slow-down/speed-up. No new camera values are invented, and combat does not stop the campaign world or the scope of any other party. Accepted replacement commands have a stable order and are applied on the next simulation step. This document revision does not mean the runtime implementation is complete.

## Design Goals and Non-Goals

The goal is to keep a single scene from owning the app lifecycle, screens, game state, and saving all at once. Only the explicit **FSM** changes app flow, and **VContainer** scopes own object lifetimes.

Goals of the first increment:

- Separation of responsibilities between `Bootstrap.unity` and `Foundation.unity`
- An app FSM with a single transition authority
- An explicit parent relationship between the App scope and screen child scopes
- A typed contract for illegal, duplicate, failed, and retried results
- An automated quality gate that blocks static Singletons and service locators

Non-goals of the first increment:

- Implementing campaign, world map, combat, or actual saving features
- Creating empty assemblies for future modules
- Copying all packages from the Makcha project

## Scene Ownership

| Scene | Ownership Scope | May Have | Must Not Have |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Bootstrap.unity` | Process | App FSM, scene catalog, scene loader, logger, App `LifetimeScope` | Screen camera, screen UI, world and combat state |
| `Foundation.unity` | Screen | Camera, lighting, screen presenter, Foundation `LifetimeScope` | Global app state, save implementation, objects from other scenes |

The build order is fixed as Bootstrap 0, Foundation 1. Only Bootstrap owns the process root. Foundation is a child scope that explicitly takes the App scope as its parent, and it is disposed before unload.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    Bootstrap[Bootstrap scene\nAppLifetimeScope]
    Foundation[Foundation scene\nFoundationLifetimeScope]
    Fsm[Application FSM]
    Loader[Scene loader adapter]

    Bootstrap --> Fsm
    Bootstrap --> Loader
    Bootstrap -->|explicit parent| Foundation
    Fsm -->|authorized request only| Loader

```

`SceneManager.LoadScene*` may be called only by the App''s scene loader adapter. If a presenter, view, or domain service changes scenes directly, the quality gate fails.

## FSM Contract

### States and Triggers

| Current State | Trigger | Next State | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Booting` | `OpenFoundation` | `Transitioning` | Start Foundation staging load |
| `Transitioning` | `SceneLoadSucceeded` | `Foundation` | Commit prepared scene lease |
| `Transitioning` | `SceneLoadFailed` | `Faulted` | Discard staging, preserve error and retry information |
| `Faulted` | `Retry` | `Transitioning` | Retry the same destination with a new transition ID |

Any combination not in the table is `Rejected(IllegalTransition)` and changes no state, scene, or save. Any other request during `Transitioning` is `Rejected(Busy)` and does not start a second load. A request for the same destination that has already reached Foundation returns the current receipt.

### Transition Sequence

1. Check the state, guards, and active transition without side effects.
2. Create a transition ID and generation, and block input.
3. Load the target scene as staging.
4. Use VContainer parent enqueue to create the screen scope under the App scope.
5. Wait for the ready signals of the required camera, presenter, and scope.
6. Commit the stable state and scene lease only when the generation matches the current request.
7. Dispose of the previous child scope and unload the previous scene.
8. Restore input and return the receipt.

Do not change the stable state before ready. Instead of fixed sleeps and polling, first set up completion/readiness subscriptions, then wait for the exact signal.

## VContainer Contract

```text
AppLifetimeScope (Bootstrap, process)
└── FoundationLifetimeScope (Foundation scene)

```

- Pin `jp.hadashikick.vcontainer` `1.19.0`, as confirmed in Makcha.
- Each production scene has exactly one `LifetimeScope`.
- The default lifetime for mutable application services is `Scoped`.
- A child can resolve parent contracts, but a parent must not resolve or hold child implementations.
- If a required serialized reference is missing, the scope build fails immediately.
- Before the scene unloads, clean up child cancellation tokens, subscriptions, tasks, and resources, and then dispose of the scope.
- Product code does not call `Container.Resolve`; constructor injection is the default.

## Singleton Contract

A **Singleton** is a single instance within the current VContainer scope, not a static global object.

Allowed:

- Deeply immutable constants and value tables
- The App scope''s scene catalog, logger, scene loader, and FSM coordinator
- Campaign truth and ledger in a future campaign scope
- Presenters and input routers in a scene scope

Forbidden:

- Mutable static `Instance`, `Current`, and global collections/caches
- Registering the current campaign, selected unit, current battle, or scene view as an App Singleton
- Keeping scene services alive with `DontDestroyOnLoad`
- Finding dependencies through a service locator or runtime object search

`DontDestroyOnLoad` is allowed in only one place: the Bootstrap ownership file.

## Repository Boundary

**Repository** interfaces and DTOs belong in the Unity-free inward assembly. Unity/App adapters implement them using the filesystem or Unity APIs.

```csharp
public interface ICampaignRepository
{
    ValueTask<CampaignLoadResult> LoadAsync(
        SaveSlotId slot,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken);

    ValueTask<CampaignCommitResult> CommitAsync(
        CampaignSnapshot expected,
        CampaignMutation mutation,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken);
}

```

- Do not put `GameObject`, `Transform`, `Vector*`, `ScriptableObject`, `Scene`, or VContainer types in interfaces.
- Return versioned snapshots and receipts instead of mutable collections or live Unity objects.
- Presentation sees only command/query contracts, not the concrete Repository or the save API.
- The same request/result ID with the same payload returns the existing receipt. A different payload under the same ID is rejected as a conflict.
- In the future, Battle receives only an immutable `BattleContext` and does not resolve the campaign Repository.

The first Foundation increment does not implement the campaign Repository. The quality gate protects only the boundary for now.

## Saving and Determinism

In the future, the save envelope will contain the format/rules/content versions, campaign seed, integer world tick, canonical snapshot revision, event-ledger hash, per-purpose RNG stream states, pending battle and settlement receipts, and a checksum.

- Follow this order: write to temp, flush, verify by re-reading, preserve a backup, and swap atomically.
- The domain does not call `PlayerPrefs` or `File.*` directly.
- Separate RNG into per-purpose streams, such as WorldEvents, Encounters, and BattleHit.
- Do not use wall clock, frame order, instance ID, `GetHashCode`, or collection iteration order as rule inputs.
- The same seed and canonical command log must produce the same state/ledger hash.
- The moment a scene finishes loading must not affect domain results.

## Failure and Cancellation

Transition results distinguish `Completed`, `Rejected`, `Cancelled`, and `Failed`. Null or silent ignoring is not allowed.

- Guard failures and illegal transitions are rejected before any side effects.
- Load/scope/readiness failures clean up only the staging and keep the previous stable scene.
- Cancellation during the preparation phase is `Cancelled` and is not reported as a load failure.
- Cancellation during the commit phase is deferred until the commit or rollback finishes deterministically.
- A retry uses a new transition ID but must not create duplicate effects for the same domain request.
- Late callbacks compare generations so they cannot update a replaced screen.

Lifecycle rules:

- Every `OnEnable +=` must have a matching `OnDisable -=` in the same type.
- Subscriptions made at injection must be released in `Dispose` or `OnDestroy`.
- Scope dispose proceeds in this order: block commands, cancel tokens, detach events, observe tasks, release resources, dispose child scopes, unload the scene.
- Unobserved fire-and-forget tasks and `async void` in business flow are forbidden.

## Module Dependencies

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    App[Janseon.App\nUnity + VContainer] --> Presentation[Janseon.Presentation]
    App --> Contracts[Janseon.Application.Contracts]
    Presentation --> Contracts
    Data[Janseon.Data\nUnity adapters] --> Contracts
    Sim[Janseon.Sim\nUnity-free] --> Contracts
    Contracts --> Core[Janseon.Core\nUnity-free]

```

The Unity-free assembly sets `noEngineReferences: true` and does not reference UnityEngine, VContainer, App, or Presentation. For now it starts as a single module, `Janseon.Foundation`, and no empty future assemblies are created before a real second responsibility and caller exist.

## Test Strategy

EditMode FSM tests:

1. From Booting, OpenFoundation starts the load exactly once.
2. Do not commit to Foundation before ready.
3. Foundation and the receipt are created only after the success signal.
4. An illegal transition leaves the state unchanged, with zero scene calls.
5. A duplicate request while in progress is Busy, and no additional load occurs.
6. A load failure discards the staging and leaves Faulted and retry information.
7. Retry recovers using a new transition ID.

PlayMode tests and manual QA enter Foundation from the real Bootstrap and observe allowed transitions and rejection results on the public flow surface. Tests do not use fixed sleeps; they subscribe first to a fake completion source or the real readiness signal.

## Quality Gate

`node tools/architecture/check-unity-architecture.mjs` checks the following:

- `SceneManager.LoadScene*` and `DontDestroyOnLoad` outside approved files
- `FindObject*`, `GameObject.Find`, and mutable static `Instance`/`Current` in runtime
- UnityEngine/VContainer references in the Repository/Core path
- Build order of Bootstrap 0 and Foundation 1, and the existence of scene files
- Number of production `LifetimeScope`s in the content scene

The gate itself must also pass a mutation proof that turns it RED on a temporary forbidden sample. After that, Unity import, compile, EditMode, PlayMode, and an actual Editor screenshot are verified.

## Completion Contract

The responsibilities of Bootstrap and Foundation must be separated, only the FSM may approve transitions, and no stable state may change before ready. The App/child scope boundary, illegal, duplicate, failed, and retried cases, the ban on static Singletons, and the Unity-free Repository boundary must be confirmed by automated tests and quality gates.

## POC Presentation History

The current Unity POC uses the original Oddland assets and a left-right side-scrolling combat screen. This presentation is historical record and not the target runtime contract.

The target combat camera is the 3D free-command viewpoint the owner confirmed on 2026-09-24. Combat has no pause and no slow-down or speed-up. This section does not mean implementation is complete.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Character-Art-Direction', 'art', 'character-art-direction', 'Character art direction', 'The product goal is a character that combines realistic proportions with the art style of Tatsuki Fujimoto (author of 《Chainsaw Man》) (owner-confirmed 2026-09-28). Faces and equipment outlines are built so they can be identified on the strategic map and in the 3D free-command battle camera. The execution status of the production stages below is distinguished by per-asset receipts.', 'accepted', 11, 'art/Character-Art-Direction.md', '# Character art direction

The product goal is a character that combines realistic proportions with the art style of Tatsuki Fujimoto (author of 《Chainsaw Man》) (owner-confirmed 2026-09-28). Faces and equipment outlines are built so they can be identified on the strategic map and in the 3D free-command battle camera. The execution status of the production stages below is distinguished by per-asset receipts.

## Art style

Characters are built with natural proportions. Do not use deformation, big eyes, or SD (super-deformed) proportions. Faces follow real bone structure; eyes are small and narrow, and the nose bridge, cheekbones, and jawline are kept realistic.

Expressions are not exaggerated. A blank look and fatigue are the default, and emotion is shown through small changes in the eyebrows and the corners of the mouth. Characters are distinguished by one or two strong silhouette features, such as bangs that fall to the space between the eyebrows.

Lines are thin, and their thickness wavers slightly. Shading is applied with rough hatching or a hard single-step shadow over broad flat color; do not use soft gradients, gloss, rim light, or bloom. Colors are a limited, desaturated palette, with one accent color per scene.

In 3D, this art style is carried over with 1–2 step cel shading, outlines with a slight wavering in thickness, and hatching textures on shadowed surfaces. PBR gloss is turned off. The shader configuration is finalized through actual render tests.

The portrait camera uses a 3/4 angle, eye level, and a narrow field of view, leaving margins like a film still. Decisive moments are shown as still-frame staging that splits a single action across several scenes.

Do not copy the characters, devils, logos, or scenes of 《Chainsaw Man》. In generation prompts, do not write only the artist''s name; describe the features above in sentences.

The 3D base model for a character is made by running Tripo image-to-3D once to produce a full-body mesh. Rigging is done not with Tripo auto-rigging but with Blender Auto-Rig Pro from the avatar-gen character-tool. Foot soldiers are also made in the same art style and with the same pipeline, to fit the world setting.

## Body and Face

Use the male and female body packages stored in ART-ASSETS/avatar-gen as the default forms. Assemble the parts in Blender while preserving the face''s identity and the locked areas of the body. Match skin tone, eyes, and costume materials so they read as a single character, and check them from multiple angles on the actual screen.

## Hair and Costume

For male hair, paint the hair color and hairline onto the head surface, and make only the parts that protrude outward as a separate mesh. The construction method for female hair is decided according to each character''s design.

For costumes, use a model with verified textures attached. When assembling Tripo parts made from reference images, check the body, the joints, the materials, and the external textures. Exchange files containing the editing source and textures are identified by the role and hash in the ART-ASSETS manifest.

### Costume Silhouette and Winter

The basic costume silhouette follows 1940s attire, the World War II era. In this world an eternal winter continues, and the more often a person goes up to the surface, the thicker the winter clothing they wear.

The higher a character''s tier, the thinner the clothing they wear. Tier and clothing thickness are inversely proportional, while surface exposure and clothing thickness are directly proportional. The factors that determine clothing thickness are how often one goes up to the surface and the degree of exposure to cold.

## Full Body and Portrait

2D Avatar Gen generates portrait and full-body images by combining face, hair, body, outfit, and accessories from per-character JSON. Slots describe shape, color, material, and attachment position independently of the character wearing them, and the selected face and body are kept even when the outfit is changed. For the body, body type, weight, bust size, and height are entered; for the face, eye color, eye shape, eyebrows, lip color, mouth shape, nose, and facial hair are each entered. Items without numeric values are left unspecified. Portraits commonly use a calm pose: a medium close-up at eye level that captures the whole head, both shoulders, and the upper chest, with the head and torso turned slightly to the right of the frame.

3D Avatar Gen handles model assembly, rigging, skinning, animation, and 3D rendering. It separates its execution entry point, settings, generation contract, and outputs from those of 2D Avatar Gen, and each technique is run and verified independently. 2D portrait and full-body PNGs are generated from the designated art-style reference and per-character JSON, and face identity, body type, outfit, props, and composition are checked against the original. The hashes of generation requests, references, and outputs are linked with per-image review verdicts, and runtime import of game assets is verified separately.

## Review Screen

In Blender, check the front, side, and three-quarter views along with the wireframe and material views. In Unity, check character proportions, hair silhouette, and the readability of equipment and materials at the actual strategy and combat camera distances and angles. Selected visual evidence is kept linked to the asset manifest.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Campaign-Movement-296', 'proposals', 'campaign-movement-296', 'Time-Based Campaign Movement Proposal — Decision 14 / #296', '', 'proposal', 12, 'proposals/Campaign-Movement-296.md', '# Time-Based Campaign Movement Proposal — Decision 14 / #296

The current baseline is Owner Intent Decision 14: time-based hero squad movement, changing targets mid-move, five stances, and the same contract for AI. It also follows the per-target event transitions, screen separation, and the station-less domain contract. The MOV-01 to MOV-07 figures and conversions below are proposals awaiting the owner''s individual approval, separate from the document merge. Simultaneous strategy turns and turn-based movement points are not the current design, and adjudication follows the original TRPG core rules system.

## Source Comparison and Unit/Coefficient Approval Proposal, 2026-09-29

[Document] The [SJ Games Lite guide](https://www.sjgames.com/gurps/Lite/) and the [official sales page](https://warehouse23.com/products/gurps-lite-fourth-edition) are sources for confirming the 4th edition guide. The rules text was checked directly against [a copy archived on DocDroid](https://www.docdroid.net/lBGne5B/gurps-4e-lite-pdf). It has 32 pages in total, and p.1 reads AUGUST 2004 EDITION / Rev. 07/12/04, SHA-256 56aa72431c4b8a728b3e6eb8efe3b72f3f50afef2b960b55184ae70275a92fb9. The printed and PDF page numbers are the same within the ranges below. The metadata modification date of 2008-08-06 is not interpreted as the edition revision date, and no claim is made that the file is byte-identical to the latest official distribution file. The PDF original is not copied into the repository; only the facts are summarized.

| Page | Confirmed Facts from the Source | Boundary with This Proposal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 5 | BL=ST²/5 lb. If 10 lb or more, round to the nearest integer. | The research input weight is an integer in units of 1/5 lb. Approval of actual equipment weights and the UI''s kg conversion are separate matters. |
| 6 | Basic Speed=(HT+DX)/4, keeping the decimal. The starting value of Basic Move is its rounded-down value, in yd/s. | A character''s directly purchased or modified Move is not overwritten by the default. It is not used directly as the campaign walking speed. |
| 22 | Encumbrance limits are BL×1/2/3/6/10, with Move multipliers 1/.8/.6/.4/.2. Round down after applying encumbrance; encumbrance alone never brings Move below 1. | Loads over 10BL are rejected in this walking study. This does not mean the source''s short-duration over-limit carrying is impossible. |
| 22–23 | The ideal daily distance for Hiking = 10×effective Move miles. A successful Hiking roll gives +20%. Terrain multipliers are .2/.5/1/1.25. | Daily activity hours, the mapping of roads and underground to terrain, and stance multipliers are new design. Skill-roll bonuses are not included in this numerical example. |
| 29·31 | When HP < max HP/3 or FP < max FP/3, Move is halved, rounded up, for each. | The order of encumbrance → injury → fatigue is a candidate that makes the combined interpretation explicit. No automatic speed is generated for HP≤0, FP≤0, or inability to walk. |
| 23·31 | Running uses FP≤1/3 while ordinary fatigue uses FP<1/3, so the boundaries differ. For Hiking, refer to p.31. | This Hiking-based candidate uses the strict <. The Running rule is neither declared a typo nor unified. |
| 31 | Road Hiking costs 1 + encumbrance level FP per hour, and ordinary fatigue recovers 1 FP per 10 minutes of quiet rest. | State snapshot verification is separate from actual fatigue events, rest schedules, and sleep/thirst. It does not guarantee that 8 continuous hours without rest is possible. |

[Inference/New Proposal] An individual''s M is obtained by applying the encumbrance multiplier to the explicitly input Basic Move and rounding down (minimum 1); if HP is strictly below 1/3, ceil(M/2); if FP is strictly below 1/3, ceil(M/2) again. This order is not cited as a unified formula from the source as a whole. If both apply, ceil(ceil(M/2)/2)=ceil(M/4). BL is a candidate that is calculated from the approved base ST and is not reduced again by fatigue. It follows the p.31 wording that excludes ST-based derived quantities, but if the character has modifiers, an explicit approved BL/Move contract must come first. The BL/Basic Move derivation helper supports only hypothetical walkers with no such ability modifiers. The max HP/FP of 12 below are not defaults derived from ST/HT but explicit inputs assuming separately purchased or modified values. Distinguish this from the Lite p.6 defaults of HP=ST and FP=HT.

| ID | Recommended Candidate | Alternatives and Differences | Approval Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| MOV-01 Units | T=world seconds, D=path meters, W=converted millimeters of average terrain. W is not a coordinate. | Stored as an integer plus a remainder with a fixed denominator. The wall-clock compression ratio is not an input to this formula and is decided separately. | Pending |
| MOV-02 Walking Baseline | A comparison baseline obtained by dividing the daily 10×Move miles over an 8-hour activity window: K=2794/5 W/T/Move. | With 12 hours, K=5588/15. Compared with the 8-hour option, speed is 2/3 and travel time is 1.5 times. The 8 hours is not a fact from the source. | Pending |
| MOV-03 Squad | The minimum of all members'' M. Load includes personal equipment, consumables, and assigned shared baggage. | The average is excluded from this candidate because it would require straggler/transport rules for slower members. | Pending |
| MOV-04 State | Encumbrance → HP → FP. HP/FP strictly <1/3. HP≤0, FP≤0, inability to walk, and over-limit encumbrance are out-of-scope errors. | Unconsciousness/transport and will checks are separate contracts. Rejection differs from the source''s absolute immobility. | Pending |
| MOV-05 Stance | Normal ×1, forced march ×3/2, ambush ×1/2, garrison/camp ×0. | The multipliers are new design. The forced march''s combat restrictions and hit-readiness, and ambush detection and recovery effects, are not substituted by a multiplier. | Pending |
| MOV-06 Edges | The working multipliers 5/2/1/4⁄5 are the reciprocals of terrain .2/.5/1/1.25. edgeW=ceil(D×1000×multiplier)+entry W. | Automatically assigning multipliers from the route layer alone is forbidden. The example is a hypothetical map that explicitly specifies road 4/5, entrance 1, and underground 2. | Pending |
| MOV-07 Entrances | Example entrance of 50 m + additional 100000W. Entry overhead work is also proportional to squad speed. | With a fixed 60-second wait, every squad gets the same delay, but a separate timer is needed. This calculation recommends the workload option. | Pending |

World seconds are a unit for numerical verification and do not specify the FixedUpdate interval or a player-controlled speed multiplier. The recommended 8 hours is a baseline for converting daily distance into speed over the activity window. Actual daily travel must be recalculated according to state changes, meals, rest, sleep, and road conditions. The ratio of world time to wall-clock time, the conversion of the remainder when stance changes, and the timing of fatigue/rest events remain open items before approval and implementation.

| Hypothetical Squad | Explicit Input | M and Speed Verification |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A: Commander 1 + Soldiers 8 | Commander ST10 DX12 HT12 Basic Move6, load 15lb. Each soldier ST10 DX10 HT10 Move5, load 30lb. All HP/FP12/12, able to walk. | Commander 6, soldiers floor(5×.8)=4 → squad M4. Normal 11176/5, forced march 16764/5, ambush 5588/5 W/T. |
| B: Commander 1 + Soldiers 12 | Commander ST15 DX12 HT12 Move6, load 40lb. Each soldier ST10 DX10 HT10 Move5, load 50lb. All HP/FP12/12, able to walk. | BL commander 45lb, soldiers 20lb. Soldiers floor(5×.6)=3 → M3. Normal 8382/5, forced march 12573/5, ambush 4191/5 W/T. |
| B''s Thresholds and Overlaps | If one soldier has HP4/12·FP4/12, the threshold is the same. Compare using only HP3/12, or HP3/12·FP3/12. | Same threshold: M3; only HP low: ceil(3/2)=2; both HP and FP low: ceil(2/2)=1. No arbitrary .75 coefficient is used. |

| Hypothetical Route/Time | A Normal | B Normal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Road 1000m×4/5 | 800000W | 800000W |
| Layer transfer 50m×1+100000W | 150000W; cumulative 950000W | 150000W; cumulative 950000W |
| Underground 1000m×2 | 2000000W; total 2950000W | 2000000W; total 2950000W |
| 300T | 670560W, mid-road | 502920W, mid-road |
| 600T | 1341120W, 391120W progressed on the underground trunk edge | 1005840W, 55840W progressed on the underground trunk edge |
| 300T forced march | 1005840W, entering underground | 754380W, mid-road |
| First whole-second arrival observed | ceil(2950000×5/11176)=1320T | ceil(2950000×5/8382)=1760T |

The example map''s S0/S1/U0/U1 are not actual station or depot coordinates. The 600T underground progress is workload; the physical movement, accounting for the 2× underground cost, is 195560mm and 27920mm respectively. Trunk-edge cost validation, control-zone stops, full rejection on blockade, goal replacement, the same function for AI, and hashing reuse the existing time-orders research. The new source-calculation cross-check connects the source derivation, thresholds, load boundaries, squad minimums, and stances with the actual-unit examples. The new evidence path is GAME RESEARCH/verification/campaign-movement-source-20260929/README.md. Do not change the coefficient table to accepted without approvedAt/approvedBy/evidence.

## Current Proposal: Goals, Routes, and World Time

Both players and AI are checked for current position, full route, access rights, layer, and combat lock. A limited research example that can accept a goal replacement has been added. After advancing with existing commands up to the authoritative event, it verifies controllerId, eventTime, and expectedRevision, then atomically accepts a new route that preserves the current edge and the past prefix as they are. Progress W and the remainder are kept. On failure, the existing command and position at the event time are preserved. Mid-edge reversal, changing the current edge''s cost, and full re-search are not implemented.

The unit and personal derivation candidates for 2026-09-29 are specified in MOV-01~07 below. The abstract T/D/W and composite speeds of the existing time-motion/time-orders examples are preserved for verifying the calculation structure. The new source-calculation.mjs cross-checks the personal base values confirmed from the source text and the specified campaign conversions, and is not connected to the product. Using the minimum across all walkers, including the commander, attached heroes, and soldiers, as the squad baseline without excluding non-walkers is a new aggregation proposal.

This is a draft that first seeks approval for the meaning of integer distance u and world-time u, and takes speed as an integer numerator n over a positive denominator d. For an interval''s elapsed time Δt, scaled=n×Δt+remainder, advanced distance=floor(scaled/d), and new remainder=scaled mod d. Intermediate products are computed with BigInt/checked integers, and the remainder is stored so that fractions are not discarded every frame. For the same n, d, and the same route and event interval, applying Δt in split pieces yields the same final distance and remainder. When the goal is reached, progress is clamped to the goal distance, and leftover movement is not saved for the next command.

Elapsed-time intervals are split at authoritative events, and the new speed is not multiplied over the entire past interval. Goal replacement under the conditions of a fixed denominator and a retained current edge is tested with the time-orders example. Denominator changes, stance switches, mid-edge reversal, dynamic blockades, encounters, and exact arrival events are subject to separate approval/verification. The research deals with positions and ledger candidates and does not implement full simultaneous world resolution.

## Current Proposal: Layers, Access, Combat, and Empty World

The surface territory and subway lines are separate layers, and only explicitly designated entrance/transfer edges change layers. Route adjacency, layer, access rights, and blockades are checked, and a command that is illegal from the start is not partially executed. After a valid command is accepted, dynamic blockades/encounters are re-evaluated as time events, and no policy that rewinds distance validly moved in the past is arbitrarily created. Stopping at ZOC endpoints and passage restrictions are review candidates that preserve the existing meaning, but time-based encounter and restart policies must be finalized separately. The old turn costs 750/1000/1250 for road, underground, and transfer are not reused as speed or distance.

Shared online campaign time has no pause, slowdown, or fast-forward usable by players. When the last player leaves, the world stops, and the wall-clock time elapsed while the world was empty is not caught up after reconnecting. A squad in combat receives no new movement commands until the result is settled. The calculator''s battleLocked interval is a minimal proposal that suspends only that squad''s progress while world time continues. Other squads do not stop, and combat does not monopolize world time. The five stance types, inherited/temporary squad leaders, hero/soldier separation, and the soldier maximum of 20 are retained.

## Current Offline Verification and Open Items

GAME RESEARCH/verification/campaign-movement-20260928/time-motion.mjs and time-motion.test.mjs are research examples not connected to the product. Inputting hypothetical A speed 7/3, B speed 5/2, and hypothetical road distance 10 and entrance distance 5, it confirms A distance 11/remainder 2 and B distance 12/remainder 1 at Δt5. The units are integers for testing, not real-world units, and are not approved values. It splits elapsed times 0, 1, 5, 7, and 20 into every possible two-piece division and compares them with whole application, and checks remainder clearing on arrival, empty world, combat lock, illegal route/access rights, integer range, and input immutability.

The source edition, page numbers, and load/HP/FP calculations were cross-checked and recorded in the source table below. The remaining items awaiting approval are MOV-01~07, state transitions, fatigue accumulation/rest, the duration of the forced-march hit penalty, full-map search, dynamic blockades/collisions/return from combat, exact arrival events, denominator changes, ledger order, and retransmission. The new research calculator''s input checks are not an approved-data validator or authenticity verification of approval records. This is not judged as #296 final PASS, #297 ready to start, or Core implementation complete.

Time-based cost candidate: edgeW=ceil(distanceD×workNumerator/workDenominator)+entryWorkW. It requires positive distance/numerator/denominator, non-negative additional work, and a total within the safe integer range. Roads/surface are surface, underground is subway, and only an explicit transfer permits a layer change. Cost errors, blockades, no access rights, and intermediate control zones reject the entire route. Failing to arrive within the horizon is legal progress and not a cost failure. Entry workload is a new candidate whose time varies with speed, and is distinguished from a fixed waiting time.

Synthetic example (not product coefficients): A normal/forced march/ambush = 4/2, 6/2, 2/2 W/T; B = 3/2, 5/2, 1/2 W/T; both garrison/camp = 0/2. All five speeds are input. Forced-march attack/interception cannot be initiated but can be hit, and camp attack cannot be initiated; these are checked separately. Stance changes, concealment detection, and recovery are not implemented. On the map, S0→S1 road 8D×1/2=4W, S1→U0 entrance 2D×1+2W=4W, U0→U1 underground 6D×2=12W, for a total of 20W.

Hand calculation ΔT5: A normal 10W/r0, 2W after U0; B normal 7W/r1 (7.5W), 3.5W during entry. A forced march 15W/r0, B forced march 12W/r1. A normal reaches U1 at 10T; B normal at 13T has 19.5W, and is observed arriving at U1 at 14T (continuous arrival time 40/3T). If B replaces its route at t5 with U0→U2 at a cost of 4W, it keeps the 7.5W, and after +3T×1.5=4.5W arrives at U2 at t8. W is not a physical coordinate, and the 3D interpolation contract is not implemented.

The reachable-range example lists the nodes newly reached within horizonT along the specified route. At ΔT5, A reaches S1 and U0, and B reaches S1. A full-map shortest-path search does not exist yet. If U0 is a control zone, the unit stops at 8W and rejects a general MOVE restart. A blockade on the last edge, no access rights, no actor authority, cost denominator 0/negative/overflow, and a stale revision do not change the existing command, time, or progress. The cost ceil example 8D×1/3=3W is also tested.

The additional research files time-orders.mjs/time-orders.test.mjs use only Node built-in modules. AI and players use the same function, authority, speed, and cost. The command ID, authoritative time, revision, actor, and the semantic fields of cost, route, stance, and progress are serialized in sorted order and chained to the previous SHA256. Display metadata/hostOrder are excluded and route order is preserved. Replaying the same ledger gives the same hash, and a cost change gives a different hash. Time splitting guarantees only the same position; it does not mean that different event ledgers produce the same hash.

The verification packet is in GAME RESEARCH/verification/campaign-movement-20260928, and time-orders-proposal.md contains the derivation, sources, hand calculations, and an open-items table. It passed the existing 24 tests: history 12 + time intervals 4 + events 8. It was also confirmed that nested display metadata does not change the replay hash or route judgment for stops and goal replacement, while actual cost changes are detected. The 12 history tests are not proof of the latest time design. This is an unapproved research fixture, not an approved-data validator, Unity Core, or completed network replay.

## Inputs and events to verify at the 2026-10-01 implementation handoff

Do not rebuild the MOV-01~07 table, and do not treat the fact of a merge as approval. Each row used for implementation must link the approved value, unit, rule version, approvedBy, approvedAt, and evidence in the same input revision. Proposed values, missing approval evidence, approval for a different version, and references that do not match the actual squad or route are all subject to handoff hold. This section states the requirements for an approved-input checker; it does not implement an executable checker or authenticity verification of approval records.

| Acceptance Cases | Input / Action | Expected Result and Responsible Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Screen Switching and World Progress | Switch between the life, strategy, and combat screens while two different squads are moving. | Per #36, hiding a screen must not stop the world time or progress of unrelated squads. Compare the #297 Core record against the #284 display. |
| Domain Without a Station | Use a station-less domain permitted by #37 as the destination, but supply the actual access route as a separate input. | Domain ownership alone must not create a station ID, a subway edge, or free entry. If there is no approved route, do not display it as reachable. |
| Workload and Display Coordinates | Compare progress along edges that have the same distance but different terrain costs. | Do not use W directly as a 3D coordinate or an actual travel distance. Check the projection from workload progress to physical position against the coordinate contract agreed with #279/#284. |
| Arrival, Stance, and Blockade Events | Present inputs in which the arrival time overlaps with a stance change or a dynamic blockade, as well as a change to the speed denominator. | First fix the exact arrival moment, the handling of remainders, the event order, and the restart policy. Where no policy exists, do not treat the result of an existing research example as the product''s correct answer. |
| Distinguishing Combat Locks | While one squad is in combat, issue a movement order to another squad, and observe the end of the engagement and the campaign settlement as separate events. | Campaign movement is locked until that squad''s result is settled. Verify the locking and unlocking of battlefield orders separately under the combat contract. A single shared lock must not replace the two scopes. |
| Missing Approved Input | One of the MOV approval evidence, the #298 formation, the #299 location, or the rule version is missing or inconsistent. | Do not promote it to a product setup or movement order, and do not change the campaign or ledger. Do not substitute the hypothetical A/B and S0/S1/U0/U1 for actual Sinjongmok squads or warehouses. |

The cases above are acceptance specifications to be run after the new Core/API is provided, and they are not pass records. Distinguish them from the existing source-calculation and time-orders verification. The remaining decisions are MOV-01~07, the mapping between world time and wall-clock time, fatigue, rest, and stance events, the order of arrival, collision, and dynamic blockade, and the actual formation, battlefield, and warehouse locations. Add only approved decisions to the corresponding rows, and preserve the historical source.b* and source.sha256.

## Historical Comparison: Discarded Turn Budget Draft v0.1

All source.b* blocks below are initial research and proposals from before Decision 14 was confirmed. Do not read them as current recommendations, preserved contracts, or approval requests. The turn budget model in movement.mjs and movement.test.mjs is also for historical comparison and is not a basis for the current time-based implementation. source.sha256 and proposal-source.md are frozen as initial provenance and must not be overwritten by this revision.

## Historical Record — Campaign Movement Numeric Review Proposal — #296

Status: proposal / NEW DESIGN v0.1 / awaiting explicit approval from @islee23520. Comparison against the original source pages is incomplete. Do not use as a product Core or data default. Existing characters, martial arts, equipment, lineages, and accepted rules are not changed.

### Historical Record — Sources and Scope of Verification

Request #296 requires explicit approval of values, units, and coefficients. Do not begin the #297 Core implementation before approval. Preserve source.b18–b26 on hero squads and concurrent progress. The fixed source.sha256 is 3bd57c0dbaee7da56e8bdbdf43889033a292d4874bbd9a747a771174c7d2d988.

The concepts of turn reach boundaries, stances, roads, multi-turn movement, and enemy ZOC were confirmed at [Total War Academy](https://academy.totalwar.com/campaign-moving-armies-exploration-and-stances/) (accessed 2026-09-28, HTML). It does not provide cost figures. Do not certify against the latest Warhammer III patch values, and do not copy naval transition rules to underground transfers.

[SJ Games Lite guide](https://www.sjgames.com/gurps/lite/) and [product 31-0004](https://warehouse23.com/products/gurps-lite-fourth-edition) confirm that it is a 32-page summary of the 4th edition. Because the Lite body PDF could not be obtained, the exact edition, printed/PDF page numbers, rounding, and original HP/FP rules are unverified. On the first page of the [official CharacterSheet.pdf](https://www.sjgames.com/gurps/resources/CharacterSheet.pdf), the ENCUMBRANCE/MOVE table confirms BL=ST²/5, the BL multipliers 1·2·3·6·10, and the Move multipliers 1·0.8·0.6·0.4·0.2. This is not a citation of the Lite body text. The index in the [Basic Set excerpt](https://www.sjgames.com/gurps/books/Basic/characters.pdf) is not a substitute source for the rule text or the Lite pages.

### Historical Record — Contracts to Preserve

Preserve the following: a maximum of 20 foot soldiers (heroes separate), a temporary foot-soldier squad leader when the commander is lost, the same command contract for AI, no priority by submission time or host, one world-time step per turn resolution, locking subsequent strategy commands only for squads taking part in combat, and splitting and merging only on the same node and floor. Movement, attack, interception, reinforcement, and stance are separate commands. A forced march cannot initiate an attack or interception but can be attacked, and camping cannot initiate an attack.

### Historical Record — Proposed Values and Integer Calculation

Do not convert one time step into hours, days, or km. The storage unit for abstract movement points (MP) is 1000u=1MP, with Q=1000. All coefficients and the way units are applied are new designs. basicMove is a non-negative integer from approved character input, and no default is created if it is missing. Do not automatically infer it from GURPS Basic Speed or apply CP adjustments.

personalU=floor(basicMove×1000×encPermille×healthPermille×fatiguePermille/1000³). squadBaseU is the minimum personalU among all actually walking members, including the commander, assigned heroes, and foot soldiers. turnBudgetU=floor(squadBaseU×stancePermille/1000). Round down only at the end, with no intermediate rounding down. Do not exclude members who cannot walk from the calculation, and do not put them on unapproved transport.

Load is summed per individual across equipment, weapons, armor, consumables, and assigned shared loads. It is compared in the same integer units as the approved BL, and if it equals a boundary, the lower tier applies. The limits are BL×[1,2,3,6,10], and the coefficients are [1000,800,600,400,200]. Anything over 10BL is rejected as OVER_CAPACITY. This proposal does not fix the rounding of the original BL.

Candidate state coefficients: health normal 1000 / walking-wounded 750 / unable to walk unaided 0; fatigue normal 1000 / fatigued 750 / exhausted 500 / unable to move 0. The thresholds for mapping HP and FP to these states, and the accumulation of recovery and fatigue, are unsupported before approval. These numbers are not GURPS facts.

Candidate stance coefficients: normal 1000, forced march 1500, ambush 500. Movement is rejected while garrison or camp is active. The movement stance is locked before the first move, and after moving only an ending stance is allowed. Releasing the ending stance does not resume movement or refill the budget within the same turn. Maintain usedForcedMarchThisTurn so that returning to normal does not clear the attack and interception restriction. This state transition must be approved and verified under a separate stance command contract.

### Historical Record — Edges, Reach, and Reservations

Candidate base edge costs (u): surface road 750, surface normal 1000, surface rough 1500, underground line 1000, explicitly specified entry/transfer 1250. edgeCostU=ceil(baseCostU×terrainPermille/1000)+surchargeU. Since base terrain is already reflected in the cost, apply additional adjustments only from explicitly approved inputs (test input 1000/0). The real-world length of a single edge is undecided, and the policy for splitting long segments or overriding costs is awaiting approval.

A surface edge must be on the same surface floor, and an underground edge on the same underground floor. Floor changes are allowed only for connection pairs specified on the map. Lack of a pass, a blockade, or a wrong floor is illegal rather than infinite cost. Every route is checked in the order adjacency → floor → pass → blockade → stance → ZOC → total cost, and within the same check stage the failure with the earlier route index is returned. Format, duplicate ID, and range errors are rejected before the route check. On failure, the position, budget, and composition remain unchanged, and no prefix is executed automatically.

Entering an enemy ZOC costs the normal amount and ends movement. Only an endpoint is allowed, and a command that exits past an intermediate ZOC is rejected entirely. Record stop=ZOC without converting the remaining accounting budget to 0. If the starting point is in a ZOC, a normal MOVE is not possible, and a separate retreat, attack, or permitted detour contract is needed. Reach is computed with a positive-cost Dijkstra, ZOC nodes are not expanded, and ties use the lexicographic order of the ASCII edgeId array. This search is outside the scope of the research calculator implementation.

A multi-turn reservation is a non-binding destination and intended route. Each turn the map, authority, enemies, and load are rechecked, and that turn''s command is confirmed separately. Do not automatically truncate and execute a command that exceeds the day''s budget. The remaining budget is not carried over, and if a single edge exceeds the turn budget, partial edge progress is not saved either.

### Historical Record — Hypothetical Squad Verification

These are not figures from real character cards. A is 1 commander (Basic Move 6, BL 20, load 15) and 8 foot soldiers (each Move 5, BL 20, load 30); B is 1 commander (Move 6, BL 45, load 40) and 12 foot soldiers (each Move 5, BL 20, load 50). At normal health and fatigue 1000, A has Normal 4000 / Forced March 6000 / Ambush 2000u, and B has Normal 3000 / Forced March 4500 / Ambush 1500u. If one B foot soldier is wounded at 750, that gives 2250u; if fatigue 750 is added on top, 1687u.

In the hypothetical map, S0→S1 is road 750, S1→S2 is road 750, S2→U0 is entrance 1250, U0→U1 is underground 1000, and U1→U2 is underground 1000. A''s Normal move pays 3750 to reach U1, leaving 250; B''s Normal move pays 2750 to reach U0, leaving 250. If B orders a move to U1, the whole order is rejected and B stays at S0 with 3000. A''s Forced March pays 4750 to reach U2, leaving 1250; B''s Forced March pays 3750 to reach U1, leaving 750.

If U1 is a ZOC, A''s Normal move keeps the remaining 250 and a stop marker after arriving at U1, and a Forced March order to U2 is rejected in full. Blocking an entrance edge also causes full rejection. On a route costing 4000, a budget of 4000 succeeds and 3999 fails. B''s multi-turn reservation can be expected to reach U0 on the first turn and U2 on the next, but the actual state is rechecked every turn.

### Historical Record — Determinism and Verification Scope

The offline packet uses arbitrary-precision BigInt multiplication and integer floor/ceil. External JSON numbers accept only safe non-negative integers, are rejected if they exceed the result limit, and are never clamped. Stable IDs are restricted to ASCII, and SHA-256 is applied to UTF-8 JSON with sorted object keys. Arrays that are semantically sets, such as map nodes/edges, squad members, and commands, are sorted by explicit ID, while the order of route arrays is preserved. Duplicate IDs are rejected before hashing. Costs, coefficients, inputs, and results go into the hash; submission time, host order, and translations do not. The packet hash is not the Core replay hash.

Simultaneous resolution must be validated against the same turn snapshot. Stable storage order must not be used as a priority for deciding the game winner. Valid-route conflicts, crossing movement, information/ambush, dynamic blockades, and combat context require a separately approved conflictRuleVersion. The cumulative cost/budget rational-number time model is only a candidate under review and is neither adopted nor implemented in this document. Retransmission receipts, command DAGs, conflict resolution, stance transitions, and authenticity verification of approval records are also not included in the completion claim of this single-MOVE calculator.

Verification packet location: movement.mjs and movement.test.mjs in the local research folder research/2026-09-28/tripothon/movement. It uses only Node''s built-in test/crypto modules. It runs two squads, road/underground/entrance, forced march, ZOC, blockade, boundary and 1u-short cases, malformed formats, duplicate IDs, overflow, immutability on rejection, and array-order-invariant hashing. This is research material separate from the GDD and is not released by a GDD PR alone.

### Historical Record — Decisions Remaining Before Approval

What remains: explicit approval record from @islee23520; the Lite source file/edition/exact page and rounding and HP/FP cross-check; units and all new coefficients; HP/FP state mapping; edge length policy; wounded transport; the readiness penalty for being hit during a forced march; stance change policy; ambush/scouting/dynamic collision; and the approved-data validator contract. Passing the draft''s coefficient check does not clear this gate. Distinguish between finishing a proposal for owner review and the full approval of #296.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Narrative-Direction', 'proposals', 'narrative-direction', 'Narrative Contribution Direction: The Same Seoul, Different Adventures', '', 'proposal', 13, 'proposals/Narrative-Direction.md', '# Narrative Contribution Direction: The Same Seoul, Different Adventures

Status: proposed, not implemented, not playtested.



## 1. Authority and Scope of This Document

This document is a review proposal for Sin Jongmok (berkshirehathaways)''s story development and meta-layer design contributions. It does not replace the canon, runtime contracts, or cast of the existing product. Merging the document means the proposal is archived and under review; it is not a confirmation of setting details or approval to implement the next product module. What can be adopted from the narrative research is the causal reading method of the CK2 original: opening → desire and constraint → institutions and relationships → trigger → choice and known cost → effect → follow-up state. Starting from the approved actions and rights of existing Seoul characters, institutions, stations, and subways, we mark separately what is preserved, what is reconstructed to fit Seoul''s conditions, and what is rejected. We separate edition differences between the original and the CK2 Fanfork from observations of CK3 systems, and we do not substitute North American place names, proper nouns, bank debt law, or death inheritance as Seoul facts. The full approved texts of the [Myeongbu Church](/wiki/Faith-Culture-Schism/) `DOC:Faith-Culture-Schism` and [Im Chowon](/wiki/people/person-0087) `K087` are owned by the WIKI, and here only the IDs and approved editions are referenced. The new scenes of A-P1/B-P1/C-P1/D-P1 are unapproved and are not adopted in this document.

This document does not change the scope, order, or completion rate of the current ToDo. Implementation of new events will be agreed upon separately with its own scope and issue. Document review and paper experiments are not prerequisites for current product work.

`docs/proposals/` is a review space. This proposal is not added to the Wiki source or navigation list under `docs/game-logic/`, and the remote Wiki is not published. Follow the contribution guidelines and the [delivery policy](/gdd/decisions/adr-001/). Do not touch the files, approvals, or progress of the existing cast curation #14 and #15.

## 2. Central Direction

> Depending on who you did what with, the same Seoul leads to a different adventure.

Players do not need to save the city or become rulers from the start. Pay, equipment, companions, exploration, and the safety of the station where they stay are motivation enough. Events are designed so that actions taken for those motives affect the next location, the people they meet, and the solutions available.

This direction does not claim to have invented a new relationship system. [The game''s core](/gdd/design/game-thesis/) already has the persistence of failure, character-centered politics, and routes and passage rights, and ambition and relationships already involve action through characters'' motives and memories. Our contribution is to create scenes in which those principles can be observed in an actual expedition.

There are three core experiences. In exploration, the player is curious about the next place; in execution, their own plan works; and in return, they recognize what they did and its consequences. Even without reading the dialogue and setting, the goals and approaches of major events should differ. However, not every slice-of-life scene, such as a meal, a joke, or a reunion, is required to have a mechanical effect.

## 3. Contributions and Responsibility Boundaries

| Responsible For | Responsibility | Not Decided Alone |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sin Jongmok, narrative and play experience direction | Central question, order of discovery, characters'' purposes and conflicts, representative events, tone and emotion, experience review | Others'' work assignments, product scope, cast canon, combat rules, implementation schedule |
| AI design partner | Cross-checking existing documents, drafting events and dialogue, checking branches and failures, separating implementation requirements, verification records | Acting on behalf of owner approval, assuming test results, claiming completion of unimplemented features |
| Development team and project owner | Technical and tactical design, state model, production cost, scope approval, integration and merging | Negotiate differences instead of replacing narrative intent with arbitrary dialogue and enemy placement |

The size of the first contribution is not measured by the volume of a setting book. We look at whether an ordinary transport mission becomes an event in which the player remembers who they guarded the gate with and why. When story and play conflict, we first fix the combination of motive, information, goal, and existing actions instead of immediately demanding new features.

## 4. Three Design Principles

### The Order of Discovery Creates Attachment

Before explaining a nation''s history, you meet one person. After working with that person, you meet them again. Then that person''s circumstances connect to a larger dispute. Avoid the development where every commission turns out to be part of a grand conspiracy, and leave room for jobs that end ordinarily.

### A Character''s Purpose Changes the Current Plan

Do not stop at attaching a backstory to a character. Write down what they want now, what they can compromise on, and the line they will protect. Where possible, a companion''s circumstances should be revealed before departure so the player can plan around them. Do not convert every relationship into an affinity bonus or use companions only as keys to locked doors.

When connecting to the existing cast, cross-check affiliation, rank, location, life or death, and relationships against canon. If a character is absent or dead, do not automatically generate a new leader or resurrect them for the scene. Even if the personal payoff disappears because a role is unfilled, the physical objective must remain solvable.

### Consequences Are Confirmed in the Next Action

Actions such as repairing a door, securing a detour, or escorting a convoy should change at least one of the later objectives, access routes, or participants. State changes are shown in the scene rather than hidden only in result text. The round-trip structure of reusing the same location is a first experiment, not a formula forced onto every quest.

Combat, negotiation, exploration, and non-intervention for personal gain are all valid play. Do not automatically cut global reputation just because the player refused to help, and do not force missed narrative to replay on another path.

## 5. Adopt, Defer, Reject

| Category | Content | Judgment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First validation | An event where the objectives and solutions of the same battlefield change depending on past actions | Causality and production cost can be reviewed together at a single location. |
| Next validation | Solutions that vary by companion; a market missing from the map | Choose one at a time after the first event is confirmed to be fun. |
| Keep as mood | A history of a station that became a state because the emergency duty never ended; individual responsibility and power | Embed it in daily life and characters'' actions rather than a lesson to be explained. |
| Deferred | Infiltrating a funeral procession, a ceasefire wedding, broadcasts and wide-area reputation | This is only a list of candidates, not a production request. |
| Separate agreement | Im Hajun''s life or death, the truth of his disappearance, and the answers and endings of the three wills | This document neither confirms nor overturns the existing central narrative. |
| Rejected | Automatic punishment for prior good deeds, repeated betrayal, indestructible essential characters, forced convergence of choices | Invalidating the player''s plans and causality for the sake of surprise. |
| Out of scope for this round | A full autonomous character narrative engine, new economic and diplomatic systems | Do not substitute system scale for the fun of small incidents. |

## 6. The Joy of Success and Fairness of Information

Opening a route must bring real benefits such as supplies, trade, easier travel, or reunions. New conflicts arise from separate actors and goals, and are not handled by detecting good deeds and spawning enemies. Successes in which no one is hurt, and the satisfaction of defeating an obvious enemy, are also allowed.

Immediate dangers and known costs are shown before a choice is made. Facts, inferences, and rumors are distinguished. Even when characters interpret events differently, the actual events and the settlement results must be consistent. Rewards and relationship changes must be grounded in witnessing, reporting, or participation. Only cases confirmed in the Seoul source material are adjudicated: in the Samseong Station roster and water contract, separate O Gyeongjae''s position from Im Chowon''s sealed testimony and unfinished maintenance training; Sin Jongmok''s attempt to recover the transceiver unit is conditioned on the daily power supply that depends on the same part and on the residents'' consent. The Yongsan exchange''s existing medical-call priority, reduced freight dispatch, and single-car operating-rights costs are not to be converted into bank principal, arrears, or seizure. Im Hajun''s disappearance is not a death hook, and Bak Nuri''s operational responsibility and O Gyeongjae''s position as senior pastor are not automatically inherited by Im Chowon. Platform access, railway trunk lines, and service restoration do not mean county ownership or a new overlord. The order of the new refrigeration/relay negotiations, the presentation of records, and the costs and outcomes of choosing to participate or hold back are not yet approved incidents.

Competence, humor, rest, and the restoration of daily life are also part of the narrative. Do not use every warm scene as foreshadowing for a later tragedy. Do not have the author punish players with a hidden morality score for choosing personal gain or a military solution.

## 7. First Deliverables and the Transition to Implementation

The first packet consists of two documents: this direction paper and the [Representative Incident Design Document](/gdd/proposals/scenario-hold-the-gate/). The incident design document includes prior information, the cast''s motivations, departure choices, return conditions, tactical objectives, rewards, failure and non-intervention, and a list of additional implementation work.

Distinguish between design and implementation in the [Campaign Loop](/gdd/rules/campaign-loop/), [Tactical Combat](/gdd/rules/personal-resolution-battle/), and [Strategy–Battle Roundtrip](/gdd/rules/strategy-battle-roundtrip/). Do not claim that specific door interactions, convoys, or persistent relationships are implemented merely because repair and escort appear in the genre documents.

To move to implementation after review, the owner and the development team must agree on the hypothesis of one representative incident, the minimum required features, cost and schedule, the connection to the canonical cast, state ownership, and conclusion and settlement. Record document acceptance, paper experiment, technical prototype, and product integration each as a separate completion state.

## 8. Experience Validation

The first experiment compares different pasts placed in the same location. Use the observation criteria in the incident design document, and do not claim the fun has been validated before results are in.

| Question | What to observe | Signals for design review |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Why did this situation come about? | The player connects their earlier actions with their current goal. | Before hearing an explanation, it feels like a random event. |
| What did you do differently? | Routes, division of roles, and choices among repair, negotiation, and combat change. | Only the same enemy-annihilation strategy is advantageous across all histories. |
| Whom do you remember? | The player talks about themselves and the events they went through, rather than memorizing characters'' names. | Companions and on-site characters all remain only as resource bonuses. |
| If you did it again, what would you change? | The player has concrete expectations about a different plan and different companions. | The player says they would only change the reward numbers, or avoids revisiting. |

We do not grade whether the player chose a predetermined ethical answer. Even if they made a different choice, it meets the goal if they understand the causes and consequences and tell it as their own adventure.

## 9. Requested Review Decision

In this PR, decide whether to accept the contribution direction and the representative incident as subjects for review. Do not fold them into the current module''s additional work. If the first incident fails, first revise the fatigue of repeat visits, the characters'' motivations, and the tactical objectives, and do not add a full narrative engine.

The goal the player should remember is the following scene. At first, the door I opened in order to pass through; later, I am guarding it, keeping it open until the people I promised to bring have passed through. Even for players who do not see that scene, a different outcome that fits their own choices must remain.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.owner-approved-batch-d', 'proposals', 'owner-approved-batch-d', 'Approved Proposals — SQ-01·IND-01·RUL-01·CARE-01·CAP-01·ROG-01·ECO-01', '', 'proposal', 14, 'proposals/owner-approved-batch-d.md', '# Approved Proposals — SQ-01·IND-01·RUL-01·CARE-01·CAP-01·ROG-01·ECO-01

A bundle of proposals approved by the owner on 2026-10-10 (on). For ECO-01, only the approved storage cap (storage 400, warehouse +400) and the expansion of designated priorities apply. Because it has not yet been reflected in the canon, it is submitted only as a proposal document.

## SQ-01 Soldier Squad Size Formula

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves blank: RBATTLE-06 says squad size is determined by the commander''s stats, state level, and settlement level, but the formula is undecided. — Source path: rules-battle-clauses.json RBATTLE-06

Proposed rule: size = clamp(6 + floor(martial skill/2) + state level (ruler 2 · vassal 1 · other 0) + floor(R/40), 6, 14). No universal cap is set, only a safe range. Heroes are counted separately from the soldier count.

Genre basis: In CK3, commander ability affects troop efficiency, and domain size affects levy size.

Balance impact: Basic characters lead squads of 6–8; rulers and high-R characters lead up to 14.

Current temporary value in code: src/engine/battle.ts squadCap coefficient

Alternative: Fixed 10 per squad (current).

Code link: on (recommended) / off (fixed at 10)

## IND-01 Independent Founding (Unaffiliated Characters Acquiring Domains)

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves blank: RWARFARE-37 says that when an unaffiliated character acquires a domain on their own, an independent state is established, but there is no method or condition for acquisition. — Source path: rules-factions-warfare-clauses.json RWARFARE-37·04

Proposed rule: When an unaffiliated (S00) character stakes 60 funds and 30 reputation and settles on a tile (excluding the capital and the last 6 tiles) of a state that has collapsed to legitimacy L below 35, an independent state is established. The identifier is X-hash8 (seed, founder, tile) and is permanent; the state''s name and order are not identifiers. The new state''s legitimacy is 20 (new local claim) and its power is 15.

Genre basis: In CK3, an unaffiliated adventurer carves off land from a weakened lord and opens an independent domain.

Balance impact: Requires 60 funds and a legitimacy collapse, so it is uncommon.

Current temporary value in code: src/engine/world.ts foundState value

Alternative: Unaffiliated characters cannot acquire domains (current GDD).

Code link: on (recommended) / off

## RUL-01 Leader''s Death, Vacancy, Acting Leadership, and Successor (D09 3.2~3.4)

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves blank: D09 says that when a leader dies or loses their position, the position and the organization move separately, but it has no death risk, vacancy period, or succession order. — Source path: mechanism-design-index-d09.json 3.2~3.4

Proposed rule: An NPC leader dies each week with probability (age − 54) × 0.00005 from age 55 (capped at 2%). On death the post falls vacant (legitimacy −3) and the top-ranked member of the same state''s hierarchy acts as deputy, while the faction, domain, and relationships remain. After 4 weeks, the post passes to a wiki character of the same clan, then to the next in the same state''s hierarchy. A former leader ousted in a coup stays alive and only loses the post.

Genre basis: CK3: ruler dies → successor under the succession law, regency period.

Balance impact: A leader aged 70 dies about 5% of the time each year.

Current temporary values in code: world.ts RUL values

Alternative: Leaders never die (current).

Code link: on (recommended) / off

## CARE-01 Treatment Request (D02 Examples 1·3)

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves out: D02 says a treatment request takes effect only if it passes the location and resource checks, and that resending for the same cause produces no new result, but it gives no treatment cost or recovery amount. — Source path: mechanism-design-index-d02.json Examples 1·3

Proposed rule: An injured character (health below 60 or with a status ailment) who pays 3 funds at home gains +12 health. A request with the same cause key is processed only once. If the character is away from home or lacks funds, the request does not take effect.

Genre basis: CK3: healing and medicine actions spend a cost and raise health.

Balance impact: 12 health is equivalent to about 2 weeks of rest.

Current temporary values in code: care.ts CARE

Alternative: No treatment request (before the current version).

Code link: on (recommended) / off

## CAP-01 Detention and Ransom After a Defeat (D02 Example 2 · RBATTLE-09)

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves out: RBATTLE-09 treats capture as a per-character detention outcome, but gives no probability, duration, or way to be released. — Source path: mechanism-design-index-d02.json Example 2 · rules-battle RBATTLE-09

Proposed rule: When a battle ends in defeat, a character is detained with 25% probability (4 weeks). While detained, movement and attending appointments are blocked (interviews, letters, and negotiations require separate requests), and the character is released when the term ends or a ransom of 30 is paid. The character and identifiers are preserved.

Genre basis: CK3: a defeated ruler is taken prisoner and released by ransom.

Balance impact: A single defeat blocks a character for 4 weeks.

Current temporary values in code: care.ts CARE

Alternative: No capture.

Code link: on (recommended) / off

## ROG-01 Rogue Attacks and the Leader''s Judgment (RWARFARE-35)

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves out: RWARFARE-35 says the leader punishes, compensates, or approves after the fact, but does not say when a rogue attack occurs. — Source path: rules-factions-warfare-clauses.json RWARFARE-35

Proposed rule: Against a neighbor with tension of 55 or higher (not at war or allied), a military commander (hierarchy rank 3–4) of a state with a leader launches a rogue attack at 1.5% per week. If the leader''s ambition is expansionist and strength is at least 1.1×, it is approved after the fact (tension set to the declaration-of-war threshold of 70); otherwise it is punished with compensation (tension −15, strength −1). Neither outcome directly opens a war.

Genre basis: CK3: a vassal''s unilateral action is subject to the ruler''s judgment.

Balance impact: Roughly once every 1.1 years.

Current temporary values in code: world.ts rogueWeek

Alternative: No rogue attacks.

Code link: on (recommended) / off

## ECO-01 Multi-Facility Economy (Labor, Power, Capability Tags, Receipts)

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves out: The GDD only defines the per-facility labor and power allocation order and the zero-demand production formula; it has no world state for which facilities a base has, how many, and how they operate. — Source path: rules-economy-logistics-clauses.json RECON-05·10·11·21·23·24

Proposed rule: Base facilities = up to 2 main facilities read from occupation tags + auxiliary power generation if a powered facility exists. Daily settlement: labor W = max(1, floor(household members × 0.6) − (health < 40 ? 1 : 0)) is assigned to facilities by policy priority (power 1 · water supply 2 · medical 3 · workshop 4 · market 5 · warehouse 6); ties are broken by facility ID, then labor ID. Fill = min(labor ratio, power ratio); output = base 100 × facility condition R/100 × fill (if demand is 0, fill = 1 → 80 at 80% condition). During a blackout, powered facilities other than generation produce 0. Medicine, repair, and power tags do not substitute for one another, and if labor falls short, work is partially completed. Weekly effects: the market yields +1.2 funds/day per 100 output, medical output cares for the sick (health up to +2), and workshop output adjusts facility condition (cap +15, −0.25/week if neglected). Extension (owner approved 2026-10-10): the output and storage stages require a storage reservation (an empty slot), and if there is none they wait at the previous completed stage. The base storage cap is 400, +400 if a warehouse facility exists. The player can set the designated priority (RECON-05).

Genre basis: Nowland tabulates workers, inputs, and outputs per building and provides a labor priority UI. CK3 holding buildings only have construction and upkeep and no concept of operation, so this rule follows Nowland.

Balance impact: A household (3–5 members) can barely run 1–2 facilities. The market''s maximum of +8.4 funds/week is about 12% of monthly income (about 9×30).

Current temporary values in code: src/engine/facilities.ts SPEC · src/engine/eco.ts ecoWeek

Alternative: Use only monthly aggregated output and no receipts.

Code link: On (recommended) / Off (as in the original GDD)');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.owner-approved-d09-offices', 'proposals', 'owner-approved-d09-offices', 'Approved Proposal — OFC-01', '', 'proposal', 15, 'proposals/owner-approved-d09-offices.md', '# Approved Proposal — OFC-01

This is the cleanup of D09 Example A, decided by the owner on 2026-10-10: holding multiple offices is allowed, and each person holds only one noble title. Since it is not yet reflected in the canon, it is submitted only as a proposal document (merging is up to the owner).

## OFC-01 Holding Multiple Offices and One Noble Title (D09 Example A)

Status: Proposal (owner approved 2026-10-10, pre-merge).

What the GDD leaves out: D09 Example A says one person can hold two positions, but it gives no distinction or cap between offices and noble titles. — Source path: mechanism-design-index-d09.json Example A · 3.1

Proposed rule: Offices (duties such as leader, administrator, commander, and treasurer) can be held by one person in multiples, and each office has exactly one holder. A noble title (a title of rights) is limited to one per person. Domains are property (D13), so one title can have multiple tiles under it. When an office holder dies, that office falls vacant.

Genre basis: CK3: one character can hold several court positions, but titles are ranked and each character has one representative title.

Balance impact: Duties may pile up on a single capable vassal, but the title cap prevents power concentration.

Current temporary values in code: world.ts setTitle·setOffice

Alternative: One position per person (previous invariant).

Code link: Applied (decided)');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.owner-approved-defaults-off-keep', 'proposals', 'owner-approved-defaults-off-keep', 'Approved Proposal Bundle B — Off · Keep Current Values (BET-01 Off · LD-01 · SV-01 3 cases/8 weeks · TR-01)', '', 'proposal', 16, 'proposals/owner-approved-defaults-off-keep.md', '# Approved Proposal Bundle B — Off · Keep Current Values (BET-01 Off · LD-01 · SV-01 3 cases/8 weeks · TR-01)

On 2026-10-10 the owner approved BET-01 as off by default, and LD-01, SV-01 (3 cases/8 weeks), and TR-01 as keeping their current values. This is a proposal document pending reflection in the canon.

## BET-01 The Three Conditions of Betrayal (NPC Factions)

Status: Proposed (owner approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: Off — because it changes the world map significantly, re-tune the simulation averages after the owner decides to turn it on.

What the canon leaves out: There is no action that violates a treaty or oath. (Source: rules-factions-warfare-clauses.json RWARFARE-34)

Proposed rule: When there is an alliance (a treaty to violate), a named leader carries it out, and that leader knew of a trigger event (the ally''s defeat within the last 8 weeks) and has a motive (ambition land/revenge/promotion & strength advantage of 1.3×), the alliance is broken with 4% probability per week, with tension +40 and legitimacy −8. If any one of the three is missing, it is not betrayal.

Genre basis: CK3 puts a casus belli and reputation cost on breaking alliances, and NPC rulers'' ambitions serve as motives.

Balance impact: The world map changes substantially, so NPC history differs for the same seed.

Implementation: src/engine/world.ts betrayalWeek

## LD-01 Effect Sizes of Logistics Load Failure and Collapse Decisions

Status: Proposed (owner approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: Keep current values — the effect on simulation average lifespan is small.

What the canon leaves out: The canon does not set the effect sizes of the five failure (75–99) decisions and the five collapse (100) outcomes. (Source: rules-economy-logistics-clauses.json RECON-20)

Proposed rule: Retreat −25 · Abandon (funds −15%, −15) · Requisition (−12, reputation −3) · Split (−14, relationships −4) · Press On (health −3); Stranding 90 · Surrender 60 (reputation −10) · Disband 60 (household members −1) · Rescue 60 (funds −30%) · Last Stand 85 (clash opens).

Genre basis: In Nowland, crisis choices trade resource costs against morale costs.

Balance impact: A single decision costs about 1–3 weeks'' worth of losses.

Implementation: src/engine/crisis.ts loadResolve

## SV-01 Micro-Line Promotion Threshold

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: keep 3 events/8 weeks — keeps the world ledger from growing too large.

What the canon leaves open: there is no threshold at which a character''s micro line becomes a world event. (Basis: rules-save-determinism-clauses.json RSAVE-08)

Proposed rule: when the same character has 3 events of the same kind within 8 weeks, they are promoted into one world event (lines already promoted are not counted again). The "Succession Support" line has no triggering action yet.

Genre basis: CK3 fires a chain event when accumulated relationship events cross a threshold.

Balance impact: 3 events can be reached with two pacifications and a talk.

Implementation: src/engine/micro.ts MICRO

## TR-01 Strategic Map Travel Cost Coefficients

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: keep current values — to be re-set when costs are tied to real time and resources.

What the canon leaves open: there are no base costs per segment or condition surcharge coefficients (nor encounter probabilities). (Basis: rules-travel-encounters-clauses.json RTRAVEL-05~10)

Proposed rule: floor transition 1; injury 0.25, pain 0.15, fatigue 0.2, overload 0.3; darkness 15%, flooding 30%, debris 20%, fire 50%; hostile control 40%, no travel pass +60%; party +10% per person; stealth +20%; escort +25%. Base encounter chance 20% + noise 5% per level.

Genre basis: in Nowland and CK3, travel stacks terrain and seasonal surcharges multiplicatively.

Balance impact: travel cost is display-only, so it does not yet affect the economy.

Implementation: src/engine/travel.ts TRAVEL_PLACEHOLDER');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.owner-approved-defaults-on-c', 'proposals', 'owner-approved-defaults-on-c', 'Approved Proposal Bundle C — On (PRC-01·STR-01·SCH-01)', '', 'proposal', 17, 'proposals/owner-approved-defaults-on-c.md', '# Approved Proposal Bundle C — On (PRC-01·STR-01·SCH-01)

These are proposals the owner approved on 2026-10-10 with the default set to On. They are not yet in the canon, so they are posted only as proposal documents. The stress tiers 0–99/100–199/200–299/300+ are values verified from the reference book; all other numbers are provisional.

## PRC-01 Market Price Multiplier (Scarcity, Isolation, Import Dependence)

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — the GDD coefficient 0.35 stays as is, and only the scarcity term is proposed.

What the canon leaves open: RECON-07 only says a scarcity term is added to the price multiplier, but gives no formula for it. (Basis: rules-economy-logistics-clauses.json RECON-07·08)

Proposed rule: purchase unit price = 1.0 × (1 + scarcity + 0.35 × isolation × import dependence). Scarcity = clamp(1 − stock/(3 days of need), 0, 1) × 0.5. Isolation = 1 − K. Import dependence: 0 for the agriculture trade (self-sufficient stations take no penalty), 0.5 otherwise. Prices do not create stock. Replaces the existing fixed 1.1.

Genre basis: in both CK3 and Nowland, scarcity and blockades are reflected in prices.

Balance impact: during a blockade, the food purchase unit price rises to at most about 1.7×. On ordinary shortage days it is 1.0–1.5×.

Implementation: src/engine/sim.ts marketPrice / src/engine/cargo.ts

## STR-01 Stress (The Cost of Choices Against One''s Nature)

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — the tier boundaries are verified values and the rest are small.

What the canon leaves open: the CK3 reference book''s stress tiers (0–99/100/200/300+) are verified, but the triggers and the breakdown re-trigger wait are unconfirmed. (Basis: references/ref-crusader-kings-3.json §10.1·10.2)

Proposed rule: if a character whose Threat = communal/principled, Reward = material, or Scheme = principled has a public value above 20, they take (excess/5, max 14) × 2 stress. Recovery is −2 per week. At tiers 1, 2, and 3, mood drops by the tier number each week; at tier 2 and above, health drops by (tier − 1). A breakdown event occurs only after an 8-week wait at tier 1 or above (no immediate repeat each time 100 is reached): relationship −3 × tier, stress −40.

Genre basis: CK3 stress is a device that keeps choices from being converted into gold.

Balance impact: if a character with strong values repeatedly makes conflicting choices, they reach tier 1 within a few weeks.

Implementation: src/engine/intrigue.ts

## SCH-01 Schemes (Target, Requirements, Separating Success from Discovery)

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — the effects are small and do not affect the world map.

What the canon leaves open: only the structure of schemes is referenced; the numbers are unconfirmed. (Basis: references/ref-crusader-kings-3.json §8.3)

Proposed rule: a 4-week scheme costing 5 funds against a rival or someone with a grudge. Success = clamp(0.3 + 0.06 × records skill, 0.1, 0.85); discovery = clamp(0.35 − 0.03 × records skill, 0.05, 0.5), rolled with separate hashes. On success: target''s mood −6, loyalty −4. On discovery: reputation −3, relationship −4. Stress is added.

Genre basis: in CK3 schemes, success and discovery are distinct variables.

Balance impact: at records skill 3, success is 48% and discovery is 26%.

Implementation: src/engine/intrigue.ts');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.owner-approved-defaults-on', 'proposals', 'owner-approved-defaults-on', 'Approved Proposal Bundle A — On (ECO-01·CHT-01·BLK-01·PRJ-01·BT-01·KIT-01·SET-01)', '', 'proposal', 18, 'proposals/owner-approved-defaults-on.md', '# Approved Proposal Bundle A — On (ECO-01·CHT-01·BLK-01·PRJ-01·BT-01·KIT-01·SET-01)

These are proposals the owner approved on 2026-10-10 with the default set to On. They are not yet in the canon, so they are posted only as proposal documents. The nation→kit assignment table in KIT-01 is a provisional assignment from a stable hash and requires owner confirmation.

## ECO-01 Multi-Facility Economy (Labor, Power, Capability Tags, Receipts)

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — it is the core of making a stronghold feel like it "works," and it uses the GDD formulas (RECON-05·21·23·24) as they are. If turned off, only the existing monthly output remains.

What the canon leaves open: the GDD only sets the per-facility order of staffing and power allocation and the zero-demand production formula; there is no world state for which facilities a stronghold has, how many, or how they work. (Basis: rules-economy-logistics-clauses.json RECON-05·10·11·21·23·24)

Proposed rule: stronghold facilities = up to 2 main facilities read from occupation tags, plus auxiliary power generation if there are powered facilities. Daily settlement: workforce W = max(1, floor(household × 0.6) − (health < 40 ? 1 : 0)) is assigned to facilities by policy priority (power 1 · water supply 2 · infirmary 3 · workshop 4 · market 5 · storehouse 6); ties are broken by facility ID, then worker ID. Fill = min(workforce ratio, power ratio); output = base 100 × facility condition R/100 × fill (if demand is 0, fill = 1 → 80 at 80% condition). During a blackout, all powered facilities other than power generation produce 0. Medicine, repair, and power tags do not substitute for one another, and if workforce runs short the work is only partially completed. Weekly effects: market output gives +1.2 funds/day per 100, infirmary output tends the sick (health up to +2), and workshop output corrects facility condition (cap +15, −0.25/week if neglected).

Genre basis: Nowland tabulates workers, inputs, and outputs per building and provides a labor priority UI. CK3 holding buildings only have construction and upkeep and no concept of operation, so this rule follows Nowland.

Balance impact: A household (3–5 members) can barely run 1–2 facilities. The market''s maximum of +8.4 funds/week is about 12% of monthly income (about 9×30).

Implementation: src/engine/facilities.ts SPEC · src/engine/eco.ts ecoWeek

## CHT-01 Order Charter Victory and Growth Path

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — the GDD explicitly specifies multiple endings, and all values are separated out as proposals.

What the canon leaves open: there is no charter condition evaluation state (it is only specified that victory is not just a single Seoul unification). (Basis: rules-campaign-loop-clauses.json RCAMPAIGN-24·26)

Proposed rule: growth path: Capture → Stabilization → Specialization (two or more facilities at utilization 0.5↑) → Route Projection (alliances 1↑ or trade skill 3↑) → Order Charter. Failing stabilization (stage < Stabilization) or overextension O > 1.5 puts you in a Recovery posture, which can return only to Stabilization. Four charter conditions: Route Coalition (3 alliances) · Independent Station Pact (integration stage held for 8 weeks) · Trade Order (6 trade micro lines) · Technical Restoration (mastery 3). Fulfilling any one records that ending and the game continues, so multiple endings can remain together.

Genre basis: besides unification and crown victories, CK3 has achievements for fulfilling "dynasty goals." Nowland evaluates settlement prosperity goals along several branches. Neither closes victory down to a single path.

Balance impact: holding integration for 8 weeks requires O ≤ 1 and maintained authority/cooperation, so it opens up after the mid-game.

Implementation: src/engine/eco.ts charterWeek

## BLK-01 Blockade and Total Isolation

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — it fills the ST-02 gap and upholds the GDD wording (preserve cause and recovery conditions rather than deleting).

What the canon leaves open: from a single-person viewpoint there is no case where K = 0 (K minimum is 0.7). (Basis: rules-strongholds-clauses.json RSTRONGHOLDS-06·18)

Proposed rule: when an isolation-type crisis strikes, a blockade lasts N = 2 + floor(O) weeks. During the blockade, K = 0 (cause and recovery conditions are shown), trade income is cut in half (imports and exports 0), and local stockpiles and power generation continue. Conditions for lifting: the period elapses or a facility repair project is completed.

Genre basis: in CK3, sieges and blockades are time-limited and their lifting conditions are visible. Nowland constantly displays trade route blockage warnings.

Balance impact: you lose up to about 50% of monthly income for 2–4 weeks. The crisis probability itself is determined by the existing formula.

Implementation: src/engine/eco.ts openBlockade

## PRJ-01 Stronghold Project Workload

Status: Proposal (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — the workload range is the GDD value, and pause-preservation matches economy-owner.build.

What the canon leaves open: there is no action to start a major project (workload 100, only 40–240 exist). (Basis: rules-strongholds-clauses.json RSTRONGHOLDS-12)

Proposed rule: two projects (facility repair and barrier reinforcement). Workload = clamp(100 + round(20 × O) + (barrier 30), 40, 240). Weekly progress = workforce × 10 × (0.5 + 0.5 × R/100), materials cost 2 funds/week. If materials run out, work halts, but progress is preserved and spent amounts are not refunded. On completion: repair = facility condition +12 (blockade lifted), barrier = reputation +6.

Genre basis: in Nowland, construction centers on stage-by-stage labor and material consumption and on pausing and resuming.

Balance impact: with 3 household members it takes about 8 weeks; overextension takes longer.

Implementation: src/engine/eco.ts startProject

## BT-01 Reinforcements (fixed at battle start)

Status: Proposed (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — it meets the GDD requirements (fixed at start, separate force) and has little impact.

What the canon leaves blank: there is no input for the conditions under which reinforcements arrive. (Basis: rules-battle-clauses.json RBATTLE-21)

Proposed rule: if reputation is 40 or higher and household size is 3 or higher, a relief force sent by a neighbor joins as a separate squad in round 3. Size = min(6, 2+floor(reputation/30)). The condition is determined once at battle start, and external state is not queried again mid-battle.

Genre basis: in Total War, reinforcements arrive at an arrival time set by pre-battle deployment rules.

Balance impact: a high-reputation character gets 2–6 extra units, an advantage of roughly 10–25%.

Implementation: src/engine/battle.ts reinforcementFor

## KIT-01 Mixed Contracts and Cultural Identity Kits

Status: Proposed (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — the effect is small and it respects the GDD''s "weights only, not automatic approval." The kit assignment table needs owner confirmation.

What the canon leaves blank: there is no rite/culture key state and no vassal contract clauses. (Basis: rules-factions-warfare-clauses.json RWARFARE-39·41)

Proposed rule: when a vassal contract is created, record both rites (liege key + vassal key) on one line. Nation→kit is a provisional assignment by stable hash (5 types). Kits are used only as evaluation weights: Eastern targets get +5 to the vassal revolt-risk threshold, and for Central Records, conferring a title is invalid if record-keeping skill is below 2. The other kits are display-only.

Genre basis: in CK3, culture and faith change vassal contract terms and acceptance weights (not automatic acceptance).

Balance impact: the impact is limited to two kits.

Implementation: src/engine/kits.ts

## SET-01 Same-Week Settlement Order

Status: Proposed (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On — it has no cost.

What the canon leaves blank: the implementation had no ordering for when multiple NPC thresholds coincide in the same week. (Basis: rules-strongholds-clauses.json RSTRONGHOLDS-20)

Proposed rule: multiple wars and thresholds in the same week are settled in stable ID order (random numbers do not change the order).

Genre basis: CK3''s monthly settlement also processes in deterministic ID order.

Balance impact: no change in outcomes; only reproducibility is guaranteed.

Implementation: src/engine/world.ts worldWeek');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.owner-approved-mst-01', 'proposals', 'owner-approved-mst-01', 'Approved Proposal — MST-01 Multi-Stronghold State', '', 'proposal', 19, 'proposals/owner-approved-mst-01.md', '# Approved Proposal — MST-01 Multi-Stronghold State

This is a proposal the owner approved on 2026-10-10 with the default set to On. It is submitted only as a proposal document because it has not yet been merged into the canon. The formulas in RCAMPAIGN-17·21 are unchanged; only the stronghold list composition and distance decay are proposed (provisional).

## MST-01 Multi-Stronghold State (Capital + Granted Fiefs)

Status: Proposed (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On.

What the GDD leaves blank: RCAMPAIGN-17 (stronghold-weighted average) and 21 (target weighting toward the weaker stronghold) presuppose multiple strongholds, but in this game a character holds only one stronghold. The GDD has no stronghold list composition or per-distance values. — Basis paths: rules-campaign-clauses.json RCAMPAIGN-17·19·21

Proposed rule: stronghold list = my nation''s capital tile + the fief tiles I have received (max 7). Transfer tiles (4 or more neighbors) have weight 1.5, all others 1.0. A stronghold h tiles away from the capital gets shortage ×(1+0.15h), connectivity ×0.9^h, authority and cooperation −3h, and isolation +0.1 if at war. Crisis probability is based on the stronghold-weighted average shortage and isolation, and the crisis target leans toward the weaker stronghold via the GDD weighting formula (w×(1+2×shortage+2×isolation+(1−min(S,F,L)/100))). The target is recorded in the ledger.

Genre basis: in CK3, the farther a holding or fief is, the weaker the control, and rebellions and crises arise from the weakest holding.

Balance impact: a character with no fiefs has a single stronghold, so it is the same as now. As fiefs increase, crisis targets become more dispersed.

Provisional values in current code: coefficients in src/engine/stations.ts

Alternative: fix at a single stronghold (current behavior).

Code link: On (recommended) (default) / Off (single stronghold)

Implementation: src/engine/stations.ts');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.owner-approved-unr-01', 'proposals', 'owner-approved-unr-01', 'Approved Proposal — UNR-01 Collective Pressure (Grievance, Cohesion, Leverage Mapping)', '', 'proposal', 20, 'proposals/owner-approved-unr-01.md', '# Approved Proposal — UNR-01 Collective Pressure (Grievance, Cohesion, Leverage Mapping)

This is a proposal the owner approved on 2026-10-10 with the default set to On. It is submitted only as a proposal document because it has not yet been merged into the canon. The pressure formula and thresholds are unchanged from RWARFARE-09·13; only the mapping from stronghold measurements to G·C·V is proposed (provisional).

## UNR-01 Collective Pressure (Grievance, Cohesion, Leverage Mapping)

Status: Proposed (owner-approved 2026-10-10, not yet merged). Recommended default: On.

What the GDD leaves blank: RWARFARE-09·13 define the pressure formula and thresholds (40/70, leverage 0.75, 2 consecutive weeks), but there is no mapping that reads grievance G, cohesion C, and leverage V from stronghold measurements. — Basis paths: rules-factions-warfare-clauses.json RWARFARE-06·08·09·13

Proposed rule: G = 100×(0.6×shortage S + 0.4×(1−L/100)), C = cooperation L, V = clamp(0.5×(1−K)+0.4×S+(grain<60 ? 0.2:0), 0, 1). Pressure follows the GDD formula as is, and coercive demands (strike, blockade, secession) open only when pressure ≥70 and V ≥0.75 for 2 consecutive weeks. When opened, the situation coercive-demand → choice event (accept, negotiate, suppress). The trust T update clamps event impact × credibility (= my fulfillment reliability Rel) to −25–25: accepting a grant +8, grievance −4, rebellion risk −10.

Genre basis: in CK3, when vassal grievances and faction pressure cross a threshold, an ultimatum event opens.

Balance impact: it opens only when grain shortage and isolation coincide, so it rarely appears in peacetime.

Provisional values in current code: src/engine/unrest.ts measure

Alternative: random event.

Code link: On (recommended) (default) / Off (as in the original GDD)

Implementation: src/engine/unrest.ts');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.pa-01-aging-ability-decay', 'proposals', 'pa-01-aging-ability-decay', 'PA-01 Aging Ability Decline — Specification (Status: "Proposed", awaiting GDD PR)', '', 'proposal', 21, 'proposals/pa-01-aging-ability-decay.md', '# PA-01 Aging Ability Decline — Specification (Status: "Proposed", awaiting GDD PR)

Code: `src/engine/aging.ts` (pure formula) · `src/engine/sim.ts applyAging` (applied on birthday). Tests: `tests/specs.test.ts`.

## 1. What the GDD Leaves Blank

D02 (Living, Needs, Health, Mood) and `character-sheet-formulas.json` have a rule for health 0, but do not define the formula, starting age, or target abilities for a "penalty that accumulates with age." This spec is a proposal to fill that gap. It is not GDD canon.

## 2. Rules

On every **birthday (the day age increases)**, if age a ≥ 50, make one check.

1. Pool P(a): a∈[50,59] → {ST, DX}; a≥60 → {ST, DX, IQ}.

2. Failure probability `p(a,HT) = clamp( base + perYear×(a−50) − htRelief×(HT−htRef), minP, maxP )`.

3. If the roll u1∈[0,1) is less than p, it is a **failure**. The target ability is `P[ floor(u2×|P|) ]`.

4. On failure, if that ability''s current value > the floor (8), that ability drops by 1 (cumulative, `run.flags.aging_ST|DX|IQ`) and health drops by 2 (game side). At or below the floor, there is no change ("blocked by the floor").

5. u1 and u2 are `hash32(seed|aging1|cid|a)` and `hash32(seed|aging2|cid|a)` / 2³² (they do not consume the random number stream, so they do not disturb other outcomes).

6. The original wiki sheet (cache) is not modified. Displayed ability score = wiki value − cumulative decline.

7. Derived values (HP=ST, etc.) are recalculated according to the GDD derivation formulas. Values whose derivation formula is undecided keep their undecided marking.

## 3. Parameter Table

| Name | Value | Range (adjustable) | Meaning |

|---|---|---|---|

| startPhysical | 50 | 45–60 | Age at which ST·DX checks begin |

| startMental | 60 | 55–70 | Age at which IQ enters the pool |

| base | 0.15 | 0.05–0.30 | Base failure probability at the starting age |

| perYear | 0.03 | 0.01–0.05 | Increase per year of age |

| htRelief | 0.02 | 0–0.04 | Decrease per HT point |

| htRef | 10 | Fixed | Reference HT |

| minP / maxP | 0.05 / 0.95 | — | Probability clamp |

| floor | 8 | 6–10 | Ability floor |

| healthPerLoss | 2 | 0–5 | Health deduction on decline (game side) |

## 4. Examples

- Age 50, HT 10: p = 0.15. u1=0.10 → failure, u2=0.3 → pool{ST,DX}[0]=ST −1.

- Age 60, HT 10: p = 0.15+0.03×10 = 0.45. u1=0.40 → failure, u2=0.99 → IQ −1.

- Age 70, HT 14: p = 0.15+0.60−0.08 = 0.67.

- Age 50, HT 30: p = clamp(0.15−0.40)=0.05 (minimum).

- Age 60, ST already 8, u1=0.1, u2=0 → failure, but “blocked by the floor”, no change.

Expected decline (HT 10, 30 checks from age 50→80): Σp ≈ 0.15…1.02 clamped → about 17 times, total ability change about −17 (spread across the three abilities).

## 5. Test Cases (tests/specs.test.ts)

Age 49 p=0 · example values at ages 50/60/70 · clamp · pool composition · ability selection (u2) · floor block. Engine determinism: life records from the same seed are identical (tests/engine.test.ts).

## 6. Genre Basis and Balance

CK3 has age-bracket frailty traits, and GURPS rolls HT every year from age 50 (ability decline). The goal is to keep average lifespan in the 70s, and decline creates succession pressure in late life. Alternative: a continuous curve (max HP reduction).

## 7. GDD PR Draft

Title: `D02: Add ability decline judgment due to aging (proposal)`

Body: Add the following section to “D02 Life, Needs, Health, Mood”. “A character aged 50 or older makes one aging check each birthday. Failure probability is p = clamp(0.15 + 0.03×(a−50) − 0.02×(HT−10), 0.05, 0.95). On failure, one of the pool ({ST, DX}, and {ST, DX, IQ} from age 60) is chosen and loses 1 point (floor 8). The check''s random number uses a deterministic hash, not the character stream.” Parameters are placed in a table as owner-adjustable. Basis: the HT and health-0 rules in character-sheet-formulas.json. Status: proposed.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.pw-01-war-declaration-threshold', 'proposals', 'pw-01-war-declaration-threshold', 'PW-01 War Declaration Threshold — Specification (Status: “Proposed”, awaiting GDD PR)', '', 'proposal', 22, 'proposals/pw-01-war-declaration-threshold.md', '# PW-01 War Declaration Threshold — Specification (Status: “Proposed”, awaiting GDD PR)

Code: `src/engine/warrules.ts` (pure formulas) · `src/engine/world.ts` (TUNE, used by worldWeek). Setting switch: “Design Doc Implementation Status” tab (Calm/Turbulent/Cold War).

## 1. What the GDD Leaves Blank

`rules/rules-warfare-clauses.json` establishes that war is not a troop-count comparison (-19), the ranges of legitimacy L (0–100) and strength S (0–100), and that unilateral attacks do not open a state war (-35), but **it has nothing on at what tension value a declaration of war occurs, the mobilization period, or the ceasefire threshold**.

## 2. Rules (weekly settlement, in state ID order)

**Tension** weekly for each pair (a,b): `Δ = borderPush×min(5, jeopgyeongkan)/5 + goalPush×(hwakjang yangmang-in jjok su) − tensionCool − (dongmaeng allyCalm) − (bongsinssang 1)` (jeopgyeongkan = border tiles; hwakjang yangmang-in jjok su = number of sides with an expansion ambition; dongmaeng = alliance; bongsinssang = vassal pair); tension = clamp(tension+Δ, 0, 100). Expansion ambition = {promotion, land, revenge}.

**Declaration possible** (a against b) = all true: ① tension ≥ declareTension ② S_a ≥ minStrengthRatio×S_b ③ L_a ≥ minLegitimacy ④ ongoing wars < maxWars ⑤ neither is in another war ⑥ not allies ⑦ not a vassal pair ⑧ a''s ambition is expansion ⑨ no cooldown on the pair or on either state.

**Candidate score** = tension + 5×claim tiles + (5 if the ambition is revenge). Each week, the one pair with the highest score declares (only ties use a random number from the `world` stream).

**After conclusion**, pair cooldown = peaceCooldown weeks, both states'' rest = stateRest weeks, pair tension = 10.

## 3. Parameter Table (Presets)

| Name | Calm (default) | Turbulent | Cold War | Meaning |

|---|---|---|---|---|

| declareTension | 70 | 60 | 85 | Declaration threshold |

| maxWars | 2 | 3 | 1 | Cap on simultaneous wars |

| peaceCooldown | 156 weeks | 78 weeks | 260 weeks | Ban on renewed war between the same pair (weeks) |

| stateRest | 156 weeks | (same) | (same) | State rest after war ends |

| minStrengthRatio | 0.6 | | | If the weaker side is below 60% of the stronger side, it cannot declare |

| minLegitimacy | 25 | | | Legitimacy floor |

| borderPush / goalPush / tensionCool / allyCalm | 0.8 / 0.3 / 0.6 / 1.5 | | | Tension coefficients |

## 4. Examples

- 3 border tiles, only one side with expansion ambition: Δ = 0.8×3/5 + 0.3 − 0.6 = **0.18**/week → about 390 weeks (7.5 years) to reach 70. 9 border tiles, both sides expansionist: 0.8+0.6−0.6 = **0.8**/week → about 88 weeks.

- If allied, Δ −1.5 → tension decreases.

- Tension 75, S_a 80, S_b 60, L_a 50, 0 wars, expansion ambition → declaration possible. At tension 69, blocked by `tension`. S_a 35 · S_b 60 → `strength`.

- Candidate score 72, 3 claim tiles, revenge → 92.

Simulation report (`npm run world-report`): target of about 100 wars per century under the Calm preset.

## 5. Tests (tests/specs.test.ts, tests/world.test.ts)

Blocking per condition · 3 tension Δ examples · candidate score · invariants (one holder per tile, cap on simultaneous wars) · same seed, same ledger.

## 6. Genre Basis and Balance

In CK3, you need a casus belli to declare war, and wars end by war score. The casus belli in this spec are “claim, border clash, border tension,” and tension corresponds to CK3''s opinion and accumulated grievances. Calm yields about 100 wars per century.

## 7. GDD PR Draft

Title: `rules-warfare: add tension/propaganda thresholds and a cap on simultaneous wars (proposal)`

Body: Add a “War Declaration Conditions” section. Pair tension for states is updated weekly by the formula above, and a declaration is made by the single highest-scoring pair when all nine conditions of §2 are met. The default Calm (70/2/156) and the optional presets are placed in a table. Unilateral attacks (-35) do not go through this section. Basis: -19 (war is not a troop-count comparison), -20 (S 0–100), legitimacy L 0–100. Status: proposed.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Scenario-Hold-the-Gate', 'proposals', 'scenario-hold-the-gate', 'Signature Event Design: The Siege That Doesn''t Close the Door', '', 'proposal', 23, 'proposals/Scenario-Hold-the-Gate.md', '# Signature Event Design: The Siege That Doesn''t Close the Door

Status: proposed, not implemented, not playtested.



## 1. Experience Hypothesis and Scope

> At first, I open the door so that I can pass. On the return, I guard that door in an open state until someone else has passed through.

The hypothesis is that when an earlier choice changes the goals and solutions of the same place, the player understands the change in the world as their own story. This event is the first case for examining the hypothesis, not a rule that turns every expedition into a round-trip siege.

The candidate setting is an invented connecting segment between Yeongdeungpo, Sindorim, and Guro. It does not claim to reproduce actual historical blueprints, the existence of freight doors, or facility structures. There is room to use the three stations from the existing ToDo, but door states, convoys, and character reactions are proposals that need separate implementation. No new states, required leaders, or mystery answers are added.

The first paper experiment uses one connecting segment and departure/return state cards. It does not request new art for Seongsu or the entire line.

## 2. Cast and Conflict

The roles in the following table are not canonical character IDs. At implementation review, existing cast members whose location, affiliation, position, life/death status, and relationships fit must be assigned. If no eligible character exists, character-centered payoff is put on hold, and names or leaders are not auto-generated.

| Role | What They Want Now | Compromise and Action | Narrative Function |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| The Expedition and Companions | Carry the supplies safely and get paid. | Chooses among negotiation, detour, work, forcing through, and non-intervention. | Starts from the player''s actual objective. |
| Site Supervisor | Keeps their own shift and the passage safe, and maintains authorized passage. | Can negotiate once ownership of the goods and the scope of passage are confirmed. | At first an obstacle; later, if conditions are right, a co-defender. |
| Transport Lead | Sends the announced supplies to their destination and brings the travelers back. | Considers escorting, adjusting the schedule, and conceding part of the cargo. | Shows the benefit of the opened route through concrete people and supplies. |
| Armed Recovery Party | Wants to recover the disputed cargo or enforce their own contract. | Reveals their goals and compromise conditions through advance clues. | A party to the conflict with a separate purpose, not an automatically generated penalty. |

Do not establish the recovery party''s claims as true. Their legitimacy, violence, and chain of command will be decided in the follow-up cast and faction review. The first experiment uses a role card that discloses their intent, and does not publish a new hostile group as canon.

The Site Supervisor''s cooperation is used only when all of these conditions are met: they are alive, still on site, their own shift is included in the transport, and they are not immediately hostile to the player. If the player killed or antagonized them, the same help and dialogue are not played.

## 3. Space and Advance Information

The connecting section should read as a safe-side assembly point, an operable door in the main passage, an outside waiting point, and a narrow maintenance passage. Travel is possible in both directions, but the main passage and the maintenance passage have different transport capacities.

Before the choice, show the following.

- The maintenance passage allows only people and light loads; carts cannot pass through.
- A repaired door can be opened and closed. If destroyed, it stays open and cannot be closed.
- Physical opening does not automatically grant passage permission or ownership.
- Distinguish whether the permission is limited to the expedition or also includes the designated transport party.
- Give the player a chance to confirm the transport schedule, known recovery party approaches, and the commission''s pay and conditions.

Separate confirmed information from unverified rumors. Do not secretly add risks that punish good deeds. The costs of repair and negotiation should appear on screen as agreed-upon figures, and this document does not fix global balance.

## 4. Departure Choices and Immediate Rewards

| Resolution | Method of Passage | Physical State and Rights Left Behind | Immediate Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Negotiation | Work with on-site personnel to open a usable door or obtain conditions for opening it. | The door is usable. Permissions for the expedition and the transport party are each stated explicitly. | Preserve the cargo and obtain approved trade and passage. |
| Repair | Restore the device with the necessary skills and resources. | An operable door. Passage rights must be secured separately. | The transportable volume and the next movement options increase. |
| Forcing Through | Resolve the clash and pass by destroying the door. | A door left open that cannot be closed. Passage rights and hostility are separate matters. | Gain immediate movement and a chance to carry out the commission. |
| Detour | Send people and light loads through the maintenance passage. | The main passage is unchanged. Only information about the detour is newly gained. | Reduces exposure and earns pay for light transport. |

This is not a request to add negotiation and repair as separate buttons. They are action combinations possible within the content, and their connection to current features will be agreed upon in Section 9.

When the original commission''s completion conditions are met in Guro, the promised pay is given. Participation in the return siege is not attached as a hidden condition of the original pay. Follow-up transport moves only if the route is open and permissions are in place. If only part of the cargo was transported, apply the partial compensation announced before departure.

## 5. Conditions for the Return Event to Occur

The return battle is not a mandatory spectator scene. It becomes a candidate only when the following conditions are met together.

1. An actual transport schedule and designated cargo exist, and the passage conditions are in place.
2. The time and route conditions for the transport party and the recovery party to meet in that section are satisfied.
3. The recovery party''s objective has not yet been resolved.
4. The player is on site or has decided to take part in the escort.

Merely because the player opened the door, the game does not spawn a retrieval squad. Resolving the supply dispute beforehand or changing the transport schedule can prevent combat. If the player has left, the game does not forcibly move them to another location. In the first paper test, non-intervention is satisfied by ending the scene and recording the stated state; it does not require implementing a full remote simulation.

| History at Departure | Differences at Return | Prohibited Handling |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Secure a door that can be operated through repair or negotiation | Plan the door operation and the passage of personnel together. | Arbitrarily breaking the door so it matches the forced-entry route |
| Destroy the door and pass through | Defend by position and detour in a passage that cannot be closed. | Restoring the door for the sake of the return scene |
| Use the detour only | The cargo door is closed, and light movement and follow-up negotiation remain. | Automatically opening the door and the convoy just to show combat |
| Resolve the dispute in advance | Confirm the transport results and the reunion without combat. | Always adding a new raid for the sake of fun |

## 6. Return Scene and Play Objectives

On the representative route, the convoy is at an outside waiting point and the retrieval squad is approaching. Closing the door right away makes a safe-side defense easier, but leaves behind people who have not yet passed through. The player holds the passage while moving personnel and cargo inside.

The pre-agreed objectives are protecting personnel and completing the transport. Wiping out the enemy is only one solution, not the sole end condition. Before combat, fix the participants, door state, passage, each person''s position, objectives, retreat route, enemy intent, and reinforcement conditions. During combat, do not re-read the outer campaign state to change the objectives or the enemy.

| Approach | Player''s Judgment | Required Follow-up Features |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Direct Defense | Which character guards the passage, and who helps with the transport? | Escort objective, display of occupancy and objective progress |
| Use the Maintenance Passage | Reroute the people first and leave the large cargo behind? | Per-target movement restrictions, multiple escape points |
| Use Door Operation | How should movement and opening/closing be combined? | Action costs, passage/occupancy checks, door state reflection |
| Negotiation or Cargo Concession | Change the contract terms to secure people''s safety first? | Partial supply settlement, non-combat resolution conditions |
| Withdrawal | When not every promise can be kept, what do you preserve before leaving? | Handling of legal retreat routes and partial results |

If there is a field supervisor who meets the role conditions, they guard the door on the same side as the player, unlike at the start. This is not an affinity reversal; it is because their own shift crew is now outside as well. An example line is "Don''t close it. Our crew is still out there." This line is not a confirmed line of a canonical character, and it can be used only when that character and shift crew actually exist.

### End and Limits

Full success is when the agreed protection targets and delivery commitments are met, or when a negotiation guaranteeing transport safety is completed. Conceding some cargo, rescuing some targets, and withdrawing are separate partial results. It must be possible to resolve the scenario without wiping out the enemy.

Closing the door once does not immediately lock in a narrative failure. The protection targets can be moved by reopening the door, detouring, or negotiating. The outcome ends when the safety, capture, death, or departure of every target is confirmed, or when the party leaves by a legal retreat route. Infinite reinforcements and endless reward repetition are prohibited, and the limits and objective checks will be specified numerically in a later prototype.

## 7. Success, Failure, Absence, and Non-intervention

| Situation | Result to Leave | Player Choices to Preserve |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full Success | Supplies arrive, trade becomes possible, and the participating characters remember the actual events | Do not automatically impose an additional disaster after success. |
| Protect personnel by conceding cargo | The people return, and the delivery amount and payment are reduced as agreed | Do not treat negotiation as a fake success inferior to combat. |
| Part of the convoy captured | Only the characters concerned remain as prisoners, and recovery is conditional. | Do not gloss over it as having returned every character safely. |
| Field supervisor dead, departed, or hostile | There is no joint-defense line and no support. | Do not automatically generate a replacement character with the same name. |
| Absence of a key companion | That person''s skills and relationship resolution are unavailable, but another valid route exists. | Do not lock the entire incident just because a particular companion is absent. |
| Party Withdrawal / Defeat | Record injuries, supplies, location, survival or death, and per-objective results exactly as they occurred. | Do not erase failure by reloading or with immortality protection. |
| Non-intervention / Detour | Keep the original commission payment and any route information obtained. | Do not impose a forced-retreat battle, a hidden morality-score penalty, or an unexplained global grudge. |
| Encounter Conditions Expire | The encounter ends with a safe passage or a different scene that fits the current conditions. | Do not revive a dispute that has already been resolved just for the sake of a showcase scene. |

Relationship changes spread only to the parties involved, witnesses, and people who received a trustworthy report. Avoid presentation in which someone the news has not yet reached instantly knows every fact. The first experiment is limited to the few characters involved and does not require a new global rumor system.

## 8. Linking State and Settlement

The following items are a list of meanings for implementation review; they do not finalize any new save schema or API names.

| Category | Meaning to Preserve |
| --- | --- |
| Physical State | Door damage and whether it can be operated, discovery of detours, and passage capability |
| Legal and Social State | Who and what the permit covers, ownership claims, and hostility toward the on-site supervisor |
| Event Conditions | Transport schedule, whether the dispute is resolved, participation choices, and the locations and life-or-death status of related characters |
| Tactical Inputs | Participants, terrain, objectives, locations of personnel and cargo, door state, retreat routes, seed, and reinforcement conditions |
| Result | Per-objective results, character fates, actual supply changes, time, location, route changes, and relationship events |

Maintain the authority defined in [Strategy–Battle Roundtrip](/gdd/rules/strategy-battle-roundtrip/). The campaign owns persistent state, and combat generates results from fixed inputs. Do not let the UI and the battle-end handler each apply relationship rewards or door damage separately. Reflect the result only once through the existing ResultId and settlement contract. Re-delivering the same result returns the earlier receipt and does not create new rewards or relationship events.

The same initial state, version, seed, and command log must produce the same tactical and settlement results. Do not hide results and re-roll them for the sake of dramatic story development. This document does not add an implementation of file saving and loading, nor does it claim that one is complete.

## 9. Existing Foundations and Additional Implementation

| Category | Scope | Handling in This PR |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Product Goal Contract | Three stations, the flow of negotiation, detour, and combat, the deterministic core, and a single settlement | Refer to the scope recorded in the ToDo. Unity was not rerun for this work. |
| Existing Design Contract | Repair, transport, rescue, and escape objectives; character relationships; strategy–tactics roundtrip | Follows the [tactics document](/gdd/rules/personal-resolution-battle/) and the canonical source, and is not treated as implemented. |
| Content Design | Casting, order of information disclosure, meaning of rewards, dialogue, and result scenes | Reviewed in this proposal packet. |
| Additional Implementation Candidates | Door and detour states, per-target passage, convoy and escort objectives, partial results, and follow-ups for a small number of relationships | Will not be started without the owner''s and development team''s scope approval and a separate issue. |
| Lower Priority | Full character simulation, dynamic broadcasts, automatic event casting, and a new economy | Not introduced in this experiment. |

## 10. Minimum Validation and Hold Criteria

One set of the paper experiment compares the same space under different starting histories. Three conditions are used: an operable door, a destroyed door, and an unopened main passage. Keep role cards and prior information identical, and change only past actions and the states derived from them. Swap the order of the two conditions, and record answers given before hearing the explanation separately from answers given after learning it.

The observation record notes each participant''s history, reasons for choices, resolution actions, causal explanations, characters they remember, next plans, and rules that blocked progress. The criteria below are proposed small-scale exploratory criteria, not statistical validation.

- The first exploratory session targets three new participants. No recruitment or session has taken place yet.
- If at least two participants can state, without explanation, the relationship between an earlier choice and the current objective, it is a positive signal of causal communication.
- Across the whole session, two substantially different resolution methods must be observed, and at least one non-combat path must reach an outcome.
- If the same annihilation strategy is the only best option under every history, or the objective feels the same even when the door state changes, revisit the battlefield design first.
- If repeat visits feel like rework rather than discovering change, or if a companion''s circumstances read only as a forced constraint, do not expand the scope.

As disproof tests, review cards that force combat on the detour route, cards in which a dead supervisor helps, cards in which a convoy passes without a permit, and results that grant rewards twice. If no intended disproof is found, revise the event state table to be clearer. Passing the document checker does not replace this paper experiment or the fun validation.

## 11. Completion of This Document and Next Decisions

What this document completes is a review proposal that includes character motivations, prior information, choices and follow-up conditions, settlement boundaries, and validation methods. Playtesting, a Unity prototype, art integration, and approval of the canonical cast are separate.

If the owner accepts this event as a validation target, the cast linkage and the minimum prototype scope will be discussed. Until the fun is validated, do not write follow-up events in bulk or expand into full siege, economy, or diplomacy systems.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Station-Interior-Design', 'proposals', 'station-interior-design', 'Proposed Interior Design Standards for Stations, Underground Facilities, and Settlement Buildings', '', 'proposal', 24, 'proposals/Station-Interior-Design.md', '# Proposed Interior Design Standards for Stations, Underground Facilities, and Settlement Buildings

Status: Proposal. This does not imply changes to approved base or economy rules, or a runtime implementation. Whether to accept per-facility field measurements, commercial adaptation rights, and the opening setting will be reviewed separately.

## Scope and Handling of Unverified Material

Passenger rail facilities within the current administrative boundary of Seoul are surveyed as a union of candidates, together with existing wiki buildings used as settlements and explicitly selected major underground buildings. For school, medical, community center, administrative, logistics, and maintenance buildings, confirm the actual existing spaces and the selection scope. Do not use wording about the former expansion beyond Seoul as grounds for current activity spaces. The existing 334 game station IDs and the 427-building ledger are not the same as a full census of real facilities, and are not to be deleted or renumbered.

Even if the current boundary and the latest geometry have not been confirmed, the candidate survey and a source-based neutral design draft proceed. Record the boundary edition, effective date, hash, gaps in currency, and items to be acquired, and do not mix official and community editions with different dates. Do not turn unverified items into certified current status, and keep them able to be addressed by later data updates. The real-world survey date, the source data''s reference and revision dates, the boundary''s effective date, the facility''s completion and opening dates, and the fictional opening date are each separate values.

## Canonical Ownership and Approved Rules

The GDD owns meaning, data types, relationships, constraints, and decision status. The current Wiki source owns facility identity, observation references, and approved opening facts. The external asset source owns the prototype geometry, edit state, adaptation losses, and the basis for rights. GAME consumes only projections approved afterward. A GDD snapshot is not used in place of the current Wiki. This proposal grants no new owners, titles, characters, eras, facility prices, widths, floor heights, capacities, production volumes, or fuel formulas.

It references facility slot RSTRONGHOLDS-11 and occupancy unit RSTRONGHOLDS-25 in [Stronghold and Territory Rules](/gdd/rules/rules-strongholds-clauses/), and in [Economy and Logistics Rules](/gdd/rules/rules-economy-logistics-clauses/) it references advance reservation of essential consumption, actual labor and tool/material consumption, orders and bottlenecks, and education contracts. It maintains the stable IDs, source hashes, generation receipts, movement graph, and separation of metric boundaries from [Game Data Ledger and Schema Contract](/gdd/design/data-catalog-and-schema/). The proposals in this document do not overwrite these accepted documents.

## Proposed Data Types for Facilities, Wings, Levels, Spaces, and Connections

| Entity | Proposed Fields and Data Types | Meaning and Constraints |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Source Row | sourceRowRef: {provider, namespace, code, revision} | Preserve the original row. An alias or an identical name is not evidence of the same facility. |
| Facility | facilityId: permanent string; facilityType: category; complexId, buildingId, wikiAnchorRefs: references | Distinguish station, underground-shopping, underground-public, mixed-complex, school, medical, civic, logistics, workshop, and other. Transfer complexes, line stops, and game nodes have IDs separate from facilities and are linked through a many-to-many junction table. |
| Wing / Annex | wingId: optional permanent string; facilityRef: reference | Create one only when there is an actually distinct wing. Unconfirmed is different from not applicable, and fictional wings are not generated automatically. |
| Actual Level | levelId: permanent string; facilityRef, wingRef: references; label: string; z, verticalDatum: value or unconfirmed | A label such as B2 and the actual height are separate. Identical level names with different heights, and half-levels, are permitted; heights are not derived by multiplying the level number by a constant. |
| Space | spaceId: permanent string; levelRef: reference; sourceFeatureRefs: array of references; shape, quantity: values per evidence | Rooms, corridors, halls, equipment, sanitation, storage, and loading spaces, along with doors, walls, columns, and entrances, are mapped to the source. Width, depth, or room count are not generated from a single area figure. |
| Connection Candidates and Verified Connections | assertedConnection: facility-level connection statement, facilityRefs, evidenceRefs; pendingEndpoint: confirmed references and unconfirmed items with reasons; VERIFIED connector: connectorId, from/to {facilityRef, levelRef, spaceRef, anchorRef}, type, direction, intermediateStops, evidenceRefs | A facility-level connection claim is preserved as an assertedConnection, and any unconfirmed part of either end is left as a pendingEndpoint. No fake spaces, rooms, or coordinates are filled in. Only a VERIFIED connector, once the actual facility, level, space, and anchor at both ends, the connection evidence, and any needed evidence for inter-level or elevation correspondence are confirmed, is admitted to the traversal graph. Interior passages are not created from adjacent footprints, the same Bn, or a railway line map alone. |
| Shared Area | shared spaceRef, connectorRef: single physical ID; facilityRefs: array of references | A station, a shopping arcade, and another building reference the same common area. The area is not double-counted, and the managing party, right of passage, and operating status are kept as separate relationships. |

## Judge Measurement, Topology, and Rights Independently

Review identity, metric geometry, topological connection, date and currency, and rights to use the source material each separately. Preserve each feature''s measured, inferred, topological, authored, or unknown status, along with the measurement method, unit, error, source edition, page, and SHA-256. A non-scale guide map is topological evidence, not a surveyed floor plan. Facility-level connection claims and topological confirmation are not forced to require metric measurement, and the actual endpoints of a VERIFIED connector and any needed inter-level correspondence can also be confirmed through topological evidence. Promotion to metric requires separate measurement evidence, and promotion to commercial production requires separate usage rights. OSM layer, level, level:ref, and building:levels are different raw values, and layer is not converted into a level count. Confirmation on one axis does not substitute for passing on another.

The source material''s rights holder, scope of permission, license, attribution, and commercial adaptation review are separate from ownership, management rights, occupancy, and right of passage in the fictional world. Public availability for viewing or the existence of a real facility owner alone does not establish the right to reuse a drawing. Even if measurement data exists, if usage rights are unconfirmed, promotion of that source for adaptation remains undecided. Missing data, and non-public or obscured parts, are not reconstructed by guesswork.

| Value | Meaning | Values Not to Be Confused With |
| --- | --- | --- |
| unknown | The data or the basis for judgment is unconfirmed. If it is a number, express it as null with a reason for being unconfirmed. | 0, none, passable, unclaimed land |
| none | It has been confirmed that the target does not exist. Leave evidence of absence. | Not surveyed, data missing |
| unclaimed | A state in which unclaimed land has been confirmed in the fictional rights record. | Owner unconfirmed, no rights holder in the real source material |
| not-applicable | This item does not apply to that entity, and there is a reason. | Asserting absence because an optional field was omitted |

## Separate Actual Levels, Living Functions, and Abstract Slots

An actual level is the architectural structure, a functional zone is the usage plan for that space, and a facility slot is the abstract capacity under the approved stronghold rules. The default slots of 2 for a regular station and 3 for a transfer station are not read as a number of rooms or levels. The existing four-function levels are not promoted to four physical levels, and the global flooding template is not promoted to per-station observation. Deep levels are not added to surface stations, and basements are not automatically generated for schools or hospitals. Unconfirmed spaces remain unconfirmed in the prototype, and schematic adaptations are marked as creative work.

The metric prototype preserves the coordinate system, origin, axes, vertical datum, sourceFeatureId, and diagonals, curves, slopes, and connections. The Maptsukuri adaptation has a separate mapId, geometryHash, level and compartment references, and object correspondence table, and distinguishes m/cell from px/cell. Record the crop origin, transformation, compartment boundary connections, dimensional displacement, losses, manualEditRevision, edit state, and export hash. Design displacement is different from measurement error, and an orthogonal adaptation is not inverse-transformed into a surveyed prototype. PNG and dd2vtt output does not mean the 3D implementation is complete.

## Ask About Winter Living Functions for Each Facility

Review reuse of existing forms first, but do not settle here whether new construction is allowed or what it costs. Maintain the Wiki''s eternal winter setting and review cold exposure at actual entrances, wind protection, heating compartments, and ventilation. Distinguish the baseline temperature of the living area from the observation, heating, and outside-air intrusion of the actual space, and do not assume that the same level has the same depth in meters or the same current temperature. The presence of power and electrical storage alone does not mean heating is solved. The rainy-season and flooding templates are not copied as per-station facts without verification.

| Function Under Review | Question to Ask of the Space | Boundary to Record |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sleeping, Rations, Medical Care, and Education | Who uses which actual space, and how is it separated from waiting, movement, and evacuation routes? | Space references, existing evidence for the acting party, and usage constraints. Do not create capacities, educational effects, or new characters. |
| Water Supply, Cooking, Sanitation, and Waste | How are drinking and cooking water separated from livestock and contaminated water, and from drainage and waste routes? | Confirmation status of sources, inputs, outputs, and processing constraints. Do not create new consumption or throughput figures. |
| Storage, Logistics, Loading, and Maintenance | Is there evidence for the receiving entrance, the connection''s width and height, inter-level transport, and the space for tools, materials, and storage? | Unconfirmed effective width, height, and load capacity are null. Unconfirmed passages are not passed as logistics-capable. |
| Power, Electrical Storage, Heating, and Ventilation | What are the actual constraints on equipment spaces, fuel, exhaust, heat loss, and safety separation? | Separate facility existence, whether it works at opening, and its in-operation status, and do not invent heating or power-generation formulas. |
| Checkpoints, Defense, Evacuation | For which function are the confirmed exits, stairs, and doors used, and which routes are affected if they are blocked? | Separate physical connection, right of passage, opening blockade, and runtime change. |

Do not turn the full list of candidate functions into a mandatory list for every facility. Leave the reason for adoption, non-adoption, or an undecided status according to the evidence and constraints for stations, shopping areas, schools, medical facilities, and community service centers. Do not treat the discovery of a technical record as an immediate tech unlock, a modern institution''s purpose as continued operation after the collapse, or real institution names and staff as fictional criminal actors.

## Separate definition, opening seed, and progression state

An immutable definition binds designId, definitionRevision, sourceEditionRefs, each source hash, geometryHash, and stable facility/space/connection IDs. The opening seed references that definition and provides only the approved initial ownership, occupation, blockade, and facility operating states. A campaign delta in progress references the causing event, time, target ID, and the base definition/seed edition. Do not copy characters, titles of nobility, or real-time operations into the asset sidecar.

Partial holding, facility management, right of passage, whole-building occupation, administrative-district slot rights, and electoral-district titles are separate relationships. None of them grants another automatically. No-man''s-land is valid, and no lords, population, or political placements are generated without evidence. The existence of a physical door or passage does not imply that it is open, working, or permitted for access. Do not overwrite current ownership, blockade, operation, or damage states by re-importing initial values.

## Data Updates and Manual Edit Conflicts

A re-import does not modify the existing accepted definition; it prepares a candidate that freezes a new source edition and input hashes. It compares sourceFeature changes, deletions, splits, and merges together with a stable-ID crosswalk, and checks that the base geometryHash and manualEditRevision match the current edited version. Raw observed values, manual adaptations, and edit history are preserved. IDs are not merged merely because they share a name.

A stale source hash, a broken spatial reference, a confirmed treatment of an unverified connection, different edited versions of the same mapId, and an overlap between a source change and a manual edit are reported as conflicts, and promotion of the candidate is refused. When validation fails or approval is pending, the bytes of the last accepted definition, observed values, edited version, and campaign delta are preserved. Conflict resolution is recorded with the grounds for the change as a separate manual revision, and after explicit verification and acceptance it references a new immutable definition. No reverse writes or fill regeneration are applied to the source. Applying a new seed and converting an in-progress campaign are separate follow-up GAME acceptance issues, and this document does not permit automatic migration.

## Boundary Between Review and Follow-up Acceptance

Report separately, per facility, the target denominator and the review status and reasons for identity, metric, topology, rights, currency, adaptation, and living functions. Do not drop unmeasured facilities from the denominator, and do not call the candidate processing rate a full-survey measurement rate. assertedConnection and pendingEndpoint are valid partial survey records and do not block completion of the survey package wholesale. However, pending items are excluded from the traversal graph and from the numerator of the confirmed-connection ratio, and are preserved in the unconfirmed list. For example, keep the Our Gallery–Simincheong connection wording, but if the actual endpoint on the Simincheong side is unconfirmed, do not create a room or coordinates, and leave it as a pendingEndpoint. Do not promote it to a VERIFIED connector before both ends and the required inter-floor correspondence are verified. Reconciling source, rights, hash, and both-end references is done separately from reviewing the actual rendered text. Substituting slots for room counts, creating nonexistent floors, assigning fixed lords, copying a rainy-season template for winter, double-counting the area of shared passages, and automatically joining the same B2 remain rejected candidates and are not put into the canon.

This proposal does not provide per-facility floor plans, measured values, opening settings, a new validator, or game features. Unconfirmed detailed archetypes and rights are follow-up survey items and are not conditions that block the draft. Reflecting it in the distribution canon requires actual approval and verification for that scope, and consumption in Unity requires separate import, serialization, execution, and save verification.

## Basis for Review

Read the current canon at the links above for approval rules and data contracts. The worldbuilding standard is the entirety of WORLD_BUILDING_GUIDE.md from the Wiki-owned source, together with World-and-Subway-Layers, not the GDD snapshot. This proposal follows the scope within Seoul, winter life, partial occupation and no-man''s-land, and preservation of undecided values. Real-world per-facility materials and usage rights are reviewed separately in the external source ledger, together with edition, URL, and hash.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.ui-01-campaign-time-control', 'proposals', 'ui-01-campaign-time-control', 'UI-01 Campaign Time Control — Specification (Status: 「Proposal」, Awaiting GDD PR)', '', 'proposal', 25, 'proposals/ui-01-campaign-time-control.md', '# UI-01 Campaign Time Control — Specification (Status: 「Proposal」, Awaiting GDD PR)

Code: `src/engine/speed.ts` (pure formula) · `src/main.ts curSpeed/startTimer/tick`.

## 1. What the GDD Leaves Blank

SP-1.0.0 states, 「This document does not add new campaign time control,」 and battles have no pause, speed control, or command queue (ui-implementation-pipeline). Nothing has been decided about stopping or speeding up the campaign clock.

## 2. Rules

- Speed level L ∈ {1, 2, 4, 8}. Day length `msPerDay(L) = round(100 / L)` ms.

- **Auto-pause** (the clock stops and resuming is manual): awaiting a decision (event, policy, or age question), result window open, battle open. Battle stops the campaign clock and runs only in its own real time (no pause or speed control, GDD unchanged).

- **Speed cap** `cap = min( hpRatio<0.25 ? 2 : 8, nae nara jeonjaeng jung (my country at war) ? 4 : 8 )`. Effective speed = min(L, cap). When the cap applies, the timer is immediately readjusted to the new value.

- 「Pause」 is the user''s pause button (play/pause). The day and month step buttons advance manually while paused.

## 3. Parameter Table

| Name | Value | Adjustment Range |

|---|---|---|

| Levels | 1,2,4,8 | 1–16 |

| Default day length | 100ms | 50–250 |

| HP danger threshold / cap | 0.25 / 2× | 0.1–0.4 / 1–4 |

| Cap during war | 4× | 1–8 |

## 4. Examples

msPerDay: 1×=100, 2×=50, 4×=25, 8×=13ms. If you choose 8× and your nation enters a war, the effective speed is 4×. If HP then drops to 20%, the effective speed is 2×. When a pending decision appears, the game auto-pauses.

## 5. Tests (tests/specs.test.ts)

Day length · 3 auto-pause cases · 4 cap cases (peacetime/war/HP/both) · effective speed. ui-smoke: speed buttons 1/2/4/8 displayed.

## 6. Genre Basis and Balance

CK3, Norland, and Total War campaigns provide pause and 3–5 speed levels. In Norland the clock stops when a window opens. Lowering the cap from 8× during danger or war reduces missed events.

## 7. GDD PR Draft

Title: `campaign-loop: add campaign time control (pause, 4-level speed) and auto-pause rules (proposal)`

Body: Add a 「Campaign Clock」 section. Speeds 1/2/4/8, auto-pause on pending decisions, results, and battle, and a cap during danger or war. Battle keeps no pause, speed control, or command queue, as before. The sentence in SP-1.0.0, 「Do not add new campaign time control,」 is replaced by this section. Status: Proposal.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Ref-Crusader-Kings-3', 'references', 'ref-crusader-kings-3', 'Reference Receipt Identification — Not Build-Dependent', '', 'accepted', 26, 'references/Ref-Crusader-Kings-3.md', '> [!NOTE]

## Reference Receipt Identification — Not Build-Dependent

The latest independent verification, depth-verification-20261027.md:1855-1900 (§9), judges the static evidence of the corrected depth-01/02/03 and the gap inventory as a PASS for synthesis input. The initial FAIL history is preserved. depth-04 is a PASS for limited static fidelity. This does not guarantee overall gameplay meaning, runtime causality, installation integrity, combat damage formulas, or recovery of the original Ghidra project metadata. The references below are only CK3 structural references and not authority for the Seoul product, and the mismatched totals, coping effects, clan formula, and legacy costs are not adopted as Seoul rules.

The product source is linked to https://github.com/islee23520/seoul-dengoku/blob/3e3c03349eafaaf88347d119a4bdd0c6d44abc53/AGENTS.md and the GAME/Backend paths at the same revision. CK3 research receipts are identified by the file names and hashes below, which contain no personal information, and neither a local sibling repository nor an ignored evidence directory is used as a prerequisite for materialize/build. Copyrighted game files and original binary content are not brought in. Readers who do not know where the reproduction receipts are can read the verification limits in this canon.

## 2026-10-27 Code Anatomy Reference Boundary

CK3 references: see mechanics-01-time-and-pulse.md:11-43, mechanics-02-character-rpg.md:125-173, mechanics-03-schemes.md:11-173, mechanics-04-war-and-combat.md:11-95, mechanics-05-economy.md:54-98, mechanics-06-vassals-court-council.md:11-95, mechanics-07-succession.md:182-209, mechanics-08-faith-culture.md:165-207, and internal-architecture-synthesis.md:117-126. Installation integrity, the full execution order, hot reload, and multiplayer/replay reuse of commands are unproven. CK3 figures are not Seoul rules. The in-depth research totals and clan formula are not adopted from summaries that remain in an independent-verification mismatch state. The queue in depth-04-war-and-binary.md:1317-1426,1628-1635 is for submission/insertion, and the actual consumption loop and damage formula remain unrecovered.

[Separation Contract Between Seoul Facts and Product Authority](/gdd/rules/game-logic-principles/)

> Source handoff: shared research index. Mentions of the local sources.json and raw-text packets are records from the time of research and do not mean that raw materials are included in this delivery. Missing IDs remain unconfirmed.
> This is an auto-generated document. The original was derived from an internal research document in the repository, and work titles and original identifiers are omitted from the public text and kept in the internal research ledger.

# First Comparison Target Game Mechanics Reference

- Written: 2026-09-06. Task ID: `st_01a078b9`.
- Target: a dynasty-centered grand strategy game for PC. It explains the basic governance structures of feudal, clan, and tribal rule. The cultural divergence and fusion of the court expansion and the regional struggles of the regional expansion are treated separately as expansion rules.
- Scope: a mechanics document in 10 chapters for reading how the game works. It is not a data dump covering every patch and DLC event ID, per-tier building costs, regional unit types, and faith lists. It must not be used as a table of figures for the latest 2026 build.
- Research limits: only the designated local report was read beforehand, and 5 wiki API pages were requested. **All 5 returned a browser verification HTML page instead of JSON, so the source text could not be obtained.** No further searches, workaround queries, or game launches were performed. Wikis that could not be read are not passed off as evidence for confirmed rules.

### How to read the evidence labels

- **[Documented]**: a fact stated explicitly in the local report that was read. The original source S-ID is noted alongside, but this does not mean the original source was re-read in this task.
- **[Inferred]**: strategic implications or explanatory calculations derived from the stated mechanics. Distinct from rules the game itself publishes.
- **[Unverified]**: prior knowledge about the known base-game rules that could not be checked against source text or gameplay in this research. Exact figures, whether they apply to the current patch, and DLC conditions await verification. If this label appears in a heading, it applies to all tables and lists beneath it.
- **Scope of citation**: A notation such as `[woncheon-M02; wonmun mihwakbo]` (source-M02; original text not obtained) records a failed lookup of the relevant wiki and the place for follow-up checking. It does not mean that the preceding sentence has been proven by that original text. S19–S33 inherit the original source numbers from the earlier local report.
- **Important version boundary**: after release, schemes, clan contracts, combat advantage, activities and travel, diseases, governments, and culture were overhauled. Figures from different periods are not merged into a single "current formula."

## Verification Status and Version Boundaries

This document is derived from a research document written on 2026-09-06. That research read only the designated local report and requested 5 original-source wikis, but all 5 returned a browser verification screen instead of the source text, so the source text could not be obtained. Therefore, the [Documented] label in this document means that the local report was read, not that the original sources were reopened and cross-checked.

The table below divides the authority of what this document states. Verified means facts stated explicitly in the local report that was read, Inferred means explanations derived from those facts, and Unverified means items that could not be checked against source text or gameplay in this research. Figures in unverified items are not adopted numbers and are not carried over into Seoul''s rule values.

| Status | What applies in this document | Whether it can be adopted |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Verified | Items written in the report that was read, such as the fact that characters and dynasties are the basic viewpoint, the distinction between the five skills and personal prowess, and the fact that council tasks are tied to the assignee''s skills | Only within the scope of the facts. Do not extend them into numerical values |
| Version Boundary | After release, schemes, clan contracts, combat advantage, activities and travel, diseases, governments, and culture were overhauled. This document is not a table of figures for the current 2026 build | Figures from different periods are not merged into a single current formula |
| Unverified | Items marked Unverified in their headings, such as start years, opinion values by succession law, resource exchange rates, demesne and development, lifestyle perk details, and dynasty legacy tiers | Adoption prohibited. Not even to be listed as candidates before research |

The original work''s institution names, titles, and source identifiers are not repeated in public documents. The internal cross-reference linking source text to adoption candidates is kept in the research ledger, and this document leaves only what is to be read in Seoul.

- Tables and lists in a section whose heading is marked Unverified are Unverified throughout that section.
- Formulas written as inferences are not formulas published by the game.
- No figure in this document becomes a coefficient of Seoul''s rules without the owner''s approval.

## 1. Overview

### 1.1 Unit of Play and Goals

**[Document]** This game''s basic viewpoint is not a permanent state object but the ruling **character and their dynasty**. In the character window you view traits, skills, health, family, heirs, relationships, court, and vassals as connected, and you select territory and rulers on the map. An individual''s health is not tactical combat HP but an attribute related to the risk of survival and death.[Source-M01][S19][S21][S31]

**[Unverified]** The representative base historical start dates are 867 and 1066, and later expansions added other start dates such as 1178. The traditional default end year is 1453. The player handles marriage, education, administration, schemes, and war in parallel on a pausable real-time calendar. End conditions vary depending on the rules and the playable government. In traditional landed play, losing an heir of the same dynasty who can inherit, or losing playable territory, can result in game over, but this must not be applied as is to expansion play such as landless adventurers.

**[Inferred]** Therefore the key measure of success is not just coloring the map. "Does the power base remain after death," "does the house produce multiple rulers," and "were the faith, culture, and lineage plans realized" are parallel goals. The current ruler''s victory can create the next ruler''s fragmentation.

### 1.2 Three Layers of State [Unverified]

| Layer | Main state owned | What happens on transition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Character | Age, personality, skills, lifestyle perks, health, stress, personal relationships, honor and faith | When the character dies, their ability to make choices and their web of relationships end or are reorganized. Personal honor and faith are not treated as a shared dynasty balance. |
| Title and Holding | Title laws, liege, vassal relationships, buildings, development, control | Even when the holder changes through succession, war, grant, or revocation, the holding''s material state largely remains. |
| House and Dynasty | Kin lineage, house head and dynasty head, dynasty renown, legacy | Long-term investments and bloodline continue even when the player character changes. |

## 2. Core Loop

### 2.1 From Pause to the Next Crisis

**[Documented]** Character status, the important warnings and pending-matter bundles at the top, council tasks, and target selection on the map are the entry points of the repeated decisions. Events link the relevant characters and choices, and a choice may carry a stress burden if it does not fit the personality.[S19][S27][S29][S30]

**[Inferred]** One cycle goes as follows.

1. **Diagnosis**: Check heirs, demesne limit, income and military expenses, vassal discontent, alliances and wars, and stress and health.
2. **Goal selection**: Choose a near-term goal such as inheritance stability, duchy consolidation, a specific marriage, cultural innovation, or faith reform.
3. **Character assignment**: Place suitable people as spouses, wards, councilors, commanders, and scheme partners.
4. **Action commitment**: Invest resources, time, and political risk in construction, marriage, gifts, schemes, securing a casus belli, and declaring war.
5. **Time progression**: Monthly income and growth, pregnancy, birth and death, events, tasks and schemes, and army movements all proceed together.
6. **Handling the aftermath**: Respond to prisoner ransoms, new vassals, control of occupied land, debt, stress, and worsening relationships.
7. **Generational handover**: Redistribute titles and territory through death and succession, and run the diagnosis again with the new character.

### 2.2 Actions Occupy Time, Resources, and People at the Same Time [Unverified]

- A single councilor focuses on a specific task. If a steward is placed on development growth, that steward cannot perform other administrative tasks during that period.
- Personal schemes and hostile schemes have limits on how many can run at once, and perks and rules change these limits. Improving one relationship delays other marriage and friendship goals.
- Calling up an army means accepting higher upkeep than in peacetime and a risk of troop losses. Even a defensive victory can be a financial loss.
- Marriage is a long-term contract that binds not only a diplomatic alliance but also the children''s dynasty, genetics, inheritance rights, and court position.
- If you disinherit a character using dynasty renown, a permanent legacy purchase that the same resource could have bought is delayed.

### 2.3 The Boundary Between Information and Certainty

**[Document]** Nested tooltips explain terms and the causes behind calculations, but not every event reveals every outcome. `hidden_effect` exists, and `ai_chance` is the AI''s choice weight, not the player''s success rate. Some single-choice events also merely confirm a result that has already occurred. You must distinguish known costs, conditions, chances of success or discovery, and hidden outcomes.[S25][S28][S29][S31]

## 3. Progression Structure

### 3.1 Personal Growth and Era Growth [Unverified]

- **Childhood**: Through education focus and guardians, it influences adult skills, education traits, and personality formation. Personality is not equipment that lets you equip unlimited desired bonuses.
- **Adulthood**: You earn lifestyle experience and unlock perks, and accumulate wealth and prestige through marriage, children, conquest, and activities.
- **Old Age**: Prepare for declining health, inheritance distribution, underage heirs and regents, and rebellions right after succession. A long-lived ruler''s relationship assets are not copied intact to the heir.
- **Dynasty Growth**: Bloodline expansion through marriage and an increase in independent rulers raise house renown, and legacies grant effects to characters across multiple generations.
- **Cultural Era**: The innovation pool expands through the Tribal Era, Early Medieval, High Medieval, and Late Medieval. Both calendar conditions and existing innovation research requirements are considered, so paying gold does not immediately grant primogeniture.[Source-M05; original text not obtained]

### 3.2 Three Questions of Succession [Unverified]

1. **Who is eligible**: Gender laws such as male-only, male-preference, equal, female-preference, and female-only; religion and government conditions; and legitimacy, oath, and disinheritance status.
2. **How is it divided**: Realm succession laws such as Confederate Partition, Partition, High Partition, and single-heir inheritance.
3. **Is there a separate law for a specific title**: Elective titles can choose a different heir from the realm succession law. Do not equate succession of the primary title with succession of all county titles.[Source-M02; original text not obtained]

### 3.3 Major Succession Laws and Opinion Effects [Unverified]

| Law | Title Distribution | Opinion and Power Effects and Risks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Confederate Partition | Divides titles among eligible children, and if conditions are met, can newly create equal-rank titles for distribution. The primary heir gets priority for the primary title and the capital base. | In exchange for giving shares to multiple children, new kingdoms may arise and break away. Partition-type laws are structurally more favorable to non-primary-heir children than single-heir inheritance. |
| Partition | Divides the titles you hold, but does not create new titles because of inheritance. | If you held a second kingdom title, it can still split apart. "No new titles created" does not mean "no fragmentation." |
| High Partition | Gives the primary heir at least half of the titles first and divides the rest. | It makes it easier for the primary heir to maintain power, but does not eliminate the other children''s shares and claims. |
| Primogeniture | Single-heir succession through the line of the eldest eligible child. | Favorable for preserving territory, and excluded relatives become a source of grievance and justification for conflict. There are technology, authority, and special-power conditions. |
| Ultimogeniture | Single-heir succession through the line of the youngest eligible child. | The possibility of a long reign and the risk of an underage succession both increase. |
| House Seniority | The oldest eligible member of the house succeeds. | The age structure of the entire house becomes more important than planning around your own children, and short reigns may recur. |
| Elective | Electors for each title vote according to candidate eligibility, liking, skills, and political interests. Candidate and elector rules differ among Feudal, Scandinavian, Saxon, Tanist, and other elections. | Even if the ruler supports the candidate they want, victory is not guaranteed. The Feudal election''s direct-vassal opinion of `+10` is an example of a known base rule, but this time it could not be cross-checked against the original text. Do not generalize it to the applicable titles or to other election laws. |

The **exact opinion values and scope of application** of the laws themselves, such as Partition and Primogeniture, could not be confirmed at present. Do not sum up relatives'' inheritance grievances, vassals'' grievances over authority changes, the law bonuses of election laws, and "opinion of predecessor" into a single fixed modifier. For law changes, the required innovation, crown authority, vassal consent, and prestige costs must be checked separately in the relevant UI.[Source-M02; original text not obtained]

**[Inference: Partition Example]** If a king holding Kingdoms A and B has two equally eligible sons and there is no separate election or exception, the primary heir may receive A and the other son may receive B, becoming an independent king of equal rank. Conversely, if the primary heir inherits an empire and his brother receives only a kingdom under that empire, the brother usually remains a vassal. The exact distribution also reflects existing grants made during the ruler''s lifetime and the title hierarchy, so "simply dividing counties by the number of children" is not the actual algorithm.

### 3.4 Means and Costs of Succession Preparation [Unverified]

Alternatives include granting titles during one''s lifetime, managing the primary title and capital, introducing elections and influencing votes, lawful disinheritance, capital oaths permitted by religion, controlling marriage and childbirth, and creating higher titles. Disinheritance may carry dynasty renown, personal prestige, and family relationship costs, and murder carries the risks of a failed hostile scheme, discovery, and kinslaying. Destroying a title creates vassal grievances and prestige costs, and does not necessarily prevent Confederate Partition from regenerating titles.[Source-M02][Source-M06; original text not obtained]

## 4. Economy

### 4.1 Major Resources That Are Not Interchangeable [Unverified]

| Resource | Typical Inflows | Typical Expenditures and Uses | Separate Cumulative Metrics |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Gold | Demesne taxes, vassal payments, ransoms, plunder, church support, events | Construction, recruiting and maintaining men-at-arms, levies, mercenaries, gifts and bribes, activities, title creation | Debt creates restrictions on actions and military and political pressure. |
| Prestige | Titles and status, wars, activities and events, marriage | Some casus belli, law and culture changes, tribal troops and construction, etc. | The Level of Fame is distinguished from the balance held. |
| Piety | Learning, clergy, pilgrimage, and doctrine-aligned actions; wars and events | Holy wars, church requests, conversion, faith creation and reformation, etc. | The Level of Devotion is distinct from the held balance. |
| Dynasty Renown | Living dynasty members, conditional contributions from independent rulers and marriage ties, special effects | Dynasty legacy, disinheritance, and other head-of-dynasty actions | The dynasty''s Level of Splendor is distinct from its balance. |

Simply spending a resource does not lower the corresponding renown level by the same amount. Cases where the act itself is judged dishonorable or impious are separate. Personal resources and shared dynasty resources must not be mixed.

### 4.2 Domain, Development, Control [Unverified]

- **Domain Limit**: The cap on how many holdings a ruler can hold directly. It varies with Stewardship skill, title rank, spousal support, perks, culture, and so on. The traditional base rule of `+1` domain limit per `6` Stewardship is a figure that needs version cross-checking.
- **Exceeding the Domain Limit**: Penalties apply to tax and levy efficiency. This is why it is better to grant land to loyal vassals than to hold it all directly without limit. The latest per-excess-holding rate is unverified.
- **Development**: The foundation of military and economic output and cultural innovation. The known base effect for feudal and clan governments is `+0.5%` tax and levies per point of development, but government-specific exceptions and the latest figures are unverified.
- **Control**: The level of governing enforcement in a county, usually `0–100`. It drops through occupation, revolt, and events, and is restored through Marshal tasks and the like. The known base rule is `-1%` tax and `-0.5%` levies for each point of control below 100, but the current application, caps, and special effects are unverified.
- **Buildings**: Gold is paid up front, and after the construction time they yield effects such as taxes, troops, supplies, and men-at-arms garrisons. They are constrained by terrain, holding type, cultural innovations, and building slots and tiers. Men-at-arms garrison bonuses must not be mixed with the realm-wide building bonuses from launch.

**[Inference: economic judgment formula]** `wol sunsuip (monthly net income) = jikhal suip (direct income) + bongsin suip (vassal income) + gita jeonggi suip (other regular income) - gunbi (military expenses) - gungjeong·jikchaek·gita jeonggi biyong (court, office, and other regular costs)`. This is an accounting formula that describes the line items, not an engine formula that reproduces the order in which every game modifier is applied. The simple payback period of a construction is `geonseolbi (construction cost) / chuga wol sunsuip (additional monthly net income)`, but because it ignores ownership changes through death, conquest, and inheritance, it cannot be the whole basis for long-term decisions.

### 4.3 Vassal Income and Government [Unverified]

- **Feudal**: The vassal contract sets tax and levy obligations. Higher obligations generally lower vassal opinion, and changing them unfavorably without a pretext or a corresponding concession can create a tyranny problem. Special clauses such as wall, military service, and council rights are also subject to negotiation.
- **Clan**: In the early rules, opinion of the ruler directly mattered for tax and troop contributions. Later rules introduced tax jurisdictions, tax collectors, and tax policies. The two systems must not be presented with the same current formula.
- **Tribal**: Prestige level matters for vassal contributions and governing ability, and honor is spent instead of gold on some military and construction costs. Feudalization is a transition that changes the economic base and the structure of military costs.
- **Theocratic and Republic Vassals**: They pay according to religion- and government-specific rules. In particular, distinguish faiths in which clergy support affects temple income from faiths in which the ruler holds temples directly.
- **Administrative, Nomadic, and Landless**: Each has a separate economic and succession system by expansion, so the feudal contract formula must not be applied as is. The detailed figures for each have not been verified in this document.

### 4.4 Costs Do Not Reduce to a Single Fixed Table [Unverified]

The price and opinion effect of a gift vary with the recipient''s status and relevant modifiers. Completing a fabricated claim may require gold. Court positions and military upkeep are monthly maintenance costs. For mercenaries, the up-front contract cost and the contract duration are what matter, and bribing a scheme ally is a one-time cost. Therefore it is wrong to price every target the same, as in ''one scheme = 100 gold''.

## 5. Characters/Party

### 5.1 The Five Skills and Separate Prowess

**[Documented]** This game distinguishes the five main skills of Diplomacy, Martial, Stewardship, Intrigue, and Learning from personal Prowess. Traits and modifiers affect skills, and health relates to the risk of death. Instead of a fixed party as in a tactical RPG, there is an overlapping network of characters across family, court, council, vassals, and army.[S21][S27][S31]

The details of the following roles and figures are **[Unverified]**.

| Skill | Main Function | Representative Lifestyle Perk Trees |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Diplomacy | General opinion, diplomatic offers, persuasion and befriending, honor-related actions | Diplomat / August / Family Hierarch |
| Martial | Leading armies, commander advantage, military tasks and war-related choices | Strategist / Overseer / Gallant |
| Stewardship | Domain limit and taxes, construction and finance tasks | Avaricious / Architect / Administrator |
| Intrigue | Hostile schemes, counterintelligence, choices involving blackmail, seduction, and fear | Schemer / Seducer / Torturer |
| Learning | Religious and cultural research, medicine, health, and education-related actions | Whole of Body / Scholar / Theologian |
| Prowess, Separate | Personal combat strength, duels, and injury risk as a knight | A personal combat ability separate from the five basic lifestyles. Do not confuse it with the commander''s Martial skill. |

### 5.2 Lifestyle Experience and Perks [Unverified]

Each of the five basic lifestyles provides three focuses and three perk trees. You gain experience in the chosen lifestyle, and spending perk points to unlock nodes along their prerequisites leads to the finished trait. Education traits, genius traits, and other effects affect experience gain. The known base values are `25` monthly experience, `1,000` experience per perk point, and a `5-year` wait to change focus, but they have not been cross-checked against the latest patch.

Perks do more than a simple `+1 skill`; they open new actions. Examples include Diplomacy''s befriend scheme, Stewardship''s demanding gold in exchange for a pretext, Intrigue''s abduction, and Learning''s pretext for buying faith. The Prophet perk''s `-50%` cost to create or reform a faith is a known representative effect, but the current value is unverified. Do not generalize that ''this game always has exactly five kinds'', including the additional lifestyles from later expansions.[S32: only the link between lifestyles and character narratives is documented]

### 5.3 Family, Inheritance, Relationships [Unverified]

- The form of marriage determines the children''s dynasty. Distinguish ordinary patrilineal marriage from matrilineal marriage, and do not overlook the heir''s dynasty by looking only at the alliance.
- Congenital traits can have inheritance probabilities, enhancement, and recessive transmission. Even if both parents have the same positive trait, a complete result is not guaranteed in every case.
- Education traits, personality traits, congenital traits, lifestyle-completion traits, and marks of disease, injury, and crime are separate categories.
- Friend, best friend, lover, soulmate, rival, and nemesis are relationship states different from a simple opinion score. Relationships are involved in event, stress, and scheme conditions.[S20: relationship categories and opinion direction are documented]

### 5.4 Council and Character Assignment

**[Documented]** The effects of council tasks are tied to the assignee''s skill, and appointment restrictions and requirements exist. Sorting and filtering candidates links duties with characters.[S19][S27]

The basic duties and costs below are **[Unverified]**.

| Position | Skill/Function | Real Costs and Conflicts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chancellor | Diplomacy: tasks related to foreign relations, domestic vassal relations, and consolidating titular claims | There is a political incentive to appoint a powerful vassal instead of an outstanding commoner. |
| Marshal | Combat: increases control, organizes the army, trains knights, etc. | You cannot maximize both rebel-region stability and combat strength at the same time. |
| Steward | Administration: collects taxes, increases development, promotes culture | The opportunity cost among short-term taxes, long-term growth, and assimilation. |
| Spymaster | Scheming: supports or obstructs schemes, secret searches | A disloyal, high-skill spymaster becomes a dangerous figure when schemes target you. |
| Court Priest/Bishop | Learning: conversion, religious relations, fabricating claims, etc. | Whether they can be directly appointed or dismissed depends on the faith''s doctrine, and completing a fabrication may cost gold. |
| Spouse | General support or support for a specific skill | The known base transfer rate for a focused specific skill is 50% of the spouse''s relevant skill, and 20% each for general support, but exceptions for current-version, regent, and spouse eligibility are unconfirmed. |

Ignoring a powerful vassal''s demand for a council seat can incur the known base opinion modifier `-40`. This does not mean that "all councillors are hired with a salary." Distinguish council positions from salaried court positions. Eligibility, faith, and vassal contracts limit the freedom of appointment.

