| D18 | Settlement · Records | 44 | 44 | 0 | 0 | 0 | None |

## What Is Not Adopted

- A row whose document verification is finished is still not a Seoul number. Adoption requires an owner decision.
- Figures in unresolved rows are not adoption candidates.
- Not mapped and conflicting are not verified. Figures from those rows are not brought in as Seoul rules.
- Entries based only on figures from community wikis or videos are not adopted, because they have no primary source.
- Figures from different builds are not merged into a single formula.
- Work titles, developer names, and source identifiers are not written in public documents. Source cross-checks are kept in the internal research ledger.

The number of adopted figures is 0. Document verification means the source was read, not that it was brought in as a Seoul rule. The 58 unresolved rows will not change to verified until the research is finished and the source and edition are identified.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Asset-Pipeline', 'design', 'asset-pipeline', 'Art Asset Production and Storage Pipeline', 'This document covers characters, portraits, props, UI, and title art from production through import into the game. Each stage is recorded with the actual implementation status distinguished. The default character path is to assemble the approved avatar body and the parts produced in Blender.', 'accepted', 29, 'Asset-Pipeline.md', '# Art Asset Production and Storage Pipeline

This document covers characters, portraits, props, UI, and title art from production through import into the game. Each stage is recorded with the actual implementation status distinguished. The default character path is to assemble the approved avatar body and the parts produced in Blender.

## Intent and Production Path

When starting asset work, record the type, intended use, original source, usage rights, production tools, and required final format. The intent graph compiler currently operates on the existing path for each asset type. The fields and branch checks for Tripo, Auto-Rig Pro, and 3D portraits are target contracts, and implementation status is recorded separately.

- Characters: 2D Avatar Gen generates portrait and full-body PNGs from the face, body, hair, outfit, and accessory slots of the character JSON. 3D Avatar Gen runs as a separate model, rigging, and render process, and the two technologies keep their entry points, settings, generation contracts, and outputs separate.
- Props: Build or clean up sources whose rights and origin have been verified in Blender, and export them in the format the game needs.
- UI, title, and concept: Keep the 2D image generation results made through CLIProxy along with editable source files, and separately review the deliverables that will be used in the game.

## Character 3D Production

Start from the male and female body packages registered in the manifest at ART-ASSETS/avatar-gen. For 3D parts such as hair and outfits, prepare reference images, create them in the Tripo web version, and assemble them with the body in Blender. Tripo work is done in the Aside browser. 2D image generation is also used for reference sheets for Tripo input and for UI and title art. The origin and usage rights of the generation model and input images are recorded per asset.

In Blender work, preserve the original and create a new result. After checking body and face identity, boundaries, UVs, and texture connections, fit the parts and apply the project''s own toon material. Audit both sides of the mesh first, plan only the places that need repair, and re-verify the applied result in a new Blender process. The current automatic repair scope is limited.

Rigging and skinning use Blender''s Auto-Rig Pro, and animation is produced in Blender. character-tool supports rigging and export tasks. For each character, whether rigging and animation were run and the inspection results are recorded in the receipt.

## Portrait Production

2D portraits and full-body generation use the common JSON emitter in TOOL/avatar-gen/portrait/2d. Distinguish character identity and swappable slots from the designated art-style reference, and pass the common camera/pose and per-character appearance conditions in the actual request. Check the resulting PNG''s size, content, difference from the source, and hash, and link a per-image verdict. 3D renders record the source object and result hash in an independent Blender process, and the source mesh and Unity runtime import are each verified separately.

## Storage of Sources and Outputs

Editable .blend files, FBX files with external textures, approved textures and GLB files, and selected review images are kept in ART-ASSETS/<esetgun>/ (<asset group>). TOOL/ owns the production and verification code. Each asset group''s manifest.json records stable roles, repository-relative paths, SHA-256 hashes of the source and result, input provenance and usage rights, and approval status. Binaries such as Blender, image, FBX, and GLB files are committed with Git LFS and pushed to the remote, and only then is the local working folder cleaned up.

Candidates and temporary renders are gathered in the project''s .omo/evidence/<jageopmyeong>/ (<task name>). Adopted editable sources, textures, and exchange files are uploaded to ART-ASSETS. Before deleting the working folder, enumerate all regular and ignored files, and check the manifest hashes against the files in the remote repository.

## Unity Import and On-Screen Verification

Re-import the FBX and external textures independently, and check material linkage, tool version, real-world units, size, and pivot. The web GLB and Unity FBX use the same element IDs and names from avatar-contract.json, and the coordinate axes and handedness conversion are stated explicitly. Check the same element display states in Three.js and Unity.

After importing into Unity, check whether shapes and materials read clearly in the actual screens of the strategic map and the 3D free-command battle camera. Inspect from multiple angles using pan, orbit, and zoom. Runtime-slot import of game assets goes through a separate provenance and import inspection.

## UI art import

Unity UI uses textures imported by AssetDatabase and the importer settings. When compositing multiple transparent images, apply source-over alpha compositing to preserve the color and alpha of the lower layers.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Data-Catalog-and-Schema', 'design', 'data-catalog-and-schema', 'Game Data Ledger and Schema Contract', '', 'accepted', 30, 'Data-Catalog-and-Schema.md', '# Game Data Ledger and Schema Contract

The current product plan gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 single-player contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupation, and online proxy clauses of this document are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the replacement scope of [Single-Player Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are converted automatically.

This document is the canonical source for the game dataset, configuration values, CSV, and logical database contracts viewed in `/gdd`. It is not a document for creating a new database. It identifies the owning paths of the existing JSON, CSV, and canonical documents, and fixes the formats and relationships that the runtime and tools must observe when reading and exporting data.

## Ownership Principles

- The GDD owns rules, data types, relationships, constraints, units, default values, and decision status.
- LORE owns the in-world facts about characters, states, regions, stations, and cultures.
- GAME data is a projection of the approved canonical source that the runtime reads. The GDD does not restate the same values.
- WEB outputs and `design-store/` are derivatives for publishing and search. They are not edited as canonical sources.
- CSV is an exchange output made from JSON and canonical documents. The same row is not edited separately in CSV and JSON.

## Current Datasets

| Dataset | Format | Canonical Source · Owning Path | Schema | Current Records | Decision Status | Verification |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Character Values · Desires | JSON | `LORE/name-pools/values-cast.json` | `janseon.values.cast.v2` | 1,004 people | In use | `verify-cast`, cross-check against the name and status ledger |
| Character Gender | JSON | `LORE/name-pools/gender-cast.json` | `seoul-dengoku.cast-gender.v2` | 1,004 people | Includes user locks | Full check of women and men, user locks take priority |
| Organization Values · Policies | JSON | `LORE/name-pools/values-orgs.json` | `janseon.values.orgs.v1` | 37 organizations | In use | Organization ID and state reference check |
| Seoul Movement Graph | JSON | `GAME/Assets/Janseon/Data/Content/SeoulWorldGraph.json` | `seoul-world-graph-v1` | 334 stations · 435 edges | Runtime projection | Station ID, edge endpoints, and fingerprint check |
| Station Occupation Change Ledger | JSON | `LORE/places/station-control-overrides.json` | `seoul-station-control.v1` | 0 changes | Opening Baseline | Only explicitly stated changes overwrite the indicator territory baseline |
| Station Internal Ledger | JSON | `LORE/regions/station-interiors.json` | `station-interior.v1` | 334 stations | Authoring Ledger | Full cross-check of 334 stations in the station catalog |
| Administrative Dong Authoring Ledger | JSON Bundle | `LORE/regions/content/*.json` | Regional Content Contract | 427 dong | Authoring Ledger | Full check of 25 districts and 427 dong, plus source and canonical link validation |
| UI Flow and Wireframes | JSON | `GDD/system-design/total-war-ui/*.json` | Version by Document | Flows, Screens, and Connections | Design Materials | Generator and HTML Render Check |

## Field Contract

### Character

| Display Name | Machine Field | Data Type | Unit and Range | Required | Owner and Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Character Identifier | `character_id` | String | Permanent ID | Yes | Character card ledger. Do not use names as join keys |
| Name | `name` | String | Korean display name | Yes | LORE/characters |
| State Identifier | `state` | String | `S00` or `S01`–`S16` | Yes | State Ledger |
| Values | `values` | Object | −100..100 per axis | Yes | `values-cast.json` |
| Desires | `desire` | Object | −100..100 per axis, or a locked category value | Yes | `values-cast.json` |
| Gender | `gender` | String | Female or male | Yes | `gender-cast.json` |
| Lock Status | `locked` | Boolean | True or false | Yes | Protection of User-Defined Values |

The current `values-cast.json` includes names, but not every row has a permanent `character_id`. Before loading into a database or exporting CSV relations, the permanent IDs from the character card ledger must be joined in. Establishing relations by name alone is in a `migration-required` state.

A 0 on a values axis means the character has not yet leaned toward that axis; it is not a value that scores indifference as a virtue. When registering a new character or organization, fill in every values field. Organization values use the same axes and −100..100 scale as characters, and the numeric ledger is `values-orgs.json`.

### Movement Graph

| Display Name | Machine Field | Data Type | Constraint |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Station Identifier | `stations[].id` | String | Unique across the entire graph |
| Korean station name | `stations[].nameKo` | String | Actual station name spelling |
| Administrative District | `stations[].district` | String | One of Seoul''s 25 districts |
| Latitude and Longitude | `lat`, `lon` | Real number | WGS84, used only for map display |
| Edge Endpoints | `edges[].a`, `edges[].b` | Station ID | Both stations must exist; self-edges are prohibited |

Adjacency between surface administrative dong is not a movement edge. Do not automatically merge the station graph''s edges with the 427-dong surface boundaries.

## Logical Database Relations

This table is a relation contract prior to implementation. Do not build an actual server datastore or migration as part of this documentation work.

| Table | Primary Key | Main Foreign Keys | Integrity Rules |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `person` | `character_id` | `state_id` | Keep IDs after renaming; preserve user-locked gender |
| `state` | `state_id` | `capital_station_id` | `S01`–`S16`, 16 opening states |
| `organization` | `organization_id` | Many-to-many `organization_state` | Keep the IDs of the 37-organization ledger |
| `station` | `station_id` | `district_id` | All 334 stations; Korean station names are display values |
| `route_edge` | `(station_a, station_b)` | Two station IDs | Both endpoints must exist; no duplicate undirected edges |
| `region` | `region_id` | `district_id` | All 427 dong; separate the administrative-dong surface from the movement graph |
| `station_control_delta` | `delta_id` | `station_id`, `state_id` | Record baseline changes only; cause, version, and time are required |
| `dataset_receipt` | `receipt_id` | Dataset ID | Preserve the source hash, generator version, generation time, and validation result |

## CSV Export

- Character encoding is UTF-8, and the first line holds machine field names.
- Cell delimiters and quoting follow the RFC 4180 rules.
- Do not flatten arrays or objects into arbitrary strings in a single cell. Split them into relationship CSVs or keep the JSON source.
- Exported CSVs carry `schema_version`, `source_path`, `source_sha256`, and `generated_at` together as a receipt.
- CSV is a generated artifact. Make edits in the canonical JSON/Markdown and export again.

## Versions and Validation

1. Record the schema name and version in the file''s `schema` field or in a separate receipt.
2. Deleting a field, changing its meaning, or changing the ID format creates a new version. Do not change the meaning within the same version.
3. If there is no default value, leave it as `Mijeong` (undecided). Do not fill in common game values as estimated defaults.
4. The generator records its input path, input hash, and its own version in the receipt.
5. The validator does not check only record counts; it checks ID uniqueness, reference existence, ranges, user locks, and the canonical hash.
6. `/gdd` shows this contract and the current statistics, but does not copy the entire source JSON as a new canonical source.

## Regional Data Publication Boundary

The official wiki `/wiki/world/World-and-Subway-Layers` owns the world description, territory, control status, and map navigation for the 427 dong. `/gdd` explains only region IDs, file structure, the generator, validation rules, and relationships. Do not build the map a second time or duplicate the region selection UI.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Design-Requirements', 'design', 'design-requirements', 'Design Requirement Tickets', '', 'accepted', 31, 'Design-Requirements.md', '# Design Requirement Tickets

The current product plan gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 single-player contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupation, and online proxy clauses of this document are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the replacement scope of [Single-Player Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are converted automatically.

MDA layers are design vocabulary. The development unit is the GitHub requirement ticket.

In each cell, write what that element is.

## Current Goal (2026-09-19)

Combat adjudicates the attack of each individual soldier. It does not create continuous move/attack/dodge inputs for individual heroes. Characters aim for natural, realistic proportions, restrained expressions, and low-saturation flat colors, outlines, and hatching. The combat camera is a 3D free command viewpoint with pan, orbit, and zoom, and angle and FOV values are not set. Combat has no pause, slow-down, or fast-forward. Orders are previewed and confirmed for squads before engagement, and a squad that has entered melee engagement receives no new orders. Retreat is a battle-wide order, and there is no order queue.

## Viewpoint

On 2026-09-24, the owner confirmed the combat camera as a 3D free command viewpoint.

## Combat Format

As of 2026-09-28, the goal is to deploy and direct squads before engagement and to resolve engagement through per-soldier adjudication.

Named heroes and rank-and-file squads are separated. A hero is a separate character not counted in the number of rank-and-file soldiers. Unit capacity varies with the commander''s personal stats, the state''s level, and the settlement''s level, and there is no common cap. The input definitions and formula are undecided. Combat is adjudicated per individual, and no fixed cap is placed on the total number of squads, number of heroes, or army size. Supply, overextension, and the number of hero commanders limit the scale.

Decks, draws, recharging, and owner cards are not included in the new goal.

The target art uses realistic proportions and the art style of Fujimoto Tatsuki. Retargeting and SD conversion are prohibited.

## Screen Goals

The screen goal is the 12 screens of the #101 UI/UX screen contract plus 4 auxiliary mockups for death and succession. The existing HTML screens are starting material and not the current target count. Index #101. Report `/ui-ux-refs/`. The current Unity board cell names are not changed. The draft document for the unit command screen is placed in `GDD/system-design/total-war-ui/`.

## Visual Acceptance

Title is #8. UI kit, icons, and tiles are #23. Promotion slot is #26. Acceptance gate is #27.

## Implementation Order

The verification steps below are the order for finishing visual acceptance, slot connection, and the visual gate. The next product document after that is individual-adjudication combat. Card Assignment (#98) is not set as the next implementation. Squad Orders (#99) and Casualty Depiction (#100) remain as old tickets, and no new values are created in this cell.

## Product Handover Boundary of the Prototype Contract

The product preserves the character-viewpoint system contract of [Campaign Progression and Crisis](/gdd/rules/campaign-progression/). Title, menu, character selection, and game are mutually exclusive screen lifetimes, and map selection separates the body, target, location, and command authority. Politics, titles, surface, and underground are divided, while state names, state colors, and administrative/territorial boundaries are displayed consistently. Portraits use registered assets only, and the map anchors, pan, zoom, off-screen exclusion, and body circle display are maintained. Relationships and factions are displayed as body-centered ledger nodes, types, and directed edges, and no inferred relationships are created. Replacing the engine or web build is a separate technical handover, and the undecided details GP-01~14 are not filled in without approval.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Development-Roadmap', 'design', 'development-roadmap', 'Development Roadmap', '', 'accepted', 32, 'Development-Roadmap.md', '# Development Roadmap

The current product plan gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 single-player contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupation, and online proxy clauses of this document are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the replacement scope of [Single-Player Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are converted automatically.

This roadmap reflects the current module of Concept.md, `Seoul jeonyeok haiteumaep jeollyakmaep` (Seoul-wide heightmap strategy map), and the measured status in ToDo.md. It describes dependencies and failure criteria, not a schedule commitment.

## Target Form (Revised 2026-09-19)

The goal is **4X + RPG** and **individual-adjudication combat**. One character''s life, livelihood, relationships, marriage, and technology lead into production, logistics, character politics, mobilization, and war. Residents are a mixed aggregate/individual model, livelihood is a per-state institution, and technology use centers on skilled practitioners. Unit capacity varies with the commander''s personal stats, the state''s level, and the settlement''s level, and there is no common cap. The map goal and the currently known world are limited to within Seoul''s administrative boundary. Directly acquiring or manipulating territory outside Seoul is not a current goal, and detailed terrain and movement rules inside Seoul are undecided. Characters aim for natural, realistic proportions, restrained expressions, flat colors, outlines, and hatching.

The strategy screen is a 3D heightmap, and the main combat inputs are unit selection and replacing the current order. Determinism (same seed + accepted command record) is maintained.

The combat camera is a 3D free command viewpoint with pan, orbit, and zoom confirmed by the owner. There are no angle or FOV values. Combat has no pause, slow-down, or fast-forward. Orders are given only to squads before engagement, and a squad that has entered melee engagement receives no new orders. There is no order queue.

| Layer | Goals | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 4X Strategy | Explore, expand, develop, and conquer lines and stations, so that causality can be read through limited state transitions | The official wiki has the Seoul 427-dong opening territory map and the web expedition map. Unity runtime movement uses the 334-station graph, and Area 1 content is three stations |
| RPG | Character growth, relationships, livelihood, and positions enter campaign party and battlefield unit composition | Original character data exists (412 characters). Card assignment (#98) is a discarded goal, and no new rules are created in this cell. |
| Individual-Resolution Combat | Pre-battle formation, unit selection and order swaps before engagement, melee engagement locks, and the distinction between whole-battle retreat, rout, and surrender | The screen contract for the new goal is defined in documentation. |

## Current Stage

- Foundation: Bootstrap App scope/FSM, exclusive screen lease, VContainer static contract, architecture gate
- Core loop (ToDo 5–12): deterministic Core IDs, commands, RNG streams, and ledger hashes; a three-station line of Yeongdeungpo–Sindorim–Guro; a six-stage campaign including negotiation and detours; exactly-once settlement; uGUI screens including MainTitle
- Seoul regional data (2026-09-13): 25 districts and 427 neighborhoods (administrative dong) under the 2026-07-01 boundaries, OSM snapshot 2026-09-04T23:00:00Z. `TOOL/tools/regions` re-reconciles each neighborhood''s residents, livelihoods, hazards, and actions against 1,011,022 candidate ledger entries. Map browsing is handled by `World-and-Subway-Layers` on the official wiki. The 334 stations and 435 edges form a travel graph, not proof of Seoul''s land area. There is no internal Unity grid or 16-state simulation.
- Generated-art foundation: promotion of 6 historical props to source-bound BOM and runtime hookup, promotion of 3 role characters (Explorer, Medic, Patrol), 9 UI kit candidates (awaiting visual acceptance), TRELLIS v1 optional host contract frozen
- Worldbuilding and character source: per-state rosters (Cast-State-01–16, 412 characters) and relationship ledger, hostile groups G01–G27 published, narrative placements B001–B047, ISO world, family, and hostile diagrams, monster placement ledger M001–M042, World-Narrative-Atlas integration
- Public wiki: the mounted mirror of the canonical LORE and GDD is synchronized with the source commit. Unpublished fragments are tracked only in tables.
- Repository verification tools: LFS pre-smudge check, Cast-Index relationship count (outgoing edge) contract verification, leak prevention for unpublished fragments in the wiki builder, fresh-clone PlayMode setup procedure

## Next Verification Stages (Order-Dependent)

1. Seoul-wide strategy map (Decision 10, current module) — Use an offline bake of the geography bundle (`GAME-REFERENCE/data/seoul-geography-20260830/`) to build 9 z11 terrain mesh chunks, district boundaries, and a waterway overlay, and place them on the Foundation screen. Replace GenreContract and the contract tests with the new contract (RED-first). The bake output must yield an identical SHA-256 across two runs.
2. Visual acceptance (parked) — Independent 4-axis re-review of the title (#8) and the UI kit and icons (#23), plus the owner''s visual approval. A raw FAIL on COMPOSITION or PRODUCT_POLISH has no exemption. The target art uses realistic proportions and the art style of Fujimoto Tatsuki.
3. UI finalization — Complete visual acceptance and slot hookup (remainder of #59) while preserving stable element names, scene flow, and the determinism contract.
4. Slot hookup (ToDo week 16, #26) — Connect only promoted assets to uGUI runtime slots and remove temporary visuals
5. Final acceptance gate (ToDo week 17, #27) — Pass in a single run: the architecture gate, all batchmode PlayMode paths, 4-axis visual PASS, BOM fail-closed, a macOS Development player smoke test on the same Unity revision, and the temporary-resource cleanup receipt
6. Narrative and collaboration follow-up (#41) — Agreement with external contributors on the narrative direction role and its boundaries
7. Combat presentation finalization — The target screen is a 3D spectator view of individual-resolution combat, and the camera is a 3D free-command viewpoint. The 3D assets, SFX, and VFX in the Oddland donation payload (`GAME/Assets/Quarantine/Oddland/`, see the donation section of [How Assets Come In](/gdd/design/asset-pipeline/)) must pass the BOM promotion gate before being hooked up to runtime slots. The full 16-state, 412-character campaign, saving, diplomacy, and sieges are separate modules that follow.

If a stage fails, work does not proceed to the next stage.

## Verification Gates

| Gate | Pass Condition |
| --- | --- |
| Simulation | Run 100 fixed seeds twice and confirm that state and ledger hashes match |
| Map | Does not misinterpret Unity routes, floors, transfers, or blockades |
| Regional Compendium | All 427 neighborhoods covered, 0 area omissions/overlaps, re-reconciliation against original PBF candidates, rejection of empty content, anchors from other neighborhoods, and date confusion. `--geometry-only` does not count as complete |
| Combat Round Trip | 0 duplicate settlements, successful restoration at save boundaries |
| Characters | 0 major occlusion, orientation, or boundary defects in actual Play Mode captures |
| Visuals | Independent PASS on COMPOSITION / SPRITE_FIDELITY / TYPOGRAPHY / PRODUCT_POLISH |
| Assets | 0 missing rights, tool, or conversion BOM entries for release candidates |
| Build | Compilation, tests, and target build succeed on the fixed Unity revision |

The next product module is not started until the current module passes the gates above.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Game-References', 'design', 'game-references', 'Reference Game Research — What Was Borrowed and From Whom', '', 'accepted', 33, 'Game-References.md', '# Reference Game Research — What Was Borrowed and From Whom

The current reference games are limited to **Crusader Kings 3 (CK3)** and **Norland**. The overall game goal is **4X + RPG**, and the combat goal is **Norland-style individual-resolution combat**. The combat camera is the 3D free-command viewpoint confirmed by the owner, and combat has no pause. Titles that include turn-based combat are excluded from the current references, and the original past research is preserved internally in the repository under `RESEARCH/canon-reference/`. The CK2 **After the End** original (`Ofaloaf/postapoc` 0.9, `61eb04ee2b42e8a7fcdc659597fe2f30e6df07f8`) and the Fanfork (`9Kbits/ateff` 1.1.1, `8565e230ac49a092096bc122e112374f5df5418c`) are **research sources** used to compare the causal structure of this narrative; they are not additions to the current gameplay and UI reference list above, and no place names, laws, or figures from the originals have been adopted. The CK3 [system encyclopedia](/gdd/references/ref-crusader-kings-3/) is only a comparison source for titles, succession, and event presentation, and is not evidence for CK2 characters, faiths, or events. Do not mark original-only events as verified by the Fanfork, and do not substitute CK3 lore for CK2 narrative grounds.

> The application of Janseon on this page is a design direction and implementation proposal. The six presets, the 16-state strategy map,
> capture and recruitment, persistent injuries, and so on do not mean that all of them are already implemented in the current runtime.
> For the current completion status, check the development roadmap and the repository''s ToDo.md.

## Borrowing Principles

1. Every borrowing is recorded with its original source — gaps are not filled with guesses.
2. When multiple games offer different solutions to the same problem, list them side by side as options and state why Janseon chose the one it did.
3. Also state what was not borrowed — this prevents inaccurate associations.
4. Game UI research and layout design use the CK3 and Norland materials on [Interface In Game](https://interfaceingame.com/) as the primary standard. If screen materials for a title are insufficient, the same title''s official guides and actual gameplay videos serve as supplementary sources. The information hierarchy and layout principles of the screens may be referenced, but the original''s art, emblems, icons, and typefaces are not copied as they are.

## UI Reference Standards

The campaign map, battle preparation, real-time combat, diplomacy, technology, and result screens are designed according to the layout principles below. Distinguish the top status area, the right action menu, and the bottom context panel, and in combat make the battlefield state readable through the force balance and the minimap.

The current conversion scope covers not only combat and movement but also the connections among daily life, relationships, livelihoods, production, logistics, marriage, diplomacy, technology transmission, mobilization, and raids. It is changed based on each state''s institutions in Seoul: Nationwide and the authority of a single character, and does not adopt outside status systems, single religions, cultures, resource tables, or figures wholesale. Research sources are preserved in the internal ledger. For the confirmed direction and the pending inputs and reflection order of the four axes, follow [Campaign Progression and Crises](/gdd/rules/campaign-progression/).

- Campaign: a central 3D map, a top resource and status bar, a bottom context panel that changes with the selected target, and a right-side group of turn, diplomacy, and technology actions.
- Battle preparation: overlay deployable zones and boundary lines on the same 3D battlefield, and keep the bottom unit cards and the top battle-start controls.
- Real-time combat: keep the central battlefield as clear as possible, with a bottom unit card rail, selected unit info at the lower left, commands and the minimap at the lower right, and the force balance at the top center.

All layout ratios are measured against the 16:9 render frame of the Unity Game View, not the whole web page. Document titles, screen tabs, and source links may sit outside the game frame, but the coordinates and occupancy of the actual HUD, strategy map, and combat viewport are evaluated only within the 16:9 frame.

## Borrowing Matrix

| Criteria | Current References | What to Keep | Excluded |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Grand Strategy | CK3 | Characters, Houses, and Succession | Turn-Based Combat Titles |
| Combat | Norland | Individual-Resolution Combat | Turn-Based Combat Titles |

## Seven-Dimension Summary

The current comparison baselines are CK3 and Norland. Past recommendations from other titles are kept as research records, but turn-based combat grammar is not used as a current reference.

| Dimension | Best Model | Residual Recommendation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Current Baseline | CK3 | Characters, Houses, Succession, and Grand Strategy |
| Combat Baseline | Norland | Individually Resolved Combat and Command Without Pause |

## Three-Surface Structure

Core finding of the genre research: maps are distinguished not by size but by the player''s verb.

| Surface | Question | Form | Borrowed From |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **S Strategic Route Map** | Who rules where, and what should be prioritized? | All of Seoul → 16 state spheres of influence · 3D heightmap | CK3 information mode; display of territorial control for Seoul''s 16 states |
| **W Station-to-Station Travel** | Where will the party go, and at what cost? | Current station → destination station · routes, supplies, events | Norland''s travel and ongoing costs |
| N Non-Map Management Surface | Whom should we prepare, and what should we settle? | Intermission · encounters · deployment · settlement | CK3 events |

## Starting Presets

The kind of deprivation creates the difficulty — what differs is not a numeric adjustment but "what is missing." For detailed figures, see `Starting-Presets.md`.

| Preset | Difficulty | Core Deprivation | Long-Term Rules |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Wandering Trio | ★★ | Base · Supplies | None (Standard) |
| Former Autonomous Patrol | ★ | Relationships (treated as traitors) | Restoring the Sindorim Autonomous Council is the first gate |
| Merchant Survivors | ★★ | Combat Strength | Trade profit +20% / combatant cost +50% |
| Deserter Platoon | ★★★★ | Wide-Area Hostility | Pursuers spawn periodically |
| Orphan Band | ★★★★★ | Everything | Growth ×2 / morale damage ×2 / rewards −30% |
| The Last Stationmaster | ★★ | Station Responsibility | Partial control of Yeongdeungpo · exile upon loss (not game over) |

## What Was Intentionally Not Adopted

- CK3-style combat abstraction and feudal succession — because combat is not collapsed into a single dice roll. "Formations on a Grid" of 2026-09-07 was the contract at the time, and the 2026-09-19 goal is unit command.

## Capture and Recruitment

When you capture an enemy representative in battle, you choose among interrogation, handover, and hiring negotiation. Recruitment is split into three routes: rescue → trust → joining (core companions) / contract labor (paid, fixed-term) / the in-station labor market. No core companion can be bought outright with money — relationships are the gate.

## Open-Source Implementation Reference (2026-09-17)

The open-source survey is a record of past implementation approaches. The current reference games are limited to CK3 and Noland, and no open-source work that includes turn-based combat is adopted as a current reference.

| Game | Reference Points | Domain |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Current References | CK3 | Character-Driven Grand Strategy |
| Current References | Norland | Individual-Resolution Combat |

Mechanism-level citations are in the open-source section of [Borrowed Mechanisms Compendium §14](/gdd/references/ref-mechanism-index/), and the full original text is in the repository''s internal survey document.

## Detailed Encyclopedia (Wiki Pages)

The full mechanisms of each game can be read on the following wiki pages:

- [Crusader Kings 3](/gdd/references/ref-crusader-kings-3/) — character-driven grand strategy (succession, dynasties, war)
- Noland — individually resolved combat and settlement and relationship management');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Game-Thesis', 'design', 'game-thesis', 'Overview', '', 'accepted', 34, 'Game-Thesis.md', '# Overview

The current design version is the SP-1.0.0 single-player build. Actions, titles, war, bestowal, succession, asset/knowledge transfer, relationships, and permanent effects follow the [Single-Player Detailed Design](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). The earlier shared-world, host, and joiner design is no longer a premise for execution and does not mean implementation is complete.

The player is not a hero. They are a person standing in front of a station in Seoul in 2126. That spot is the orientation. Exploration, expansion, development, and conquest open outward from that spot. It is recorded in the world ledger. This is the 4X of this game. A loop of returning to a base after work is not the genre.

Drifting in as a nobody is only one rooting path. You may also start with a body that has bloodlines and affiliations. For the order, see [Online User Journey](/gdd/design/online-user-journey/).

We do not provide only an ending that gathers Seoul into a single nation. Line alliances, independent stations, Lectern Rule, trade networks, technological restoration. The legitimacy built through play becomes the order.

The RPG progresses through a character''s abilities, action proficiencies, traits, and relationships. The base abilities of leadership, strategy, negotiation, management, insight, learning, skill, and stamina range from 0–20, and 10 is the level of an experienced practitioner in that field. The final ability, reflecting traits, items, and current condition, ranges from 0–100 and never exceeds 100 under any effect. Action proficiencies such as repair, medicine, transport, escort, negotiation, and exploration are expressed separately from abilities, from 0–99.9. Traits are gained through events and actions, and traits affect performance, choices, stress, and relationships. Opening effects with a narrative basis may allow final abilities above 20. The specific formulas for resolution, growth, and effect composition will be designed separately, and previously issued values are not automatically converted.

In combat, you organize and instruct your units before engagement. Once engaged, each individual soldier''s attacks are resolved, and a squad in melee engagement does not receive new orders. You do not directly input combos for individual heroes. [Combat](/gdd/rules/personal-resolution-battle/). The body is resolved in six parts: left arm, right arm, left leg, right leg, head, and torso. Damage level and functional state are distinguished for each part, and disease progression is recorded separately. Combat and plague affect the same bodily state. Limited or lost function in a part affects the livelihoods and actions available, and prosthetics are fitted only after amputation.

Named heroes are separate from rank-and-file squads and are not counted in the troop total. Unit size varies with the commander''s personal stats, the level of the state, and the level of the settlement, and there is no universal cap. Input definitions and formulas are undecided. There is also no fixed cap on overall army size or the number of heroes; supply, overextension, and the number of hero commanders limit scale.

The wuxia system of martial arts, forms, and schools is not used. Organizations and institutions that depended on it are redesigned to fit governance, livelihoods, religion, the military, and community management. Strategy and leadership are an organization''s planning and command, while skill is judgment and execution in individual combat. Heroic powerhouses are allowed, but high personal combat power does not automatically grant command, troops, equipment, or intelligence. A character''s public profile explains their abilities and traits through their actual life, actions, and relationships.

The strategic map covers two layers within Seoul''s administrative boundary: the surface and the real subway. Directly acquiring or manipulating territory outside Seoul is excluded from the current goals. Details of movement and layer connections within Seoul are undecided. The combat camera is a 3D free command view with pan, orbit, and zoom, and no angle or FOV values are set. The top-down diagram is a blueprint for explaining space, not the actual in-game render. There is no pause, slow-down, or speed-up in combat. Target characters are rendered with natural, realistic proportions and restrained expressions, using flat colors, outlines, and hatching. The original bytes of existing donated assets are preserved.

Failure remains in the world. Injury, capture, death, supplies, grudges. Characters are the unit of politics. Rank, memory, and heirs change who holds the nation. A line is both land and supplies. Every change is tracked in the ledger.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Home', 'design', 'home', '《Seoul Metro Dynasties》', '', 'accepted', 35, 'Home.md', '# 《Seoul Metro Dynasties》

Character sheets, calculations, and rules and situations by domain are read in the [Character and Domain Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/). It is a design proposal that distinguishes the approved scope from alternatives not adopted.

The current design version is the SP-1.0.0 single-player build. Actions, titles, war, bestowal, succession, asset/knowledge transfer, relationships, and permanent effects follow the [Single-Player Detailed Design](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). The earlier shared-world, host, and joiner design is no longer a premise for execution and does not mean implementation is complete.

> **A 4X + RPG that links one character''s livelihood, relationships, rank, and succession to domain management and line politics in the subway network of a collapsed Seoul. Combat resolves each individual soldier.**

《Seoul Metro Dynasties》 is a character-driven grand strategy RPG that uses the subway network of **post-collapse Seoul** as the framework for territory, logistics, survival, and legitimacy. The collapse began with the Great Blackout of 2030, and that night is recorded in the prologue. The game is set in 2126 CE, a century later. The order the campaign depicts is a line society split by what people watch over and by faith. The player is a nobody. Being a nobody is only one rooting path. Follow the [Online User Journey](/gdd/design/online-user-journey/) for the full sequence. Starting with a small party, you move between stations and lines, building livelihood, relationships, rank, and influence within factions.

In combat, each individual soldier''s attack is resolved by weapon, armor, combat skill, physical condition, and mood. Before engagement, the player deploys squads and directs movement, attack targets, fire, and ambushes, but gives no new orders to squads in melee engagement. Retreat is a battle-wide command. Characters aim for natural, realistic proportions, restrained expressions, and low-saturation flat colors, outlines, and hatching. Livelihood, subsistence, production, logistics, marriage, diplomacy, technology transfer, and mobilization are within the design scope of being linked through the same characters and world time, and are not yet implemented.

The strategy screen is a 3D heightmap world map (Decision 10). The combat camera is a 3D free command view with pan, orbit, and zoom, confirmed by the owner. No angle or FOV values are set. There is no pause, slow-down, or speed-up in combat, and the campaign world and other parties continue to flow regardless of the battle. Orders are previewed on squads before engagement and then confirmed, and squads in melee engagement receive no new orders. Retreat is a battle-wide command. There is no command queue. The original bytes of the Odland assets are preserved.

## Document Guide

Follows the table of contents of Namu Wiki''s [New Series/Setting](https://namu.wiki/w/%EC%83%88%20%EC%8B%9C%EB%A6%AC%EC%A6%88/%EC%84%A4%EC%A0%95). Implementation and Unity documents are placed at the very bottom.

