The Seoul Regional Compendium is authored data and a viewing screen for 427 date-fixed administrative dongs. It does not mean that Unity station interior terrain or the 16-state campaign has been implemented.

Generated art slots are not yet connected to the runtime screens. Approving and connecting art slots is a separate acceptance condition, and code verification alone does not complete the whole module. The 16-state campaign simulation, the aggregated casualty display, and items 13–17 of `ToDo.md` are not yet complete. A design written in a document does not mean it has been implemented.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Issue-101-Ui-Ux-Decisions', 'design', 'issue-101-ui-ux-decisions', '#101 UI/UX Screen Contract — Confirmed Decisions 1–9', '', 'accepted', 36, 'Issue-101-Ui-Ux-Decisions.md', '# #101 UI/UX Screen Contract — Confirmed Decisions 1–9

- Status: #101 decisions 1–9 confirmed. Later changes to combat objectives are marked in the notes below
- Recorded: 2026-09-16, supplemented 2026-09-17; combat objective change note 2026-09-19
- Basis issue: #101
- Scope: screen structure, information presentation, and future Unity element names of the HTML mockup
- Out of scope: Unity implementation, server/network, numeric formulas, data storage



## Decision 1 — Separate the Screens to Be Judged Independently



| Order | Top-Level Screens | Question the Screen Must Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Select Starting Character | Which character in the world do I start as? |
| 2 | Starting Presets | What deprivations and long-term rules am I accepting? |
| 3 | Recruit World Characters | Whom can I meet and invite to join me? |
| 4 | Base Hub | Who can I meet and what can I do at the current base? |
| 5 | Character Dialogue | How do my relationship with the other person and the results of my choices change? |
| 6 | Select Sortie Members | Who from the roster is joining this expedition? |
| 7 | Strategic Map | Where can I head in the world I know? |
| 8 | Station-to-Station Travel | What route, cost, and risk am I accepting to reach the destination? |
| 9 | Encounter | How do I respond to the situation I have run into? |
| 10 | Pre-Battle Formation Editing | Where and how do I deploy the participating members? |
| 11 | Combat | How do I command real-time engagements through unit orders? |
| 12 | Settlement | What did my choices and the battle leave on the characters and the world? |



## Decision 2 — Status and place persist; situations open only when context arises

- **Status**: Continuously shows the player character''s current status even in normal times.
- **Place**: Continuously shows the current location even in normal times.
- **Situation**: Shown additionally only when an event is foreshadowed or actually confronted.
- 



## Decision 3 — Facts are earned through activity; predictions are widened through ability

The strategic map and base information are not an omniscient world map. The map POV follows the selected main body''s location and known information. Faction political territory is the default, while the subway/railway layer is optional and off by default. The current settlement and participating-battle entry share a single context-dependent button. In normal times it opens the main body''s actual current settlement; when the main body is engaged, it opens the participating battle; and it cannot be used while the main body is traveling. Remote battles are visible only on the strategic map and do not change this button. The settlement and participating-battle viewports are mutually exclusive. Approval of the UI layout is not approval of incorrect location, authority, or participation actions.

- Only stations and lines that the player has learned of directly are displayed as exact facts.
- No exact station, line, or faction information is provided for undiscovered areas.
- Information gathered through direct activity is promoted to confirmed fact.
- Indirect information such as hearsay is displayed as rumor, distinct from fact.
- The range and accuracy of what can be predicted vary with the player''s level and related stats.
- Predictions show their range, likelihood, and confidence together so that they do not look like confirmed facts.



## Decision 4 — Separate relationship trust from information reliability





- Facts witnessed together and operational information needed for a joint mission are shared with the party.
- 
- 
- The screen expresses these two judgments with separate positions and markers.



## Decision 5 — Project the shared world ledger according to what each character knows



- Hub bulletin board: News relevant to the current hub and nearby known routes.
- Strategy Map: Change markers on the display layers showing known factions'' political territories, diplomacy, fiefs, titles, offices, battle situations, and selections.
- Character Records: Events delivered to the player that are related to the character in question.
- A world record opened when needed: the source, timing, and verification status of events I have learned about.



## Decision 6 — Conflicts between Attachment and Presets are announced before confirmation



## Decision 7 — Hide who controls a character, but still allow recruiting already-claimed characters







## Decision 8 — Death and Succession Are Included as Four Auxiliary Mockups



1. Designated heir in life: distinguish whether the designated person is currently valid from the expected title, assets, and disputes.
2. 
3. 
4. 





## Decision 9 — Stable Element Names Shared by the Mockups and Unity





## Remaining #101 Decisions



## To Be Handed to Follow-Up Implementation Issues

The following items will be handled in separate issues that turn the screen contracts into implementable specifications.

- Storing station and line discovery status and information sources
- Information staleness and refresh rules
- Calculating prediction range, accuracy, and confidence per stat
- Information sharing between parties and other players, and server synchronization
- Accepting, declining, and offline handling of recruitment offers, and shared party permissions
- Implementing the finalized HTML mockups in Unity uGUI and TMP');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Korean-Terminology-and-Naming', 'design', 'korean-terminology-and-naming', 'Korean Orthography and Naming Rules', '', 'accepted', 37, 'Korean-Terminology-and-Naming.md', '# Korean Orthography and Naming Rules

This document applies to the names of nations, organizations, families, offices, characters, training, technologies, equipment, facilities, events, data fields, and UI in 《Seoul: Nationwide》. It neither avoids Sino-Korean words nor converts every word into Sino-Korean. It prefers Korean that readers can understand at once, and uses familiar Sino-Korean words to convey formality, expertise, or historicity.

## Basis and Scope of Application

The general provisions of the National Institute of Korean Language''s 「Hangul Orthography」 set the principle that standard words are written as they sound while conforming to grammar. The 「Foreign Word Transcription Rules」 write loanwords with the Hangul letters currently in use and prescribe basic principles such as final consonants and tensed sounds. These norms are the basis for orthography; they do not create the setting''s own proper names for us.

This document applies to the following scope.

- Names of nations, characters, organizations, offices, schools, technologies, and objects in LORE
- Display names of rules, commands, data fields, and setting values in the GDD
- Titles, tables, filters, and help text shown in `/wiki` and `/gdd`
- Korean descriptions and user-facing display names in CSV, JSON, and database designs

## Name Classification and Ownership Ledger

| Name Type | Semantic Ownership | Basic Rules | Separate Verification |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Nations and Political Organizations | LORE/factions | The name must convey the regime, authority, and central station | Misidentification as a successor to a real institution; trademarks; reputation |
| Private Organizations, Families, and Merchant Houses | LORE/factions·LORE/characters | Keep real place names, clan seats of origin, and historical facts, and give invented activity names a distinctive form | Trademark and real-organization confusion |
| Offices and Positions | LORE/offices | Separate the official names of each modern institution from the common tiers | Ban the discarded Joseon-office translation layer |
| Character | LORE/characters·name-pools | Prioritize user-locked names, clan seats of origin, and generational name syllables | Identity with real persons; consent; commercial use |
| Training and Behavioral Proficiency | LORE/culture | Use names that convey the actual action and expertise, and distinguish them from competencies, offices, and equipment | Do not restore the martial-arts system; do not automatically grant proficiency from occupations |
| Technologies, Machines, and Facilities | LORE/technology·GDD | Mix common Sino-Korean words and loanwords to suit the function | Product names, trademarks, and abbreviations of original terms |
| Weapons, Equipment, and Goods | LORE/goods and the GDD data dictionary | Real generic names and model names belong to the factual layer; invented product names belong to the rights ledger | Trademarks, designs, and false performance claims |
| Place Names, Facilities, and Stations | LORE/places | Use real Seoul place names and station names as they are | Date of abolition or renaming, and sources |
| Events and Eras | LORE/chronology | Reflect the cause, place, and result of the event in the name | Misidentification with real events; basis for dates |
| Rules, Commands, and States | GDD/rules | Manage Korean display names paired with stable machine IDs | Do not expose English IDs on user-facing surfaces |
| UI Menus and Filters | GDD/UI·WEB | Short Korean action names; reuse canonical terms | No duplicate translations for the same concept |
| Data fields and CSV columns | GDD Data Dictionary | English machine ID + Korean display name + data type + unit + source | Key changes, migrations, and versions |

Record each new name in the name ledger with `name_id`, `kind`, `formal_name_ko`, `field_alias_ko`, `machine_id`, `owner_path`, `source_kind`, `rights_status`, `rights_checked_on`, `replaces`, and `status`. For names whose ledger entries are not yet implemented, keep the same entry in the canonical document table.

## Vocabulary Layer

| Layer | Usage | Examples | Principle |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Native Korean words | Everyday, field, and oral nicknames | Gatehand, Bamboo-Pole Hand, Lens Breath | Keep them short and easy to say |
| Sino-Korean words | Institutions, formal usage, specialized concepts, and official names | Floodgate Breathing Method, Control Authority, Reconnaissance, Settlement | Combine familiar words whose meaning is clear in Korean |
| Loanwords | International technology and modern objects that are hard to replace | Physical AI, Lens, Sensor | Write them in Hangul according to the National Institute of Korean Language''s loanword orthography |
| Machine identifiers | Code and data exchange | `hero-person-0001`, `movement_budget` | Do not expose them as-is in user-facing text; provide a Korean display name and a mapping table |

Layers can be mixed even within a single sentence. Use the common name of equipment, such as the lens, and action names, such as aiming and reconnaissance, according to their function. Do not turn an equipment name into the name of a new ability or a martial arts technique.

## Sino-Korean words

- Sino-Korean words are written in Hangul. The reader should be able to guess the meaning, as with `gameungjojunbeop` (sympathetic-response aiming method), `howicheolbyeokjin` (guard iron-wall formation), and `cheokhu` (scout).
- Hanja may be added in parentheses only at first appearance, where distinguishing homonyms or explaining etymology is necessary. Do not repeat Hanja in every heading and table.
- Prefer words that appear in dictionaries or are in common use in specialized Korean fields. Even if words are joined together, do not use the result if its full meaning cannot be explained in one sentence.
- Words such as `singong`, `jeolmaek`, and `cheongwang`, which imply transcendence more than an actual effect, are used only when the corresponding setting exists. Do not exaggerate abilities for the sake of atmosphere.
- Do not simply convert Chinese or Japanese proper names of schools and martial arts into Sino-Korean readings and use them as original creations.

## Loanwords

- For loanwords, use the Hangul spelling by default and do not repeat the original-language spelling in body text. Machine identifiers and source names are exceptions.
- Project notation follows the Hangul spellings that are already locked, such as `pijikeol eiai` (Physical AI), `hyumeonoideu` (humanoid), `atteullaseu` (Atlas), `senseo` (sensor), `renjeu` (lens), `modyul` (module), and `peurotokol` (protocol).
- Names of works, companies, and products are used only in the research layer that states factual sources. Do not revive them as names of organizations, schools, or products created for the year 2126.
- When an abbreviation of the original language is hard to read as is, give it only in the data description after the official Hangul name. Do not leave a bare abbreviation in the UI.

## Organizations, Titles, and Schools

- Factual names that raise no legal issues and are not protected as trademarks, such as historical figures, place names, events, martial arts, and generic names, are used as they are for realism. Do not apply automatic substitution just to avoid real names.
- Do not conclude that a name is safe merely because a trademark search found no results. For proper names intended for commercial release, record the target countries, goods/services classes, rights status, search date, and evidence URLs in the rights ledger.
- Review copyright separately from the name itself, looking at creative expression, descriptions, sentences, character composition, and distinctive technical depictions within a work. Do not change historical facts or generic martial arts names because of copyright.
- Real organization names may be used as they are in the factual layer. However, any use that makes a currently existing organization the perpetrator of fictional crimes, massacres, or conspiracies, or that could be mistaken for an official successor, must go through a separate review of rights, reputation, and confusion.
- Fictional organizations should have names that reveal what they do and what authority they hold. Do not use names that read like criminal successors of real institutions.
- Titles distinguish the modern institution-specific designations of the 16 nations from the common tiers. Do not use Joseon-era official titles as an intermediate translation layer.
- Name training and proficiency after the actions actually performed. Do not use organization names or titles as personal abilities, and do not create names or promotion systems for martial arts, techniques, or schools.
- Keep the display names for competence, proficiency, traits, and body-part status distinct in meaning. Do not group individual combat skill, unit deployment, production work, and administrative authority under the same name or suffix system.

## GDD and Data Notation

- The `/gdd` tables and the data dictionary list the Korean display name, stable machine ID, data type, unit, allowed range, default value, source, and confirmation status separately.
- The GDD owns design terms such as hero classes, campaign roles, and movement stances. The wiki owns characters'' life stories and in-world nicknames.
- If a value has not been decided, write `mijeong` (undecided) and the party who decides it. Do not fill in common game values as arbitrary defaults.
- English keys in CSV and JSON are an exchange contract. Provide Korean display names in the wiki, GDD body text, and UI.

## Rights Status

| Status | Meaning | Usage Rules |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fact-public` | Historical facts, place names, generic names, and public-domain names | Record the source and date, and use as is |
| `generic-unregistered` | Generic names, or names for which no protected trademark was found within the search scope | Record the search scope, country, goods class, and search date, then use |
| `fiction-original` | Project-original names | Use after conflict search and semantic review |
| `licensed` | Names with confirmed permission or license | Record the scope of permission and obligations together |
| `review-required` | Possible conflict with trademarks, real people, real organizations, or proper names from other works | Review before public release or launch; never describe an unreviewed state as safe |
| `retired` | Deprecated and Replaced Names | Not used in new body text or UI; only the replacement ID is kept |

## Review Questions

Before adding a new term, ask the following.

1. Is this the right layer (native Korean, Sino-Korean, or loanword) for how this concept is used?
2. Do the official name and its alias avoid explaining the same function twice in different words?
3. Can the full meaning of the Sino-Korean term be explained in one natural Korean sentence?
4. Does the loanword spelling conform to the project''s existing locks and the National Institute of Korean Language norms?
5. Has no proper name of a real organization, work, or product been carried over as a name in the creative work?
6. Have historical names, generic names, and unregistered names not been changed unnecessarily without a legal basis?
7. If it is a creative proper name for commercial release, have the trademark''s target country, goods class, search date, and basis been recorded?
8. Are no machine IDs or internal English tags exposed on user-facing surfaces?
9. Which document, LORE or GDD, owns the meaning of this term?

## Sources

- National Institute of Korean Language, 「Hangeul Spelling Rules」, language norms, accessed 2026-09-20: https://korean.go.kr/kornorms/regltn/regltnView.do#a
- National Institute of Korean Language, 「Loanword Transcription Rules」, language norms, accessed 2026-09-20: https://korean.go.kr/kornorms/regltn/regltnView.do
- Namuwiki''s `mugong/mongnok` (martial arts/list) was used only as a reference for comparing the naming conventions of `geombeop·gwonbeop·bobeop·jinbeop·simbeop·gong` (sword method, fist method, footwork, formation, mind method, and skill) that Korean web wuxia readers recognize. Names, descriptions, and techniques from individual works are not taken. Accessed 2026-09-20: https://namu.wiki/w/%EB%AC%B4%EA%B3%B5/%EB%AA%A9%EB%A1%9D');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Online-User-Journey', 'design', 'online-user-journey', 'Online User Journey', '', 'accepted', 38, 'Online-User-Journey.md', '# Online User Journey

The current product plan gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 single-player contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupation, and online proxy clauses of this document are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the replacement scope of [Single-Player Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are converted automatically.



## Premise

1. Active play takes place in **a world hosted by a headless authoritative server**. The Unity client connects over localhost to a bundled headless server child process, or to an optionally installed dedicated server of the same server format. Events caused by users and NPCs are recorded in that world''s single ledger, and each participant is left with signed checkpoint receipts and projection metadata that can verify the current world and ledger position and preserve their own archive and bindings. Only when the checkpoint''s immutable fork policy allows it are complete host-capable world snapshots distributed or exported; participants who are barred from forking are not given state complete enough to seed a server. After death without a valid designated successor, a new incarnation begins in **a new world**. The product session, hosting, and checkpoint contracts are defined by ADR-008.
2. The player is not a state or a faction. The player is **a single living figure of the world**. They may be a member of some group, or someone''s son or daughter.
3. Incarnation has two paths: **Random** and **Custom**. Both enter the same world.
4. [Expedition](/gdd/rules/campaign-loop/) is the session loop **after** entry. It does not replace account, incarnation, or shared entry.
5. An **account** is a key pointing to Steam entitlement and signed-client evidence, plus the world participant public key and recovery secret. A **session** is a connection credential and is volatile. Occupancy of a **figure (`personId`)** and the world–figure binding are kept in checkpoints. The **archive** is the play and death record kept locally by the participant, and the host world holds the original.

Accounts, incarnation, lineage checkpoints, and the headless authoritative server do not exist yet.

## Six Stages

| # | Stage | What the Player Does | Related Documents | Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | Account | Enters with Steam entitlement, a signed client, and the world participant public key and recovery secret. An account is not a figure | ADR-008 | Steam AppID and payment protocol are a **GAP**. Without a signed client, local single-player only |
| 1 | Incarnation | After the title and main menu, select a starting figure available in the product from a map-based figure list, and begin from that figure''s POV | Starting Presets, Figure Card Contract, Figure Compendium, Succession, Name Roster, World Ledger | Occupancy contract finalized. One living participant per personId. Occupancy does not expire along with the session |
| 2 | Entry | Appear as that figure in a later-era Seoul that is already running | Sixteen States of Seoul, Chronology | Campaign time flows only while at least one player is open. On reconnection, you see the world of the checkpoint verified by the recovery secret |
| 3 | Loop | Base → Preparation → Expedition → Encounter → Battle → Settlement | [Expedition](/gdd/rules/campaign-loop/), [How UI Gets Into Code](/gdd/design/ui-implementation-pipeline/) | Other users'' parties at the same station are visible. Battle is a closed session for the party. There is no automatic return after settlement |
| 4 | Ledger | User commands and NPC decisions accumulate in the same event ledger and change the world state | [Does the Same Choice Produce the Same Result](/gdd/rules/save-and-determinism/), Ambitions, [From Strategy to Battle](/gdd/rules/strategy-battle-roundtrip/) | The headless server''s authoritative ledger is canonical. Participants receive signed receipts and projection metadata along with their own archive and figure records, and only fork-permitting checkpoints distribute a complete host snapshot |
| 5 | Death, Succession, and New Start | The dead figure remains in the existing world. If a valid designated successor exists at the moment of death, play continues as that figure in the same world; if not, that play is game over | Succession, Name Roster, World Ledger, Dynasty and Vassal Politics | The local archive is kept. The host world holds the original. After game over, a new incarnation begins in a new world |

## 0. Account

An account is a key. It is neither the body you control nor a name. It is not the same as a session credential.

- In active play, an account controls only one living personId in that world.
- When that figure dies, the account remains and moves on to stage 5.
- After game over, the new incarnation is a new figure in a new world, not a figure in the existing world. The previous figure''s information and assets are not carried over.
- The root of the product account is the world participant public key and recovery secret. SteamID is optional evidence attached to the Steam session. LAN play without Steam works through direct transport.
- If the signing client and local DRM attestation are not verified, only local saves and single-player can be read. The client cannot open or join multiplayer, or sign checkpoints.
- Session credentials disappear when the connection ends. Occupancy of a `personId` does not expire when a session ends or goes idle. It is released only when the participant explicitly gives it up. A host kick blocks rejoining, but it does not release the occupancy or erase the character''s record.
- The rejoin identity is the world participant''s recovery secret. If the secret is lost, the host can reissue it after confirming the person outside the game, and the old secret is revoked. There is no automatic in-game identity proof.
- This document does not state the partner values for the Steam AppID, packages, or payment protocol. **GAP.**

The current canon has no account screen. The first slot of the board in [How UI Enters Code](/gdd/design/ui-implementation-pipeline/) is the title screen.

## 1. Incarnation

The player does not bring in a blank commander from outside. The player becomes **one person** who already exists in the Seoul of the later age.

In the current product, the only character open in the starting selection is Sin Jongmok. Settling the selection flow in the list does not mean the whole character ledger is unlocked for play. Neither the monarch nor Han Jaemok is fixed as a default body, and the broader POC sample is not carried over into product unlocks. New character creation is on hold. The random and custom incarnations and the presets below are preserved as contracts for later journeys and are not features offered on the current selection screen. The approved Sin Jongmok starting scenario is maintained, and the other characters and place arrangements of the experimental POC are not moved into the opening position. Detailed placement and additional unlock conditions are not newly set in this document.

A named personId is one living account. No exception is made for rank. The Character Compendium, the cast of characters, and kin created during incarnation all fall under this.

If two accounts choose the same personId, the second occupancy request is rejected. An unlocked character is not assigned as a substitute, and random reselection does not widen product eligibility.

### Random

The world picks one seat that is currently empty.

- Candidates: a named character who is not occupied, or a lower seat that is vacant or created by succession, the name roster, or the world ledger.
- A personId that is already occupied is removed from the draw.
- The seat already has its affiliation, rank, parent or guardian, and livelihood. The player receives that history.
- The random additional roster is an NPC candidate experiment. It is not an incarnation register.

### Custom

The player writes the background directly. The fields follow the character card contract.

| Field | What it does in incarnation |
| --- | --- |
| Full name, gender, appearance | The common creation step of the existing presets. This alone does not complete incarnation. |
| Community of origin | Birthplace, pre-collapse residence, nationality and residency status |
| Affiliation | The Sixteen States, remnant military, migrant corridors, unaffiliated |
| Parent or guardian | Kinship, adoption, or apprenticeship with a living world character. "None" is also a value, and only then does the character become a nameless drifter. |
| Livelihood and rank | The seven livelihoods, and the work actually signed in that ledger |
| Conscription history | Same as the card contract. Military service is not stamped by a hash. |

Custom does not overwrite a personId that is already occupied. An overwrite request is rejected.

The parent is either a living world character or "None." If "None," the character is a nameless branch.

A player can choose another user''s personId as a parent. The child is a new personId. Two accounts do not occupy the parent seat together.

If the character chosen as parent is alive, that relationship is created in the ledger as a single line.

### Scarcity Presets

The six starting presets are **difficulties (what is missing)**. They do not replace identity.

Order: incarnation (who you are) → preset (what you start with). Example: after incarnating as the son of a Guro warlord, you choose the "Deserter Platoon" scarcity. The "commander" in the preset table is a role within the party, not a blank body made outside the world.

If the bloodline or affiliation from incarnation conflicts with a starting preset''s long-term rules, incarnation wins. The conflict is recorded in the ledger as a single line. This document does not modify the preset table.

The current sentence that creates only name, gender, and appearance becomes part of this stage.

A starting preset is a long-term rule that persists throughout the campaign, not a bonus that disappears right after the start.

Starting companions are placed randomly from the world''s Character Compendium under each preset''s conditions.

The preset''s starting headcount of 3 to 6 is the size of the campaign party, not the size of a soldier squad or an army corps.

## 2. Entry

When incarnation ends, that character appears in the later-age Seoul that is **already in progress**.

- A world has one campaign. The headless server''s world is that world, and on reconnection, once the participant binding is confirmed with the recovery secret, the player is shown the current world from the verified checkpoint.
- World time flows only while at least one player is connected. Its units are the days and weeks of the [Expedition](/gdd/rules/campaign-loop/). When the headcount reaches 0, the server writes and publishes a verified checkpoint and then stops the world and NPC simulation.
- Routes, strongholds, deaths, and treaties changed by other users are visible.
- An offline occupied character remains in the world as a daily-life and political NPC. It cannot start an expedition or make decisions reserved for players. It can be wounded or killed. Rank signatures and official documents are handled only through the ambition-driven NPC politics.
- Ticks are run by the headless authoritative server (ADR-008). Steam and cron do not run the simulation on its behalf. The cloud does not keep world time running continuously.

## 3. Loop

After entry, the [Expedition](/gdd/rules/campaign-loop/) has six stages.

1. Things to do at the stronghold
2. Expedition preparation
3. Strategic-layer expedition
4. Encounter
5. Tactical combat
6. Applying results

The screen board keeps [How UI Enters Code](/gdd/design/ui-implementation-pipeline/). This document adds no slots to that board. Account, incarnation, and entry are stages attached **before** the board. Stage 5 combat follows individual resolution, pre-engagement orders, melee lock, and full retreat. No card decks or direct hero control are included.

If another user''s party is at the same station or on the same line, that party is visible.

Combat is a closed session for each party. Use [Strategy-to-Battle Roundtrip](/gdd/rules/strategy-battle-roundtrip/). There is no shared battlefield for formations. Combat has no pause. The campaign world and other parties keep running regardless of combat. Do not write that controls on the combat screen stop other parties'' campaign time.

Settlement is in ascending world-time order. If world times are equal, it is in ascending order of the cause ID string.

If two ResultIds modify the same stronghold or line cell, only the earlier one is applied. The later one receives a rejection receipt.

Submitting the same ResultId again returns the receipt that already exists.

## 4. Ledger

What users do and what NPCs do accumulate in the **same ledger**, even when they are of different kinds.

- The authoritative ledger lives on the headless server (ADR-008). The verified checkpoint is the world canon. Participants keep signed receipts, projection metadata, and their own archives and character records, and receive a full host snapshot only at fork-permitting checkpoints.
- Seed replay is done only inside closed combat. It can also be used in closed encounters.
- One line: cause ID, world time, actor, target, magnitude, rules version. This is the same as the micro-records of succession, the name roster, and the world ledger.
- The actor field is `user` or `npc`.
- When the threshold is exceeded, the world event from [Does the Same Choice Give the Same Result](/gdd/rules/save-and-determinism/) is issued once.
- Whether the actor is a user character or an NPC, there is only one path for changing strongholds, prices, passage, and legitimacy.

Local save files and same-seed replay are the single-player campaign contract. The canon of a multiplayer world is the headless server''s ledger, and hosting begins only from a fully verified atomic checkpoint. Hosting a received checkpoint is always a permanent fork into a new lineage. The detailed schema is defined by [Does the Same Choice Give the Same Result](/gdd/rules/save-and-determinism/).

The current module treats file saving itself as a non-goal. The ledger in this chapter is a product premise.

## 5. Death, Succession, Re-entry



- A dead character remains in the ledger as dead. The corpse, relationships, debts, and grudges remain in the world. Previous characters are not erased.
- A play heir can be designated while alive. Status or membership in a particular state or region is not a prerequisite for designating a play heir. The candidate must be a living character other than oneself, and a character already controlled by another account cannot be designated. Whether a character can be recruited is separate from the transfer of play control.
- The pre-designated heir status is guidance as of the current moment, for warnings and a chance to redesignate. Final validity is determined at the **moment of death**. If the designated character has died or is controlled by another account, they are not treated as a valid designated heir. One personId is never bound to two accounts at the same time.
- If there is a valid designated heir, the account''s control binding is moved to `heirPersonId` and play continues in the **same world**. The heir starts with their own knowledge, abilities, and relationships. They know only what was actually passed on from the deceased, and it is not copied automatically.
- If there is no designated heir, or the heir is not valid at the moment of death, it is **game over**. A new incarnation afterward is a separate play in a **new world** and inherits none of the deceased''s information, assets, or relationships. The new world starts from a lineage opened by a local child server or a dedicated server. Hosting a received checkpoint creates a new lineage, not the parent lineage (ADR-008).
- Succession of play control differs from succession of office, rank, and assets. Rank follows the per-title order of dynasty and vassal politics, and local custom, doctrine, confirmation, and disputes. If the deceased held no rank, there is no rank succession ruling either. Personal belongings, office and organizational property, and contractual rights are distinguished, and the exact distribution formula will be set in later system design.
- If a party leader dies, party succession follows the existing rules. Party leader succession is not the same as account binding.
- At the moment of death, the succession rule already set is evaluated immediately. The same applies when an offline-occupied character dies. If there is a valid heir, the binding is moved; if not, a game over is recorded. An offline heir is an everyday and political NPC until reconnecting.
- Each participant keeps a local archive of the records of characters they played or who died. The host world holds the original records. If the local archive is lost, it can be rebuilt from records signed by the host after the recovery secret and a human check. Viewing the archive does not count as the new character gaining in-game knowledge.

Another user can be a parent. Even if the parent dies, the child character remains in the world.

## Screen Order

The opening part the player actually sees. It is an implementation target, not the current board.

1. Account (Steam permissions, signed client, participant recovery secret. AppID and payment are **GAP**)
2. Entering the current play world. The session creation, joining, and checkpoint contract is ADR-008; the screen UX is a GAP for later online design
3. Incarnation: Random / Custom
4. Scarcity Presets
5. After the result is applied, the expedition can continue, and return is one of the five endings

The 12 screens of Decision 6 in `Intent.md` are UI/UX mockup targets and correspond to steps 3–5 of this order. The four death and succession contexts are separate supplementary mockups. Account, incarnation, and shared entry are steps 0–2 of this document.

## What This Does Not Do

- Does not erase the six stages of the [Expedition](/gdd/rules/campaign-loop/) or the six starting presets.
- This document does not define the Unity netcode implementation or the saved-byte schema. The product session contract is set by ADR-008, and the checkpoint rules by [Does the Same Choice Give the Same Result](/gdd/rules/save-and-determinism/).
- The online journey is a separate product contract.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Open-Decisions', 'design', 'open-decisions', 'Open Decisions List', '', 'accepted', 39, 'Open-Decisions.md', '# Open Decisions List

The current product plan gives priority to the detailed SP-1.0.0 single-player contract. It uses one player, one main body, NPCs, and a single campaign save, and the shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, occupation, and online proxy clauses of this document are preserved as history and are not used as current execution premises. The contracts for actual movement, on-site participation, authority, single settlement, and save errors are retained. In case of conflict, follow the replacement scope of [Single-Player Detailed Design SP-1.0.0](/gdd/system-design/singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0/). This does not mean implementation is complete or that existing saves are converted automatically.

Baseline commit: `origin/main` `e1895238` (WEB submodule split after PR #213 merge `93854514`). This page creates no new design. It regroups into four columns only the items already written in the canon that are unlocked or not yet migrated.

Session pointer `01a0bd33-a52f-7896-a7b5-eb87a89237dd` is not in this device''s Codex/OMO transcripts. The sources below are the `origin/main` files and the interview ledger.

This is not an implementation claim. Unity element names, bindings, and data identifiers are not created here. Where there is no value, leave it as `TBD` and do not fill in common game numbers. The rules are the same as the [Data Catalog](/gdd/design/data-catalog-and-schema/) and [Korean Terminology](/gdd/design/korean-terminology-and-naming/).

## Column Division

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Owner Unconfirmed | The question is open, or only a default proposal exists because of a timeout. It is not a value the owner chose directly |
| Design Proposal | A rules document proposed only the structure, and user confirmation and data dictionary registration have not yet happened |
| Canon Not Migrated | Confirmed in the interview but not yet present at this `origin/main` tip |
| Numbers Prohibited | Items already written as not to be locked. This list does not create numbers |

The campaign POV is tied to the actual location and known information of the primary character selected from the map list. The current product''s starting unlock is Sinjongmok only, and new creation is on hold. The POV diagram and viewer from the earlier PR #213 were discarded in the latest GDD cleanup and are neither a current contract nor a restoration target. Class names and temporary squad leader authority were closed by the owner''s decision of 2026-09-24. The unit roster''s independent skill inputs, the state/settlement level and formulas, and the effects, costs, and rates of livelihood, marriage, and technology are undecided. The current map and play scope is within the Seoul administrative boundary, and direct acquisition or manipulation of territory outside Seoul is not a current goal. Details of Seoul''s internal terrain, movement, and floor connections are undecided. The earlier Chungnam-region POC review included Daejeon, and the Daejeon and Sejong administrative IDs remain in the research sources of that time, but these are not to be used as the current WIKI map or info detail or as the product''s opening location.

## Owner Unconfirmed

No items remain in this column. OD-CAM (combat camera) and OD-PAUSE (combat pause) were closed by the owner''s decision of 2026-09-24 and moved to the closed adjacent items below.

The default-proposal sentences repeated in [Home](/gdd/design/home/), [Design Requirements](/gdd/design/design-requirements/), [What This Game Is](/gdd/design/game-thesis/), and [Development Roadmap](/gdd/design/development-roadmap/) were also corrected to match this decision. The same sentence in the parent repository''s ToDo.md must be corrected separately.

## Design Proposal

The source is the `Design Proposal` row of the decision status table in [Hero Squads and Time-Based Strategic Movement](/gdd/rules/hero-squads-and-time-based-movement/). Classes are not automatically assigned to wiki character descriptions.

| ID | Item | Current State |
| --- | --- | --- |
| OD-MOVE | Movement speed, stance, and control values | Squad pieces move toward their target point as time passes (Decision 14). That document does not lock the movement speed values |

## Canon Not Migrated

This distinguishes the contracts the owner chose from the items whose details are not yet set. ADR-008 is the approved canon in canon/locales/ko-KR/adr-008.json. Do not reopen the session contract as unmigrated or undecided.

| ID | Bundle | Status | Next Surfaces |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| OD-SESS | Local-First Steam Sessions and World Lifecycle | ADR-008 now holds the host session, recovery key, lineage, branching policy, LAN, multiplayer eligibility, and last-participant-leaves checkpoint. Implementation completion is a separate matter. | [Local-First Sessions and Lineage Checkpoints](/gdd/decisions/adr-008/) |
| OD-PRESET | Epiphyte Start Preset Reinterpretation | The interview confirmed 17 items, including keeping identity, reinterpreting only scarcity and pressure, excluding possessed companions, re-consent for expeditions, and sealed transport of disputed assets. The start presets are still the old design proposals and numeric targets. | Canonical-source migration PR. This page does not change the preset numbers. |
| OD-POV | Campaign POV and Authority UX | The six interview items entered the blueprint through PR #213. What remains is the movement speed proposal (OD-MOVE) in the hero squad document, not an open question about the POV itself. | Campaign POV and Authority |

## Numbers Prohibited

Below are values that the canonical source has locked against creating. Do not guess default values.

| ID | Item | Lock Statement | Canonical Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| OD-SQUADMIN | Minimum Squad Size | This Document Does Not Decide | [Personal Resolution Battle](/gdd/rules/personal-resolution-battle/) |
| OD-FOV | Camera Angle and Field of View | Not Newly Locked | Intent.md, Design.md |
| OD-CARDNUM | Morale Threshold | Decision 11 annotated that the open values of Decision 3 must not be created under the new goal. | Intent.md Decision 3 Superseding Note |
| OD-CAPTURE | New Capture Probability and Capacity | Not Created by Decision 11 | Intent.md Decision 11 |

The online session cap of 10 is the number of concurrent participating players. It is not a cap on heroes, squads, or factions.

## Closed Adjacent Items

- The product-goal combat is personal resolution. It does not include direct hero control and follows pre-engagement orders, melee lock, and whole-battle retreat.
- The player is not a nation but the one living person they currently control. The campaign viewpoint is bound to that person''s location and known information (PR #213).
- The hero commander of a moving squad, a single body location, and a shared world of up to 10 players are retained. Movement is time-based. Unit capacity varies with the commander''s personal stats, the level of the state, and the level of the settlement, and there is no common cap (Decision 15). The formula is undecided.
- No local QA server will be built. Review follows the remote build.
- The combat camera is free 3D command (pan, orbit, zoom). Combat has no pause, no slow-down or speed-up, and no command queue. Angle and field-of-view values are not locked (OD-CAM, OD-PAUSE, owner decision of 2026-09-24).
- A hero has one primary combat class and a secondary role tag. The class names are Guardian, Breaker, Staff Officer, Relief, Scout, and Correspondent. Abilities and values are not decided (OD-CLASS, owner decision of 2026-09-24).
- A temporary squad leader issues the same orders as a formal hero commander, but the command effect is weaker. The effect values are undecided (OD-TEMPLEAD, owner decision of 2026-09-24).
- The simultaneous-turn time limit (OD-TURNTIME, 2026-09-24) was discarded when Decision 14 (2026-09-28) eliminated turns. There is no pause in shared-world campaign time.
- There are five strategic stances: Normal March, Forced March, Ambush, Garrison, and Encamp. Recovery and supply values are not decided (OD-STANCE, owner decision of 2026-09-24).
- No fixed cap is set on the number of squads, heroes, or army size. Supply, overextension, and the number of hero commanders limit scale (OD-ARMYCAP, owner decision of 2026-09-24).

## Order of Next Questions

The front of the play timeline comes first. Pick only one at a time.

1. OD-SESS — Whether PR #212 has been merged. This list does not write the ADR body in its place.
2. OD-PRESET — Whether to move the 17 confirmed items into `Starting-Presets.md` and the journey document
3. OD-MOVE — Lock the movement speed proposal

New combat values and Unity names fall outside this order.

## Gameplay Open Decisions Ledger

Gameplay open items are managed in GP-01 to GP-14 of [Campaign Progression and Crises](/gdd/rules/campaign-progression/) by question, proposal, input/output, failure/verification cases, and canonical ownership. The prototype''s character placements, times, prices, capacities, and damage values are not grounds for confirmation. Title decrees are entered after the wiki is finalized and are currently unassigned. The selected body, NPC authority, on-site participation, the four map modes, real adjacent stations, and exclusive scenes remain direct decisions. Product direction A (a standalone game) and B (an actual CK3 mod) are not yet chosen. With identical inputs in G1 to G9, the following are each compared as preserved, abstracted, lost, additionally implemented, or unverified: notification and ownership/enforcement (G1), documents, technologies, and positions (G2), different elections and inheritance (G3), service and territory holding (G4), station access, county lookup, and actual arrival (G5), rites, beliefs, institutional policy, and skill (G6), information on hidden events (G7), body succession and knowledge (G8), and single settlement by ResultId (G9). In X1 direct experience, A has a design responsibility to preserve the body, companions, actual arrival, six body parts, and the 3D pan/orbit/zoom goal, while B''s event and stat representation explicitly loses direct performance, continuous spatial interaction with companions, and 3D tactical judgment. Recording part states does not bring that direct experience back. This loss is a loss in the compared representations, not a verdict that it is universally impossible in the CK3 engine. For both directions, actual engine, save, and mod/DLC verification for G1 to G9 and X1 is NOT_RUN, and cost and duration are unmeasured. This comparison does not change holdings, vassalage, or facility rights, and does not choose between A and B.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Research-Sources', 'design', 'research-sources', 'Research Source Registry', '', 'accepted', 40, 'Research-Sources.md', '# Research Source Registry

