INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d01', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d01', 'D01 Characters · Abilities · Traits · Sheets', '', 'proposal', 108, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d01.md', '# D01 Characters · Abilities · Traits · Sheets

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/characters-and-persons/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

## 1. Purpose and Actors

D01 covers a character''s permanent identity and the records of their abilities, skills, and traits. The player acts as one primary character per campaign. NPCs are other characters who use the same action contracts for movement, labor, education, politics, and combat. Being the primary character does not grant anyone else''s body or authority. The primary character, squad leader, combat commander, rights holder, and institutional officer are distinct roles, and one character may hold several of them. States and institutions own the procedures for offices, rights, and property, and a character sheet does not manufacture success for an institutional procedure that has not been filled in.

Abilities, proficiencies, traits, temporary situational modifiers, offices, titles of nobility, possession, effective control, and military command are kept distinct. Holding a public office does not turn an institution''s treasury into personal property or assign every troop to the holder. The Sumun State monarch''s election by contract council, the Central Technology Preservation Institute director''s selection by examination, and the Register Church session moderator''s confirmation of elders are procedures defined in the setting, not ability-score formulas.

## 2. Player Decisions and Eligibility

At the opening, one person is chosen as the primary character from among all living opening characters. Candidates are not excluded for having or lacking an affiliation or office. Dead characters are not candidates for the opening primary character. Selection eligibility is kept separate from the basis for the actual starting location. The handling policy for age and detention restrictions and for location conflicts is unapproved. The concrete method for recording and registering the primary character link is also undecided. These gaps do not invalidate the primary character selection itself or the meaning of the permanent ID.

During play, the player engages through the primary character''s actions and valid command authority. Being able to see an NPC or search for their name is not enough to force that NPC to act. For each action, the following are checked: survival, detention, and actual capability to perform it; authority; location or transmission; resources and reservations; the other party''s will; and institutional procedure. High trust does not make a physically impossible action possible. Existing designated genders are kept, and unspecified values remain undetermined until set in each state''s own setup.

## 3. Identity and Information Visible to the Reader

Names are used for search and browsing, and when candidates share the same name, the reader checks the distinguishing information and selects the character with the corresponding permanent ID. Final identity is determined by the stable character ID, not by the name string. Renaming or having a namesake is not a reason to reissue an ID. Character and event IDs are permanent. Only identities that have already been approved are treated as registered identities, and identity verification alone does not issue a new character or any rights.

The current world''s survival, location, affiliation, and authority are read from that world''s ledger and the applicable version. A past approved register is the basis for confirming historical identity in that version and is not the authority on current world state. The historical record of a dead person can be found, but finding that record does not amount to resurrection, permission to act, or selection as the primary character. Record version conflicts and incompatible saves are reported explicitly and are not covered over by automatic renumbering, migration, or mixing of rules.

The player must be shown the target, the currently known state and the point in time on which it is based, eligibility to perform the action, the scope of authority, and the reason for refusal or pending status, each kept distinct. This information requirement is not a rule that discloses every private state of other characters. D01 does not newly establish a worldwide information-disclosure policy. Use of names and portraits is limited to the recorded project scope and does not add any right to reuse them in external derivative works.

## 4. Action: Before / Execution / After

Before: Confirm the actor and target IDs, the action type, the basis of authority, the ledger version, the location and contact route, conditions, resources, and the causing event. The order of checks is: target existence and version → capability such as survival and detention → authority → actual location and transmission → resources and exclusive reservations → the other party''s will and institutional procedure. Identity verification is the basis for identity within this sequence and does not substitute for the remaining checks.

Execution: Carry out the actual action under valid conditions. Skill-specific education requires real progress, and whether a teacher, records, tools, a location, and a learner are needed is declared item by item. The same required conditions are not imposed on every skill, and a skill is not unlocked merely by holding a document. Identifying an individual from an aggregate population is not the creation of a new person, and the same person is not double-booked for two tasks at the same time.

After: Record the actual completion result and progress, consumption, and the identity of the event and its outcome. Resending the same event does not recreate the result. New abilities, skills, and derived values have not yet been finalized. Having a history of actual actions alone does not grant undetermined values.

## 5. Failure, Interruption, Death, and Saving

If death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the remaining work is rechecked. Materials already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed. Remaining resources are released according to the relevant contract, and the specific interruption policy for future reservations is classed as undecided. Death affects the world''s survival state and eligibility to perform actions. The technical property that a pure identity lookup does not write to world state is not to be read as "the sheet or tasks do not change when a character dies in the world."

Insufficient eligibility, version conflict, inability to perform, and institutional indeterminacy are explained as distinct cases. An ability value with no source or approval is not replaced with 0, and free success is not created. Saving and loading continue the records of compatible versions, and past records are not converted to the latest rules without an approved migration contract.

## 6. Confirmed Boundaries Where This Meets Combat and War

If the primary character is a combat commander, they can coordinate every squad attached to their registered command scope through valid orders such as direct selection, movement, stance, and attack. The existence of a separate squad leader does not block this authority. Enemy forces, unattached independent forces, and allied forces outside the scope cannot be controlled merely because they are visible.

If the primary character is a squad leader, higher orders arrive immediately during combat as a system signal, and the character can choose to follow, partially carry out, or act independently. Transmission and execution are different. The evaluation covers the order''s purpose, time of receipt, feasibility, squad state, actual action, contribution, losses, civilian harm, and any higher-level change or cancellation, and compliance and performance are recorded separately. Evaluation and memory are attributed to the issuing commander as a character, and the same result ID is not applied twice. The original issuer and history are preserved even when the commander is replaced. No formula attaching a fixed trust penalty to disobedience has been approved.

Individual combat records always retain three scopes: the default individual baseline, the reader''s individual redesign and situational modifiers, and unit aggregation. Functions, coefficients, upper and lower bounds, channels, damage distribution, and time conversion are undecided. Character random numbers are kept separate from combat, encounter, and world-event random numbers. The detailed random-number convention is undecided.

Any unit that actually arrives at a combat in progress may take part. Participation is not restricted by origin, rank, the initial register, or an invented arrival time window. A unit that arrives after a settlement reference time is excluded only from the aggregate at that reference time and does not remain in transit permanently. The fact of arrival must be reflected in the next valid state, and formulas for arrival intervals, deployment, capacity, and automatic transfer of command are not created here. Combat has no pause, speed control, or order queue. Immediate combat orders do not make strategic movement or remote communication instantaneous.

Every character may hold valid right and casus belli instances, but holding them alone does not confer the authority to declare war, troops, or command. Starting a war requires a valid registered casus belli. The war score W ranges from -100 to 100, and the opponent''s score is -W. At termination, the casus belli and the beneficiary''s eligibility are rechecked. If the casus belli is already void, the voiding condition is applied before the win-or-loss effect of ±100, and the matter is handled by that casus belli''s return and residual rules. A void claim is not turned into a blank-slate reinforcement or a victory to create rights without basis. At a valid ±100, the war ends immediately with the win-or-loss effect set in the casus belli version and the fixed target scope, and no additional capture, occupation, or forced demand exploiting response delay is permitted. Rights and territory outside the target and the rights of third parties cannot be forcibly transferred by score alone.

In a claim-backed war, the attack authorizer is distinguished from the beneficiary designated in the casus belli. The beneficiary''s consent, affiliation or support contract, the validity of the claim, the target title of nobility, and institutional eligibility to exercise it are checked, and the right won in victory is transferred to that eligible beneficiary. The attacking commander or occupier does not hold it personally in their place. Legal transfer of rights, maintenance or return of occupation, effective control on the ground, and operator cooperation and actual handover are all separate matters. The rank of the title the beneficiary receives and the superior contract are also rechecked, so victory alone does not unconditionally make them a lesser vassal of the attacker.

A custom negotiation in which both sides are below an absolute value of 100 is an approved original expansion of Seoul. Items not registered in the price version lock signing and settlement, while validly registered items can be negotiated. A price assessment at or below the war score does not by itself trigger automatic acceptance. Time and combat continue during negotiation, and if ±100 is reached before acceptance, forced termination by casus belli takes priority, after the casus belli and beneficiary are rechecked and the voiding condition is given precedence. A character is treated not as a commodity but as the subject of a valid act of release, repatriation, interview, or voluntary attachment.

## 7. Cross-Domain Ownership

| Topic | Ownership and D01 Boundary |
| --- | --- |
| Orders, Authority, and Schedules | D03 handles actual requests, transmission, authority, and schedules, while D01 handles character identity and sheet records. |
| Population, Employment, and Rationing | D04 preserves total population, labor, and reservations, and D01 individualization is not population generation. |
| Relationships and Evaluation | D07 handles observation, memory, and relationship reflection, and D01 identity retains the original issuer. |
| Titles, Rights, and Casus Belli | D10 handles per-type effect, confirmation, and succession, and identity verification does not grant rights. |
| Education and Knowledge | D14 handles actual access, education, and progress, and D01 proficiency records are kept separate from document ownership. |
| Squads and Combat | D15 handles attachment, actual participation, and combat execution, while D01 handles the three scopes of individual records. |
| War, Negotiation, and Handover | D16 and D17 handle casus belli termination, negotiation, and actual handover, and D01 identifies the rights holder. |
| Time and Saving | D18 handles single world time and version compatibility and restoration. A screen transition is not a duplication or rewind of the primary character. |

## 8. Meaningful Cases

**Opening selection.** Before: A is a living opening character with no affiliation or office, and B is a dead opening character. Action: Examine the two candidates and choose the primary character. After: A is the selected target and B is excluded. A gains no new office or institutional assets. If the basis for A''s starting location is in conflict, the way it is handled is marked as an unapproved alternative.

**Homonym Search.** Before: Two people with different permanent IDs use the same display name. Action: The player finds candidates through a name search, reviews the distinguishing information, and selects one ID. After: Only that ID''s records are referenced, and neither commands to another person nor treating the first name match as identity occurs. There is no need to prohibit name search.

**Lesson Interruption.** Before: A character has accumulated real education progress and consumed supplies. Action: The teacher dies before the next lesson, and the conditions for conducting it are rechecked. After: Completed progress and consumption remain, and the dead teacher does not continue to conduct lessons. Releasing or suspending future tool reservations is a policy alternative, not an alternative that erases past progress.

**Squad Leader''s Independent Action.** Before: The main-body squad leader receives a superior commander''s order immediately. Action: The squad leader looks at actual feasibility and carries out the order in part or acts independently. After: Compliance and actual results are recorded separately, and the issuing commander remembers those results. A good result is not turned into compliance, and disobedience alone does not produce a fixed penalty.

**Reinforcements After the Reference Time.** Before: A battle is in progress, and a reinforcing unit actually arrives after the settlement reference time. Action: The arrival is confirmed. After: The unit is not counted in the earlier reference-time tally, but participation in the ongoing battle is allowed. Exclusion from the earlier tally is not turned into a permanent movement state or a refusal of participation.

**Valid Casus Belli and Designated Beneficiary.** Before: Attacker A supports the claim of beneficiary B, and B''s consent, support contract, claim, target title, and eligibility to exercise are all valid. Action: A reaches W=100, and the casus belli and B''s eligibility are rechecked at conclusion. After: The right designated in the casus belli transfers to B, and A''s occupation alone does not make it A''s personal right. Third-party rights outside the target are preserved, and on-site control, operator cooperation, and actual handover are checked separately.

**Casus Belli Invalidated Before Score Conclusion.** Before: A casus belli that was valid at the declaration of war is invalid at the conclusion check, and the attacking side''s score is 100. Action: The conditions for invalidating the casus belli are checked first. After: No right is newly granted through a victory effect, and the return and residual rules for that casus belli apply. Costs for prisoners, ceasefire, and cancellation follow the casus belli policy and are not automatically replaced by the claim effects of a white peace enforcement.

## 9. Current Verified Inputs from the Two Source Games and Limits of Applying Them to Seoul

**Crusader Kings III.** The reference installed version is 1.20.0.3, build 25652598, checksum 8e45; this is distinguished as the installation authority, and DLC, mods, and scenarios are left unconfirmed. Script constants, conditions, helpers by range, and adjustments have been confirmed only within the quoted scope. The addition/subtraction in derived calculations and the difference between the education value of 10 and the additive description in the documentation are already limited source differences and do not amount to a complete native formula. The language–TL correspondence is a surveyed unresolved equivalence and gives no default of 0 or conversion formula. The five-versus-six non-equivalence of innate traits is likewise limited to that scope. Seoul records the individual baselines but does not adopt them as its own CP, proficiency, or derived formulas.

**Norland.** The wiki''s pinned version, the MAIN announcement, the BETA announcement, and the historical installed build 25211575 are separate authorities. The old wiki on loyalty and the announcement of relationship-centered changes are not merged into the same current build. The blanket claim that talents are absent also does not match the patch''s talent reintroduction, politician experience, and Duelist changes. The past action experience table, the announcement halving teacher and knowledge experience, and the usage-increase values in the historical data are different channels and cannot produce the current growth rate. The effective skill ×2 and ticket ×1.5 in cultural combat are non-equivalent mechanisms in different units. Per-action eligibility is not merged into a single universal lord eligibility. Seoul records the actual actions, education, and states under the same contract but does not transplant the source game''s constants, probabilities, or roles as they are.

## 10. Concrete Alternatives for Unapproved Policies

The options below are not finalized action rules. They compare the use players need, the intended outcome, the remaining gap, and the consequences of each choice. No option changes any boundary that has already been finalized.

**GP-01 Opening Placement.** The use is to determine the actual starting location after a surviving candidate is selected. The ideal is to keep all surviving candidates while preventing unfounded placement; the gaps are the starting constraints of age and detention and the handling of location conflicts. A keeps candidate eligibility and leaves only the conflicting location unresolved, deferring the starting placement. B shows the scenario''s explicit substitute placement together with the basis for the original location. A avoids a wrong location but delays the start, while B raises the likelihood of starting but requires a basis for the substitute. For age and detention, the options remaining are one that does not exclude the candidate and one that keeps selection eligibility while only notifying of the actual starting constraints. Neither option selects a dead candidate or excludes a surviving candidate because of affiliation or position.

**GP-10 Knowledge Access.** The use is resuming lessons and using facilities after losing a teacher. The ideal is preserving actual access, progress, and consumption; the gaps are future reservations, access to records, and requirements by repair and crafting. A releases future reservations when the teacher is lost so that other learners can use the facility. B keeps valid reservations and suspends lessons to preserve the slot for resumption, but facility occupancy becomes longer. Which is possible, a teacher-centered path or a record- and tool-centered path, is undecided per skill and is not forced across all skills. Neither option erases past progress or consumption or automatically generates teachers or skills.

**GP-11 Evaluation Information.** The use is the issuing commander''s later judgments of trust and assignment. The ideal is separating compliance from performance and reflecting actual observation; the gaps are the display of incomplete evidence and re-evaluation after it is supplemented. A leaves only the insufficient items unresolved and shows the observed items. B shows the result provisionally and finalizes the evaluation after the evidence is supplemented. A provides a quick partial explanation and B a consistent full explanation, but finalization is delayed. Neither option imposes a fixed disobedience penalty, changes the issuer, or counts the same result twice. The numerical function is undecided.

**T68-D01-ISSUANCE Action History and Sheet.** The use is to understand the origin of ability records. The ideal is to distinguish, for each person, the actual action basis and the finalized versus undecided scope of the sheet; the gaps are the histories and values of additional characters. A shows that character''s basis and undecided items on the individual sheet. B shows shared events together while marking each person''s results separately. A makes the individual''s scope clear but repeats common events, while B makes it easy to compare event context but can be confused with others'' results. The two options are only alternatives for displaying information and not a choice of policy for issuing additional values, and participation in an event alone does not grant the same abilities to everyone.

**T68-PF-01 Value Axes.** The use is to understand a character''s desires and the basis of their choices. The ideal is an explanation of values distinct from CP; the gaps are undecided values and narrative–quantity mismatches. A marks undecided values as unknown. B shows together the descriptions that have a basis and the undecided axes, distinguishing them. A reduces misunderstanding but offers less explanation, while B adds context for choices but requires distinguishing numbers from descriptions. Neither option uses undecided values as finalized behavior coefficients.

**T68-PF-03 Character Background.** The use is to understand birth community, language, livelihood, and access to equipment. The ideal is to distinguish fact, inference, and unknown; the gap is the basis for each individual''s background. A marks unconfirmed fields as unknown. B shows facts with a basis and inferences separately. A avoids false certainty, and B conveys more background, but inferences must not mix with facts. Neither option grants paid skills, CP, or equipment ownership on the basis of language markers or occupation alone.

**T68-PF-05 Candidate Information.** The use is, while exploring characters, to distinguish selectable targets from candidates that are still undecided. The ideal is a clear display of permanent ID, identity, and scope of use; the gap is the mismatch in identity, biography, and rights per candidate. A places undecided candidates in a separate list and does not link them to action targets. B links only finalized candidates to the selection list and leaves the rest as undecided information. A makes it easy to compare all candidates but has no action linkage, and B makes it easy to find usable targets but must be distinguished from the full list. The two options are ways of displaying candidates and do not grant registration, abilities, or rights because someone is on the roster.

**T68-PF-07 Personalization and Generation.** The use is to track individuals within labor aggregates. The ideal is preserving population and labor along with actual training evidence; the gaps are newly generated triggers, age, candidates, and occupation-derived martial skills and values. A deals only with personalizing characters in existing aggregates. B treats the inflow of future new characters as a separate path while distinguishing the basis for inflow from the personalization of existing characters. A preserves clearly, and B can handle population changes in the world but additionally needs a generation policy. Neither option increases population through personalization or grants proficiency or unit eligibility through a generation marker alone. Generation rates and ability values are undecided.

## 11. Remaining Gaps in Values and Conclusion

Ability, skill, derived-value, TL, language, combat, and growth functions and the values on additional individual sheets are undecided. Neither the two historical specimens nor the record-matching procedure finalizes values for all characters. Unknown values are not filled with 0 or treated as free successes for actions.

There is no confirmation of the printing of a copy that matches the original text of the GURPS Basic Set 2004 Fourth Edition. The standards for abilities, derived values, TL, languages, reputation, skill costs, skill entries, success and contests, and attack, defense, damage, injury, and modifiers are not yet closed. The public Lite or preview, the source game''s constants, and the historical roster cannot close these gaps.

The use of the numerical alternatives is action and combat calculation, and the ideal is a result in which units, editions, and individual bases are visible. The remaining gaps are the original text, Seoul''s calculation functions, and per-individual values. A keeps the basic individual baseline, and B is a direction of an original individual redesign explicitly compared with that baseline. A helps trace the original baseline, and B makes it easier to handle Seoul''s situation but needs more original functions. Unit aggregation is handled separately in either direction. Without unsourced values or 0 substitution, three scopes must all be distinguished: the basic individual baseline, the original individual redesign and situational modifiers, and unit aggregation. No calculation is selected or executed now.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d02', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d02', 'D02 Daily Life · Needs · Health · Mood', '', 'proposal', 109, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d02.md', '# D02 Daily Life · Needs · Health · Mood

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/characters-and-persons/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

Daily-life actions are requests that run up against a character''s actual condition, supplies, location, schedule, and the other party''s will. This document separates the action boundaries that are settled from the design alternatives not yet chosen. The arithmetic for health, fatigue, mood, and rations, and the rules for carrying out treatment, are undecided.

## 1. Actors and Player Decisions

The player takes part in the campaign as a single main-body character. NPCs use the same action contract, and each action checks that actor''s own actual condition. The main body is never duplicated or replaced with another character''s state.

The daily-life actions that the player and capable NPCs can choose are applying for rations, buying water and food, requesting lodging or treatment, resting, and attending an appointment. These are separate requests, so making a treatment request does not mean rations were received or rest was taken. A ration window checks eligibility and actual supplies, a seller checks supplies and trading authority, and a lodging or treatment provider checks location, skill, resources, and consent. Whether a request can be made differs from whether the service succeeds. Which provider accepts whose request, and the concrete rules for treatment methods and consent procedures, have not yet been chosen.

The contract of the detaining party limits the scope of permitted interviews, letters, and negotiations. A detained character cannot carry out free movement or field work on the grounds of needs or appointments. Roles in treatment, care, and command are confirmed through the current character and actual authority. Role samples from past state names, capitals, or succession methods are not used as the authority of a current treatment provider or the world ledger.

## 2. Eligibility and Execution Checks

A request needs an actor, a target, an action type, a basis of authority, the target ledger version, a location and contact route, conditions, resources, and a causing event. A target can be searched by name, but a name match alone does not establish that it is the same character. The permanent identifier resolves identity.

The check order is: target existence and version → feasibility, such as being alive or detained → authority → actual location and delivery → resources and exclusive reservations → the other party''s will and institutional procedure. If an action is impossible because of safety, physiology, or detention, needs and schedule cannot override that impossibility. A new total order among safety, physiology, and detention is not set. Injury is not a list that blanket-prohibits every action but a state that changes the conditions for performing each action. The amount of that change is undecided.

When needs and schedule conflict, the approved dispositions are named Defer, Refuse, and Negotiate. Who chooses among them and under what conditions, as well as the acceptance probability and the re-evaluation order, are undecided. High trust cannot create a healer, water, or a place that does not exist, nor can it substitute for command authority, property rights, passage rights, or consent that are absent.

## 3. Before the Action, the Action, and After the Action

Before the action, the actor''s actual state, the request target and version, location and delivery, resources and reservations, and authority and consent are checked. The request is kept separate from the execution result. Only performance and consumption that actually took place during the action are recorded in the ledger that owns them. Merely recording a request does not improve health or mood or issue supplies. While treatment types and effect sizes are undecided, no success figures or recovery formulas are attached.

If death, dismissal, occupation, loss of contact, or the like occurs after approval but before execution, the reserved work is checked again. In-world cancellation or death is not replaced with a rejection code from the record validator. Progress already completed and supplies already consumed are preserved, and the remaining resources are released under the relevant contract. D02 does not take over ownership of death, succession, release from detention, or property settlement.

## 4. Failure, Duplication, and Loading

Infeasibility, lack of authority, non-arrival, shortage of supplies, reservation conflict, and absence of consent are each failure reasons at their respective check stages. A failed request is not turned into successful treatment, nor retried under another person''s identity. Re-sending the same causing event does not produce a new result, and a completed result is not applied again. This does not mean it is permanently forbidden to file the same daily-life request again with a new cause.

Loading restores the current stage and reservations at a consistent save boundary. Quitting and loading are not in-world death or departure. A version mismatch or a corrupted save is an explicit error, and there is no silent renumbering, mixing of rules, or substitution with a new campaign. A failed request or an unchosen disposition likewise does not erase or change the main body or the permanent identifier.

## 5. Information and Authority

Execution judgments read the actor''s actual state, but that does not mean every NPC''s private health and needs are revealed to the player. Information that a request was observed differs from information that treatment was actually carried out. The target name is for searching, and homonyms and name changes are still distinguished by the permanent identifier. Provider authority, available locations, supplies that have actually arrived, and consent are each confirmed separately, and neither trust nor historical rosters can substitute for them. The specific scope of information disclosure and the update interval remain as undecided alternatives.

Daily-life state can be an input to combat feasibility, but it does not create command authority. The combat commander''s main body commands all attached squads within the registered command scope. A main-body squad leader receives system orders immediately but may choose to follow, partially carry out, or act independently. Impossible performance is distinguished from arbitrary disobedience, and compliance from results, and these are left as an assessment of the commander character. No fixed trust penalty is set. No pause, speed control, or order queue is added to combat. Any unit that actually arrives is eligible to join as reinforcement, and daily-life state does not newly create restrictions based on the initial roster, origin, or rank. A war''s valid casus belli, goal limits, ±100 forced termination, and separate negotiation are the boundaries of D16, and D02 does not convert health or mood into war score. At termination, demands are not applied on score alone; the casus belli and the current eligibility of the explicitly named beneficiary are rechecked first. If the casus belli''s basis has disappeared, the invalidation rules for that casus belli are checked, so ±100 does not make an invalid casus belli valid. If A wins to restore the claim of an eligible character B, the beneficiary of the right is the B named in the casus belli, and A does not receive all the occupied territory. D02''s treatment and ration requests do not carry out this transfer of rights on its behalf.

## 6. Ownership and Delivery by Domain

| State and Result | Ownership and the Role of D02 |
| --- | --- |
| Character Identity and Main Body | D01 owns identity, and D12 owns death, succession, and main-body transition. D02 references the actual actor. |
| Health, Needs, Mood, and Injury Conditions | Design subject of D02. Figures, recovery, mood changes, and the amount of change in injury conditions are undecided. |
| Schedules, Orders, and Duplicate Reservations | D03. D02 checks the available time and valid orders. |
| Rations, Labor, and Population | D04. The points of contact are ration applications and the effect of health on labor; the specific quantities are undecided. |
| Supplies, Production, and Economic Reservations | D05; actual movement and arrival belong to D06. D02 reads only what is actually available. |
| Relationships, Memory, and Assessment | D07. It passes on observed performance but does not create a trust-change formula. |
| Rights, Passage, and Institutional Authority | D10 and the related ownership contracts. The possibility of holding a right is distinguished from the authority actually valid for this action now. |
| Detention and Prisoners | The legal detention/main-body boundary of D12 and D13, and the war prisoner ledger of D16. Permanent detention is not death. D02 reads the permitted scope. |
| Combat Performance and War | D15 and D16. It conveys the feasibility of living conditions and does not redefine command, reinforcement, or war termination. |

Verified arrivals at or before the consumption cutoff are included in that cutoff. If the times are the same, reservation, production, and consumption follow after arrival verification. Supplies that arrive after a cutoff are excluded only from that cutoff, and even after they actually arrive they do not remain permanently in transit. The details of the cutoff interval, split cargo, and total order have not yet been decided.

## 7. Four Daily-Life Scenes

### Example 1: An Injured Character''s Treatment Request

Before: A schedule to rest at the lodging remains, but the injured body does not meet the conditions to receive treatment at the current location. There is also no healer, space, or water. Action: The character files a treatment request. Judgment: Safety, physiology, and the actual location and resources are checked. The rest schedule, thirst, and high trust cannot create treatment conditions that do not exist. After: The request does not become treatment, and the injured state and the schedule remain as they were. Who chooses among Defer, Refuse, and Negotiate is undecided, so this example does not choose a disposition. It also does not assume whether the character is detained.

### Example 2: Attending an Appointment While Detained

Before: The appointment location requires free movement, but the character''s detention contract does not allow that movement. Action: The character requests to attend the appointment. Judgment: The permitted scope of detention takes precedence over needs and schedule. After: Attendance is not carried out. Permitted interviews, letters, and negotiations are each separate requests and are not automatic substitute actions. Even if a disposition has not yet been chosen, the character, main body, and permanent identifier are retained.

### Example 3: Re-sending a Treatment Request Record

Before: A character''s treatment request was observed, but the carrying out of treatment was not confirmed. Action: The request record for the same causing event is delivered again. Judgment: It is checked whether the result has already been completed. After: No new result or additional consumption arises from the same cause. The mere existence of a record does not raise health or select a medication or procedure. Another person''s record with the same name has a different permanent identifier, so it is not merged into the same request.

### Example 4: Interruption After Approval and Late Arrival

Before: A daily-life service job has progress already completed and supplies already consumed, with a reservation remaining. The supply cargo actually arrives after this consumption cutoff. Action: During execution, an in-world cancellation occurs or the actor dies. Judgment: The remaining performance is checked again and passed on to the ownership contracts for reservation, death, and settlement. After: Completed progress and consumption are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released under the contract. The cargo that arrived late is not retroactively included in the past cutoff, but the fact of its arrival remains and becomes subject to later availability checks. No new recovery rate, refund rate, or consumption cycle is assumed.

## 8. Current Verification Basis from the Two Source Games and the Boundary of Application to Seoul

**Crusader Kings III, 1.20.0.3 / build 25652598:** In the material re-evaluating D02''s 13 source occurrences and 97 citations, we distinguish 10 document claims, 2 unresolved native mechanisms (C01 mortality, C12 disabled harm.2051), and 1 finite non-equivalent counterpart (C11). C01 is read according to the define, not the check interval in the comment. The serious illness treatment C05 removes the trait directly and applies a cooldown and immunity, which distinguishes it from the recovery path of ordinary treatment. C11''s EP3 condition of stored amount ≤ 0.6 of the maximum and its 10-year dehydration stage are conditions of the source game, not Seoul''s water stock or daily consumption. Age-related expenses and incapacity flags, callback and lower-bound delays, the selected coping chain, and treatment weights are read only within the limits stated in the material. The other party''s weight is not treated unconditionally as an acceptance probability. The branch, active DLC, and mods are unconfirmed. In Seoul it is used as comparative material for separating treatment request, execution, and recovery, and the source game''s figures are not adopted.

**Norland:** Population revision 1316 describes a Prisoner Barn where workers give prisoners food and drink, but Patch #43-45 describes prisoners using warehouse food directly with no wardens needed. This is an edition-bound documentary contradiction, and dates alone do not settle which takes execution priority. The equivalence of worker/warden and barn/barracks is also unconfirmed. The multi-source rows are Actions 1318, Knowledge 1293, Warfare 1319, and States 1262, and each citation is distinguished by edition. In Seoul, this is used as comparison material that checks detention status, ration provider, and actual access separately, and no feeding route is adopted automatically.

The research on the two source works is secured input. Remaining uncertainty about the native mechanics is not to be turned into a missing research input or an approval of Seoul rules.

## 9. Unadopted Alternatives and Comparisons Needed for Decisions

Below are concrete comparison alternatives; they are not adopted as current policy or coefficients. Each QA is a design question to check after a choice is made.

| Item | Use / Ideal | Concrete Alternatives | Gap / Reason | QA |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Disposition Choice | Handle conflicts between needs and schedules through deferral, refusal, or negotiation; the choosing party and the outcome must be explainable. | A: The actor proposes a disposition and the other party decides whether to accept. B: The party who receives the request decides with reasons attached, and the actor may request negotiation. | The choosing party, conditions, and reassessment order are undecided. The same name does not imply an automatic-acceptance algorithm. | Does negotiation never turn an impossible request into a physical success? Do NPCs undergo the same check? |
| Treatment Provision / Consent / Information | Check provider, location, skill, resources, and consent; the request must be distinguished from the performance. | A: The provider presents feasible treatment options and the requester consents. B: The requester presents the desired treatment and the provider checks whether it is feasible. For information, A: show only observed facts; B: show details after an authorized examination. | Provider selection, the consent procedure, and the scope of medical information disclosure are undecided. The current provider cannot be determined from a historical roster. | Are consent and performance conditions each checked? Is unobserved health never shown as established fact? |
| GP-04/population-ration | Settle rations, purchases, wages, consumption, and shortages; the same person and the actual goods must be linked without duplication. | A: Settle eligibility and reservations per consumption deadline. B: Settle per individual provision event and aggregate into the consumption deadline. | Quantities, wages, cycles, and shortage handling are undecided. This is a Seoul economic policy choice. | Are duplicate reservations never paid out twice? Is an actual arrival after the deadline never left permanently in transit? |
| GP-04/health-personal-effects | Arithmetic of injury, recovery, labor, and health; a verified individual baseline is required. | A: Keep the original Basic baseline and apply only Seoul conditions separately. B: Specify an original individual redesign that is comparable to that baseline. | Without the original Basic and an exact choice of figures, HP/FP, recovery, and derived arithmetic cannot be closed. Only the direction in which injury changes conditions is settled. | Are the original and the changes distinguished? Is a condition change never turned into a ban on all actions? |
| GP-11/command-evaluation-interface | Pass living and injury states to performability and observation; compliance and performance must be separated. | A: Link the cause event to both the grounds for inability to perform and the actual outcome. B: Keep the grounds observation and the outcome record separate and link them by event identity. | Slot linkage, information disclosure, and evaluation policy are undecided. Command authority, immediate dispatch, and the principal body''s autonomous choice are settled and are not subject to reselection. | Are impossible performance and arbitrary disobedience distinguished? Does good performance never overwrite compliance? |
| GP-11/combat-calculation | Individual attack, defense, DR, damage, and injury versus unit aggregation; three scopes must be maintained: the individual baseline, original redesign and situational adjustment, and unit aggregation. | A: Compute individual results first and aggregate them into the unit result. B: Derive aggregation channels from the verified individual baseline and distribute them to individual losses. | The Basic original, functions, channels, loss distribution, and real-time conversion are undecided. Approval of a scope name is not official approval. | Is the same individual baseline being compared? Is health status never turned into an arbitrary damage formula? |
| T68-NUMERIC-DESIGN-ASSUMPTIONS/seoul-assumptions | Compare assumptions about mood, time, economy, and score; the reason for selection must be traceable. | A: Compare mood changes driven by events. B: Compare mood changes driven by state duration. | POC figures, coefficients, and cycles are not adopted. Legitimacy and war score from other domains are not automatically converted into mood. | Are assumed values never displayed as confirmed coefficients? Is the D16 closing criterion left unchanged? |
| T68-NUMERIC-DESIGN-ASSUMPTIONS/health-derived-assumptions | Compare health, fatigue, and derived effects; a Basic baseline and sources are required. | A: Pass fatigue along as a separate state. B: Pass it along as a performance condition derived from the verified individual baseline. | Existing assumed values cannot be carried over without the Basic original and individual approval. | Is the same assumption never applied twice to both health and labor? Is an unsecured original never claimed to have been secured? |

The comparison row for combat arithmetic preserves two separate dependency identities together. `GP-11/combat-calculation` and `T68-COMBAT-THREE-DIMENSIONS/three-scope-calculation` overlap in content but are distinct source items. The latter is an arithmetic dependency on the basic individual baseline, original redesign and situational adjustment, and unit aggregation, and it requires Basic C01, C03, C04, C05, and C06. Refer to the use, ideal, A/B alternatives, gap, reason, and QA of the GP-11 combat calculation row above as a shared comparison, but do not merge the two identities or approve the arithmetic.

The absence of the original Basic Set Fourth Edition has not been resolved. HP/FP, injury, recovery, derived abilities, and related arithmetic remain undecided until the original materials and exact choices are in place. The cases of nonexistent water and seats are comparisons of presence versus absence, not formulas for health, mood, or rations.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d03', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d03', 'D03 Actions, Orders, Authority, and Schedules — Reader Design Document', '', 'proposal', 110, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d03.md', '# D03 Actions, Orders, Authority, and Schedules — Reader Design Document

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/game-logic-principles/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

- Basis: the settled clauses of SP-1.0.0 and the already approved decision scope of the campaign and combat rules. Reading this document does not substitute for approval, and unapproved quantities and formulas are not set (Chapter 12).

## 1. What This Domain Covers

D03 covers **a character''s actions, orders, authority, and schedule**. One player continues the campaign through one **principal body character**, and other characters in the world are NPCs using the same action contract. The player''s on-site actions read that one principal body''s actual position and movement, and NPC orders read the actual position and movement of that NPC. The principal body is not duplicated, and strategic movement is not turned into teleportation to the battle scene.

This document reads the already approved rules side by side as **one combat signal** and **one strategic remote order**. What follows is the list of approved policies, and each item is attributed to the approval decision it belongs to.

- Separation of axes: rank, title, possession, effective control, and military command are distinct axes.
- Delegation gate: it holds only when membership, consent, confirmation, and scope are in place, and it does not transfer ownership.
- Attachment scope: the principal body commander directly commands all attached squads within the registered scope. Even if each squad has its own separate squad leader, this does not block higher command authority.
- Actual contact: strategic remote orders are delivered along the actual contact routes available at that time.
- Safety first: safety, physiological needs, and detention incapacity take precedence over needs and schedules.
- No pause, no speed control, no order queue.
- Authorized record keeper: record manipulation is performed only by authorized record, notary, and genealogy officials acting on the orders of a sovereign or office holder.

No values converting these policies into score or time have been approved. Identifiers are permanent, revision conflicts and storage errors are stated explicitly, and no automatic renumbering, automatic migration, or silent mixing of rules is done.

## 2. Actors

| Actor | What They Do | Basis |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Principal Character (Player) | The one character who carries the campaign forward. Selecting a different character does not change who holds the principal role. | sp1.b3, campaign-progression transition table, `Bonche sijak` (main body start) row |
| NPC | Every other character who uses the same action contract as the principal | sp1.b3 |
| Principal Battle Commander | Holds the command scope and assigned squad list registered in the battle session, and directly controls every battle-map squad in that scope with valid orders such as select, move, stance, and attack. | sp1.b111 |
| Principal Squad Leader | Receives the superior commander''s directive immediately as a system signal and chooses one of: follow, partially carry out, or act independently. Input is not locked, and the order is not forcibly executed. | sp1.b112, sp1.b113 |
| Superior Commander (Sender) | Sends the directive. The receipt record and the evaluation result belong to this commander character. | sp1.b112, sp1.b114 |
| Evaluating Party (Commander Character) | Evaluates at the moment they learn of the outcome through observation or reports, and uses the result as the basis for their memory of that principal and for later assignment and trust decisions. | sp1.b114 |
| Local Commander (NPC) | Judges automatic battle orders against the prior objective, stance, and authority, together with actual observed information, capabilities, and objectives. A new remote order must go through contact, receipt, and acceptance. | combat-formula.20261003.pipeline stage 1 |
| Authorized Record, Notary, and Genealogy Officer | Receives orders from a lord or office holder and carries out record manipulation (gathering/drafting evidence → composing a claim → recognizing or rejecting its validity). A direct manipulation path for a principal without an office is not adopted. | claims-owner.20261003.fabrication |
| Delegator and Proxy | A delegation is in exactly one of these states: valid, suspended, expired, or revoked. If the proxy dies, the original holder''s rights remain. | sp1.b38–b43 |

The actors above are not separate principals; they are roles that a single principal can take on, plus roles of other NPCs. The state names and office titles in the history ledger are not proof of command authority in the current world. The valid records of current character, assignment, delegation, and location must be checked. The cases below are hypothetical situations that illustrate the roles and do not fix a cast or quantities.

## 3. Decisions the Player Actually Makes

1. **Battle Command (Real Time)** — If the principal is the battle commander, they directly select, move, set the stance of, and attack with every squad within the assigned scope. A squad is not excluded from control merely because it has a separate squad leader. Because there is no pause, speed control, or order queue, this choice is always made in real time. The same orders cannot be given to units outside the scope (enemies, independent units in sight, allied forces outside the command scope).
2. **Squad Leader''s Choice** — If the principal is a squad leader, they choose one of following, partially following, or acting independently in response to a directive. This choice is a named decision, not a probability or a compulsion.
3. **Sending Strategic Remote Orders** — Sends orders such as movement to targets within the delegated scope. The order travels along an actual route such as a messenger, radio, or contact network, and is executed only after the target receives and accepts it.
4. **Responding to Received Requests** — For both NPCs and the principal, if a received order conflicts with their desires or schedule, it is left as one of: hold, refuse, or negotiate. An order does not erase an individual''s own goals.
5. **Choosing Who Performs Record Manipulation (Indirect)** — Record manipulation happens only through an authorized officer. There is no path for a principal without an office to directly alter a claim in the ledger.

The action screen distinguishes `Ganeung` (possible), `Seonhaengjogeon Bujok` (insufficient prerequisites), `Gwonhan Eopseum` (no authority), `Eungdap Daegi` (awaiting response), and `Jedo Mijeong` (system undetermined), and displays the basis for each (Chapter 8).

## 4. Eligibility — Actual Checks by Path

Whether an action is permitted is determined by the actual checks set for each path. The order of checks is fixed (sp1.b12): **target existence and version → feasibility (alive, not detained, etc.) → authority → actual location/delivery → resource and exclusive reservation → the other party''s will/institutional procedure**. An action that is physically impossible is never made to succeed through high trust.

**Battle Directive (Battle Signal)** — Authority is determined by the command scope and assigned squad list registered in this battle (sp1.b111). This signal arrives immediately, without passing through strategic contact routes such as messengers or radio (sp1.b112). The record and notary officer is not a requirement of this path. Receipt records the world time, superior order identifier, sending commander, target squad, objective, permitted scope, and version.

**Strategic Remote Order** — The delegation must be valid by passing the membership, consent, ratification, and scope gates, and there must be an actual contact route (messenger, radio, contact network) usable at that time. The target receives it and can accept it only if there is no inability to perform due to safety, physiology, or detention. If the authority ended before receipt, the arrived order has its authority checked again. The record officer is not a requirement of this path.

**Record Manipulation** — Performed only by an authorized record, notary, and genealogy officer who has received an order from a lord or office holder. Checks the accessible record, genealogy, and ratification paths, as well as the duration, cost, and risk of detection. A direct manipulation path for a principal without an office is not adopted.

**Common Battle Constraints** — There is no pause, speed control, or order queue. The holding of and waiting for a response to a strategic request differs from this battle queue. An authority failure or physical impossibility is not put into a queue to be automatically made to succeed later.

The boundary is also clear. Enemy forces, independent forces that are not attached, and allied forces outside the command range are not within that command range, even if they appear on the battle map (sp1.b111). A lookup does not create command authority. Belonging to the same faction does not permit direct control. A request to look up or summon a related person does not mean that person has arrived (campaign-progression transition table, rows `Sojaeji` (location) and `Daehwa·Sanghojagyong` (dialogue/interaction)).

## 5. States and Transitions

### 5.1 State Sequence of a Strategic Order

Draft → authority check → in transit → received by target → accept/hold/reject → scheduled → executed → completed/cancelled (campaign-progression transition table, row `Myeongnyeong` (order)).

- Delivery, receipt, and acceptance are separate stages. The fact of delivery alone does not start execution.
- If the order conflicts with desires, goals, or an existing schedule, a hold, refusal, or negotiation is recorded.
- The detailed numbers for delivery time, acceptance judgment, and priority are undecided (Chapter 12).

### 5.2 Battle Directive Pipeline

Directive → immediate receipt → choice to follow or act independently → actual execution → per-order evaluation → reflected in commander''s memory and relationship (sp1.b115).

- The receipt record keeps the world time, superior order identifier, sending commander, target squad, objective, permitted scope, and version (sp1.b112).
- Only the current directive and performance state, and the history of their updates, are kept. No new queue is created.
- The same order evaluation is not applied twice for the same result identifier. When a commander is replaced, existing orders and evaluations preserve the original sender and history and are rechecked under the new commander''s authority.

### 5.3 States of a Delegation

A delegation is exactly one of **valid, suspended, expired, or revoked**. A delegation does not transfer ownership, and if the proxy dies, the original holder''s rights remain (sp1.b42). A revocation proposal requires the contract''s revocation clause, evidence of violation, and a ratification procedure (sp1.b43).

### 5.4 State Sequence of Record Manipulation

Gathering/drafting evidence → composing a claim → recognizing or rejecting validity. Even when the manipulation is finished, it does not become possession (claims-owner.20261003.fabrication).

### 5.5 Storage and Versioning Obligations

Identifiers are permanent, and revision conflicts and storage errors are stated explicitly. There is no automatic renumbering, automatic transfer, or silent mixing of rules. Resending the same event identifier does not produce a new result, and if a death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the reserved task is rechecked (sp1.b13).

## 6. Approved Judgments and Recording Obligations

Action permission judgments follow only the actual checks by path in Chapter 4. No formula that converts policy into scores or time has been approved. What is approved is the recording obligations below.

- Every directive receipt records the world time, superior order identifier, sending commander, target squad, objective, permitted scope, and version (sp1.b112).
- Resending the same event identifier does not produce a new result (sp1.b13).
- The same order evaluation is not applied twice for the same result identifier (sp1.b115).
- Compliance and performance are recorded as separate items (sp1.b114).
- When a commander is replaced, existing orders and evaluations preserve the original sender and history and are rechecked under the new commander''s authority (sp1.b115).
- An order that cannot be carried out, and an invalid directive issued after the commander''s death, are recorded as an execution failure and distinguished from willful disobedience (sp1.b114).
- Identifiers are permanent, and revision conflicts and storage errors are stated explicitly. There is no automatic renumbering, automatic transfer, or silent mixing of rules.

## 7. Failure and Preservation

**Distinguish unexecutable and refused orders from orders awaiting a response or on hold.** A hold is not execution approval, nor is it a combat order queue. Re-evaluating or re-proposing after an obstacle clears is an unapproved alternative in 12.1.

- Without a route, there is no reception. A remote order with no usable courier, radio, or contact network is not executed.
- If authority ends before reception (delegation expires or is revoked, or the scope is lost), the order that arrives is checked again and is not executed.
- An order that cannot be carried out, or an invalid order issued after the commander has died, is an **execution failure** and is distinguished from willful disobedience (sp1.b114).
- If an order is canceled in the world, or issuing it becomes invalid because the commander died, the unexecuted remainder is not carried out. Records of the cancellation, death, and execution failure are kept, and consumption, progress, and losses that have already occurred are preserved.
- A failed order is not left sitting in a queue, and combat is not paused to wait for it.

**Facts that have already occurred are preserved.**

- Supplies already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed; only the remaining reservations are released according to the contract (sp1.b13).
- An order refused before execution does not change the recipient''s location, objective, or schedule as an execution result. If it is canceled after the recipient has already begun carrying it out, the actual movement, consumption, and progress are preserved, and only the remaining reservations and schedule are re-checked according to the contract.
- There is no automatic trust reduction fixed to disobedience (T68-TRUST-EXISTING). The increase, decrease, decay, and duration of directional trust and loyalty based on evaluation have not yet been decided, and this does not mean the change amount is 0. The already approved classification thresholds are used as they are: friend at `T ≥ 25`, enemy at `T ≤ -25`, and nemesis at `T ≤ -50`; evaluation does not change those thresholds themselves (sp1.b79, b80, b114; 12.3).
- Even if a player''s action produced a good result, compliance and performance are recorded separately (sp1.b114).
- When someone dies, the survivors'' relationships and mourning events remain, and the deceased''s relationship values are not copied to the successor (sp1.b86).

## 8. Boundaries of Information and Authority

- The action screen distinguishes `Ganeung` (possible), `Seonhaengjogeon bujok` (insufficient prerequisites), `Gwonhan eopseum` (no authority), `Eungdap daegi` (awaiting response), and `Jedo mijeong` (system undetermined), and shows the basis. It does not leak hidden facts through the displayed reason, and explains with `Hwagindoen geungeo bujok` (insufficient confirmed basis) (sp1.b14).
- The immediacy of combat signals applies only to combat orders. Remote contact and character movement on the strategic map are not handled instantly (sp1.b112).
- Viewing does not create command authority. Access conditions for distant places, detention, combat, and loss of contact differ by interaction (campaign-progression transition table, `Daehwa·sanghojagyong` (dialogue/interaction) row).
- High trust does not create command authority that does not exist, nor actions that are physically impossible.
- People, units, and places can be searched by name. Namesakes, name changes, and title turnover are distinguished by stable identifiers and editions, and a name search does not establish identity or authority.
- An evaluator updates their own trust and memory at the point they learn of an event through observation or report. A method in which hidden betrayals are known instantly anywhere in the world is not used (sp1.b83). The world''s record of hidden facts is distinguished from the facts each character knows through observation or report (sp1.b83). Relevant history, sightings, memories, and current trust are not reset by saving or by a change of body (sp1.b88).

## 9. Cross-Domain Ownership

D03 owns the skeleton of orders, and each domain owns the substance of the items in the table below. D03 only reads these values and does not redefine them.

| Domain | What D03 Reads | What D03 Does Not Do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D01 Characters and Identity | Character identifiers and editions, opening survival choices | Carrying out the identity check itself |
| D02 Daily Life and Needs | Safety and physiological state (priority of feasibility) | Needs model and numerical design |
| D05 Economy, Production, and Resources | Local stock verified at the deadline and arrived cargo | Design of cargo distribution, stock calculation, and consumption order |
| D06 Movement and Contact | Existence of actual contact routes (courier, radio, contact network), actual movement and arrival | Travel time and cost formulas, map and observation design |
| D07 Relationships and Memory | Current values of directional trust and loyalty, fixed thresholds for friend, enemy, and nemesis classification, attribution of evaluations | Relationship change functions, increase, decrease, decay, and duration |
| D10 Titles and Casus Belli | Existence of right instances (such as a casus belli for opening a war) | Casus belli registration values and deadlines |
| D11 Conferral and Delegation | Delegation gate (eligibility, consent, ratification, scope) procedure | Detailed rules for revocation conditions |
| D13 Assets and Delivery | Ownership and disposability of assets under negotiation, actual delivery state | Adjudication of asset rights, delivery, and duplicate demands |
| D15 Combat and Squads | Squad and attachment lists, combat session registration scope, facts of reinforcement arrival | Combat formulas, capacity limits, and reinforcement condition values |
| D16 Diplomacy and War | Valid casus belli, war score, objective-limited forced termination | Detailed design of casus belli, war, and prisoner rules |
| D17 Negotiation, Trade, Peace, and Contracts | Registered negotiation items, price functions, acceptance and signing conditions | Design of item registration, pricing, and acceptance functions |
| D18 Time and Saving | Campaign clock, save and restore contract | Design of time event ordering and save format |

### 9.1 Consumption Boundary of War and Negotiation Commands

A war creation request first checks the validity of the registered casus belli. If there is no valid casus belli, it is rejected before reaching the forced-termination point or negotiation calculation. If an in-progress casus belli has already become invalid because its basis changed, the invalidity conditions are checked before the ±100 forced termination, so the score never creates a right that has no basis (sp1.b121). A person without an office may hold a casus belli, but that alone grants no authority to declare war, no troops, and no command authority (sp1.b118). War score ranges from -100 to 100, and the opponent''s score has the opposite sign. At ±100, a forced termination limited to the target scope of the casus belli version takes effect first, any pending negotiation proposal is discarded, and no further damage or asset demands pile up while waiting for a response (sp1.b119, b121, b127–b128). Below an absolute value of 100, item-by-item negotiation, which is a Seoul-specific extension, can be proposed. Unregistered price items block signing and settlement, and only validly registered items can be negotiated. Even though the evaluation structure subtracts the total offered price from the total demanded price, `V ≤ W` is not automatic acceptance. The price and acceptance coefficients are undecided, and signing authority, the parties'' authority to dispose of assets, actual feasibility of performance, and whether the same asset is demanded twice are checked separately. The sale of people, forced marriage, and non-consensual reassignment cannot be negotiation items. After an agreement, the transfer of assets, persons, and units must go through actual delivery and movement, and is not completed instantly by the command record alone (sp1.b124–b128). D03 only consumes this result and does not create prices, distribution, or disposal authority.

When requesting a claimant-support war, distinguish between the chief who approves it and the beneficiary who will receive the rights. The beneficiary found by name is fixed to a stable person identifier and pinned to the casus belli, and that person''s consent, affiliation/support contract, claim validity, target title, and institutional eligibility to exercise it are checked. The victory right transfers to that beneficiary and is not personally held by the attacker in their place. For example, even if a hypothetical claimant, Kim Harin, asks a chief for support, Kim Harin''s eligibility and designation as beneficiary must be confirmed, and the chief does not take Kim Harin''s rights merely because the chief won. The actual rank of the title the beneficiary gains and the superior relationship are rechecked through the higher-level contract, and equal or higher rights are not automatically converted into a lower vassal relationship (sp1.b118, b119, sp1.reference.beneficiary).

## 10. Examples — State Transition Design

The examples below are hypothetical situations that apply approved rules. They show the actions the player chooses, the checks, and the actual results, and they are neither proof of execution nor a confirmed cast.

### 10.1 Combat Signal — From Issuance to Record

- **Before**: The player''s only avatar is a squad leader, and the superior commander is an NPC. The avatar''s squad is within that commander''s registered assignment scope, and there is no in-progress order record.
- **Request**: The NPC commander issues an order for a rearward movement within the battlefield. As the avatar squad leader, the player chooses partial execution.
- **Event**: The signal arrives instantly without passing through a messenger or radio. The reception leaves the world time, order identifier, sending commander, target squad, purpose, permitted scope, and version. Because the target is within the registered assignment scope, the action of this order is permitted. The squad leader carries out a movement that avoids the danger zone through partial execution. The order does not lock input, the battle does not stop, and the signal is not queued.
- **After**: The choice made and the actual execution that follows remain as the current state, and the evaluator remembers the sender and the result identifier. An evaluation with the same result identifier is not applied twice. The squad''s rearward movement is a movement within the battlefield, not a withdrawal from the whole battle or a departure from participation. If the avatar is instead the superior commander, it can give direct orders to all assigned squads even if a separate NPC squad leader exists (sp1.b111). Movement time and the amount of evaluation change are not set by this example.

### 10.2 Strategic Remote Orders — Distinguishing Delivery, Reception, and Acceptance

- **Before**: The sender has already entrusted a command scope to the recipient, and that delegation is valid. The recipient is in a different place.
- **Request**: The sender announces a movement within that scope remotely.
- **Event**: The order moves along whichever actual route is open at that time among messenger, radio, and communication network. The recipient receives it, has no incapacity from safety, physiological, or detention causes, and accepts it if it fits their needs and schedule. If there is a conflict, it remains as one of hold, refusal, or negotiation.
- **After**: Only an accepted order moves on to execution (status column `susin → surak → yeyak → silhaeng`, i.e. received → accepted → scheduled → executed). Delivery, receipt itself, and acceptance are separate stages, so the mere fact that an order was delivered does not start its performance. Travel time, transmission cost, and acceptance probability have not been decided.

### 10.3 Failure — Absence of a Route and Loss of Authority Before Reception

- **Before**: The delegation was valid at the time of sending. Failure can occur in two branches.
- **Branch A (no route)**: There is no usable messenger, radio, or communication network. Since there is no actual contact route, there is no reception, and neither the receipt of the order nor the execution result of that order occurs. The recipient''s independent life and movement continue.
- **Branch B (authority lost)**: Before the order arrives, the delegation has expired or been revoked, or the sender has lost that scope. The arriving order has its authority rechecked and is not executed. Materials already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed, and only the remaining reservations are released according to the contract.
- **After**: This order, rejected before execution, does not turn its place, target, or schedule into an execution result. If work already in progress is canceled or interrupted by death, the completed progress and consumption remain, and the remaining work and reservations are rechecked. Whether to preserve or close an undeliverable request is an unapproved alternative in 12.1, and there is no automatic execution. The expiry period and the formula for delivery time are not set.

### 10.4 Preservation — Independent Action, Separate Records, Refusal of Record Tampering

- **Before**: The avatar squad leader has received an order (the reception record''s 7 fields as they are), and the existing directional trust and loyalty values are in place.
- **Request**: For the order handed down, choose `Dokja Haengdong` (independent action). In a separate situation, the same principal, holding no office, tries to directly alter a claim in the ledger.
- **Event**: The squad leader''s choice is a named choice, so input is not locked and there is no forced execution. Even if the actual execution result is an observation that contributes to the objective (a favorable contribution), compliance and performance are recorded as separate items, and `Dokja Haengdong` (independent action) is not changed into `Ttareugi` (following) just because the observation turned out favorable. The evaluation is attributed by sender and result identifier, and the same result identifier is never applied twice. The office-less principal''s attempt to tamper with the records is rejected, because that person is not an authorized records, notarization, or genealogy officer acting on an order from the lord or an office holder.
- **After**: There is no automatic trust reduction fixed to disobedience. How much trust and loyalty change after the evaluation is not yet decided, and this does not mean the change is 0. The classification thresholds of friend `T ≥ 25`, enemy `T ≤ -25`, and nemesis `T ≤ -50` remain as they are. A refused record tampering does not create occupancy.

### 10.5 Actual Arrival and Deadline — Point-in-Time Judgment, Not Permanent Waiting

- **Before**: A battle in which the avatar is the commander is in progress, and additional units are actually on the move. Other cargo is scheduled to arrive after this consumption deadline.
- **Decision/Event**: The player gives a valid order to the existing assigned squads. When additional units actually arrive during the battle, they can participate without restrictions based on origin, rank, initial roster, or a new time window. Permission to participate and the avatar''s command authority are separate, so it is checked again whether the additional units have been assigned.
- **After**: Arrived units are recorded as having arrived and participated, and are not permanently fixed as in transit. Cargo after the deadline is excluded from this deadline''s inventory, but becomes local inventory at the actual arrival event and is read in later judgments. Arrivals at or before the deadline are included after arrival verification. No separate restriction called a consumption deadline is placed on battle reinforcements.

## 11. External References — Crusader Kings III and Norland

This compares **version-specific document and script claims** confirmed in the two games. This does not describe the real-time behavior of the currently installed build, and no observation from either side has been adopted as a coefficient, probability, or formula for Seoul. The citations are references for reading the gate structure.

### 11.1 Crusader Kings III

- Version: installed build **1.20.0.3, build 25652598** (no evidence of a beta branch; not assumed to be the stable branch).
- Interaction sending gate order and availability conditions: `game/common/character_interactions/_character_interactions.info` lines 732–755.
- Gift availability/sending guards: `game/common/character_interactions/00_gift.txt` lines 35–54 and 88–111.
- Invitation sending and availability: `game/common/character_interactions/00_invite_to_activity.txt` lines 10–36. Delegation of acceptance judgment: the same file, lines 100–112. This range does not declare a response delay.
- Feast guest eligibility, capacity, and priority distinctions: `game/common/activities/activity_types/feast.txt` lines 707–748.
- Goal priority and progress documentation of council tasks: `game/common/council_tasks/_council_tasks.info` lines 1–13 and 36–65, `00_chancellor_tasks.txt` lines 120–134.
- Comparison of travel cancellation and army halting: `game/gui/window_travel_planner.gui` lines 1113–1123, `game/gui/window_army.gui` lines 1118–1131.
- Limits of what was read: this is a comparison of documented fragments (scripts, FAQs, tool descriptions), and the raw internal engine algorithms (overall response probability, allocation and concurrency handling) have not been confirmed.

### 11.2 Norland

- Wiki `wiki-actions` document version **1318** — a fragment of the logical AND gate of hard eligibility (hardEligibility): a necessary condition that all per-action requirements must be met. It does not prove the full requirement list or automatic acceptance after the requirements are met. There is only a document version and no game build badge.
- Developer notice **Patch #57 (BETA)** (notice identifier `dev-1842846814446802`) — a statement was confirmed that if the selected squad right-clicks a person it cannot attack, this is interpreted as a movement order to that location. Full attack eligibility, pathfinding, queueing, and frame delay were not confirmed.
- The politician''s delegation-threshold citation (alliance threshold +20) is confirmed in the body of the **BETA51** announcement. The MAIN50–53 selectors that the original dispatch material pointed to are wrong as grounds for the BETA51 edition; the same wording was confirmed in the corrected BETA51 selector. This document''s claim is verified comparison material, but it is not adopted as a Seoul rule or value.
- Limits of what was read: this is a comparison of document fragments against developer announcements, and the raw processing (dispatch timing, queues, concurrency) is unknown.

### 11.3 Comparisons Used in Seoul

CK3''s distinction between being able to send, a response, and actual execution explains why strategic orders should show delivery, receipt, and acceptance separately. Norland''s necessary conditions for meeting requirements explain why high trust alone should not pass every eligibility check. Norland''s right-click move that cannot attack is a reference for separating the input interpretation of attacking from moving, and is not adopted as Seoul''s automatic fallback policy. CK3''s response days, capacity, prices, and scores, and Norland''s political thresholds, are not transplanted into Seoul values.

## 12. Not Yet Decided: Alternatives Ready for Decision

The items below are not blanks. Each is in a decision-pending state, equipped with a question, actual consumer use, ideal outcome, current gap, rationale, and verification method (QA). No value is filled in implicitly.

### 12.1 GP-03 Message Authority (Detailed Policy for Remote Strategic Orders)

Remaining original question: *With what functions and priorities should we set the cost and time of strategic communication, delegation expiry/receipt/acceptance, conflicting orders, and the NPC reevaluation order?* Six policies are each prepared with two alternatives, and all are unapproved.

| Policy | Remaining Choice | Alternative A | Alternative B |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Delivery | After a route break becomes known, should an undelivered proposal be kept or ended? | `retain-undelivered` — Preserved as undelivered; when the route recovers, the recipient''s acceptance is resubmitted under the same identity (no automatic execution, no combat queue) | `terminate-undeliverable` — Closed as undeliverable; not revived even after recovery, and the sender needs a new proposal referencing O-A |
| Expiry | Does authority remain valid in the check at delegation endpoint E? | `exclusive-end` — Valid only up to just before E; at E and after, authority check fails | `inclusive-end` — Includes the moment E; excluded afterward |
| Revocation | After a revocation takes effect, is an unexecuted order kept blocked or closed? | `retain-blocked` — Marked as authority-blocked, only unconsumed reservations released; resuming requires a new explicit authority proposal | `cancel-invalid` — Canceled for invalid authority; later authority requires a new order referencing O-A |
| Stale Snapshot | After a target-version conflict, should re-proposal under the same lineage be allowed? | `explicit-reproposal` — After the conflict rejection, a sender-confirmed re-proposal may keep the same lineage (new authority and recipient acceptance required) | `new-order` — Closed at the same time as the conflict rejection; a new order O-B is required |
| Superseded Orders | If two valid received orders conflict: | `accepted-replacement` — Only after replacement order O-B is actually received, authority-checked, and accepted, is O-A''s unexecuted remainder canceled and its reservations atomically exchanged | `renegotiate-conflict` — Conflict recorded, then renegotiated; O-A is not replaced merely because O-B arrived (not a queue) |
| Reevaluation | After an obstacle clears, an intent that was on hold: | `observed-change-reconsider` — At the time of the observed change, identity, version, feasibility, authority, delivery, resources, and recipient consent are rechecked (refusal and negotiation are still possible) | `renewed-proposal-required` — Remains non-executing until an explicit re-proposal arrives |

- **Actual use**: The strategic order screen shows the causes of delivery, receipt, acceptance, hold, and refusal, and the world state must consistently preserve undelivered orders, expiry boundaries, conflicting orders, and reevaluation results according to the selected policy.
- **Ideal outcome**: Each choice reads with its exact question, outcome, distinguishing cases, and remaining premises, and no alternative is marked as approved.
- **Current gap**: The communication cost/time function, acceptance judgment and priority, and the global ordering of simultaneous events (belonging to GP-14/06) are not decided. The alternatives below are policy and model proposals to compare, without approval of computed values.
- **Rationale**: These policies are owner decisions that pick facts of the Seoul world (courier costs, delegation customs), not engineering defaults.
- **QA**: For each case where Alternatives A/B produce different observed outcomes (preserved vs. closed, recheck vs. wait), compare the before/event/after states against receipts.

### 12.2 GP-01 Starting Eligibility and Location (Opening Details)

- Question: The selection of all surviving opening characters is settled. How should detailed restrictions such as age and detention, as well as starting-location evidence and conflict handling, be decided?
- Consumer use: Candidate display and eligibility-reason display on the opening screen.
- Ideal: The authored ledger''s opening edition and confirmed location take priority; for unrecorded locations, only explicitly approved scenario placements are allowed.
- Gap and rationale: The values for detailed restrictions await owner input. The current POC''s adult filter is not adopted as a product restriction.
- QA: Compare selectable/unselectable results, reasons, and starting-location output for cases of future appearance, minors, and location conflicts.

### 12.3 T68-D07-EVALUATION-DECAY Evaluation Decay and Relationship Function

- Question: How should we set trust and loyalty gains and losses from order evaluations, the decay, recovery, and extinction periods, and the forgetting function?
- Gap and rationale: The existing values of directional trust and loyalty, and the classification thresholds of friend `T ≥ 25`, enemy `T ≤ -25`, and archenemy `T ≤ -50`, are approved (sp1.b79–b89). The value changes, decay, and periods resulting from evaluations are not approved, and the decay function is pending confirmation. This does not mean there is no change, nor that the thresholds themselves change.
- QA: Double-check that the same evaluation event is not applied twice, and that the post-evaluation change-amount policy remains undecided.

### 12.4 T68-D11-CONTRACT-RECALL Delegation Recall Conditions

- Question: How should we set the specific combination and deadlines of recall clauses, violation evidence, and ratification procedures?
- Gap and rationale: The requirement framework for recall proposals is approved (sp1.b43). The condition details await an owner decision.
- QA: Verify that recalling a relic without withdrawal opens a betrayal issue, and that illegal seizure is not shown as a normal grant.

### 12.5 T68-PF-03 Reinforcing Birth-Community Facts (Proposal, Not Adopted)

- Question: To what extent should the birth-community facts in the cast ledger be reinforced?
- Status: Proposal, not adopted. The canonical prose states, "Reinforcing the facts of the birth community was not adopted."
- QA: Check that the scope of reinforcement is consistent with character IDs and location evidence.

### 12.6 List of Unapproved Values

Travel time, transmission cost, acceptance probability, compliance coefficient, utility formula, tie handling, re-evaluation interval, delegation expiry period, relationship decay period, manipulation''s duration, cost, detection risk, and target scope, and the figures and deadlines for casus belli registration. These values are **undecided**, not zero or an implicit default.

### 12.7 Use, Ideal, Gap, Rationale, and QA for Unapproved Alternatives

The following A/B options are all proposals, and neither has been chosen as the default policy. The specific functions, coefficients, and periods remain unapproved.

| Question | Alternative A / Alternative B | Actual Use and Ideal | Gap / Reason | QA Distinguishing Cases |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GP-01 Starting Eligibility and Location | A: Show surviving candidates first and restrict by action when they cannot perform / B: Apply the approved scenario restrictions at the candidate stage as well | Distinguish candidate exclusion from action restriction on the opening screen | Age, detention, and location conflict policy unapproved; the current POC filter cannot substitute | Compare how the display of detained surviving candidates and their actual starting locations differ between A and B |
| D07 Evaluation and Decay | A: Change per observed event, then decay with elapsed time / B: Change per observed event, then modify only on subsequent events | Use compliance and performance separately for commanders'' memory and later assignment decisions | Change amount, period, recovery, and forgetting function unapproved; the change amount cannot be fixed at 0 | Distinguish the evaluation of good independent actions from impossible orders, and compare memory changes during periods with no events |
| D11 Recall | A: Block unexecuted authority once the contractual recall procedure is established / B: Distinguish a state of suspended authority until ratification and handover | Prevent confusion between ownership and command authority on the delegation screen and in reservation release | Recall clauses, evidence of violation, specific combinations of ratification, and deadlines unapproved | Compare order eligibility at the point when only a recall proposal exists and at the point when it actually takes effect |
| PF-03 Fact Reinforcement | A: Record only birth communities backed by evidence / B: Keep unknowns as they are and separate proposals per scenario | Distinguish facts from proposals on the character screen | Reinforcement scope not adopted; language and occupation notations cannot be converted into purchased skills | Compare the display of canonical facts and proposals when location evidence for the same ID conflicts |
| Travel Time and Contact Cost | A: Sum time and consumption by actual route segment and mode / B: Use a per-mode profile for the whole route | Let the sender distinguish expected arrival from actual consumption | Unit, speed, and cost coefficients unapproved; owned by D06 | Compare non-arrival and preserved consumption when a route is severed or a partial delivery is canceled |
| Acceptance, Priority, and Utility | A: After eligibility, an explicit priority rule / B: Compare utilities among eligible candidates | Judge NPCs'' goals and schedules together with player orders | Acceptance probability, compliance coefficient, utility formula, ties, and re-evaluation interval unapproved | Compare the reasons for deferral, refusal, or negotiation under conflicting requests with the same desire and authority, and under new observations |
| Delegation Period and Record Manipulation | A: Fixed duration/cost profile per contract or task / B: Profile by approved scope and amount of evidence | Have the contract and records officer explain the end time, consumption, and detection risk | Expiry period and manipulation duration/cost/risk/target scope unapproved | Distinguish completed progress, consumption, and remaining reservations when authority is lost mid-execution |
| Casus Belli Registration Deadline and Quantity | A: Validity conditions and deadline per type of casus belli / B: Tied to the validity of the underlying event | Check a valid casus belli and the authority to declare war separately before a war is created | Registration figures and deadlines unapproved; owned by D10/D16 | Compare the loss of the underlying basis with the absence of command authority in a holder of a valid casus belli |

Until approved, these alternatives are merely comparison proposals. Neither alternative is substituted by a success beat or an implicit default, and no undecided figure, function, or policy is treated as settled.

## 13. Summary

- **Approved**: one principal character and the same action contract, axis separation, delegation gate, assignment scope, immediacy of combat signals and the 7 reception fields, the named choices of comply, partial, and independent, actual contact routes, safety first, absence of pause, speed, and queue, single application of result identifiers, authorized records officers, and permanent identifiers with explicit conflicts.
- **Not decided**: GP-01, GP-03, T68-D07-EVALUATION-DECAY, T68-D11-CONTRACT-RECALL, and T68-PF-03 in Chapter 12, the list of figures in 12.6, and the unapproved alternatives in 12.7.
- This document remains a proposal and does not claim that gameplay has been implemented.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d04', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d04', 'Labor, Population, Employment, and Rations', '', 'proposal', 111, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d04.md', '# Labor, Population, Employment, and Rations

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/economy-and-logistics/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

One player continues the campaign as a single principal character, and every other character is an NPC using the same action contract. There is one world time. This domain describes the relationship among aggregated and individualized residents, labor reservations, the actual fulfillment of wages and rations, and unit strength. It distinguishes confirmed relationships from figures and policies that have not yet been chosen.

## Actors Involved

- **Principal characters and NPCs.** They request jobs, education, treatment, and purchases, and act according to their own schedules and conditions for carrying things out. Receiving an order does not automatically erase their desires, goals, or existing schedules.
- **Aggregated residents and individualized residents.** Individualization means taking the same person who was in the aggregate out and representing them separately; it is not the creation of a person. From the aggregate stage, every member has a distinct permanent internal ID, and individualization and later return carry that ID over unchanged. A name is an optional search clue and not the identity that distinguishes people with the same name; the stable ID of the selected record establishes identity.
- **Personnel in charge of facilities, transport, units, and treatment.** They reserve the time and resources of each role and record the results. An institution''s appointment authority, authority to dispose of goods, authority to assign civilian labor, and military command authority do not substitute for one another.
- **State institutions.** Each country''s wage and ration system is maintained. What is read in common is the actual payment, arrival, and consumption results. A particular country''s past rosters or the setting-based location of an institution alone do not create current residents'' eligibility to receive.
- **Commanders and squad leaders.** If the principal character is a combat commander, they can coordinate all squads assigned within their valid command scope. Even if there is a separate squad leader, higher authority is not blocked, and no control authority arises over visible enemy forces, independent forces, or allied forces outside the scope.

## Player Decisions and Eligibility

The player decides which members of a tally to individualize, which roles to request and when, whether to return survivors whose roles have ended to the tally, and which needs to meet through rations and purchases. Assigning another person''s labor requires the authority appropriate to that action, plus the other party''s acceptance and any institutional procedure. Looking someone up or searching by name does not by itself grant assignment authority. Who initiates a specific individualization or return, each state''s registry enrollment procedure, and the order for reconciling ration fulfillment against purchase needs are all undecided.

Eligibility is checked separately for target existence and version, survival and ability to perform, authority, actual location and contact route, resources and exclusive reservations, and the other party''s consent and institutional procedure. Lacking a skill or equipment, being in transit, and being injured are distinct states. Injury is not a blanket marker that bans every action; it changes the performance conditions of the role in question. Viewing a registry is not creating a unit. A muster requires checks for personnel acceptance, equipment, supplies, a commander, and reservations, and the details of recruitment consent and who provides equipment have not yet been decided.

## State and Rules

**Tallies and individuals are counted only once.** When one person is individualized from a tally of N people with no population events, the tally becomes N−1 and the individual is 1, so the sum remains N. That person is removed from the tally''s available labor. A return converts the same ID back into the tally and releases the individual representation, so no new person is added. A duplicate input that tries to add the individual back into the tally is rejected, and the last valid tally and individual state is kept. Birth, death, migration, and capture are population and custody events separate from representation changes. The total-population conservation example holds only when no such events occur.

**One person''s roles do not overlap.** The reservations of the same person occupy the half-open interval [start, end), which includes the start and excludes the end. Two reservations overlap when `A.sijak < B.jongnyo-igo B.sijak < A.jongnyo` (that is, A.start < B.end and B.start < A.end), and roles that overlap by a positive amount of time are not assigned. If they merely touch at a boundary, as in `A.jongnyo = B.sijak` (A.end = B.start), the end of the earlier role and the start of the next role can switch at the same moment. The verdict is the same even if the comparison or insertion order changes, and touching one reservation is never a reason to ignore an actual overlap with another. Simultaneous labor by different people does not violate this per-person exclusivity condition, and the separate capacity of facilities and equipment is checked separately. Input whose start and end are equal, or whose start is later than its end, is treated as not a valid time interval before the overlap check. This does not allow zero-hour roles or set any separate duration policy. Releasing the existing reservation and acquiring the new one in a schedule change are recorded as a single transition.

**Essential demand and actual fulfillment are different.** From daily demand, essential consumption, contracts, and supplies are reserved first, and approved orders are processed within the bottlenecks of labor, inputs, power, facilities, and storage. Ration reservation, actual issuance, arrival, and consumption are distinct states. A ration share is not double-counted as purchase demand. The settlement order is undecided between first reconciling ration fulfillment to derive the remaining purchase need, and first recording the purchase request and then offsetting it by the rations actually issued; either way, the same need and fulfillment must not be counted twice. The same labor loss caused by a shortage is applied only once across life and economy, while satisfaction, mood, loyalty, and relationships are retained separately. The labor reduction coefficient to apply and the shortage resolution procedure are undecided.

**Unit capacity follows three dimensions.** Capacity varies with the commander''s personal ability, the state''s level, and the settlement''s level, and no single cap is applied to all units. The old fixed 20 has been replaced. Which abilities and levels count, and how they are calculated, is not yet defined. Reducing capacity does not delete excess personnel, and heroes are counted separately from rank-and-file troops. Units must be reorganized while preserving people and equipment, but the reorganization cost and detailed procedure are undecided.

## Before, Action, After: Muster and Treatment

The following figures of 500, 5, and 3 are examples illustrating relationships, not capacity, creation rates, or minimum sizes. Birth, death, migration, and capture are assumed not to occur.

**Before.** The tally is 500 and individuals are 0. The five people to be individualized each have a distinct permanent ID from the tally stage onward, and individualization neither creates nor changes those IDs. The day''s essential demand is secured first, and actual receipt and consumption are confirmed separately.

**Action.** Individualizing the five makes the tally 495 and individuals 5. Three of those five can be reserved for unit roles at the same time. Each person must have no reservation overlapping an existing role. The roles of the other two are not determined by this example. If the five people''s essential shares are already reserved, they are not reserved again as additional ration reservations. Issuance, arrival, and consumption are recorded in sequence, and the same shares are not re-counted as purchase demand. If one of the three mustered people is injured, the same ID and history are kept, and the reservation is switched between the unit and treatment roles. After treatment, the person meets the return eligibility and goes back into the tally.

**After.** The tally is 496, individuals are 4, and the total is 500. Of the four individuals, two are remaining unit members and two are individuals who were not mustered. The one who returned is in the tally and is not counted again among the individuals. The past unit and treatment labor history and consumption are not erased by the return. Receiving the same event again does not increase population, labor, or supplies.

**Separate return example.** Apart from the treatment case, if all three mustered from a tally of 495 and 5 individuals return, the tally is 498 and individuals are 2. The number of returnees in the two examples rests on different assumptions.

## What Remains After Failure, Interruption, and Death

- A second role that overlaps for the same person is rejected, and the first role and the person''s identity are kept. Against an existing [10,20), [12,18) overlaps for a positive duration and is rejected, while [20,25) only touches at the boundary and is allowed. Given existing [10,20) and [25,35), a request for [20,30) touches the first reservation but overlaps the second during [25,30), so it is rejected. These numbers are examples showing the approved boundary semantics, not rules for duration, cost, or priority.
- When authority, location, or resources are insufficient, or the other party does not accept, the request''s rejection, deferral, and negotiation are distinguished. Valid existing records are not erased arbitrarily.
- If death, dismissal, occupation, loss of contact, or cancellation of the work occurs after approval, the performance conditions of the remaining work are checked again. Supplies already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed. Remaining reservations and unused resources are released or preserved according to the contract in question. A dead person''s reservation is not revived by a return. Death is a separate population event, and there is no guarantee that the population equals its pre-action value.
- Materials already used in the construction stage are not automatically refunded merely because work stops. Completed progress, unused materials, material ownership, and labor reservations are kept separate. The timing is undecided between acquiring the storage reservation on entering the output stage and securing it at an earlier stage. Whichever is chosen, completed earlier stages are not erased on the grounds of insufficient storage.
- Resending the same event ID does not create a new result. A version conflict or corrupted save is an explicit error. Saving and loading restore the current stage at a consistent boundary and are not a death or cancellation within the world.

## Boundaries of Information, Time, and Authority

A name is an optional search clue, and a stable ID resolves the identity of the selected person. People with the same name are not merged into one person. Every tally member has a distinct permanent internal ID from the tally stage onward, and individualization and return continue using the same ID. This representation approval does not determine the ID issuance algorithm, the storage schema, return eligibility, or institutional authority. A past identity registration is also not authoritative for current location, position, rights, or cast figures. An unrecorded location is distinguished from currently absent, and a person is not marked as a local worker without grounds.

Only cargo whose arrival verification time is at or before the consumption cutoff is included in that cutoff''s inventory. At the same time, arrival verification is processed first. Cargo that arrives after the cutoff is excluded only from that cutoff and does not remain forever in transit after it actually arrives. Arrival records are read at the subsequent boundaries of reservation, production, and consumption. The daily cutoff is not a turn that stops movement in the world.

Any unit that actually arrives at a battle in progress can take part as reinforcements. A battle that has already ended receives no new arrivals. Arrival cycles, placement, transfer of command, and capacity figures are separately undecided. Higher-level orders reach the main body''s squad leader immediately as a system signal, but the main body chooses to follow, partially comply, or act independently. Purpose, feasibility, actual action, contribution, losses, and the like are recorded in the commander''s memory and in later assignment decisions, distinguishing compliance from results. No fixed insubordination penalty has been set. Immediate battle orders are not instantaneous processing like strategic movement or remote contact. Battles have no pause, speed control, or command queue.

A war requires a validly registered just cause and an eligible beneficiary designated by name in that cause. A war is not created from an invalid cause. While a war is in progress, the cause''s validity and the designated beneficiary''s eligibility are rechecked before the war-score endpoint, and if either has disappeared, the corresponding invalidity rule is handled before forced termination at the endpoint is applied. −100 to +100 is the war score, not the range of a territorial objective. If the endpoint is reached while the validity of the cause and the designated beneficiary is maintained, the war is forcibly terminated with that cause''s victory or defeat effects, and no rights outside the objective are additionally acquired by force. Below an absolute value of 100, item-by-item negotiation is allowed with valid authority, price version, and fulfillment conditions. Only the signing and settlement of unregistered valuation items are blocked. Labor reservations do not change that boundary.

## Ownership in Other Domains

- **D01 Characters and Abilities:** Permanent identity and abilities. D04 refers to the unique ID assigned from the tally stage onward and does not create a new person through representation changes. The specific issuance algorithm and storage schema are separately undecided.
- **D02 Life and Health:** Satisfaction, mood, loyalty, and health, and the single application of the same shortage-related labor loss. No health coefficient is invented from wage or ration fulfillment.
- **D03 Actions and Schedules:** Authority, delivery, acceptance, deferral, negotiation, and reservation transitions. Labor feasibility and command authority are separate.
- **D05 Economy and Production:** Bottlenecks, material consumption, stage progress, and storage. D04''s labor reservations and the details of mapping construction stages are undecided.
- **D06 Movement and Cargo:** Actual movement, arrival, and per-cutoff inventory eligibility. Lookup and muster requests are not arrivals.
- **D09·D10 Institutions and Rights:** State-specific procedures and positions and powers of disposal. An institution''s location is not a ration eligibility formula.
- **D15 Combat:** Command of assigned squads, temporary squad leaders, and actual reinforcement and reorganization. A capacity reduction is not the deletion of characters.
- **D18 Time, Events, and Saves:** A single world clock, acceptance order and causal events, save boundaries, and prevention of duplicate application.

## Verified Comparison of the Two Source Works

### Crusader Kings III

This is a survey of the documentation and scripts for installed build 1.20.0.3 (build 25652598). However, the higher-level context used in the earlier manual survey, the active branch, DLC, mods, and runtime observations were not confirmed, so the document-level comparison is not extended to the full runtime behavior. The staff of a court position are characters, and there are candidate conditions and a check against duplicate occupation of the canonical position. The salary''s default value, conditional branches, and modifiers, as well as the gold/treasury payment path, are confirmed in the documentation. However, hiring, replacement, and appointment do not demonstrate an algorithm for extracting residents from aggregate counts or returning them under the same ID. The current-state variables for task start and end are also not a half-open reservation ledger. The abstract quantity of army supply is not the same as deduplicating residents'' meals and rations. The raw troop figure of 100+75=175 from estate buildings is neither the final troop count nor a resident count. What Seoul takes from this is a comparative perspective of reading candidate eligibility and actual payment separately, not those salary or troop figures.

### Norland

This is a documentation-based survey using the official community wiki''s Population oldid 1316 and Warfare oldid 1319, plus the developers'' patch notes. Only the raw-text container of Population oldid 1316 is currently unreadable, so it relies on the exact excerpts that were preserved. In neither case is this a result of verifying the actual behavior of the running build as a whole. The population is represented by individual characters and five categories. The training-ground requirement, the permanent recruitment conversion of peasants and prisoners, and the fix for ranged proxies returning after battle are confirmed, but they are not equivalent to Seoul''s temporary muster and same-ID aggregate return. After research, wage selection and direct distribution both remain. The wage cycle, unpaid wages, and the complete payment function are unconfirmed.

The one-per-day peasant food and home storage in MAIN43–45, the suppression of market purchases after distribution in Summer, and the caravan duplicate-purchase fix in MAIN54–56 are limited comparisons of duplicate prevention. They do not demonstrate a consumption ledger for all residents or a tribe-wide ration allocation. Prisoners collecting directly from the storehouse differs between versions and from the earlier wiki''s warden requirement, so it is not mixed into the conditions of any one version. The daytime assignment of the unemployed, retention of the already employed, and nighttime readjustment in MAIN50–53 are not evidence of half-open exclusive reservations. The rule in MAIN35 of one migrant per 10 points starting from a daily average peasant mood of 30 also does not complete a rounding or whole-population event formula. Do not mix MAIN''s refund at the first hour with BETA57''s refund at any time while in transit. These original figures are not adopted as Seoul''s consumption amounts, migration rates, or refund policy.

## Concrete Alternatives for Undecided Policies

None of the following alternatives is a confirmed policy or default. The arithmetic for deriving wage, ration, and headcount figures from individual ability and health has also not yet been decided.

1. **Wages, Rations, and Shortages (GP-04).** Alternative A is wages by role contract plus a bundle of essential items; B is a wage table by institution plus rations by need. It is used in D02 Living Constraints and D05 Demand Settlement. The ideal is to distinguish payment, arrival, consumption, and unmet need, and to settle without duplication. The gaps are the amounts, item ratios, periods, shortage duration, and labor-reduction coefficients. It remains an alternative because neither the system nor the figures have been chosen, and there is no basis for carrying the original''s numbers over to Seoul''s.
2. **Personalization and Return Procedure (GP-04).** A is self-application followed by acceptance by the officer in charge; B is a proposal by a valid assignment authority followed by acceptance by the individual and the institution. It is used for jobs and musters and for institutional rosters. The ideal is to preserve the same ID and population carried over from the aggregate stage, and to make clear who initiates, accepts, and records. The gaps are the ID issuance algorithm, the right to initiate, the recording location, roster registration, and return conditions. It remains an alternative because approval of aggregate-stage IDs and identity continuity alone cannot determine institutional procedures and data formats.
3. **Headcount and Reorganization (GP-08).** A is a headcount table by bracket over three inputs; B is continuous calculation over the three inputs followed by integer conversion. It is used for organization, reinforcement, and reorganization after a commander is lost. The ideal is that the basis of the inputs is visible and that excess personnel are preserved. The gaps are the types of ability, the definition of national/settlement level, the formula, rounding, and cost. It remains an alternative because only the three directions are confirmed and there is no computational relationship.
4. **Recruitment Consent, Equipment, and Reinforcement Details (GP-08).** A is an individual service contract with institutional equipment loans; B is an institutional service procedure with personal equipment use. It is used for actual organization and entry into battle. The ideal is to explain consent, equipment rights, and command scope each separately. The gaps are the conditions of consent, equipment provision and return, entry deployment, and transfer of command. It remains an alternative because the rule that any unit that actually arrives may join a battle in progress does not decide these details.
5. **Population Events.** A is reflecting an event immediately when it occurs; B is recording the event time and summing at designated boundaries. It is used for total population, location, and recruit changes. The ideal is that representational movement is separated from actual birth, death, migration, and capture. The gaps are the occurrence conditions, rates, cycles, and attribution of counts. It remains an alternative because the conservation rule alone cannot determine the event generation rate or when events are reflected.
6. **Roster Enrollment and Population Generation.** In A, the responsible institution enrolls individuals in the roster one at a time based on confirmed personal identity and current eligibility; in B, candidates are generated from confirmed group records, and each individual''s current location, rights, and training are rechecked before enrollment. It is used for cast, unit, and settlement rosters and for attributing new population events. The ideal is that the initiator, decision authority, target identity, and pre- and post-enrollment states are visible, and that historical records are not automatically converted into current rights. The gaps are which institution initiates and decides, the state when a candidate is rejected or duplicated, and which count the new population is attributed to. It remains an alternative because past rosters alone cannot establish current persons, abilities, and rights.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d05', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d05', 'D05 Economy, Production, Construction, and Resources — Seoul Operating Design', '', 'proposal', 112, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d05.md', '# D05 Economy, Production, Construction, and Resources — Seoul Operating Design

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/economy-and-logistics/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

【Approved】 marks the scope of actions already accepted, 【Proposed】 marks an alternative not yet adopted, and 【Open】 marks parts where a needed contract or figure is missing. Example quantities are not adopted as prices, production rates, or periods.

## 1. Scope and Actors

【Approved】 The player acts as a single body, and NPCs follow the same action contract. Distinguish the person performing the construction or repair, the authority who approves orders, the individual or institution that owns the assets, and the person who stores or transports goods. Institutional assets remain with the institution. Ordering construction does not confer ownership or qualification to perform it.

The player''s judgments are requesting that a stage proceed or halt, changing the priority of production orders, and checking which consumption deadline an arrival can be used for. The request authority and the concrete criteria for selecting performers are 【Open】. The ability to find people, facilities, and cargo by name is a 【Proposed】 information convenience and is not yet an approved interface. Whether something is actually the same entity is determined by its permanent ID. Past rosters do not substitute for current survival, position, authority, or supply status.

Combat has no pause, speed control, or command queue. The campaign time-control policy is not decided by this approval. Economic deadlines are not strategy turns that stop movement under a single world clock. In D15 Combat, every squad within the registered command scope assigned to a commander is a selectable target, and orders reach the squad leader immediately, but the squad leader acts autonomously and is evaluated per commander. Any unit that actually arrives at a battle in progress is a reinforcement target. In D16 War, the valid casus belli, the authority to declare war, the named eligible beneficiary, and the fixed objective scope are checked first. If this prior check fails, the war is not initiated, so it does not enter the forced ending at war score ±100 or the individual negotiation stage either. Even a validly initiated war has the casus belli''s continued validity, the named beneficiary''s current eligibility, and the fixed objective scope rechecked immediately before the forced effect is applied. If the recheck fails, the earlier effect is not forced by the ±100 value alone. Only a war that passes the recheck is forcibly ended at war score +100 or -100 with the corresponding casus belli effect and fixed objective scope, and in between it is negotiated individually using registered items. These boundaries are contracts of the combat and war owning domains and are not to be replaced with D05''s inventory and reservation formulas or authority.

## 2. Reservation, Ownership, Storage, Consumption, and Progress

| Concept | Meaning in Play |
| --- | --- |
| Construction Work Reservation | A state in which specific construction work not yet performed has been set aside. It differs from the quantity of a production order |
| Demand Reservation | Input quantities set aside for essential consumption, contracts, supply, or approved orders |
| Legal Ownership | Who is the legal owner of the goods or facility. May differ from the custodian, the carrier, and the key holder |
| Storage and Delivery | Where the goods actually are and to whom they were handed over. Rights alone do not create arrival |
| Consumed Portion | Labor, tools, and materials actually put into the work and already used. Not subject to automatic refund |
| Completed Progress and Unused Materials | Work that is finished and goods not yet used. Preserved separately from the consumed portion upon suspension, cancellation, or death |

【Approved】 If death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the reserved work is rechecked. Goods already consumed and completed progress are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released under that contract. Suspension, resumption, occupation, and transfer handle progress, material ownership, and workforce reservations separately. Occupation or the death of the person in charge alone does not hand unused materials to a new owner.

## 3. Construction and Repair: Qualification and Before/Action/After

【Approved】 Each stage consumes actual labor, tools, and materials. Only stages with qualified personnel and required inputs in place proceed. 【Open】 The stage list, costs and durations, conversion from labor to progress, how tools are consumed, performer selection, authority to request suspension, and the dismantling recovery rate have not been decided.

**Proceed Request.** Before: Check the stages that can proceed, the qualified personnel, and the required inputs. Action: Consume only what was actually put into that stage. After: Distinguish the materials used from those remaining, and calculate progress according to the confirmed stage contract. Do not add the input amount directly to the progress amount or create an arbitrary minimum progress.

**Suspension.** Before: The stage in progress has completed progress, already-spent inputs, and unused materials, each separately. Action: If an authorized suspension request is established, progress stops. After: Preserve the completed progress and unused materials, and do not automatically refund labor, tools, and materials already spent. Who takes over the remaining work and the transfer of ownership are separate decisions.

**Insufficient qualification or input.** Before: The same step has existing progress and materials, but the required qualification or input is missing. Action: The progress request is checked. After: The step does not advance. Existing progress and unused materials remain, and no new consumption or output is created. Unused materials that remain are not called a refund.

## 4. Production Demand and Priority

【Approved】 Required consumption, contract, and supply demand is reserved first, and only approved orders are processed within the limits of labor, inputs, power, facilities, and storage. A target stock level can serve as demand information, but automatic production unrelated to a material shortage, or duplicate processing of the same consumption, is not permitted.

Before: Several orders need inputs and work capacity, and some orders are already partially complete. Action: When an authority adjusts priorities, the existing reservations are released and made anew. After: Existing partially completed work and consumed amounts are preserved. Changing a reservation is not an action that removes finished goods or reruns production from the beginning. Per-item production rates, the economic period, bottleneck formulas, rounding, and tie-break allocation are undecided.

Do not confuse a construction work reservation with the demand quantity of an order. Cancelling a production order or a cargo route, or the death of someone tied to one, does not by itself release other construction work reservations. However, a policy that every field of that order or cargo is permanently immutable has not been approved. An actual cancellation or death checks the world facts and handles the remaining resources according to the applicable contract.

## 5. Cargo Arrival and Consumption Cutoff

【Approved】 If the verified arrival time is at or before the consumption cutoff, the cargo is included in the available stock for that cutoff. At the same world time, processing runs in the order of arrival verification, then reservation, production, and consumption, and causality follows acceptance order and causing event. The same arrival ID is never applied twice. Materials in transit are neither consumed nor counted at the same time as available stock at both origin and destination.

Before: The destination has a shortage demand and cargo is in transit. Action: The actual arrival and quantity are verified and compared with the common daily cutoff. After: An arrival at exactly the cutoff time is also included. Materials that actually arrive after the cutoff are excluded from **that cutoff**, but they do not remain in transit forever. The actual arrival state is reflected, and which later consumption it is used for is decided by the next applicable common daily cutoff and reservation contract.

【Approved】 The selected scope of GP-06 is fixed to the following seven directions. Splitting cargo retires the parent cargo and creates two child cargoes that carry on the parent''s history and ownership. Receipt does not become available stock until the recipient''s confirming signature. On a reservation version conflict, the existing reservation is kept, the conflict reason and current version are reported, and the requester is asked to re-propose with an explicit new intent; it is not placed in an automatic queue. The consumption cutoff is a common daily phase, not a per-station option. If a blockade and an arrival are independently proposed at the same time, they are adjudicated in input receipt order. Cargo that fails receipt verification is quarantined locally and not automatically refunded, and the destination reservation whose proof is pending is kept. Cargo not delivered by the cutoff also keeps its in-transit, unfulfilled status and destination reservation, and does not become available or consumable stock.

This selection does not mean GP-06 is fully complete. The day''s interval unit and phase values, the calendar origin and clock details, the distribution method, persistent storage, the receipt identifier and signature authentication rules, loss and shortage coefficients, the full readiness of the source originals, and actual in-game behavior are still undecided.

## 6. Proposals for Releasing Construction Work Reservations

All of the following are 【Proposals】. Checking world facts and authority is a required precondition, and neither a label attached by the requester nor a matching stored reference alone confirms death or non-use.

**Cancellation before use.** Before: A specific unperformed construction task is reserved for a specific person, and the task''s inputs have not yet been used. Action: An authorized cancellation request checks the target and whether the inputs have actually been used. Proposal A: Only the unused task reservation is released, and materials, ownership, consumed amounts, and completed progress are preserved. If inputs have already been used, this is treated as a suspension of progress rather than an unused cancellation. Proposal B: The cancellation request is left in a review state, and an authority confirms the release of the reservation. Neither option automatically generates reassignment, new work, or a refund.

**Death of the assignee.** Before: The remaining work of a partially completed construction is reserved for a specific person. Action: The current fact of death and the relevant reservation are rechecked. Proposal A: After death is confirmed, the reservation for the remaining work is released. Proposal B: The remaining work is put on temporary hold, and the reservation is cleaned up together with a reassignment decision. Completed progress, consumed amounts, unused materials, and their ownership are preserved under both alternatives. D05 does not automatically generate a successor or a new performer. If the death is unconfirmed or points to a different reservation or person, no release result is produced and the case is left in a state requiring confirmation.

A technical expression that distinguishes the same person and reservation by a stored reference is possible, but there is not yet a producer for actual deployment, death, and authority checks, nor a link to D04 labor reservations. Therefore this proposal must not be read as complete implementation or as an approved world policy.

## 7. Failure and Player Information

The player must be able to see whether a step is advancing or suspended, completed progress, inputs already used, the location and owner of unused materials, the distinction between work reservations and demand reservations, partial order completion, the verified arrival time, and whether it falls within the cutoff. This is information needed for judgment; the screen layout, access rights, and means of obtaining information are not yet designed. Unverified arrivals or deaths are not displayed as confirmed information.

If qualification, authority, or a required input is lacking, the action is not executed, and the reason and the remaining state are distinguished. In doing so, a state where the institution or approving party is not yet determined, a state where the necessary evidence could not be accessed or fact-checking is insufficient, and a state where the action was actually found to be prohibited are not shown as the same. How hidden facts are accessed or disclosed is undecided. A saved-version conflict or corruption is an explicit error and is not recovered by automatic renumbering, migration, or mixing rules. Resending the same event creates no new result, and an already applied result ID is not reapplied. The preservation rules for an actual cancellation, death, or suspension and the rejection of a technical request are separate judgments. Quitting or loading is not a death or cancellation event in the world.

## 8. Illustrative Examples

1. **Repair suspension:** Suppose 4 materials have already been used and 8 remain on site. Even if the step is suspended, the 4 do not come back, and the 8 and the completed progress are preserved. Using 4 materials does not mean progress of 4. The owner of the materials and the on-site custodian may differ.
2. **Order priority change:** After a supply order is partially complete, the priority of a contract order is changed. The earlier reservations are released and the new reservations are checked, but the partially completed supply goods and the inputs already used are not deducted again. Without bottleneck capacity, production does not take place from the new priority alone.
3. **Cutoff boundary:** Suppose there are 13 available materials before arrival and 9 in the cargo in transit. If the 9 arrive and are verified at the same time as the cutoff, 22 are available before consumption and that cargo is no longer in transit. If they arrive just after the cutoff, only 13 are usable for that cutoff, but the 9 that actually arrived are not left permanently in transit.
4. **Cancellation and death compared:** Under Proposal A, cancelling an unused construction task releases only that reservation. Even if cumulative consumption exists in other work, it does not equal whether this task was unused. If a different assignee at the same site has died, the target reservation is not released. Even if that assignee''s death is confirmed, materials already used and completed progress are preserved, and reassignment is separate.

## 9. Other Domains and Ownership Boundaries

| Owning Domain | What D05 Receives and What It Does Not Decide for Others |
| --- | --- |
| D01 Characters | Current characters and abilities. Past rosters are not used to imply qualification or consent to perform. |
| D04 Labor and Population | Permanent character IDs and labor reservation intervals. It does not replace the construction reservation link, performer selection, or death release policy. |
| D06 Movement and Cargo | Actual movement and arrival verification. D05 decides whether verified arrivals are included in the cutoff. |
| D12 Death and Body Succession | World death and succession procedures. D05 rechecks its own reservations according to those facts. |
| D13 Property and Delivery | Legal ownership, transfer, and delivery rights. Releasing a reservation alone does not change ownership. |
| D18 Clock and Saving | World time, consistent saving, and prevention of duplicate events and results. The cutoff is not a world pause. |

## 10. The Two Source Games and the Verified Comparison Scope

**Crusader Kings III PC 1.20.0.3, Steam build 25652598.** Building costs, durations, and monthly income in the preserved installed scripts, the replenishment of supplies after gold is spent on a supply purchase, and the gold refund for camp demolition were each verified within the scope of their calls. Building income is not the same as a production recipe that combines physical inputs. Even a building named Reserve Provisions does not prove Seoul''s required demand reservation or prevention of double consumption. HoldingView''s construction cancellation call is preserved, but the engine''s cancellation refund, fractional progress, and post-capture handling remain unresolved. Therefore Seoul takes only the comparison that distinguishes cost/duration/effect from consumption/reservation/ownership, and does not carry the CK3 gold refund over as an automatic material refund. Active DLC, mods, actual play, and correspondence with the current patch are outside the verified scope.

**Norland PC — preserved data build 25211575 and version-specific documents.** The Construction, Resources, and Trade materials, along with separate patch materials, distinguish construction costs and production output, knowledge, occupation, and work-time conditions, builders'' hauling and work, and storage types. The documents state that breakdown repair and fire repair have different input conditions, and that manually demolishing a completed building does not refund construction materials. Automatic distribution between warehouses is retained in the preserved summer patch document. This is not substituted for Seoul''s actual cargo arrival rules. Different patch sections on production cycles and remaining mine output are not merged into a single current formula. The cumulative algorithm for fractional tool costs, reservation of required stock, and the distinction between consumed and retained materials in unfinished cancellation or capture remain unresolved. The separate install menu version 0.5588.9777.0 does not prove that all of this historical material currently applies.

The comparison of the two source games is limited to version-specific documents, scripts, and preserved excerpts. Because some full originals could not be checked, excerpts are not expanded into complete rule texts. Unresolved engine behavior and Seoul''s figures and policies are left undecided.

## 11. Concrete Selection Alternatives and Remaining Gaps

All alternatives are 【Proposals】, and there are no confirmed coefficients.

| Selection | Consumer Use and Ideal | Concrete Alternatives | Current Gap and Reason | QA |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Steps, Recipes, Durations, and Prices | Performers and players can anticipate the required inputs and completion conditions | Fixed per-task step table / per-task step table depending on inputs and environment | GP-05 is undecided. There is no conversion from labor and tools to progress, and no number of steps. | Check empty input, insufficient required input, partial completion, and interruption/resumption against the confirmed table |
| Bottleneck, power, storage, rounding | Process approved orders only in the amount actually possible | Bottleneck minimum per decision period / recheck inputs and capacity at each work event | Production rates, economic periods, fractional accumulation, and capacity figures are not approved | Distinguish required amount 0, demand 0, saturated storage, and fractional remainder, and confirm there is no double consumption |
| Authority and performer | Player and NPC make requests with the same qualifications and authority | Facility authority designates a qualified person / contract assignment that a qualified person accepts | GP-05 and D04 linkage; no consent/authority contract | Recheck before execution upon disqualification, dismissal, detention, same-name confusion, or authority change |
| Cancel unused jobs | Clean up jobs that no longer need to be performed while explaining the loss | §6 A release immediately / B release after authority review | Cancellation authority and the check of actual use of the job are undecided | Do not use another job''s cumulative consumption as grounds for cancellation; preserve progress and ownership |
| Death of the assigned worker | Process the remaining work while protecting consumed and completed portions | §6 A release after confirmation / B hold, then reassign and clean up | World death/recheck and labor reservation linkage; no producer | Check against other persons'' deaths, stale rosters, duplicate results, and partial completion |
| GP-06 scope of choices | Use arrived stock on time and handle cargo identity, receipt, conflicts, and failures consistently | Common daily cutoff; on split, the parent retires and two children continue its history; recipient confirmation signature; reservation conflict is explained, then an explicit resubmission is proposed; simultaneous blockade and arrival inputs follow acceptance order; receipt failures are quarantined locally; undelivered reservations are retained | The seven directions are chosen. Interval unit, phase value, calendar origin, distribution method, persistent storage, receipt identification/authentication, loss/shortage coefficients, and source/native readiness remain undecided | Check immediately before/on time/immediately after, atomic preservation of parent and children, unsigned receipts, stale versions, simultaneous inputs, quarantine, and undelivered cargo against the chosen contract |
| Cancel demand/cargo | Clean up the remaining resources of each contract | On cancellation, release each contract''s remaining reservations / hold for recheck, then confirm | Technical rejection cases cannot determine the world''s cancellation policy | Leave construction job reservations untouched and preserve actual arrivals, existing consumption, and completions |
| Dismantling salvage | The player anticipates demolition losses | No salvage / return only unused, recoverable materials through dismantling work | Stage-by-stage salvage and ownership handover are undecided. The differing refund contexts of the two source works cannot be merged | Distinguish automatic refunds of already consumed inputs from the new output of dismantling work |

Calculations that depend on the GURPS 2004 Basic source text, which has not been obtained or authenticated, remain unresolved. Having written this document''s independent economic preservation scope does not mean the Basic-dependent parts or the entire domain are closed.

The two parts of the decision map `T68-NUMERIC-DESIGN-ASSUMPTIONS` are also separately unresolved. **seoul-assumptions** is a non-Basic numeric choice that sets Seoul-specific price, production rate, period, capacity, rounding, and priority coefficients; it is currently not approved and does not carry over prototype constants. **health-derived-assumptions** is the part of production and labor calculation derived from health and injury, and it is not closed until it passes both the GURPS 2004 Basic source text and Task25''s C01, C03, and C06 checks. The two parts are independent handoff items with identifiers different from GP-05 and GP-06, and neither is to be interpreted as a value of 0 or a prohibition policy.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d06', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d06', 'D06 Movement · Cargo · Maps · Observation', '', 'proposal', 113, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d06.md', '# D06 Movement · Cargo · Maps · Observation

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/travel-activities-and-landless/)

Status: Proposal. Distinguishes approved rules, chosen policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided figures. It does not mean implementation is complete. Recipient confirmation is a chosen policy, while an additional confirmation by the current custodian is an unadopted proposal. The cutoff is only a time qualification, not a final determination of available stock.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

## Actors and the World

- The player selects one of the surviving opening characters as their main body and continues the campaign with that single body. Screen transitions do not rewind time or duplicate the main body at a remote location.
- NPCs use the same movement, observation, and command contracts as the player. Being an NPC does not allow teleporting or knowing current facts without information.
- Units move along actual positions and routes. The map, commanders, squads, transport means, cargo, the cargo''s owner and custodian, and the evaluating party who judges reports are the actors of this domain.
- A name is a search tool for people to find records. The identity of a person, unit, cargo, or event selected from search results resolves to a permanent stable identifier. The same name does not mean the same entity, and a stable identifier does not forbid searching by name.
- There is one world time. Movement, cargo, and observation events share the progression of the same world.

## What the Player Decides

The player picks route candidates and attempts actual movement, sends strategic commands to distant targets, receives sourced reports and judges them, and makes arrived cargo usable according to verified receipt records. Which routes are lawful and passable, and who records a blockage and in what state, have not yet been decided. If a unit has actually arrived at an ongoing battle, that unit''s participation is handled. If the main body is a battle commander, valid commands and immediate signals can be sent only to squads that are within the command range registered in that battle session and are also on that session''s assigned squad list. Enemy forces, unassigned independent or allied forces, allied forces outside the command range, and other main bodies or other sites are not command targets.

Battle signals differ from strategic remote commands. Battles have no pause, speed control, or command queue. The main body''s squad leader may follow an immediate command, follow it only in part, or act autonomously, and the evaluating party remembers who sent the command and the result identification. The coefficients of this evaluation have not yet been decided.

## Eligibility to Act

### Movement

A unit must actually be at its departure location, and movement takes actual distance, means, load, fatigue, and route status as inputs. Changing the strategic screen cannot substitute for movement. However, route legality, impassability determination, preservation of blockage status, storage of remaining segments, information freshness, and exact settlement of failed movement are the unresolved scope of GP-02. Map lines, station markers, and the mere existence of a known track do not create adjacency or passage permission. Only physical adjacency to the actual next station, whose source, edition, and verification status have been confirmed, can become a route candidate. An express train skipping intermediate stations is not a movement edge, and service line, physical connection, and current passage status are distinguished. Unconfirmed connections are not displayed or used for movement. The current passability of a route candidate and the right of passage are checked separately. The GP-02 alternatives are limited to the following for route candidates meeting these conditions: checking the entire route before departure and rejecting it; approving only the known segments and stopping at the blocked point; or approving segment by segment and then re-proposing a detour.

### Strategic Remote Commands

A command is executed only if there is an actual communication channel and the target receives and accepts it. If any one of these is missing, the command remains unexecuted. An immediate signal sent to a battle squad leader is separate from this procedure and does not make strategic movement instantaneous.

### Observation and Reports

When the evaluating party sees something directly or receives a sourced report, that party''s knowledge and confirmation time are updated. A report is not itself the current fact of the world. An old report does not guarantee current position, and the absence of a report is not evidence that the region is empty or safe. The same fixed resource is not granted repeatedly by the same exploration, and merely reading a record does not duplicate proficiency.

### Cargo Receipt and Cutoff

Legal ownership and physical custody are separate. Even if ownership transfers, supplies in an enemy-held warehouse do not teleport into the inventory. Cargo in transit retains its asset identity and split origin, cannot be used before arrival, and is not counted simultaneously as available stock at both the origin and the destination. Institutional assets remain with the institution.

If the verified arrival time is equal to or earlier than the consumption cutoff time, that arrived cargo is included in the available quantity of that cutoff. At the same time, arrival is verified first, followed by reservation, production, and consumption. This is an approved economic and logistics rule that D05, D06, D13, and D18 consume together. Cargo that arrives after the cutoff is excluded **from that cutoff only**. Cargo is not permanently fixed as in transit, and may be judged again at the next applicable cutoff or receipt procedure.

GP-06 as a whole is still open, but the seven policies chosen by the owner are fixed separately. A cargo split retires the parent and creates two children that continue its origin, and receipt requires the recipient''s confirmation signature. A reservation conflict shows the reason for the conflict and a new path for submitting intent, and does not place the request in an automatic queue. The cutoff uses a common daily phase, and a blockade and an arrival at the same time follow the inflow approval order. Cargo that fails receipt verification is quarantined locally, and undelivered reservations are kept in a pending-evidence state. These choices do not approve the receipt identification protocol, the authentication method, clock and phase values, error limits, implementation, or canon adoption.

### Reinforcements

Any unit that actually arrives at a battle in progress may take part in it. Eligibility is not narrowed by origin, rank, the initial roster, or an arbitrarily created time window. A unit that arrives after the battle has ended does not retroactively join the finished battle. The arrival cadence, entry position, pre-command-transfer timing, and capacity formula have not been decided yet.

### Random Number Separation

The random-number purposes for movement, encounters, and observation are kept separate from those for battles, world events, and characters. The same starting value and the same choice record must be replayable, but the algorithm, seed encoding, draw order, and consumption conventions have not been decided yet.

## Before, Action, and After

**Before.** The unit is actually at the starting point of the route candidate, and the passage check for that candidate has not been completed yet. The remote unit is out of contact, and one cargo is in transit. The evaluating party has not directly seen the latest state of the destination.

**Action.** When the player proposes a movement, the unit''s actual position and the distance, means, load, fatigue, and route state are read. Until GP-02 decides route legality and how blockages are handled, no definitive result is produced. An approved movement must leave real route progress behind and is not completed by a screen transition. A strategic order sent to a remote unit is not executed until the contact route, receipt, and acceptance are in place. When a sourced report arrives, only the evaluating party''s knowledge and confirmation time are updated. If cargo is verified and arrives at the same time as the deadline, it is included in that deadline; if it arrives after the deadline, it is excluded from that deadline. A unit that actually arrives at a battle in progress may take part in it.

**After.** Only a unit that has finished an approved, actual movement is at the new position. Until GP-02 is closed, this document does not fix the exact remaining position or remaining progress in blockage cases. A remote order that was not executed remains unexecuted. A report has a source and a confirmation time and is distinguished from world facts. The quantity, ownership, custody, and source identity of arrived cargo are preserved. Late cargo remains in a state where it can be used in the next determination.

## Failure and Interruption

- The settlement of route legality, blockages, and failed movements is decided by GP-02. Until it is approved, none of the following is applied as a fixed rule: remaining at the origin, stopping at the blocked point, or preserving the remaining segment.
- A strategic order without a contact route, receipt, and acceptance is not executed.
- A handover that points to the wrong asset or receipt record is not applied to that cargo.
- Arrival after the deadline only excludes the cargo from that deadline; it does not destroy the cargo or leave it permanently stuck in transit.
- A unit that arrives at a finished battle does not take part in that battle, but the unit itself continues to exist as it is.
- Even if a world action is canceled or an actor dies, progress that has already been completed and resources that have already been consumed are not recreated or restored. The settlement of the remaining in-progress steps is decided by the relevant action, life, asset, and time contracts.
- Save-version conflicts and tampered re-transmissions of the same event are rejected at the technical boundary. This is not a universal game rule that rewinds every world action to before the action.

## Boundaries of Information, Ownership, Command, and War

A map marking is route data; it does not replace the passage determination of GP-02, which is still undecided. A report is not the whole current world, and ownership is not physical arrival. Item rights are recorded separately from position and command authority.

If the main body is a battle commander, it can send valid orders and immediate signals only to those squads that are both within the command range registered in that battle session and on that session''s attached squad list. Enemy forces, unattached independent or allied forces, allied forces outside the command range, and other main bodies or other sites are not command targets. Squad leader autonomy and order-result evaluation are valid immediately. To start a war, a registered valid casus belli is required, binding the target territory, eligible beneficiary, objective, and ending effect. War score ranges from −100 to +100, and the opponent''s score has the opposite sign. When it reaches +100 or −100, the validity of the referenced casus belli and the eligibility of the beneficiary whose name is fixed in that casus belli are re-checked first. If the casus belli is invalid, no groundless forced transfer is created and the relevant invalidity rule is applied. If the casus belli is valid, the end cannot be postponed by accumulating response delays, and the war is forcibly ended within the effects of that casus belli and the fixed war objective scope. A victorious commander or occupier does not automatically acquire the objective rights in place of the beneficiary named in the casus belli, and territory or rights outside the objective are not automatically transferred either. Item-by-item custom negotiation is allowed, but an unregistered item price blocks signing, and an offer is not automatically accepted merely because its value is at or below the war score.

## Meaningful Examples

1. **Blocked Railway Policy Comparison.** The map shows a track and a collapse report. A track marking alone does not make a route candidate or a passage permit; the physical connection to the verified, actual next station and the current passability and right of passage are checked separately. Under the actual-movement principle, a unit cannot get to the other side by a screen transition alone. However, whether to refuse at the origin, to proceed to the blocked point, and in which record to leave the cause, risk, and recovery conditions are GP-02 alternatives, and this example does not approve them.
2. **Late Cargo.** The deadline is world time 480, and the cargo arrived at 481. This cargo is not included in the 480 deadline, but it keeps its actual position and identity until the next applicable deadline.
3. **Simultaneous Arrival.** The cargo was verified at 480, the same time as the deadline. Reservation, production, and consumption proceed after arrival verification, so the cargo can be used in that deadline. 480 is an example for explaining the boundary, not an approved day length or transport speed.
4. **Battle Reinforcement.** A unit that was not on the initial roster actually arrived while the battle was in progress. It may take part regardless of origin and rank. If it arrived after the battle ended, it does not take part in that battle.
5. **Name Search.** The player searches for two people with the same name. Both appear in the list, and after selection each record is resolved by a different stable identifier.
6. **Cancellation and Consumption.** After part of the food had already been consumed during travel preparation, the plan was canceled. Cancellation does not restore the food that was eaten, nor does it erase the completed preparation progress. Only the remaining steps are halted and settled according to the relevant contract.

## Current Evidence Confirmed from the Two Source Games

### Crusader Kings 3

In the preserved data of installed version 1.20.0.3, build 25652598, defined values such as `MOVEMENT_SPEED=3`, retreat speed `4.5`, friendly territory bonus `0.2`, and movement lock `0.5` can be confirmed, but the full travel-time formula cannot. The AI employer''s movement preparation computes `Q=distance/300` from the squared distance to the capital, and when current provisions are less than Q, it **adds** Q and then starts the journey together with the residence. This is neither a general consumption formula nor a shortfall-replenishment formula. The conditions and completion events of activity-start waiting, guest delay, and transport arrival have been confirmed, but Seoul''s exact deadline-comparison formula and same-time ordering have not. Cost and risk unit conflicts in the preserved wiki were also left unresolved. These values are not Seoul''s figures.

### Noland

Data from different versions were not merged as if they were a single version. The road''s dirt path `+20%` and stone path `+40%` come from wiki revision 1385, and nectar `+30%/36 hours` comes from resource wiki revision 1129. The removal of the nectar movement bonus is from the **Patches 46-48 (MAIN)** announcement. The same announcement explains that if a raider squad''s natural travel time is shorter than 24 hours, it is made to stand still until the total travel time is at least 24 hours, but the exact waiting arrangement and the equality condition at the boundary time have not been confirmed. It also says that only **crime-scene markers** are shown in the king''s vision for the following 24 hours, and this is not to be read as a universal validity period for all observation information. Movement between buildings `−75%`, the road exception, and elderly movement `−15%` are from the **Patches 54-56 (MAIN)** announcement. The trade availability `11 AM` is from trade wiki revision 1275, and the historical parameter capture is build 25211575. The distance unit, the load-speed coupling formula, and the remaining-segment accumulator have not been confirmed. These revisions, announcements, and historical captures are separate document boundaries and are not authoritative for the current world state, and the original figures are not Seoul''s figures either.

The data from the two games serve only as comparison evidence and do not approve Seoul''s single cargo identity preservation, arrival-first settlement, or war and command locks on its behalf.

## Options Not Yet Approved

Each item records its actual use, ideal completed state, current gap, reason it is open, and QA together. The GP-06 policy that has already been chosen is excluded from the alternatives below, and this document does not approve the remaining alternatives.

1. **Route Legality, Blockage, and Failure Settlement.** Premise: map lines, station markers, and the known existence of track alone do not create an adjacent connection or passage permit. Only the physical adjacent connection to the actual next station, whose source, version, and verification status have been confirmed, can become a route candidate. An express train''s service that skips intermediate stations is not a movement edge, and the service line, physical connection, and current passage status are distinguished. Unconfirmed connections are not displayed or used for movement. The current passability and right of passage of a route candidate are checked separately. Use: whether a movement proposal is accepted and the state after interruption. Ideal: determine route authority, blockage, remaining progress, and information time in a replayable way while upholding the actual-movement principle. Alternatives: (A) full-route check and refusal before departure, (B) approve only the known segments and stop at the blocked point, (C) per-segment approval along the route and re-proposal of a detour. Gap/reason: GP-02 owns this policy and no choice has been made yet. QA: do not create movement edges or passage permits from unconfirmed connections, station markers, or track geometry; no screen-transition teleportation; distinguish stale reports from actual blockages; remaining progress matches before and after saving.
2. **Personal Movement Formula.** Use: arrival estimation and saving the remaining segment. Ideal: a verifiable formula that uses distance, means, load, fatigue, and route together. Alternatives: (A) discrete per-segment cost, (B) continuous movement rate and remaining distance, (C) standard timetable by means followed by load and fatigue adjustment. Gap/reason: without the GURPS Basic source text, the Basic-dependent numbers cannot be closed. QA: reproducibility for the same input, no arrival on unapproved routes, remaining progress matches before and after saving and loading.
3. **Strategic Message Details.** Use: recipient, acceptor, duplicate orders, delegation expiry. Ideal: an explicit procedure that preserves real contact and NPC judgment. Alternatives: (A) the most recently accepted order takes priority, (B) keep dispatch order, (C) on conflict, treat as a negotiation state. Gap/reason: all that has been approved is the contact path, receipt, and the need for acceptance. QA: no execution on a severed contact; determinism for duplicate and expired cases after reconnection.
4. **GP-06 Remaining Details.** Use: receipt identification, authentication, error limits, and the actual time of the shared daily phase. Ideal: complete the seven already-selected policies within quantity conservation and the separation of ownership and custody. Alternatives: receipt identification via (A) a separate receipt event ID, (B) a composite key of cargo ID and receipt version, (C) a pair of signature event and cargo reference. Error handling via (A) per-item resubmission, (B) resubmission in batches by cause, (C) resubmission by verification stage. Gap/reason: the owner selection exists, but the full GP-06, the authentication method, the clock and phase values, the error limits, and the implementation and canon adoption remain open. QA: quantity conservation before and after splitting, no use before signing, no consumption of quarantined cargo, retention of undelivered reservations.
5. **Reinforcement Session.** Use: battlefield entry and command handover. Ideal: a procedure that does not undermine "any unit that actually arrives may take part." Alternatives: (A) enter at the next battle update point, (B) enter immediately at a safe edge, (C) the commander chooses the entry point. Gap/reason: there is no cycle, geometry, handover, or capacity count. QA: confirm that no filters on origin, rank, or initial roster arise.
6. **Remaining Total Order at the Same Time.** Use: event conflicts that remain after first applying the economic phase (reserve, produce, consume after arrival verification) and the inflow approval order for blockade and arrival at the same time. Ideal: preserve these two approval orders as they are while sorting only the remaining events in a replayable way. Alternatives: after applying the two approval orders, (A) process the remaining events in causal-event order, (B) process the remaining events by domain phase and stable identifier order, (C) process the remaining events in input-receipt order. Gap/reason: the full ordering of events remaining after the two approval orders is unapproved. QA: preservation of arrival verification → reservation → production → consumption, preservation of the blockade and arrival inflow approval order, identical results when replaying the remaining same-time events, no multiplication from duplicate arrivals.

There are no approved Seoul values for movement speed, distance conversion, load and fatigue adjustment, information staleness period, exploration probability, message time, cost and success formula, or reinforcement cycle, geometry, handover, and capacity. The absence of Original Basic is not marked as resolved.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d07', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d07', 'D07 Relationships, Memory, Loyalty, and Evaluation — Design Document for Readers', '', 'proposal', 114, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d07.md', '# D07 Relationships, Memory, Loyalty, and Evaluation — Design Document for Readers

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/relationships-houses-and-intrigue/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

This document explains the approved scope of relationships, memory, loyalty, and command evaluation. Unadopted mechanisms and values remain undetermined.

## 1. What This Document Covers

D07 owns three things. First, the trust and memory of a character who has actually come to know something. Second, loyalty to a lord, which is a separate state. Third, the record of compliance and performance for a single order that a squad leader received in battle. What relationships do not create — the establishment of command authority, property rights, consent, right of passage, or marriage — lies outside this document, and Chapters 8 and 9 draw that boundary precisely.

The reading order is as follows. Chapter 2 covers the actors, Chapter 3 the decisions the player actually makes, and Chapter 4 eligibility. Chapters 5 and 6 are the structure of actions and the already-approved states and formulas, and Chapters 7 and 8 are the boundaries of failure, preservation, information, and authority. Chapter 9 covers cross-domain ownership, Chapter 10 four concrete cases, Chapter 11 external references (Crusader Kings III, Norland), Chapter 12 open issues and decision-ready alternatives, and Chapter 13 a summary of the boundary between what is settled and what is open.

## 2. Actors

**Player (the Embodied Character).** A single player carries the campaign forward as a character with one body. The embodied character uses the same action contract as the other characters in the world, and gains no authority outside the contract by virtue of being the embodied character.

**NPCs.** All the remaining characters in the world. They use the same action contract as the embodied character. NPCs, too, can hold, refuse, or negotiate an order. They do not rewind time by switching screens, nor create a second copy of the same body in a distant place.

**Squad Leader.** The role that receives a superior commander''s orders in battle instantly as a signal. The embodied character may be the squad leader, and an NPC may be the squad leader. Receiving an order does not lock input or force the order to be executed.

**Superior Commander (Sender).** The character who issues battle orders. The result of evaluating an order goes back to this commander''s character, and this commander builds up memory about the executing squad''s body and the basis for later assignment and trust judgments.

**Evaluator (Observer / Report Recipient).** A character who has actually seen something directly or learned of it through a report. At the moment they come to know it, they update only their own trust and their own memory. They do not correct anyone else''s trust on their behalf.

**Lord.** The object of the loyalty state. Each hero has a separate loyalty value toward their own lord, and the lord''s own loyalty is always at the maximum.

**States, institutions, and systems (owners of setting facts).** The country-specific systems that determine the establishment and termination of marriage, oaths, and command relationships are facts of the world setting. A relationship fact that has already been validly established and stored is not deleted or downgraded to an unestablished record just because the system input is undetermined. However, if a request to establish a new relationship is made and it is not sufficiently clear which system applies, or what the basis for establishment under that system is, the request is left as `Jedo Mijeong/Hwagin Geungeo Bujok` (system undetermined / insufficient basis for confirmation). This document does not invent that system.

## 3. Decisions the Player Actually Makes

**Three-Way Choice on a Battle Order.** If the embodied character is the squad leader, they receive the superior commander''s battle order instantly as a system signal, without the travel time of a messenger or radio. If the embodied character is the superior commander, they directly select squads that fall within the **intersection of the registered command scope of that battle session and the list of currently assigned squads**, and issue movement, stance, and attack orders. Allied forces, independent units, and enemy forces outside the registered scope are not command targets even if they are visible on screen. A subordinate squad leader, immediately after receiving an order, also runs the same feasibility check and exercises autonomous judgment. There is no pause, speed control, or order queue in battle. After receiving the order, they (1) follow it as is, (2) carry out only part of it, or (3) act on their own. These three branches are the decisions the player actually makes in this document. This instant delivery applies only to battle orders, and does not make distant contact on the strategic map or character movement instantaneous.

**Handling of Learned Facts.** When the embodied character sees something directly or learns of it through a report, their own trust and their own memory are updated at the moment they learn of it. This update is a result, not a choice. The choices come before it — what to watch, whose reports to receive, and what to conceal. A concealed betrayal does not change anyone''s trust until another person learns of it.

**Record of Holding, Refusal, and Negotiation.** Conditions that make it impossible to carry out right now, such as safety, physiology, or detention, come first. When needs, goals, or an already-set schedule collide with an order, it is recorded as holding, refusal, or negotiation. An order does not erase that person''s personal goals. The player reads this record and decides the next action.

## 4. Eligibility

**One Must Know in Order to Evaluate.** The person evaluating must actually know about the matter. The approach of instantly knowing a hidden betrayal from somewhere in the world is not used. Coming to know an event through observation or reports is the condition for a relationship update. The attribution of responsibility for a report and the weighting of its evidence have not yet been determined.

**Trust Does Not Create Command Authority.** Whether one can issue an order does not arise from a trust value. The type of command authority, its direction, the scope of delegation, the contact that actually reaches, and the target''s state are examined separately. Looking someone up or opening a conversation does not create command authority. High trust also does not make a physically impossible task succeed. High trust does not create property, consent, or right of passage either.

**Inability to Perform Takes Priority.** Even for the same body, conditions that make it impossible to carry out right now, such as safety, physiology, or detention, come first. These conditions block eligibility to execute an order; they are not an evaluation of that person''s will.

**Record of Order Receipt.** When a battle order is received, seven items are recorded together — world time, superior order identifier, sender commander, target squad, purpose, permitted scope, and version. Only with this record is it possible to avoid duplicate application of the same result and to re-check after a change of commander.

## 5. Structure of Actions: Before / Action / After

### 5.1 Transitions of a Battle Order

Order issued → immediate receipt (seven fields recorded) → choose follow / partial execution / independent action → actual execution → per-order evaluation → reflected in the commander''s memory and relationships. No new battle order queue is created; only the current issued/executing state and the update history are kept. The same order evaluation is never applied twice by result identifier.

### 5.2 Update Transitions of Observation and Reports

Event occurs (record of actor, target, harm/benefit, witness/report, secrecy scope, and evidence) → the evaluator comes to know it through observation or a report → at that moment, only their own trust and their own memory are updated → the updated facts are read again as input to later action judgments.

### 5.3 Four Canonical Scenes

The approved scope of this document becomes clearest in four scenes. Chapter 10 revisits these four scenes as worked cases.

**The Same Report Arrives Twice.** The same result identifier is applied only once. An evaluation that arrives again produces no second change. Resending the same event identifier does not become a new result.

**Late Discovery of a Secret.** If a betrayal that Eul concealed was not seen directly by Gap and no report reached Gap, Gap''s trust does not change. That the matter becomes public reputation and that Gap suffered direct harm are different records. No fixed national character or betrayal probability is assigned.

**Personal Trust Is Not Copied to a Successor.** The personal trust of the deceased is not copied to the successor. The new body starts from the personal relationships it already had. The previous character''s contracts, institutional obligations, and public history are each carried over by their own succession policies.

**Impossible Orders and Defied Orders.** An order that cannot be carried out, and an order that became void after the issuing commander died, are execution failures. They are not deliberate defiance. Even if independent action produced a good result, compliance and performance remain separate records. Disobedience does not automatically reduce trust by a fixed amount.

## 6. States and Already-Approved Formulas

### 6.1 Directional Trust T

Relationships between characters are read as trust, `T[A,B]`. The value with which A trusts B and the value with which B trusts A are separate. That A trusts B does not mean B trusts A at the same value. There is no separate affection value. The bands are as follows.

- `T ≥ 25` — Friend
- `T ≤ −25` — Enemy
- `T ≤ −50` — Archenemy

The classification thresholds are fixed, but each character''s trust values and the resulting classifications can change after events. The archenemy condition overlaps with the enemy condition, so an archenemy satisfies both. Which marker to show first on screen is undecided. The classification itself does not force any particular action. The upper and lower bounds of `T` itself and the per-event change amounts have not been approved (Chapter 12, item 1).

### 6.2 Relationship Facts Are Not Numbers

Family, marriage, romance, teacher and student, contracts, debts, oaths, and command are actual relationship facts, not trust values. A record alone does not establish a marriage, and which country''s marriage system applies is not decided here. When someone dies, the survivors'' family and romantic relationships and mourning events remain. Relationship modifiers are not decided here.

### 6.3 Loyalty

Loyalty to a ruler is a separate state from `0` to `100`. It reads the day''s mood and the trust toward the ruler, `T[hero, ruler]`. The ruler''s own loyalty is always `100`. If loyalty stays below `25`, it can open the way to a succession rebellion or to other heroes leaving. However, this is not a rebellion that necessarily happens every day. How long it must persist before the way opens, how much it changes per day, and which risky actions cross the threshold are not decided (Chapter 12, item 1). Accepting risky duties reads relationships as a decision input, but the specific permission threshold based on loyalty is undecided.

### 6.4 Distinct Records

Life-need fulfillment and mood, loyalty, and trust are distinct records. Shortages are not converted into decreases in loyalty or trust without cause. Which event changes which record and by how much requires approval of per-event change amounts (Chapter 12, item 1).

### 6.5 Stored Facts

The following are stored: peerage and office history, rights disputes, the fulfillment and breach of promises, captivity, injury, and death, marriage and kinship, education progress, disclosed evidence, property and debts, truce and war outcomes, witnessing and memory, and current trust. Later actions read these facts again. Loading a save and switching bodies do not reset these records.

### 6.6 Permanent Storage Is Not Permanent Fixation

Permanent storage does not mean every emotion is fixed forever. Effects with recovery, reconciliation, changes in mourning, or an expiry period are stored together with their rules and end times. The forgetting and decay function is pending confirmation. The world record of hidden facts and the facts each character knows are stored separately.

### 6.7 Sources and Records of Relationship Events

The sources of relationship events are spending time together, treatment and rescue, contract fulfillment or breach, gifts and trade, public support and insults, exposure, detention and release, and marriage, adoption, and death. For each event, the actor, target, harm/benefit, witness/report, secrecy scope, and basis are recorded. Modifiers use confirmed rules, and if undecided, no arbitrary number is entered.

### 6.8 Boundaries of a Vacant Office

When an office becomes vacant, only that office''s signing, command, and operation stop. The personal life and movement of living people and separately valid contracts are judged under each party''s own capacity. A vacancy does not cause property to be transferred, a successor to be determined, or a deputy to be appointed. Succession, body, office, and property are different outcomes.

### 6.9 Inputs to Order Evaluation

The evaluation inputs are the order''s purpose, time of receipt, feasibility, squad state, the main body''s actual actions, contribution to the objective, losses, civilian harm, and changes/cancellations by a higher order. The evaluation result is attributed to the commanding character and becomes the basis for the memory of that squad''s body and for later assignment and trust decisions. The relationship-change function, numbers, and promotion/discipline conditions are undecided, and no fixed trust penalty is automatically applied for disobedience.

## 7. Failure and Preservation

The following are preserved even amid failure and interruption.

1. The same result identifier is applied only once. An evaluation that arrives again does not produce a second change.
2. An order that cannot be carried out, and a directive that becomes void after the issuing commander dies, are execution failures. They are not deliberate defiance.
3. If death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the scheduled work is checked again. Supplies already spent and progress already made are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released according to the contract.
4. Rights changes filed at the same time are judged against a single ledger version, and conflicts are resolved by a set order of acceptance.
5. When the commander changes, existing orders and evaluations remain with their original sender and history intact, and are checked again under the new commander''s authority.
6. Even when trust changes, contracts, debts, oaths, marriages, offices, and property remain as they are. Even when an office is vacant, only that office''s signing, command, and operation stop, and personal property, peerage rights, and other valid contracts are not transferred automatically.
7. Current information is obtained through observation and reports, and is stored together with its time. The same information is not produced repeatedly as a fixed resource reward.
8. Replaying with the same starting values and the same choice record must yield the same world state and the same event record. The randomness of combat, encounters, the world, and characters are separate streams. The random numbers that pick names use only the character stream. The processing order of one stream does not change the results of another stream. Relationship judgments do not disturb the results of other streams.

Names are used for search, and the permanent character identifier is used to confirm whether it is the same character despite homonyms or name changes. Character and event identifiers are permanent. Explicit version conflicts are rejected as errors, and there is no automatic renumbering, automatic transfer of authority, or silent mixing of rules.

## 8. Boundaries of Information and Authority

**What is not known does not change.** The evaluating subject updates its own trust and memory only at the moment it learns of an event through observation or reports. There is no mechanism by which the world instantly knows of a hidden betrayal. Direct harm and public reputation are distinguished, and no fixed personality or betrayal probability is assigned to a state.

**A relationship is a decision input, not authority.** A relationship is a decision input for accepting an interview, accepting risky duties, supporting a successor, accepting a teacher, testifying, and for targets of protection, concealment, defection, and conspiracy. High trust does not create command authority, property rights, consent, or right of passage that do not exist.

**A contract does not end by itself when a relationship changes.** Even when a relationship changes, an existing contract changes only through its termination clauses and action events. That a friend has become an enemy does not automatically void all trades and unit assignments at that moment. For a contract to end, it needs that contract''s own termination conditions and a later action.

**What death does not erase.** When someone dies, the survivors'' family/romantic relationships and mourning events remain. Only the deceased''s personal trust is not copied to the successor.

## 9. Cross-Domain Ownership

This domain covers the memory and trust of people one knows, loyalty to the ruler, and the record of compliance and performance for a single order. The following are not decided here.

- **Orders and Schedules (D03).** The procedures for drafting and delivering orders and reconciling them with schedules belong to D03. This document only states that a squad leader who receives a combat directive may follow it, follow only part of it, or act independently, and that when needs and schedules collide, the result is left as deferral, refusal, or negotiation.
- **Observation Time (D06).** When information is observed and reported belongs to D06. This document takes in only that it updates its own trust and memory solely at the moment of learning, and that information is stored together with its time and not paid out repeatedly as a fixed resource.
- **Succession and Body Transfer (Succession Domain).** The procedures for succession and body transfer, and who the new body is, belong to that domain. What this document decides is that the deceased''s personal trust is not copied to the successor.
- **Combat Signals and Combat Calculation (Combat Domain).** Individual attacks, defense, damage, injury, and the allocation of losses belong to the combat domain. This document covers only the receipt of signals, the separation of compliance and performance, and the ban on applying the same result twice. Until the calculation of individual combat power, health, fatigue, and derived values is decided, evaluation results do not newly allocate injuries or losses.
- **Random Stream Separation (Save and Replay Domain).** Stream separation itself belongs to the save and replay domain. This document takes in only the rule that relationship judgments do not disturb the results of other streams.
- **National Systems (D09 Series).** The national systems that determine how marriage, oaths, and command relationships are established and ended belong to the state and institutions domain. While a country''s system is unknown, such a relationship cannot be established from recorded facts alone.

## 10. Four Concrete Cases (Design State Transitions)

The four cases below show what changes and what remains in play. Because change amounts, decay, and durations have not been approved, no resulting numbers are written anywhere in the cases, and only approved band judgments and state transitions are computed.

### Case A — Report-Based Evaluation of a Convoy Contract Breach

- **Before.** A convoy contract exists between A and B. B''s `T[B,A]` is stored at some value. The contract state is valid.
- **Action.** A breaches the contract. The event record holds the actor A, target B, harm/benefit, witness/report, secrecy scope, and basis. At time `t1`, when B learns of this through a report, B''s trust `T[B,A]` and memory are updated. This alone does not change A''s own `T[A,B]` to the same value. If A also knows of the event, A''s own-direction evaluation is handled separately.
- **After.** The friend, enemy, and archenemy classifications change only if the updated `T[B,A]` value ends up crossing a band. Because the update amount has not been approved, this case confirms only the conditional that "if the value falls to `−25` or below, enemy." The contract is not canceled by a trust change alone, and awaits that contract''s own termination conditions and follow-up actions. Even if A''s successor C becomes the main body, B''s personal trust toward C is separate, and the previous character''s contracts, institutional obligations, and public history follow their respective succession policies; no new succession policy is chosen here.
- **Basis for calculation.** The bands `25 / −25 / −50` (6.1), directionality (6.1), update at the time of observation (5.2), contract preservation (item 6 of Section 7), and no copying to the successor (5.3).

### Case B — Combat Directive, Independent Action, and the Same Result Arriving Twice

- **Before.** The main body Gap is the squad leader. There is not yet a combat order from the superior commander Byeong (Cmd).
- **Action.** Byeong''s order arrives immediately as a signal, and the receipt record holds the world time, the superior order identifier, the issuing commander, the target squad, the purpose, the permitted scope, and the version. Gap acts independently. Even if that action produces a good result, compliance (did not follow) and performance (positive) are left as two separate records.
- **After.** The evaluation result is attributed to the person of Commander Byeong. It becomes the basis for the memory held in Gap''s person and for later assignment and trust judgments. Disobedience does not automatically reduce trust by a fixed amount. If evaluation result identifier `R` reaches the system twice, the second arrival is not applied. Delivering the same evaluation again is not a new evaluation. A new, independent action is judged as a separate outcome.
- **Basis for calculation.** The seven fields of the receipt record (Chapter 4), the three-way choice (Chapter 3), the separation of compliance and performance (6.9), attribution (6.9), idempotency (5.3, item 1 of 7), reproducibility (item 8 of 7).

### Case C — Late Discovery of a Hidden Betrayal

- **Before.** At time `t0`, Eul hides a betrayal. Gap did not see it directly and received no report of it. Gap''s `T[Gap,Eul]` does not change. The hidden fact is stored separately in the world record.
- **Action.** At time `t2`, Gap receives a report. Only at that point are Gap''s trust and memory updated. Judgments Gap made about Eul between `t0` and `t2` were based on information from before the update, so they are not reversed.
- **After.** The record by which the incident becomes public reputation and the record of damage Gap suffered directly are different. If another person, Jeong (丁), does not know this fact, Jeong''s trust does not change either. Nothing like a betrayal probability fixed to a state appears anywhere in this chain.
- **Basis for calculation.** Observation gate (Chapters 4 and 8), time-point update (5.2), distinction between public reputation and direct damage (Chapter 8), separate storage of hidden facts (6.6), prohibition of fixed state-wide character (Chapter 8).

### Case D — Impossible Order, Defied Order, and Commander''s Death

- **Before.** Squad leader Mu (E) receives directives in two different scenes. This is not an example of stacking two orders in a queue. One is an order that cannot be carried out in the current state (such as a movement requiring a controlled passage). The other can be carried out, but Mu decides not to follow it.
- **Action.** The first order is classified as an execution failure. Inability conditions such as safety, physiology, and confinement take priority, and this is not defiance. The second order is classified as willful disobedience. As a separate event, if the issuing commander dies after giving an order but before it is executed, the directive''s current validity is checked again. A directive invalidated by that death remains an execution failure. It does not unconditionally turn every existing order void.
- **After.** The two classifications become different records, and the "feasibility" item in the evaluation input reads this classification. Supplies already used and progress already completed are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released according to the contract. When a commander is replaced, existing orders and evaluations keep their original sender and history and are re-checked under the new commander''s authority.
- **Basis for calculation.** Priority of inability (Chapter 4), the two sources of execution failure (5.3, item 2 of 7), preservation of resources and progress (item 3 of 7), re-check upon commander replacement (item 5 of 7).

## 11. Comparative Evidence Confirmed from the Two Source Games

The source games'' opinion, mood, loyalty, and relationships are not the same variables as Seoul''s trust. The following is the extent confirmed from preserved documents and game-file declarations, and does not mean that actual engine execution was verified. Source-game figures are comparison values and are not adopted as Seoul coefficients.

### Crusader Kings III

The game files are based on version 1.20.0.3, build 25652598, checksum 8e45. No branch has been confirmed. The separate wiki pages are of different versions: Family 34883 (classification guide), Family (relation) 34905 (1.10), Government 36619, Laws 36572, Religion 36691, and Culture 36509 (1.20), and Power sharing 36540 (1.19).

- The standard success outcome of Sway declares, after the survival check and the non-Persian branch, a target→actor opinion of +25, a modifier accumulation cap of 100, and a monthly decay of 0.05. Other success outcomes and the Persian branch differ. Because accumulation exists, this cannot be read as universal deduplication of the same report.
- The memory of a close family member''s death evaluates its conditions based on the **memory owner**, and the owner and the deceased are separate. Its duration is declared as `5 + 125·R + 25·D + 25·PR + 25·PF + 25·PW + 195·PP` years. `R` is 1 if the memory owner, or any of the owner''s close family or spouse, is a ruler, and 0 otherwise. `D` is 1 if the memory owner''s highest held title tier (`highest_held_title_tier`) is duke or above. The range is not changed by the deceased''s title tier. `PR` is 1 if there is any rival relationship between the memory owner and the player, and it is not a condition exclusive to nemesis. `PF` is the player being a friend. `PW` is 1 if, among the wards connected to the memory owner by `any_relation type=ward`, at least one is the player. `PP` is the condition that the memory owner or a parent is the player. Each Boolean term is added only once even when multiple persons qualify. It is not of the form "base memory 10 years," nor a separate preservation for a deceased owner, nor a +1000 years preservation for a player character. The memory retention period is not opinion decay. For example, if the memory owner is a count and the deceased close family member is a ruling duke, then when all other player conditions are false, `R=1, D=0`, so it is `5+125=130` years, not `155`. Conversely, if the memory owner is a duke, `D=1` even when the deceased is not a ruler or is below duke rank.
- Relationship contributions, in the owner→target direction, are friend +60, best friend +120, rival -60, nemesis -120, mentor +20, and student +30. The reciprocal correspondence of friend and rival, and of mentor and student, does not mean the opposite-direction opinions are equal. There is no corresponding declaration for grudges. This declaration alone does not mean every relationship is permanent.
- Murder discovery records knowledge, then checks blocking conditions and sends a notice. The notice event itself has no numeric opinion effect, but exposure is separate, and a surviving non-self exposer receives an owner→exposer opinion effect of -30 for 20 years. Knowledge, notice, and public exposure are distinct. known_murder blocks only some branches and does not prove general report deduplication.
- The regent loyalty score, when a ruler exists, reads opinion, relationship, personality, position, culture, empathy, conditional fear, obedience, override, and domestic power in order. The visible thresholds 75/25 are not a basis for Seoul''s loyalty range of 0..100. The wiki''s Selfless summary and the game file''s ordered exceptions are not merged into a single same-version rule.
- The hostage story''s event interval of 180..365 days, nullification of 40..60 days, adult outcome of 1..3 days, and adventurer retention of 1 day are separate declarations. Death ends the story, and continued delivery has a non-imprisonment condition. The commented-out -10..10 display is not an actual limit, and the weights do not prove a probability distribution.
- fill_coffers eligibility is Stewardship + 0.5·Learning + 0.5·Intrigue + 20·Stubborn + 20·Ruthless - 20·Generous - 20·Greedy, with independent conditions added. The function specifies no explicit upper or lower bound. Eligibility differs from actual job performance and responsibility.
- Ally summons check eligibility and war conditions first, and apply the refusal outcome when the war still exists. The refusing actor→recipient opinion is -20 for 10 years if on the attacking side, and otherwise -50 for 25 years, and government, blood-brother, and contract outcomes exist separately. An expired war, an impossible request, and a deliberate refusal are not the same case.
- Succession-related declarations include a positive predecessor scale of 0.25, a negative scale of 0.5, a 5-year modifier, and a conditional succession post-hook and regent→heir opinion. The post-hook duration formula is 21 minus the heir''s age, but the caller comment says the 20th birthday. This discrepancy is preserved. A new succession effect is not said to be the same as copying the deceased''s personal trust.

The family documents separate marriage acceptance from lineage and approval. The divorce opinion of -25 for 50 years and the close family opinion of -5 for 5 years are durations in the documents, not confirmed decay curves. Government''s collective House Unity and position eligibility are not personal loyalty, and Laws'' permission conditions are not automatic protection or consent. Power sharing separates eligibility from the outcomes of distributing and ignoring interests. Religion''s directional religious opinion and marriage permission, and Culture''s collective acceptance and individual marriage acceptance, are different. The adoption piety cost of 250 in the Religion wiki and the game file''s 150 in another domain are comparison contexts of separate authority and are not the D07 opinion formula.

### Norland

The preservation evidence is a mix of official release notices, pinned revisions of the publisher-hosted community wiki, and historical stored values. A wiki revision is not a game build, and main and beta notices are kept separate. The stored values of historical build 25211575 are not merged into the current formula because patch correspondence and branch are unconfirmed.

- Mood 0..100, thoughts from events, desires, and needs, and the effect in the direction from the slighted person to the slighting person are documented (N-D07-01/02). They are not the same as Seoul''s trust axis.
- The historical loyalty explanation for Lords 1314 says that it reads daily mood and opinion of the king, that the king is 100, and that if loyalty stays below 25 for a long time, a rebellion or defection path opens. Population 1316 describes loyalty 0 and unfriendly conditions. They have not been reconciled into a single rebellion condition in the same build. There is also an explanation in main40 that replaced the lord''s single-variable loyalty with relationships and followers, and soldier loyalty is separate (N-D07-03/04/05).
- main40 contains both the statement that the marriage relationship bonus fades over 20 years and the statement that the Spouse thought ends 20 days after marriage (N-D07-06). Whether they are separate effects or an inconsistency in one effect is unresolved. Neither duration is chosen as Seoul decay.
- beta54 and main54-56 state that a usurper receives the thought about the new ruler with the opposite sign of the relationship with the former ruler (N-D07-07/21). The identity of the actor in that wording is unresolved, and it is not arbitrarily replaced with an observer or the new king. The main -20..+20 limit (N-D07-08) is the range of that thought, not Seoul''s trust range.
- The victory loyalty probability change from 35% to 10% and the +1 percentage point per Command level (N-D07-09), and the halved frequency of political actions and sermons by player-faction politicians with a ruler relationship above 0 (N-D07-10), are local conditions of each notice. They are not merged to mean the same loyalty variable even after the replacement of lord loyalty.
- An unhappy lord with a weak will refuses most missions (N-D07-11). Inability to move is a physical inability (N-D07-12). To delegate to an ally, the receiving ally''s relationship toward the politician must satisfy `opinion[ally, politician] > allianceThreshold + 20` (N-D07-13, beta51). `allianceThreshold` itself varies by personality and edition, so it is not replaced with a fixed value here. A persuasion attempt is possible when the relationship with the threatening lord is ≥0 (N-D07-14). Eligibility to accept is not the same as success in carrying it out.
- The supervisor activity check''s prisoner productivity bonus of 10%→20% (N-D07-15) and the historical stored values 0.03/1.045/1.2 (N-D07-16) are not a general performance-evaluation formula. The exponent and units of the stored values are unconfirmed.
- The relationship display runs from the character to the selected target, and the invert toggle changes the description (N-D07-17). Witnessing a squad''s annihilation adds a thought, but no formulas for radius, confidence, late discovery, or deduplication are given (N-D07-18). A rumor in the daily report and the removal of a rumor notification are different operations (N-D07-19). A fistfight affects witnesses'' support and interest and no longer improves the opponent relationship (N-D07-20). The end of improvement is not broadened into the absence of all relationship effects.

In both source games, the comparisons of the same report arriving twice, late secret discovery, the successor''s personal trust, and inability versus defiance each retain local evidence together with unconfirmed parts. Reduced notifications are not effect deduplication, and a previous ruler keeping their own followers is not the successor inheriting relationships. The engine''s summation, rounding, randomness, decay timing, and update cycle have not been confirmed.

## 12. Open Items — Decision-Ready Alternatives

The six items below are questions that change how the game plays. This document does not choose the answers. Each item has a question, the currently confirmed boundary, consumer use, ideal outcome, current gap, reason it is open, and verification (QA) tracks.

### 12.1 Relationship and Evaluation Gain/Loss, Decay, and Duration (T68-D07-EVALUATION-DECAY/relation-evaluation)

- **Question.** Who is responsible for an event (to which character''s trust or memory should things they saw directly and things they learned through reports be attributed)? Which events raise or lower trust, loyalty, mood, and memory? How does memory fade? How long must loyalty stay below 25 before a path to rebellion or defection opens? Which actions count as risky actions?
- **Currently confirmed boundary.** Only the following are approved: directionality `T`, the thresholds `25/−25/−50`, loyalty `0..100` (ruler 100), updates at the time of observation, separate records, and the ban on transitions for tribes without gain or loss.
- **Alternative tracks.** (a) Attribution — direct witness = direct attribution to the actor / report = reflects report timing and report path / the attributed party in chained reports. (b) Gain/loss — fixed table by event type / evidence weighting (witnessed versus reported) / asymmetry by value range. (c) Decay — expires after a fixed period / monotonic decay function / reset at update time. (d) Loyalty duration — gate of N consecutive days below / path opens when first falling below, followed by recovery conditions. This document chooses none of the tracks.
- **Consumer use.** The player reads who a character who learned of an event reacts to and why, and whether that reaction is recorded in trust, loyalty, or memory, then decides the next interview or contract action. The player should be able to tell whether recovery, reconciliation, or change over time is possible, and under what conditions. Gain/loss amounts or recovery periods that are not yet decided are distinguished as unknown values.
- **Ideal outcome.** For a character who learned of and reacted to the same event, the time they acquired the information and the target of their reaction are visible, and direct harm, public reputation, trust and loyalty, and memory persistence are distinguished. Recoverable effects can be understood through defined recovery conditions and end points, and undecided change amounts and durations are not shown as if they were confirmed results.
- **Current gap.** There are no values for gain/loss amounts, decay curves, durations, or the risky-action threshold. The corresponding values in external references (CK3 `+25/0.05`, pre-replacement Norland loyalty) are only comparison baselines.
- **Reason open.** Per-event change amounts and durations have not yet been decided. Setting the existing thresholds does not by itself determine new change amounts.
- **QA.** Compare the before and after records of characters who learned of the same event, using the same starting state, observation time, and choice record. If the change amount is undecided, distinguish the fact that the event was learned from the undecided numeric result, and do not compute the undecided value as 0. Even if the same `ResultId` arrives again, the second reaction is not applied, and the trust and memory of an observer who does not yet know of the event are not changed by it. When trust is exactly `25/−25/−50`, the fixed classification conditions are maintained. Loyalty `0/100` are the two ends of the approved range and the ruler''s own loyalty is `100`; exactly `25` is not `below 25`. Even when loyalty is `below 25`, rebellion or defection is not immediately confirmed while the duration is undecided. In comparing recovery and expiry alternatives, check that the event history remains and only the end conditions of the effects defined in each alternative differ.

### 12.2 Command Evaluation Interface (GP-11/command-evaluation-interface)

- **Question.** In command evaluation, how should command authority, observed input, compliance and performance, and the result identifier be connected? Utility coefficients and casualty formulas are not the answer to this question.
- **Currently confirmed boundary.** Only the following are approved: the seven fields of the receipt record, the three-way choice, the separation of compliance and performance, attribution to the commander, idempotency of the result identifier, and the distinction between execution failure and disobedience.
- **Alternatives.** Either display compliance, performance, and the reason for inability together immediately for each command, or group and display the same command history after battle. The former lets the player read the basis for judgment right away, and the latter reduces the information burden during play. The display timing does not newly create the time of acquiring knowledge of an event or any numeric judgment.
- **Consumer use, ideal, gap, reason, and QA.** The player distinguishes the cause of failure from the result of independent action, and the ideal outcome is that inability is not mistaken for discipline. The gap is the rule that combines the nine evaluation inputs into a single judgment, and its presentation (what the main body looks at). The reason it is open is that interface policy is an owner decision. QA requires a classification cross-check of cases of impossibility, defiance, and partial execution, and a case of idempotency when the result identifier is resent. The battle-calculation part of this sub-decision (individual judgment and loss allocation) is on hold pending the Basic source text (Chapter 13).

### 12.3 Use of Character Value Axes in Relationships and Evaluation (T68-PF-01/cast-values)

- **Question.** Should a character''s value axes be used in relationships and evaluation?
- **Currently confirmed boundary.** None — this is only a proposal and has not been approved.
- **Alternatives.** Use the value axes only in descriptions and dialogue, or also as input to judging responsibility for events. The former shows differences between characters without numeric effect, and the latter produces differences in evaluating the same fact. Neither has been chosen.
- **Consumer use.** The player can read why different characters explain or take responsibility for the same event differently. The **ideal outcome** is that, if the value axes are adopted, differences in dialogue and judgment show, but unapproved axes do not secretly change trust values. The **gap** is which axis to use as input for explaining or judging responsibility for which event. The **reason** is that both the values themselves and the way they are used are in proposal status. **QA** compares cases for the same observed fact in which `T` is the same under the `description and dialogue only differ` alternative, and the result differs under the `responsibility-judgment input` alternative only when it passes through an approved event rule.

### 12.4 Use of Organization Values and Policies in Loyalty and Evaluation (T68-PF-02/organization-values)

- **Question.** Should an organization''s values and policies be used in loyalty or evaluation?
- **Currently confirmed boundary.** None — this is only a proposal and has not been approved.
- **Alternatives.** Use organization policy only for job eligibility and contract terms, or, after separate approval, also as input to evaluating individual loyalty. The former separates rights from feelings, and the latter shows the link between institutions and individual reactions. The choice is undecided.
- **Consumer use.** The player distinguishes a policy violation from personal distrust. The **ideal outcome** is that the causes of legal eligibility and of emotional change are each visible. The **gap** is the rule linking organization policy and individual loyalty. The **reason** is that neither the organizational input nor the way of using it has been adopted. **QA** compares whether, when only a policy changes, rights and trust do not automatically change together.

### 12.5 Ban on Reusing Test Numbers (T68-NUMERIC-DESIGN-ASSUMPTIONS/seoul-assumptions)

- **Question.** Should existing test numbers be used for mood, legitimacy, and score?
- **Alternatives.** Propose the needed coefficients in a new design table, or present the source games'' edition-specific values as comparison candidates. Both must explain per-event results, units, and side effects and obtain approval, and there is no route that uses test values as-is as confirmed values.
- **Currently confirmed boundary.** The already approved classification thresholds `25/−25/−50` and the loyalty range `0..100` are kept. What is unapproved are the per-event change amounts, decay rates, durations, and the new coefficients for mood, legitimacy, and score. Source-game comparison values and past test values are not Seoul coefficients.
- **Consumer use.** The player should understand, through approved causes and units, why the same event changed a given state. The **ideal outcome** is that the approved thresholds and ranges are distinguished from the new event coefficients, and unapproved numbers do not determine play results. The **gap** is the coefficients for event change amounts, decay, and duration. The **reason it is open** is that these coefficients are owner decisions. **QA** cross-checks before/after and units for each candidate event coefficient while preserving the boundary values `25/−25/−50` and loyalty `0/25/100`, and confirms that source-game comparison values or past test values are not used in result calculation when they have not been selected. The sub-part on health, fatigue, and derived-value assumptions is on hold pending the Basic source text.

### 12.6 National Institutions and the Formation of Relationships (Marriage, Oaths, Command)

- **Question.** Whose institutions determine the formation and termination of marriage, oath, and command relationships?
- **Alternatives.** Apply the formation requirements of a single jurisdiction''s institutions, or separate per-effect jurisdiction and re-ratification for multinational parties. These are alternatives in institutional design and are not a claim that any particular country already operates that way.
- **Currently fixed boundary.** Marriage, oath, and command-relationship facts that have already been validly established and stored do not disappear or get downgraded to not-established merely because the system input is undetermined. By contrast, a request to establish a new relationship remains `Jedo mijeong/hwagin geungeo bujok` (system undetermined / insufficient verifiable basis) if the applicable system or a verifiable basis for establishment is lacking. A record alone does not establish a new marriage.
- **Consumer Use.** The player distinguishes between cases where an existing relationship is preserved and cases where a new relationship cannot yet be confirmed. The **ideal outcome** is to lose no valid existing fact while also creating no new establishment without grounds. The **gap** is each country''s applicable institution and the grounds for establishment and termination. The **reason it is unresolved** is that this world-setting input is owned by another domain. **QA** compares (a) a case where a stored valid marriage is maintained as is, (b) a case where a new marriage request remains undetermined because no applicable institution or grounds exist, and (c) a case where, once the institution and grounds are confirmed, the ruling is made under that institution''s rules.

## 13. What Is Confirmed and Where the Unresolved Begins

**Confirmed (approved scope).** The single-body and same-as-NPC contract, directional trust and fixed classification thresholds, separate loyalty, updating of personal memory at the moment knowledge is acquired, separation of relationship facts from authority, immediate combat orders and squad leader autonomy, separation of compliance from performance, prohibition on reapplying the same result, and preservation of completed progress and consumption. That an approved rule exists is not the same as every number and institution being settled.

**Unresolved (what this document does not fill in).** Per-event increments and decrements, the method and duration of memory decay, loyalty persistence, change amounts, and risky-action thresholds, the judgment rules of the order-evaluation interface, whether value axes are used, whether organizational policies are used, all test numbers, each country''s relationship institutions, and all personal combat and health calculations that depend on the Basic source text. This document does not close the domain.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d08', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d08', 'D08 Family, Kinship, Marriage, and Adoption — Seoul Operating Design Document', '', 'proposal', 115, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d08.md', '# D08 Family, Kinship, Marriage, and Adoption — Seoul Operating Design Document

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/relationships-houses-and-intrigue/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

- Rule version basis: SP-1.0.0 confirmed clauses, campaign progression rules (person, authority, and labor-status contract), and faction and war clauses (marriage ownership clause).
- The states and transitions of marriage are explained through record types and separation rules. Field schemas, units, formulas, and coefficients are undetermined.

Notation rules. Each unit description carries one of the following tags.

- 【Approved】 — The scope is exactly as already accepted. This document does not extend it by a single character.
- 【Owner Choice】 — An item for which the owner chose a direction in a response. The chosen sentence is recorded as a contract draft (v1), and the rest, such as the content of the actual governing law, ages, qualifications, shares, and durations, remains undetermined.
- 【Unresolved】 — An item that cannot be determined on any basis. It is handled in §13 as a list awaiting decision.

This document contains no approved numerical values anywhere. The age threshold, the criterion for sufficient consent, the limit on the number of adoptees, the time required for ratification, the kinship proximity value, and the effect duration are all undetermined, and they are not 0 or an implicit default. The names of parties, countries, and offices in the §8 examples are placeholders for reading the rules, not approved persons, states, or institutions.

## 1. What This Domain Covers

D08 defines **what is recorded, what is established, and what is not mixed together in family and marriage**.

Covered: recording the parties'' consent to a marriage and the institution''s ratification in the event ledger; withholding the establishment ruling before the applicable institution is determined; a framework for maintaining, terminating, or re-ratifying each effect of an established marriage (alliance, property, ratification, succession) one by one; the distinction among the three paths to a claim; gender entry rules; the place called adoption; the non-transferability of synthetic beings recognized as persons; and the separation of the principal body, head seat, individual titles, and property succession.

Not covered: qualification and adjudication of claims and rights (D10), succession procedures and the principal-body transition itself (D12), rules for property inheritance and transfer (D13), state-organ elections, quorum, ties, regency, and legitimacy (D09), organizational survival and dissolution (D16), relationship, memory, and loyalty values (D07), and the event ledger, world clock, and save versions (D18). §11 sets out the ownership boundaries.

## 2. Actors

| Actor | What They Do | Basis |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The Two Parties | Leave consent to the marriage as a record. If the principal is a party, the player can directly input that party''s actions. Whether a lawful proxy or delegation can substitute for this act is determined by the applicable institution and the authority contract, and this document creates neither permission nor prohibition 【Unresolved】 | Canonical Prose b4, SP1 b3 |
| Ratifying Authority | If that country''s institution requires ratification, the authority designated by that institution records the ratification. Who the ratifying authority is cannot be known until the applicable institution is determined 【Unresolved】 | Canonical Prose b4, b16 |
| Contract Responsible for the Effects | For the alliance, property, ratification, and succession effects of an established marriage, each one''s own contract (alliance: faction and war clauses; property: D13; ratification and succession: the relevant institution) determines maintenance, termination, and re-ratification. This domain triggers a check for each effect and does not end them all at once | Marriage Ownership Clause |
| The Dead | New actions stop. Death does not establish a marriage, nor does it create effects that do not exist | Canonical Prose b18 |
| A Living Person Who Lost Their Position | Only that position''s signing, command, and administration stop. Personal life, movement, and other separately valid contracts are judged under their own qualifications | SP1 b51, Canonical Prose b17 |
| Synthetic Being (Recognized as a Person) | Not an asset that can be handed over. A family does not transfer it like property | SP1 b61 |
| Player | Engages with the world as a single principal. Acts within the principal''s own qualifications or the scope of a separately established authority contract. The fact that the principal is not a party or ratifying authority does not by itself exclude a lawful proxy or delegation, and this document does not determine the existence or scope of such authority | SP1 b3, Canonical Prose b4 |

State and institution names are merely placeholder names for institutions, and this document does not determine the content of any particular state''s marriage institution. Only the fact that institutions differ by country is 【Approved】 (Canonical Prose b15).

## 3. Decisions the Player Actually Makes

1. **Consent Record (as a party)** — If the principal is a party, they leave their own consent to the marriage as a record. 【Owner Choice】 The owner chose the direction of recording consent as "party consent." This record documents the fact of consent and is not an establishment ruling. Whether proxy or delegated consent is permitted has not yet been determined.
2. **Ratification Record (as ratifying authority)** — Whether the principal is the ratifying authority of the applicable institution, or has been lawfully delegated that authority, is judged by the applicable institution and the authority contract. The identity of the ratifying authority and the scope of delegation cannot be known until the institution is determined 【Unresolved】.
3. **Triggering Per-Effect Checks** — When, in an established marriage, the spouse dies, is detained, or loses office, the alliance, property, ratification, and succession effects are checked one by one. Each result is determined by that effect''s contract.
4. **Adoption Request** — 【Owner Choice】 The owner chose the direction of defining adoption consent as "direct party consent" (the valid consent of the named adoptive father and the adoptee). However, because the full accepted procedure does not yet exist, it cannot be determined from the current records alone whether an adoption has been established 【Unresolved】.
5. **What Is Not Chosen** — The player does not arbitrarily choose which country''s institution to apply. 【Owner Choice】 The direction the owner chose is that "only explicitly registered and authorized governing law handles family acts," and the content and qualifications of the actual governing law have not yet been registered 【Unresolved】.

[Design proposal] This is a proposal to distinguish `Girokdoem` (recorded), `Seongnip hwagin` (validity confirmed), `Seongnip mijeong(jedo mijeong)` (validity undetermined, system undetermined), `Miseongnip(sajeong myeongsi)` (not established, circumstances stated), and `Jedo mijeong` (system undetermined) when the action-result screen displays only information that the principal entity actually knows and has permission to view. The display scope follows D18''s observation/knowledge/permission contract, and this document does not create public rules that have not yet been decided. The world''s validity adjudication and whether the principal entity knows that adjudication are separate matters. If the principal entity does not know, or cannot view, whether the system is undetermined, the `Jedo mijeong` display is not automatically given either. Merely because information is not visible, the world''s validity state is not changed to undetermined or not established (§5, §10; SP1 b89).

## 4. Eligibility — What Is Settled and What Is Not

【Approved】 What is settled:

- Marriage uses that country''s own political, property, and succession institutions. Institutions differ by country. Which country''s institution applies to the two people is not settled, and one side''s institution is not picked and used while it is unknown (Canonical Prose b15).
- Recorded consent and recorded ratification are facts the institution requires; they do not substitute for the institution. If the institution is unknown, the marriage is neither called established nor called not established. Whether someone becomes a candidate also cannot yet be known (Canonical Prose b16).
- Only after a marriage is established, and even then, establishment alone does not automatically grant new claims or territory. There are three claim paths, and they do not substitute for one another: using a claim the spouse already holds, a child inheriting a claim, and fabricating a claim separately from succession rights (Canonical Prose b6, Marriage Ownership Clause).
- When death, detention, or loss of office occurs in an established marriage, the alliance, property, ratification, and succession effects are examined one by one. Each is maintained, ended, or re-ratified. They are not all ended at once, and loss of office alone does not dissolve the marriage itself (Canonical Prose b7, Marriage Ownership Clause).
- Only when it is separately settled that the marriage was not established are there no marriage effects to end. When establishment cannot be decided because the institution is unknown, it cannot even be decided whether any effects exist (Canonical Prose b8).
- Gender uses only female and male, and an already designated gender is left as is. A person without a designation is assigned according to that country''s settings. This is an entry rule for recording people and does not newly set age, gender, or candidacy qualifications (Canonical Prose b9, Gender Entry Owner Decision 2026-09-19).
- Affiliation change, relationship change, movement, and office ratification are distinct causal events. Recording a marriage does not cause affiliation, location, or office to change with it, and the reverse is likewise true (Canonical Prose b11, Person, Authority, and Labor-Status Contract).
- Succession of the principal body, succession of the head seat, individual titles, and property succession are distinct events, and a new principal does not receive a copy of the dead principal''s authority, skills, or friends (SP1 b47).
- Synthetic beings recognized as persons are excluded from the list of transferable assets (SP1 b61).
- Person, event, and result IDs are permanent, and resending the same event ID does not create a new result (SP1 b12·b13, Person, Authority, and Labor-Status Contract).

【Owner Choice】 Where the direction is settled (contract draft v1; the rest is unresolved):

| Topic | Chosen Direction | Boundaries That Remain Alongside |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Country-Specific Law | Named forum — only registered, authorized forums handle house acts | The actual content of the forum and its eligibility paths are undecided |
| Marriage Consent | Recorded as party consent (direct) | Use only when the actual forum permits that path; this is not a blanket eligibility waiver |
| Adoption Consent | Direct party consent (participants) — valid consent from the named adoptive parent and adoptee | The actual age and eligibility rules are separate; the full procedure is undecided |
| Joint Property | Per-asset signed contract (signed-contract) — every current owner signs separately for each asset | Marriage alone does not change ownership; shares, remainders, and creditor priority are undecided together with D13 |

【Open】 Still undecided: per-state eligibility, candidate ordering, how the governing regime is designated, the age threshold, the details of what counts as sufficient consent, the number of adoptions and their procedure and duration, the measure of closeness between kin, and the regime-specific rules that set maintenance, termination, and re-ratification for each effect. These values are handled in §13 as a list awaiting decision.

## 5. Establishment and Effects of Marriage — State Structure (Design Output)

This section is a **design output** that converts the approved prose into states and transitions so it can be used directly for adjudication. Because the canonical text has no sheet, this table does not replace a sheet, and the field names are not a contract.

Establishment states (for each marriage candidate pair):

| Status | Meaning | Which rule creates it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| No record | There is no consent or ratification record | Starting state |
| Record exists | A consent record and (if required) a ratification record are in the ledger. Whether the marriage is established has not yet been adjudicated | Consent/ratification recording acts |
| Establishment confirmed | The governing regime has confirmed establishment under that regime | The regime''s establishment ruling (impossible if the regime is undecided) |
| Not established (circumstance specified) | The scenario separately stipulates that the marriage was not established | Circumstance rule |
| Establishment undecided | The governing regime is not yet known. Neither established nor not established is asserted | Regime undecided |

Core transition rules (description within the 【Approved】 scope):

- Record exists → Establishment confirmed: only the governing regime''s ruling can take this path. The record itself does not create this transition.
- Record exists → Establishment undecided: if the regime is undecided, it stays in this state. **Undecided is not prohibited.** Once the regime is decided later, whether the marriage is established can be adjudicated again, and the fact that it is undecided now neither blocks nor permits later establishment (canonical prose b33).
- The "establishment alone creates no new claim or territory" restriction applies only in the establishment-confirmed state. In establishment-undecided, this restriction can neither be applied nor, conversely, can a claim or territory be said to have arisen (canonical prose b30).
- In the not-established (circumstance specified) state, there is no marriage effect to end. Subsequent death, detention, or loss of office cannot end an effect that does not exist. Establishment undecided is different from this state (canonical prose b8, b34).

Effect states (when an effect actually exists in an establishment-confirmed marriage, for each effect separately):

| Effect | Where maintenance, termination, and re-ratification are set | Role of this domain |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Alliance | The alliance contract in the faction and war clauses | Triggers a check upon death, detention, or loss of office |
| Property | D13''s property contract (per-asset signed contract, will, or security direction is the owner''s choice, §4 table) | Adds no property effect without a property contract |
| Ratification | The governing regime''s ratification rules | Is not the body that decides whether re-ratification is needed |
| Succession | D12''s succession procedure and the relevant regime | Kept separate so that effects do not pass along together with a single line of succession |

Maintaining one effect does not mean maintaining the rest, and ending one does not end the rest (canonical prose b22). An effect whose maintenance, termination, and re-ratification are undecided remains undecided (canonical prose b35).

## 6. Sample Scenario from the Canonical Prose — Cross-Border Consent and Ratification

This scenario is a verbatim transcription of canonical prose b27–b31 (【Approved】).

- **Before**: Two people are alive and belong to different states. Each state has its own marriage regime. Which regime applies to this pair is unknown. Each has their own claims, property, and positions. Starting from the circumstance in which the marriage is not yet established is an **assumption** of this step, not a conclusion drawn from not knowing the regime.
- **Action**: The consent of both people is recorded. Ratification is also recorded. All that remains is those records, and the records do not select a regime.
- **After**: Because the governing regime is unknown, establishment and effects cannot be decided from these records alone. The claim and territory restrictions attached only to an established marriage apply only after establishment is confirmed. Next, if one person loses their position while still alive, only that position''s signing, command, and operation stop, and the loss of position does not determine the marriage''s establishment or effects.

## 7. States and Records — Limits of What Is Approved

【Approved】 There are three kinds of records left in the event ledger (the marriage ownership clause): party consent, ratification, and effect attribution (when an effect actually exists). Consent, ratification, and effect attribution are separate records, and writing one does not produce the others (canonical prose b21).

【Approved】 Identifiers and versions: IDs for persons, events, and outcomes are permanent. Version mismatches and corrupted saves are explicit errors, and resending the same event ID does not create a new outcome. Concurrent rights transfers are evaluated against the same ledger version and resolved by a deterministic acceptance order (SP1 b12, b13, b93).

What is not fixed: this area has **no approved formula at all**. Field schema, units, null handling, caps, rounding, random numbers, and checkpoint format will be fixed when the sheet is written. No sheet exists yet, so this document defines none of them. World clock, event order, and save boundaries follow the D18 contract.

## 8. Four Worked Examples — State Transition Design

The four cases below are **design outputs** that apply the approved scope and the owner''s chosen direction to the state structure in §5. They are not proof of execution, and the names that appear (parties A and B, states α and β, office Γ) are placeholders. Before, Action, and After describe the scenario''s world record and the basis for the rulings. This does not mean every record or state is visible to the player; it is a proposal that the information shown on screen be limited to the Vessel''s actual knowledge and viewing authority (§10).

### Example 1 — Consent and Ratification Records from Different States (Validity Undetermined)

- **Before**: A (belonging to α) and B (belonging to β) are both alive. No marriage record exists. Each holds their own claims, property, and offices. The governing system is undetermined.
- **Action**: The consent of A and B is recorded separately (owner''s choice: recorded as consent of the parties). One ratification record is also left. Whether this record is valid or sufficient under the governing system is undetermined, and the existence of a ratification record does not establish that the β system applies. All that remains is the record.
- **State transition**: no record → record exists → **Seongnip mijeong** (validity undetermined; system undetermined). The claim-path state is `Yeollim yeobu mijeong` (whether it opens is undetermined) in all three branches. The alliance, property, ratification, and succession effect states are all `Mijeong(seongnip mijeong)` (undetermined; validity undetermined).
- **After**: the world record in this case consists of 2 consent records and 1 ratification record, for a total of **3**. Because the applicable system is undetermined, the validity adjudication stays at `Seongnip mijeong(jedo mijeong)`, and the new claim and territory remain at `Panjeong mijeong` (adjudication undetermined). Since there is no confirmed validity, the "no automatic creation from validity alone" restriction is not yet applied, and the text says neither that they were created nor that they were not created. Later, once the applicable system, explicit premise records, and relevant world events are in place, re-adjudication on that basis is possible. The screen display is a design proposal limited to the records and adjudication information that the principal entity actually knows and has permission to view, and it does not assume that all 3 world records are visible. Hidden information is distinguished from the system itself being undetermined.
- **Failure contrast**: if the scenario fixes this marriage as `Miseongnip(sajeong myeongsi)` (not established, circumstances stated), the effect state is `Eopseum` (none), and there is no effect to be ended by later death, detention, or loss of office. `Seongnip mijeong` (validity undetermined) and `Miseongnip(sajeong myeongsi)` are different states.

### Example 2 — Spouse''s Death in an Established Marriage (Decomposed by Effect)

- **Before** (scenario assumption: validity was confirmed in the past under the system that applied then): the marriage status of A and B is `Seongnip hwagin` (validity confirmed). Alliance effect: yes (the power-clause contract has a continuation rule). Property effect: yes (2 asset-specific signed contracts). Ratification effect: yes. Succession effect: yes.
- **Event**: B dies. The dead person''s new actions stop.
- **State transition (independent check per effect)**:

| Effect | Checking Party | Resulting State in This Case |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Alliance | The alliance contract in the faction and war clauses | As that contract determines — the possible outcomes and conditions among maintenance, termination, and re-ratification are also not determined here |
| Property | D13 signed contract and will direction (owner''s choice: registered wills recognized, explicit allocation) | Follows the ruling of the D13 contract |
| Ratification | Governing System | As the system determines — if the conditions for maintenance, termination, or re-ratification are undetermined, the outcome is also undetermined |
| Succession | D12 procedure and the relevant system | Candidate eligibility and order are undetermined, so candidate confirmation remains pending |

- **After**: the four effects do not merge into a single status. If any one of them has no determined continuation, termination, or re-ratification, only that effect remains `Mijeong` (undetermined). There is no blanket termination. Even if a separate principal-succession event determines a new principal to replace the deceased B, B''s relationships, authority, skills, and friends are not copied over, so the effects arising from the marriage do not transfer automatically through a single succession line. B''s death alone is not assumed to have caused a principal succession.
- **Design conclusion**: This case hands each of the four effects to its responsible contract but does not count the number of outcome combinations. The possible outcomes, conditions, and mutual constraints per effect are not yet determined, and separate checks do not imply mathematical independence. All this area guarantees is that the four effects are checked individually rather than merged into one outcome.

### Example 3 — Loss of Office While Alive (Not a Dissolution of Marriage)

- **Before**: A''s marriage status is `Seongnip hwagin` (validity confirmed). A holds office Γ and therefore has Γ''s signing, command, and operating authority.
- **Event**: A steps down from Γ while still alive.
- **State transition**: Γ''s office authority goes `Yuhyo → Jeongji` (valid → suspended). Losing the office alone does not dissolve the marriage itself. The alliance, property, ratification, and succession effects are each checked against their responsible contract, and depending on that contract may be continued, terminated, re-ratified, or left undetermined. Personal life, movement, and separate contracts are also not automatically suspended by the vacancy alone, but outcomes may differ according to the eligibility conditions of each action and contract. A vacant Γ does not automatically create inheritance, transfer, acting appointment, or absorption of authority.
- **After**: The only things established are the suspension of Γ''s office authority and `Gwanjik sangsilmaneuro honin jadong haeso eopseum` (no automatic dissolution of the marriage by loss of office alone). It is not asserted that all marriage effects remain as they were; the actual change in each effect is left to the determination result of its own contract.
- **Failure contrast**: This differs from the case where A dies — death stops all of an individual''s new actions, whereas loss of office stops only the office''s authority.

### Example 4 — Adoption Request (Procedure Undetermined → Ruling Impossible) and Preservation of a Synthetic Person

- **Before**: There is a prospective adoptive father Byeong (a human), a prospective adoptee Jeong (a human), and one synthetic being recognized as a person in this household. No adoption record exists.
- **Action**: The consent of Byeong and Jeong is recorded (owner''s choice: direct consent of the parties — valid consent of the named adoptive father and adoptee). At the same time, someone tries to add that synthetic being to the household''s property transfer list.
- **State transition**: the establishment status of the quantum marriage goes `Girok eopseum → Girok jonjae → Seongnip pandeong bulleung` (no record → record exists → establishment undeterminable), because there is no accepted procedural rule. It is not asserted that it is not established. Even if a procedure is later determined, the result is not generated automatically from the current record alone; instead, it is determined together with whether the explicit prerequisite records and related world events required by that procedure are in place. An attempt to register a synthetic being on a transfer list is `Geojeol` (rejected), because synthetic beings recognized as persons are excluded from the list of transferable assets. This rejection always applies regardless of the outcome of the quantum marriage.
- **After**: The consent records remain. Whether the adoption holds is undetermined. The synthetic being does not become an asset on any branch (marriage established or failed, adoption established or failed). Byeong''s and Jeong''s affiliation, location, and office are not changed by this record (separate causal events).
- **Design conclusion**: because the quantum procedural rule is not yet known, the permitted range of inputs and the result function cannot be defined. This does not mean the mathematical domain is the empty set, and the current result is not `Geojit` (false) but `Pandeong bulleung` (undeterminable). The non-transferability of synthetic beings is an approval rule that applies separately from the quantum determination.

## 9. The Shape of Failure, Rejection, and Preservation

【Approved】 Most failures in this area are **cannot be determined**.

- If consent and ratification have been recorded but the governing system is unknown, it cannot be determined from those records alone whether validity holds now. The records remain. A record is not validity, and an undetermined state neither blocks nor permits later validity (canonical prose b33).
- When validity cannot be determined because the system is unknown, the effects are not treated as nonexistent. Whether a subsequent death, detention, or loss of office ends the effects also cannot be determined. A dead person''s new actions stop, and a living person''s loss of office stops only the signing, command, and operation of that office (canonical prose b34).
- "There is no effect to end" holds only when the scenario separately specifies non-validity (canonical prose b8).
- Even for an established marriage, effects are not closed all at once. Among alliance, property, ratification, and succession, any maintenance, termination, or re-ratification that is not determined remains undetermined (canonical prose b35).

Principles of preservation — what does not move on any branch: the consent and ratification records themselves; the separation of each party''s claims, property, and offices (a marriage record does not merge them into one lump, canonical prose b20); the non-transferability of a synthetic person (b37); that a change of Vessel does not copy relationships or effects (b36); and that a dead Vessel''s authority, skills, and friends do not pass to the new Vessel (SP1 b47).

## 10. Boundaries of Information and Authority

- 【Approved】 The world record of hidden facts and the facts each person knows are stored separately (SP1 b89). The existence of a world record of consent, ratification, and effect attribution does not automatically create the Vessel''s knowledge or viewing authority.
- 【Approved】 The displayed reason for an action also does not leak hidden facts (SP1 b14). A received report is knowledge with a source and a confirmation time, not the whole current state of the world (SP1 b75), and when the Vessel changes, the display of inaccessible records is restricted according to the new character''s knowledge and qualifications (SP1 b94). Accordingly, the display proposal below does not automatically promote a previous Vessel''s knowledge or stale reports to a current world ruling.
- 【Design Proposal】 Of the existence and attribution of consent and ratification records, the validity ruling, the per-effect states, and whether office authority is suspended, only the information the Vessel actually knows and has viewing authority over is shown on screen. This document does not approve any access absent from the D18 observation, knowledge, and authority contract, and the detailed disclosure rules for who may know what information remain unresolved.
- What the player cannot determine from any rule in this document: which state''s system applies (system designation undetermined), the identity of the ratifying authority, whether validity holds before the system is determined, and all numeric values.【Unresolved】
- The player acts through the Vessel. Authority contracts established separately are distinguished from the Vessel''s own qualifications, and where the permitted scope of proxy and delegation is not determined, it is neither arbitrarily permitted nor excluded. A dead person has no new actions.
- 【Design proposal】When displaying an establishment determination that the principal knows about and has permission to view, distinguish `Seongnip hwagin` (established), `Miseongnip (sajeong myeongsi)` (not established, circumstances specified), and `Seongnip mijeong (jedo mijeong)` (establishment undetermined, system undetermined). Do not confuse the case where the system is undetermined in the world with the case where a world determination exists but the principal does not know it or cannot view it. Inaccessible information does not substitute for a not-established or system-undetermined determination. This display is not an approved UI contract.

## 11. Ownership and Boundaries — Other Domains

| Topic | Owning Domain | Boundary with D08 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Eligibility and ruling for claims, justifications, and rights | D10 | D08 only determines the distinction among the three claim paths. D10 determines the eligibility and outcomes for use of a spouse''s existing claim, inheritance by children, and separate manipulation |
| Succession procedure and Vessel transition | D12 | Because the new vessel does not inherit the dead vessel''s authority, skills, or friends, marriage effects are not copied down a single line of succession either. Candidate eligibility and order are undecided. |
| Property, Inheritance, and Handover | D13 | Property effects require a contract on the property side. The owner has chosen the directions of wills, explicit assignment, debt review, collateral maintenance, and reserved acceptance, but the text of that contract is covered by the D13 document. Shares, remainders, and creditor priority are undecided. |
| State Institutions, Elections, Regency, and Legitimacy | D09 | Signing, command, and operation suspension during a vacant office fall under SP1 b51. Who is eligible, assemblies, ties, regency, ratification, and legitimacy are undecided in D09 and T68-D09-COUNTRY-LAWS. |
| Survival and Dissolution of Organizations | D16 | The death of a leader alone does not automatically dissolve a state or institution (SP1 b52). Whether an organization without territory survives is undecided in T68-D16-ORGANIZATION-LIFECYCLE. |
| Relationship, Memory, and Loyalty Values | D07 | Marriage is a cause event separate from relationship records. Relationship values and decay are undecided in D07. |
| Personal Identity and Gender Authoring | D01 | The gender authoring rule (female, male; existing designations as-is) is an authoring rule jointly owned by D01/D08. It is not a new approval of age, gender, or eligibility. |
| Event Ledger, Clock, and Storage | D18 | The storage, versioning, and replay order of consent, ratification, and effect-attribution records follow the D18 contract. |

## 12. External References and Their Limits

Observations from the two games are **how those games behave**, not Seoul rules. None of the observations has been adopted as a Seoul coefficient, probability, or formula. The facts below are limited to what was confirmed from public documentation and installed scripts; they are neither raw engine internals nor measured runs.

### 12.1 Crusader Kings III

- Version attribution: installed build **1.20.0.3, build 25652598** (installed Steam distribution; no evidence of a beta branch). All four items are documentation checks against installed script values and script commands (not observed runs).
- **Adoption cost**: `common/script_values/00_basic_values.txt:1002` `medium_prestige_value = 150` (prestige points). The ordinary adoption interaction adds this value, multiplies it ×2 (base 300) for dynastyless circumstances, and conditionally multiplies it ×0 for free-culture and always-allowed rules. So 150 is not an unconditional charge but the base value of a guarded expression.
- **Divorce cost**: `common/script_values/00_basic_values.txt:2079` `divorce_cost = 100` (piety points). The cost block in question multiplies by ×0 for puppet actions. The paths for sufficient piety and manual debit are a separate scope.
- **One alternative for adoption eligibility**: `00_basic_values.txt:720` `low_fertility = 0.3` (dimensionless fertility grade). One cited adoption-eligibility alternative requires, as a logical AND, `fertility ≤ low_fertility` **and** `any_child count < 1`. This is the condition of one of several OR alternatives, not a necessary condition for adoption or a pregnancy probability.
- **Author of betrothal dissolution**: `common/character_interactions/00_marriage_interactions.txt` lines 2313–2375. Inside the outer else (no lawful grounds for dissolution + not a reserved great marriage), if the ownership scopes differ, the event `marriage_interaction.0001` is fired at the refusing party (hidden `break_betrothal` at line 76 within the event); if the ownership scopes are the same, `break_betrothal` is executed immediately in a hidden_effect. The tooltip is display only and is not the actual author.
- Correspondence with Seoul: the CK3 material we checked shows the guarded cost expressions and eligibility alternatives for adoption and divorce, and the script commands and event path for betrothal dissolution. It does not establish that adoption or divorce take effect or end immediately in actual play. In Seoul, the record of consent and ratification is separated from the determination that the applicable institution has taken effect, and the determination is deferred when the institution is undecided. The author and path branching of the cited betrothal-dissolution command are compared only with the structure of Seoul''s per-effect responsible-contract separation, and do not prove the termination of the whole marriage, the settlement of its effects, or the execution timing. The figures (150, 100, 0.3) are not Seoul values.

### 12.2 Norland

- Source attribution: the publisher-hosted community wiki (`wiki.hoodedhorse.com/Norland`) at pinned revisions — World `oldid=1284`, States `oldid=1262`, Lords `oldid=1314`, Actions `oldid=1318` — and developer announcements (Patch #25/#26, Patch #39·#40, Patch #43 BETA, Patch #57 BETA, Update #35, Patches #54–#56 bundle). All 16 items are documentation checks (raw processing not confirmed).
- **Necessary condition for marriage**: World oldid1284 — the foreign-city marriage action is possible only if the relationship between the two rulers is +5 or higher. It is a necessary condition, not full eligibility. The effects are bride relocation, gold payment (proportional to the bride city''s strength), and a +30 ruler relationship; this is a list of outcomes, not a transaction order or property-transfer rule.
- **Marker of forced marriage (historical)**: The Patch #25 announcement attached a forced-marriage incident to newlywed relationships <50, and Patch #39·#40 removed that incident and retroactively wrote the threshold as <25. The threshold mismatch between the two records remains unreconciled, and this incident is not a consent system.
- **Divorce**: Actions oldid1318, bishop entry and divorce table — marriage ends through the married lord, a bishop in the city, and a gold cost. The consent of the other party, the paying party, children, and property effects are not documented.
- **Recognition and genealogy**: States oldid1262 classifies births outside marriage or to a female ruler as bastards, and Patch #26 displays legitimate and bastard children and married neighboring houses in the family tree. Patch #43 BETA creates fake parents for initial AI lords so they are recognized as kin. Display and classification do not grant recognition authority, succession, or ownership.
- **Duration and state values**: the 3-day romance state in Patch #57 BETA, the 48-hour married-couple date cooldown in Patch #39·#40, the puppeting of parents after marriage of an AI bride under 24 in Update #35, and the 3-day childbirth and small childbirth-death probability in States oldid1262. All are conditional values in that game and are not Seoul durations or probabilities.
- **Political invalidation on death or loss of office**: the Patch #54 BETA fix item in the Patches #54–#56 bundle — the target''s death, dementia, or loss of lordship resets that politician''s strategy. It is a political-strategy invalidation, not a termination of marriage.
- Correspondence with Seoul: the Norland material we checked documents the relationship threshold (+5), the marriage outcome list, and the gold cost of bishop divorce, but this material alone does not fix the actual processing order or the point of immediate determination. In Seoul, relationship is a separate state owned by D07, and whether a marriage takes effect is judged by the applicable institution. Norland''s reset of political strategy upon death or loss of lordship is a contrasting case of state separation and does not prove whether the marriage ends or that other effects remain unchanged.

The scope of the CK3 and Norland documents and scripts we checked cannot determine Seoul''s bilateral procedures. This is not a claim that neither game as a whole has a complete bilateral procedure. Because the cost, command, and display evidence of external games does not substitute for approval of Seoul''s procedures for consent, eligibility, ratification, and taking effect, the bilateral-procedure problem in §13 is left undecided.

In the comparison, the fact that cost guards, command authors, and event paths were confirmed is separate from when actual establishment or dissolution finishes. Because the external evidence in this document contains no observation of adoption or divorce actually being executed, on-screen costs or script commands alone do not settle immediate completion or the order of effect settlement.

## 13. What Is Not Yet Decided — Pending Decision List

Each item is not a blank. It is a pending-decision state equipped with a question / actual consumer use / ideal outcome / current gap / reason / verification method (QA). No value is filled in implicitly.

1. **Designation of the applicable system (GP-09 per-country law)** — Question: which country''s marriage system applies to two people from different countries? Consumers: the validity adjudicator, the ratification screen, the campaign UI. Ideal: the "explicit jurisdiction law" direction chosen by the owner gets the registration and route qualification of an actual jurisdiction law attached. Gap: there is no registered jurisdiction law. Reason: the content of an actual jurisdiction law is a world fact and a system choice, so it requires owner input. QA: a before/after comparison in which, once a jurisdiction law is registered, the `Seongnip mijeong` (validity undetermined) in §8 example 1 is re-adjudicated under that law.
2. **Sufficiency standard for marriage eligibility and consent, and the ratifying authority (GP-09)** — Question: once the institution is set, who can marry, what suffices as consent, and who ratifies? Consumers: eligibility checks for consent and ratification recording actions. Ideal: the "recorded by the parties'' consent" choice is joined by age, eligibility, and order. Gap: all of the age threshold, eligibility, and order. Reason: pending owner decision. QA: compare the allowance and refusal of cases involving minors, detention, and existing marriage, along with the displayed reasons.
3. **Adoption procedure (GP-09)** — Question: who establishes an adoption, with whose consent and ratification, and through what procedure? Consumer: the adoption-forming action. Ideal: the "direct party consent" option gets a procedure, a limit on the number of adoptions, and a required duration attached. Gap: there is no accepted procedure itself, so validity adjudication is impossible. Reason: the procedure is the owner''s decision, and an external game does not stand in for the Seoul procedure (§12). QA: a before/after comparison in which, once the procedure is fixed, §8 example 4 changes from `Panjeong bulneung` (adjudication impossible) to an established/not-established adjudication.
4. **Joint property, shares, wills, and collateral (T68-D13-FAMILY-ASSETS)** — Question: how are a couple''s joint property, shares, wills, collateral, and overlapping dispositions to be divided? Consumer: the D13 property contract. Ideal: the "per-asset signature contract," "registered will," and "explicit assignment" choices are joined by shares, remainders, and creditor priority. Gap: shares, remainders, and creditor priority. Reason: Seoul property law is an owner decision. QA: a comparison against the D13 contract for cases of competing dispositions and collateralized estates (this document covers only the boundary).
5. **Institutional rules for maintaining, ending, and re-ratifying each effect (remainder of T68-MARRIAGE-EFFECTS)** — Question: When an established marriage''s alliance, property, ratification, and succession effects are affected by death, detention, or loss of office, how is each one determined? Consumers: the contract responsible for each effect (alliance = faction clause, property = D13, ratification and succession = institutions). Ideal: the "as that contract determines" wording in the §5 effects table is filled in with actual rules. Gap: the death, detention, and loss-of-office clauses of each effect contract. Reason: each contract''s owning domain must decide. QA: check that the four effect states in §8 Example 2 are filled in by actual contract rulings.
6. **Country-specific election, quorum, tie, regency, ratification, and legitimacy rules (T68-D09-COUNTRY-LAWS)** — Question: In each country, who elects, how is an assembly constituted, and how do ties, regency, ratification, and legitimacy differ in marriage and succession? Consumer: D09 institution adjudicator. Gap: all inputs for the 16 countries. Reason: Seoul''s institutional inputs are needed. QA: check the regency and re-ratification paths in cases of a vacant office.
7. **Survival of organizations without territory (T68-D16-ORGANIZATION-LIFECYCLE)** — Question: Does an organization with no territory persist? Consumer: organization lifecycle adjudication. Ideal: survival conditions are attached on top of the established rule that a state or institution does not vanish merely because its head dies. Gap: survival and dissolution conditions. QA: compare before and after a leadership change in a territory-less organization.
8. **Candidate registration for unconfirmed names (T68-PF-05, proposal group)** — Question: Can a person whose name has not yet been decided be put forward as a candidate for marriage or succession? Consumer: drawing up the candidate roster. Gap: because candidate eligibility is undecided, it is unknown whether such a person can be listed. Reason: this is a proposal group requiring individual identity and rights review. QA: check whether roster registration is allowed once the eligibility rules are confirmed.
9. **List of unapproved numerical values** — age thresholds, quantification of the sufficiency standard for consent, limits on the number of adoptees, time required for ratification, kinship closeness values, durations of effect persistence and expiry, and amounts of relationship change. These values are not zero or an implicit default; they are **undecided**. No document invents numbers for this list.
10. **Absence of machine sheets and case bundles** — This area has no approved machine sheet or verification case bundle. Drafting sheets is permitted work only after decisions 1–8 are made, and any sheet containing numerical values before then falls outside the approved scope. QA: check one-to-one against the list of approval grounds when drafting a sheet.
11. **Basic-dependent parts** — D08 has no calculations that depend on Basic (the original rulebook). However, since the task of obtaining the full original text is not yet complete, this document does not claim any Basic-dependent completeness.

## 14. Summary

D08 is now a **domain of record and suspension**. What is approved is all of the following: leaving consent, ratification, and effect attribution in the ledger; delegating the validity determination to the applicable institution and suspending it as `seongnip mijeong` (validity undetermined) if the institution is undetermined; dividing the effects of an established marriage into four and handing them to their respective contracts; and not mixing claims, property, offices, and principal entities with one another. The owner chose the directions of per-country law (jurisdictional law method), marriage consent (the parties directly), adoption consent (the parties directly), and joint property (per-asset signed contracts); the actual jurisdictional law, age, qualifications, procedures, shares, and periods are still blank. The rest is in the pending-decision list of §13, and this document does not choose any value on anyone''s behalf.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d09', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d09', 'D09 State, Government, Institutions, Legitimacy', '', 'proposal', 116, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d09.md', '# D09 State, Government, Institutions, Legitimacy

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/power-and-institutions/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

## 1. The People Who Run the State and the Player''s Decisions

The player continues the campaign as a single person with one body. Other characters are NPCs who use the same action contract. Opening the state screen does not turn the player into another character or grant all of the state''s powers. There is a single world time. Combat has no pause, speed control, or command queue. These combat rules do not determine time adjustment for the campaign or for institutional procedures.

Actors are the current valid officeholder, a proxy within the scope registered in the institution, a candidate, a supporter, and a ratification officer. A state is not the same entity as its head personally, and an institution''s property is not the head''s personal property. Through their own body and actual authority, the player requests institutional actions, claims eligibility as a candidate, requests support, and submits evidence. Being able to make a request is different from having that request approved.

The head of state takes part in appointments, award proposals, diplomacy, war approval, and succession procedures under the state''s institutions. A higher tier does not open up every action. Which institution a signature is made in, which unit is commanded, and which treasury can be used must each be checked. This distinction applies equally to the player and to NPCs.

## 2. Eligibility and Independent Ledgers

| Ledger | Facts to Check | What It Does Not Grant by Itself |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Character | Identity, survival, and ability to act of the same person | Office, inheritance, command authority |
| Affiliation and citizenship | Which state or organization one belongs to, and recognition | Declaring war in a state''s name, disposing of public property |
| Office | Institution, seat, jurisdiction, current holder, scope of signature | Ownership of a personal treasury, command of all forces |
| Title and Holding | Target rights and obligations, holder, superior contract, recognition | Immediate on-site use, acquisition of another office |
| Effective control | Who controls the site right now, and occupation or dispute | Lawful possession, cooperation of residents, institutional operation |
| Command | Designated units and the valid scope of command or delegation | Disposing of institution-owned property, incorporating other units |
| Property and access | Ownership, custody, use, and treasury access | Personal ownership of the property accessed |
| Relationships and memory | Trust, kinship, marriage, debts, oaths, and memory of events | Automatic election, inheritance, or appointment to a vacant seat |

Office tier is a classification used for comparison between states. The same tier is not the same office, and a higher tier does not automatically permit military command, hereditary succession, or diplomacy on behalf of the entire state. Actual authority comes from within the scope of that office and of separate contracts. A combat commander commands all squads that are included in both the command scope registered in the combat session and the list of assigned squads, but this does not remove the on-site squad leader''s autonomous judgment and assessment of orders issued immediately. A state office and this combat command contract do not replace each other.

A candidate''s eligibility, voting rights, and technical qualifications are data separate from a claim to a title of nobility. A relationship record such as spouse, kin, or adopted child does not by itself confer every right or office. Personal trust or support may change with events, but that does not by itself alter an institution''s fixed eligibility thresholds or classification criteria. Seoul has no approved values yet for its legitimacy threshold or election scores.

It must be possible to search for a person by name. Even when names are identical or a person has changed their name, identity is confirmed by the permanent ID. Records from past character registration are evidence of past identity issuance and do not replace the current state''s roster of members, candidates, or heads.

## 3. Before the Action, the Action, and After the Action

### 3.1 When Acting in a Valid Office

Before: Distinguish the person, office, institution, target of the action, and the basis for the requested authority. Check whether the holder and the actual actor are the same, whether the office is valid, and whether the request is within the scope of jurisdiction, signature, and command. For actions that require a separate contract or actual on-site access, check that eligibility as well.

Action: A player or NPC requests a signature, command, or operation. Even if a record keeper carries out claim-construction work on the instruction of a qualified head, the record keeper does not thereby become a beneficiary of the target territory.

After: A qualified action leaves behind only the effects of its own contract. Without authority, the requested institutional effect is not established. The existence of a public office does not add ownership or command authority to other ledgers. If there is no country-specific institutional input, report "institution undecided" and do not apply arbitrary success or free handling.

### 3.2 When a Living Person Loses an Office

Before: A person holds one office and may also have a personal life, movement, other offices, or separate valid contracts.

Action: The grounds for losing that office are confirmed.

After: Only the signatures, commands, and operations that depend on the lost office are suspended. Personal life, movement, and actions under separate valid contracts continue to be judged by their own qualifications. The actions of a living person are not locked wholesale. That vacancy alone does not cause inheritance, transfer of rights, appointment of a proxy, or absorption of authority into another seat. The institution''s treasury does not become personal property. If another contract expires under its own conditions, the authority under that contract also ends, but the vacancy itself does not expire a separate contract.

### 3.3 When the Head Dies

Before: The head''s office, title, property, and contracts exist as separate items.

Action: The grounds for death are confirmed. The deceased cannot begin new personal actions, and the office becomes vacant.

After: The institution and the state do not automatically dissolve merely because the head has died. The institution''s property is not converted into the head''s personal estate. For each item, the succession institution lookup, candidate eligibility check, election, nomination, examination, or hereditary procedure, ratification, transfer of rights, handover and takeover, and dispute records proceed only when each has its own grounds. Defeated candidates are not deleted, and remaining claims are recorded according to the institution. If there is no eligible person or procedure, the matter remains a vacancy or succession dispute, and no anonymous head is created to treat it as a success.

Succession of the body, succession of the head of a state or institution, and succession of individual titles and property are separate events. A new body does not inherit the previous body''s authority, skills, or friends. That said, the new body and the new head need not necessarily be different people. If the office''s institution, candidate check, procedure, and ratification have independently chosen the same person, the same person may hold it. The fact that the body changed is not itself grounds for that selection or for inheritance. Even if the body changes because of permanent detention, the detained person is still alive, so inheritance upon death is not opened.

### 3.4 Proxy for a Vacancy and the Survival of the Organization

Before: The office is vacant; check whether there is a proxy registered in the institution and a defined scope.

Action: The registered proxy requests an action within the scope.

After: Only the registered scope may be exercised. A proxy does not substitute for electing a head or for permanently transferring the office. A simple delegation contract is also not an automatic proxy appointment. Proxy authority is not granted to unregistered persons or to requests outside the scope.

The latest choice is to let the identity and obligations of a landless organization with living members persist until they are lawfully resolved. The death of its leader and the loss of its territory alone do not erase an organization. Who the surviving representative, treasury custodian, and dissolution authority are requires separate input, and no one is appointed here.

## 4. Starting Succession Methods and Nation Inputs Still Needed

The methods below are the starting settings recorded in the existing design. Do not read a method''s name as an actual candidate roster or a finished election algorithm.

| Nation | Recorded Method | Detailed Inputs Needed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Government of the Republic of Korea | Duty-Family Election | Eligible families, candidates, and votes; attribution of duty posts and acting coverage of vacancies |
| Floodgate State | Election by the Water-Contract Family Representatives'' Contract Council | Valid contracts, representatives, council quorum, and ratification |
| Transshipment State | Hereditary Succession by the Eldest Child of the Rail Family | Order of eligible children, exceptions for wills and adoption, and handover of the post and keys |
| Standards League | Guild Council and Apprentice Lineage | Linking council elections to the apprentice lineage; candidate mastery and support |
| Eastern Command | Hereditary Commander | Family eligibility, takeover of military command, and subjugation registration |
| Russian Legation Command | Dual Command of Commander and Garrison Chief | Vacancy targets, joint authority, acting coverage, and the order of handling the two seats |
| Taeuk Group, Seongha Group, Sinnae Transport | Family Inheritance | Separation of personal shares and licenses from the leader''s post, appointment, and ratification |
| Yeouido Investment Federation | Council Election by the Investing Families | Separation of share inheritance, representative selection, and chair election |
| Registry Church | Father-to-Son Succession of the Pastorate and Elder Ratification | Lineage, ordination, and ratification conditions |
| Myeongdong Archdiocese, Anguk Great Monastery | Clergy Election and Mountain Assembly Acclamation | Eligibility, procedure, and takeover of the altar and ledger |
| White Cross Order | Master''s Designation of a Disciple as Successor | Valid designation, training, and the confirming party |
| Central Technology Preservation Institute | Examination-Based Selection | Eligibility to sit the exam, results, and takeover of record-access rights |
| Jeongdong Labor Federation | Direct Election by Union Members | Eligible union members, candidates, and strike-roster authority |

The link between the Standards League''s apprentice lineage and its council election, and the vacancy under the Russian Legation Command''s dual system, are confirmed open questions. Do not arbitrarily merge them into a single system just because the wording differs.

## 5. Failure, Interruption, and Information

Unqualified requests, signatures beyond the scope of authority, and acting coverage without registration do not generate the corresponding authority. A vacancy with no candidate or with an undecided procedure is not displayed as a completed succession. Tie-breaking has not yet been approved either, so neither first-come-first-served nor arbitrary drawing is to be added. However, this undecided state is not a rule permanently forbidding succession in every nation. Once a concrete system is approved, it must be judged by that procedure.

Canceling a task in the world, or a character dying, does not undo progress already completed or resources already consumed. Remaining execution and qualifications going forward are handled by the relevant action contract. The fact that an institution keeps ownership of its treasury does not mean the treasury balance is fixed in every event. Lawful institutional spending and handovers are judged by their respective asset contracts. Results already applied are not applied again to duplicate effects or consumption, and approved rule versions are not changed silently.

The information a player needs includes the post name, institution, holder, and vacancy; the basis and scope of authority for an action; the registered scope of acting coverage; the distinction between an undecided system and a candidate''s lack of qualification; and the distinction between recognition, effective control, and the right to a title. This is a proposal for information display and is distinct from approval to omnisciently reveal every treasury balance, secret ballot, or other people''s memories. The scope of disclosure must define the viewing rights of institutions and actors.

## 6. Examples of Meaningful Judgments

### Example A: One Person Holds Two Posts

Before: A character holds a valid convoy command contract separate from their institution''s record-keeping post. Action: They lose only the record-keeping post. After: They can no longer sign ratifications based on that post, but convoy command is judged by the target and qualifications of the separate contract. Ratification authority does not shift to another post, and personal movement is not halted wholesale because of the vacancy. If the convoy contract has also expired, that is the result of a separate expiry.

### Example B: A Leader''s Death and the Same Person''s Lawful Election

Before: A leader dies, and the character chosen for the new body is also a successor candidate. Action: Separately from the body transfer, they go through the nation''s candidate screening, election, and ratification procedures. After: If the procedure independently selects that person, the new body and the leader may be the same person. If there is no system or candidate, the post remains vacant or disputed. A body transfer alone does not pass on the treasury, titles, technology, or friends.

### Example C: Acting Coverage and Institutional Spending

Before: The leader''s post is vacant, and the institution has a registered deputy and a registered scope of duties. Action: The deputy requests a signature on that duty. After: Whether it falls within scope is judged, and separate treasury-access and spending authority is also checked. Signing-deputy qualification alone does not allow the deputy to dispose of the entire treasury personally. If an approved institutional expenditure exists, the balance may change, but the owning entity does not become the deputy personally.

### Example D: Different Bases for Occupation, Arrival, and War Termination

Before: A unit has occupied the site, and another unit is on the move. Action: The arriving unit''s eligibility to participate is judged after its actual arrival. Any unit that actually arrives at an ongoing battle is permitted to join as reinforcement. After: Occupation is a record of effective control, not the acquisition of a post or the recognition of a right. A war applies a valid casus belli, goal-limited war score in the range ±100, and custom negotiation. An attacker supporting another person''s claim is distinguished from the rights beneficiary, and the continued validity of the casus belli is checked even before forced termination. Title and right transfers after termination are judged on the basis of D10, occupation and war records and organizational survival on D16, and agreements and settlement on D17. No effect occurs automatically from occupation or body transfer alone.

## 7. Ownership Boundaries with Other Domains

| Domain | Judgments Covered | What It Provides to D09 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D01·D02 | Identity, character state, and event records | Whether it is the same person, survival, permanent ID, and applied results |
| D03 | Common action and command contract | Eligibility to perform and basis of authority, checked separately from the office |
| D07 | Personal relationships, memory, loyalty, and evaluation | Distinguishing shifting support and field judgment/evaluation from fixed institutional eligibility |
| D08 | Kinship, marriage, adoption, and family law | Distinguishing records from legal candidate eligibility |
| D10 | Titles, claims, and ratification | Rights and beneficiaries distinguished from the office |
| D11 | Delegation and signature contracts, and revocation | Validity and scope of that contract alone |
| D12 | Body succession and detention/return | Body selection, separate from succession to the leader |
| D13 | Property, treasury, inheritance, and handover | Institutional ownership versus personal ownership and actual expenditure |
| D15 | Combat, command, squads, and reinforcements | Command of every squad in the intersection of the combat session''s registered command scope and the attached roster; immediate orders versus squad leaders'' autonomous judgment; reinforcements only after they actually arrive during the battle |
| D16 | Diplomacy, casus belli, war, siege, prisoners, occupation, and organizational survival and dissolution | Valid casus belli and objective-limited termination, the distinction between control and recognition, and the organizational life cycle |
| D17 | Negotiation, trade, peace, and contracts | Basis for user-defined negotiation, agreement, and settlement; linked to D16 war and D10 rights handling |
| D18 | World clock, event order, and saving | Same rule version and no duplicate application |

This table divides design responsibilities; it is not a record appointing execution code or an operational custodian.

## 8. Structures Confirmed in the Two Source Games and Limits of Comparison

### Crusader Kings III

In the preserved 1.20.0.3 / build 25652598 materials, feudal elections handle the elector set, candidate filter, vote weight, and candidate score separately. This can be used for comparisons that distinguish one person''s candidate eligibility from their votes. The overall tally and final tie resolution are not confirmed, and quorum and ties are investigated but unresolved. Do not read an unknown rule as ‘none’ or as 0.

Bureaucratic appointments involve government access, candidate tiers, score order, and the context of handling office transfers, and the restrictions on holding a council seat together with other offices are conditional. They cannot be carried over as a concurrent-office ban applying to all of Seoul. In handling finance-related vacancies, there are commands that, under conditions such as having no successor councillor, add treasury funds to a superior and destroy the title. Explicit deduction from the previous treasury and final preservation of assets are not proven. Therefore, do not say that ‘the treasury is transferred completely,’ or that it equals the continued existence of a Seoul institutional treasury.

A regency is a context in which power is shared with an existing monarch, and its validity and actual capability conditions differ. It is not the same as Seoul''s acting role, which fills in for an empty office. In tests, the annotations and the actual operands differ, so the actual values and units must be distinguished. Legitimacy uses classification thresholds multiplied by coefficients according to tier and era, but raw rounding and initialization are not proven. This material does not adopt any Seoul legitimacy formula or threshold value. General character generation or test-tube generation is likewise not evidence for ‘generating an unknown leader for every election vacancy.’

### Norland

In the preserved documents of the official wiki and official patches, king-only actions, lord candidate eligibility, politician selection, and specific relationship updates on accession and on a suzerain''s death each have different handling. The general king eligibility table does not fully explain candidate priority, ties, or the default successor. Concurrent holding of politician and successor roles is also directional and conditional, so it must not be turned into a universal permission or ban on holding multiple offices.

The politics-related threshold was 20 in main 50–53, then restored to 40 in beta 55 / main 54–56, and Actions revision 1318 also records 40. Do not use the historical 20 as a current invariant. The knowledge-based politician selection of beta 43 was changed to persuasion-based selection in main 46–48. The strict ‘greater than 6’ persuasion requirement does not include being equal to 6, but candidate ties and the actual selection order are unresolved. These values are not Seoul eligibility values.

Noble exhaustion, a suzerain''s death, and an existing lord''s resettlement of ruins are each documented facts within their own contexts. They do not prove a general rule that a new leader is generated for every vacancy. The treasury modifications concerning confiscated funds and the King''s Ring likewise do not fully prove the treasury custodian, spending authority, or survival during a vacancy. The capacity for free-lord titles is not the geometry of Seoul''s territory or the identity of an institutional office. Voting, quorum, tests, scope of acting authority, legitimacy figures, and the order of sequential processing remain within the limits of the preserved materials even in the source games.

The structures of the two source games serve as a basis for comparing Seoul''s institutions. They are limited to what was confirmed in the preserved documents and are not observations of the game running. Some materials lack a confirmed correspondence between document version and historical build, so actual behavior in that build cannot be established.

## 9. Concrete Alternatives to Decide

The following are proposals before adoption. Do not treat any alternative as approving numbers, ratifiers, or durations.

| Item | Consumer Use and Ideal | Current Gap and Reason | Concrete Alternatives | QA Questions |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Candidacy, election, quorum, ties, and ratification by state | The institutional officer confirms an eligible person as a leader with a documented basis | Only the name of the starting method exists, and election details are undecided | For the elective type, confirm by a registered electoral roll and ratification, or confirm by council agreement; for a tie, confirm after a revote or arbitration by the ratifying body | Is it ensured that no leader without a basis arises from ties, candidate exhaustion, or missing votes? |
| Heredity and Family Law | Check lineage and eligibility for public office separately | Marriage, adoption, order of eligible children, and exceptions are undecided by state | Follow the order of eligible lineage, but require ratification, or independent ratification after a house nomination | Even if the new body and the leader are the same, is there an independent basis? Is nothing inherited by a relationship notation alone? |
| Standards League·Agwansa | Connect the roles of multiple bodies consistently | Connection between the apprentice lineage and the council, and the order for dual vacancies, are undecided | Restrict candidates by apprentice lineage, then use council election or lineage recommendation plus council ratification; for dual authority, use independent acting roles or joint ratification | Does a vacancy in one seat avoid automatically absorbing the authority of another seat? |
| Acting Role and Regency | Distinguish vacancy duties from assisting the existing leader | Registering party, scope changes, revocation, and duration are undecided | Maintain only the pre-registered scope, or change the scope by a separate, lawful institutional decision | Does it block unregistered persons and out-of-scope requests? |
| Legitimacy | Shows the grounds for ratification, cooperation, and disputes | Facts, figures, and thresholds to record are undecided | Display only event records of ratifications and disputes, or add separately approved figures and fixed classifications | Does a change in personal support leave the fixed thresholds themselves unchanged? |
| Title, Ratification, and War Effects | Links D10''s judgment of rights and beneficiaries, D16''s casus belli and war termination, and D17''s negotiation and settlement | Actual titles, territories, superior contracts, ratifying bodies, and detailed termination effects are undecided | Confirm the right through an institution''s recognition document, or ratify separately after the parties agree | Does it distinguish the attacker from the beneficiary and terminate only the casus belli that remains valid? |
| Landless Organization | Handle the remaining identities and obligations lawfully | A direction for what remains has been chosen, but the representative, custodian, and dissolution procedure are undecided | Keep the existing valid representative, or designate a limited custodian through a lawful institutional procedure | Does it avoid deleting the organization merely because of death or loss of territory, and avoid creating an unauthorized representative? |
| Organization Values and Information Disclosure | Designs support judgments and record access | The value list is a proposal, and access rights are undecided | Show only public ratifications and vacancies, or disclose a detailed ledger by authority level; distinguish value changes as event records versus separate policy amendments | Are name search, ID identity, and the boundary of non-public information maintained? |
| Health and Derived Abilities as Office Qualifications | The qualification proposer links personal calculations to institutional qualifications | No approval of the original Basic or of the relevant calculations | Use only independent institutional qualifications without health figures, or read separate personal results after the original and calculations are approved | Does it avoid replacing the original with public excerpts or Lite, and avoid inserting unapproved scores? |

There are no approved numbers for legitimacy scores, exam scores, quorums, acting periods, or new costs. Whether health or derived abilities will be used as office qualifications, and if so by what criteria candidates will be distinguished, has not yet been decided.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d10', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d10', 'D10 Title, Rights, Justification, Ratification', '', 'proposal', 117, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d10.md', '# D10 Title, Rights, Justification, Ratification

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/power-and-institutions/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

This document describes situations in which a person holds a right but does not control the land, wars that support another person''s claim, and situations in which rights and on-site takeover diverge after a war. Existing approved provisions are kept, and the undecided institutions for each state are separated into the options that follow. The options are not adopted rules. Dependent scopes for which the original Basic''s identification, rules, errata, and personal judgment inputs have not been secured remain unclosed.

## 1. Actors and Player Decisions

The player is a single main-body character, and NPCs follow the same action contract. There is one world time. Moving between screens does not duplicate the main body or rewind time. Combat has no pause, speed control, or command queue. The player decides on submitting their own grounds, requesting mediation or support, receiving lawful grants, issuing commands with authority, and making demands and offers in negotiation proposals. Having a right does not mean one can declare war on behalf of a state.

| Actor | Decisions Available | Separately Required Conditions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unaffiliated, Officeless Person | Request employment, joining, or mediation; request war support for one''s own claim | Joining first is required to receive a state fief grant; command authority is required to lead state troops |
| State-Affiliated, Officeless Person | Service or duty contracts under the institution; petitions for grants | Declaring war in the state''s name, disposing of public assets, and ratifying records require separate authority |
| Claimant / Succession Candidate | Submit evidence, assert succession eligibility, request support, and be a candidate beneficiary of a casus belli | A claim in itself is not ownership, possession, or effective control |
| Title Holder | Operation, income, and duties under the contract; claims for restoring rights; permitted sub-grants | Cannot immediately use facilities they do not possess, nor arbitrarily create titles of equal or higher rank |
| Record, Notarization, and Genealogy Officer | Compose claims on the orders of a monarch or office holder with authority | Requires the relevant duty authority and accessible record, genealogy, and ratification channels; direct operation by an officeless main body is not adopted |
| Head of State | Appointments, grant proposals, diplomacy, and war authorization under the state''s institutions | Does not gain another state''s ratification authority or an unconditional right of revocation |
| Occupier | Security, supply, and negotiation within the area actually controlled | Occupation alone does not yield lawful inheritance, ownership of institutional treasuries, or the cooperation of residents |

An appointed commander issues orders within a valid command scope over all squads assigned to them. Each squad leader receives the order immediately and autonomously chooses to comply, partly comply, or act independently. Compliance and actual results are recorded separately, and the evaluator is the original issuing commander who sent the order. Even if the commander is replaced, the original issuer and history of existing orders, evaluations, and memories are preserved, and the remaining orders are rechecked under the new commander''s authority. A title or office tier does not create command authority over another commander''s units. Any unit that actually arrives at an ongoing battle may join it as reinforcement. Approval or dispatch alone does not make a unit appear on site, and reinforcement participation is not restricted by tally boundaries, initial composition, departure point, or rank. This is a linking condition to avoid confusing the transfer of rights with participation in battle.

## 2. Distinguishing Qualification from Rights

Every person may hold a list of right instances if they have eligible grounds. The permanent ID of the person and the right, type, target, grounds, issuing or succession event, effect, ratifying party, version, and inheritance and extinction conditions are distinguished. The list is an interface, not permission for free trade or duplication. Rights can be searched by name, and identity is confirmed by a stable ID. Names or holders in old registries are historical evidence, not authority that determines the current world''s holders or institutions.

| Claim Status | Already Accepted Handling | Institutions Still Needed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Latent Claim | Attributed to the designated succession eligibility while the current holder is alive | Links between children''s eligibility, succession outside bloodlines, and office succession versus title succession |
| Enforced Claim | Basis for lawful succession and restoration of rights; when the holder dies, it becomes an unenforced claim passed to eligible children | Eligible children, and each state''s confirmation, marriage, and succession exceptions |
| Unenforced Claim | Not inherited separately; a white peace in the war over that claim turns it into an enforced claim | Conditions for claim extinction, loss of eligibility, and voiding a war by casus belli |

Title-holding rights follow that title''s succession system. Eligibility for public office, voting rights, and technical qualifications are not the same kind of thing as a title claim. Improving or worsening personal relationships changes the grounds for support or acceptance; it does not mean the boundaries of a fixed comparison category, such as a rank tier, have shifted. High trust also cannot substitute for physical impossibility or lack of authority.

Marriage follows each state''s political, property, and succession systems, as well as the consent and confirmation of the parties. Establishing a marriage alone does not grant new claims or territory, nor does it share all of the spouse''s claims. Using a spouse''s existing claim, children inheriting a claim, and fabricating a claim separate from succession rights are different paths. When a death, imprisonment, or loss of office occurs, check for each alliance, property, confirmation, and heir effect whether it is maintained, terminated, or must be re-confirmed.

## 3. Before, During, and After an Action, and Failure

Before an action, check in order: the target''s existence and version, feasibility (such as whether the actor is alive and not imprisoned), authority, the actual location and communication route, resources and exclusive reservations, and the other party''s will and institutional procedures. The player is shown the satisfied conditions and the blocked conditions, distinguished by what the current body''s knowledge, records, and access to reports and qualifications can confirm. Hidden facts are not leaked through the stated reason; they are explained as a lack of confirmed grounds. Unknown prices or state systems are not filled in as success conditions.

Claim fabrication is divided into gathering and drafting evidence → composing the claim → recognition of its effect or rejection. Fabrication takes time, and only a structure that checks duration, cost, and risk of detection has been approved. Seoul''s numbers and success determinations are undecided. Completing a fabrication is neither possession nor a transfer of holding rights. If a death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the remaining work is checked again. Even if it is canceled by a world event or the person in charge dies, materials already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed; only the remaining reservations are released according to the contract.

A grant proceeds through draft → authority and target check → recipient consent → required confirmation or ratification → rights ledger change → operational takeover. One must join the state before receiving a grant, and a head may grant only titles lower than their own. The limit of two directly held duke-tier titles is an existing confirmed value. No new title is created where no actual title ID, domain, or rank exists. The holder is not changed before the required confirmation. If on-site takeover is blocked after confirmation, the result remains as rights held but not recovered, or takeover incomplete.

An established grant removes the right from the grantor''s list and registers the same title ID to the recipient. Delegation leaves the original holder in place and entrusts only specified operations, signing, or command. Appointment grants public office authority. Buildings, institutional vaults, public stockpiles, and unit command do not transfer automatically unless their inclusion is explicitly stated. Revocation requires contract clauses, evidence of violation, and a confirmation procedure. Unlawful seizure is not a normal grant but a change of control and a rights dispute.

Switching the current body, replacing the head of a state or institution, and succession to individual titles and property are separate events. A lawful procedure reselecting the same living person is also not inheritance upon death. Even if the body changes because of permanent imprisonment, the imprisoned person remains alive. The previous body''s rights, authority, skills, and friends are not copied to the new body. The death of a proxy does not extinguish the original holder''s rights. A vacancy is not an automatic dissolution of a state or institution.

## 4. Casus Belli, War Score, and Negotiation

Holding a casus belli does not grant authority to declare war, troops, or command. Every war requires a validly registered casus belli at its start. The actor''s authority, the opponent, the target title and domain, the beneficiary, eligibility, cost, objectives, and the effects of attacker victory, defender victory, white peace, surrender, and voiding are all fixed. Restoring a recognized right, supporting another eligible person''s claim, and remedying a breach of an approved higher-level contract are bundles for registration review, not a finished list of casus belli. Personal debts, passage disputes, and water supply cutoffs are not automatically made into grounds for conquest.

When supporting another person''s claim, the beneficiary named in that casus belli is the standard. The attacker is not given all the territory. The new holder and higher-level contracts are checked. A war refers to the casus belli version at the time it began, and if the grounds change, the voiding conditions of that casus belli are explicitly checked. Occupation outside the objectives does not widen the scope of transfer. Effective control, legal confirmation, title rights, public office, and personal property are independent. The transfer of rights at termination does not mean residents'' cooperation, restoration of facilities, or completed integration.

War score W ranges from -100 to 100, and the opponent''s is -W. In a war between monarchs, the combat score is capped at 50. Capturing the actual head of that war is +100; capturing the designated primary, second, and third heirs is +50, +25, and +10; and occupying the capital is +10. For a single war or event ID, the addition applies only once, and the same person or the same event is not counted twice. An election candidate is not arbitrarily designated as the primary heir. +30 makes a white peace request possible but is not automatic acceptance. Voluntary surrender follows the result defined by each casus belli.

Occupation contribution is based on the scope and scale of the strongholds the defender holds. If full control of the casus belli''s objectives persists, cumulative contribution over time is gained, and if part of it is recovered, the conditions are checked again. Duration during which the attacker fails to secure the objectives contributes to the defending side. The denominator, partial occupation, duration, rate of increase, and peace acceptance modifiers are undecided. A tactical victory alone does not automatically dissolve an organization that holds no territory.

If W=100, the opponent''s forced acceptance of -100 is recorded, and the war is immediately terminated and settled with the victory and defeat effects of the casus belli. No additional captures, occupations, or property demands are accumulated during the response delay. If the casus belli is already void, the voiding conditions are checked first. +100 is not authority to forcibly acquire territory, assets, or persons outside the objectives.

A negotiation proposal has the war and proposal versions, signing authority, the target and beneficiary, a list of items, and the timing, deadline, and breach effects of performance. Items distinguish promises such as rights transfers, asset payments and returns, prisoner release and repatriation, passage, water supply, rations, escorts, confirmations, higher-level contracts, and ceasefires. Trading in persons, forced marriage, and general assignment without consent are not allowed. Each item''s ID, quantity, scope, authority to dispose, valuation inputs, price version, and feasibility are checked, and the same ledger asset is not demanded twice. The negotiation price score and the war score are separate records, and negotiation neither consumes nor manipulates the combat score.

In an interim negotiation, the player may reduce the demands within the casus belli or propose mutually agreed substitute payments or obligations. Assets, persons, and territory the opponent cannot dispose of, and third parties'' rights, cannot be included. Forced cession outside the objectives is not allowed, and a voluntary separate transaction is not disguised as a forced effect. The evaluation structure is the sum of the demanded items'' prices minus the sum of the offered items'' prices, and acceptance considers not only the score but also control, supply, succession, prisoners, obligations, relationships, memory, and feasibility. A proposal is not automatically accepted merely because its price is at or below the score. Unregistered items appear as ''valuation pending'' and block signing and settlement of a finalized proposal.

Time and combat continue during negotiation. If ±100 is reached before acceptance, forced termination by casus belli takes precedence and the unaccepted proposal is discarded. For acceptance, 100, and death at the same boundary, only one termination is determined by the confirmed event order and versions. Termination proceeds through input lock → recheck of casus belli and beneficiary → rights transfer → occupation retention or return → prisoner handling → reparations, ceasefire, and higher-level contracts → cancellation and recheck of orders in progress → event record. Deliveries, repatriations, and follow-up obligations after signing are not copied at the moment before completion.

## 5. Information the Player Reads and Domain Ownership

The rights and casus belli screens display only information that the current body''s knowledge, access to records and reports, and qualifications can confirm. Within that scope, the rights screen distinguishes a person''s searchable name and ID; the right''s type, target, grounds, effect, confirmation, and version; and the current legal holder versus the on-site possessor. Under the same access conditions, the casus belli screen distinguishes the attacker and beneficiary, the target domain, and each termination effect and voiding condition. The internal ledgers of rights, control, and world facts are maintained separately from what the character knows, and viewing a screen does not reveal hidden world facts. Unconfirmed information is shown as unknown or as lacking confirmed grounds, and even a rejection reason does not leak hidden facts. When the body changes, the display of reports and records is rechecked against the new character''s knowledge, qualifications, and access. If simultaneous changes conflict on the same ledger version, they are resolved by a deterministic acceptance order and other settlements are reviewed again. Resending the same event does not create a new right. A rules version mismatch or corrupted save is an explicit error, and there is no automatic renumbering, migration, or silent mixing of rules.

D10 covers the types of rights, claim states, and the boundaries of a casus belli''s target and beneficiary. The state and government domain (D09) owns the confirming bodies and each state''s qualifications, elections, and acting arrangements, and the grant domain (D11) owns grant, delegation, and revocation contracts. The house and relationship domain owns the establishment of marriage and relationships between the parties, and the succession domain owns death and body transitions. D15 (combat, command, squads, roster size) covers actual tactical combat, orders, squads, and capture results observed on site. D16 (diplomacy, war, sieges, prisoners) owns the campaign contracts for war, diplomacy, sieges, and prisoners, and checks whether an observed individual detention result corresponds to the institutional figure in that war, along with its campaign effects. D17 (negotiation, trade, peace, contracts) owns negotiation proposals, trades, peace, and performance contracts. The asset domain owns property attribution and delivery. This explanation of the connections does not approve any domain''s undecided costs or systems.

## 6. Cases Where Outcomes Diverge

**The Landless Claimant.** Garam, previously unaffiliated and holding no office, holds a claim, and another person possesses the objective. Garam asks an authorized head for support. Without a valid registered casus belli, no war is created, and the claim and possession remain as they are. Because the records officer has no valid order, fabrication does not begin either. Accepting the request and registering are separate conditions.

**Termination of a War Supporting Another''s Claim.** Previously, the attacker Narae is at war under a valid casus belli supporting the claim of the beneficiary Daon. Even if Narae occupies a stronghold outside the objectives and the score reaches +100, the casus belli and beneficiary are rechecked before settlement. If valid, only that version''s target is processed with Daon as the standard, and higher-level contracts are checked. Narae does not acquire all the territory, and no institutional vault is transferred. If it is already void, the voiding effects apply. Even when rights are transferred, on-site takeover and restoration may remain.

**Selection upon Death and Survival.** Before death, Raon holds pressed claims and institutional offices. On death, the path leading to an unpressed claim is checked for eligible children, but child eligibility requires input from the national institution. Institutional property, offices, and the player''s main body are not copied along with it. Under permanent detention, death inheritance is not opened. A living Raon retaking an office through lawful reselection is likewise not a claim-inheritance event.

**Interrupted Record Work.** Beforehand, the person in charge wrote up some materials and consumed supplies in authorized claim-construction work. If dismissal, death, or loss of access blocks the remaining work, completed progress and consumption are preserved, and the remaining reservations are released under the contract. Unfinished work is not turned into a completed claim or territory. Who takes over the remaining progress is an option below.

## 7. Confirmed Structures of the Two Originals and Differences from Seoul

The verified sources for CK3 are the preserved scripts of 1.20.0.3, build 25652598, and a community wiki from a separate point in time. Title hierarchy and de jure parents, actual holders, terrain, and offices are different scopes. A claim changes as a character–title relationship and is not evidence of a tradable inventory item. Fabrication leads, after work progress, to events, payments, and the granting of unpressed claims, and differs from occupation. A white peace strengthens only the weak claims on the target list, while defeat or surrender handles the corresponding claim. Title and vassal transactions from victory are separate from changes in occupation control. Automatic death inheritance, list storage, native enumeration, rounding, and active paths still have verification limits.

Differences among CK3 sources are also retained. The wiki description of a fixed 20% for duchy fabrication is not the same as a conditional weighted branch. The old path of requesting from a religious head is distinguished as a pressed claim, while Buy Claim is distinguished as payment in faith resources and an unpressed claim, and differs from the path of buying things with gold. Partial costs of supporting another house''s claim are not read as the final war declaration cost. The powerful-vassal condition for creating an empire is ''at least half'' under the default-disabled rule, not an unconditional majority-ratification law. We do not write that the confirmed title-tier symbol mapping is ''unrecovered,'' but we also do not say that native automatic calculation has been confirmed. PAM requests, old requests, empire rules, and de jure changes are different paths. These figures, probabilities, and religious institutions are not transplanted into Seoul.

Norland distinguishes the World 1284, Lords 1314, Actions 1318, and States 1262 wiki versions from individual beta/main developer announcements. Grant Title and recall are character roles, not Seoul''s grants of territorial titles. Region, city, and town terrain are not grantable legal titles. Gold plundering, relationship changes, and vassalization from ordinary city attacks are separate transitions, so victory is not read as ownership. Peace treaty expiration is not claim expiration, and lowborn and lesser lord are not the lowest tier of territorial titles. The emperor condition counts the number of states; it is not a title tier.

Norland''s vassal action restrictions, qualitative tribute dependence, and the order of demand, refusal, and victory are limited to their respective document versions. The Patches 54–56 MAIN announcement states that a neighboring state newly vassalized by a player cannot rebel for 3 in-game days. This is not a general protection that also prevents invasion or title recall. The timer start, refresh, native application, and correspondence to the actual build are unconfirmed. The Debt of Honor vassalization path in beta57 is separate evidence from a separate version. The pre-hire requirement in beta40 and the combined Hire and Grant Title command in beta54 are version changes. The inauguration occurring after a grant in main50–53 does not prove a transaction that requires legal ratification beforehand. Crown transfer and succession preservation are crown and role states, not a universal hierarchy of title rights. Tribute calculation, AI acceptance, hidden conditions, and the native application order are unconfirmed. Seoul''s pre-grant ratification is based on Seoul''s approval clause.

## 8. Unapproved Options: Purpose, Ideal, Gaps, Reasons, QA

The following are alternatives for filling real gameplay gaps, and none of them has been selected. The originals'' unconfirmed algorithms are not filled in with 0 or ''none.''

| Question and Purpose | Ideal Outcome | Specific Alternatives and Reasons for Choosing | Remaining Gaps and Verification Cases (QA) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GP-07 · National Institutions: Fixing the Target of Grant and Ratification | The current title and the jurisdictional institution are clear | A: ratification by each state''s central registry; B: ratification by the institution of the relevant region. A makes conflicts easy to compare, while B expresses decentralized institutions. | Actual IDs, territories, holders, superior contracts, and institutions are undecided. Check that when an old registry differs from current control, each is displayed and no nonexistent ID is generated. |
| GP-09 · State Family Law: Eligible Persons for Latent and Inherited Claims | The claim path is not confused with office and property eligibility | A: among children recognized by the state, those eligible under the relevant title law; B: after recognizing the parent–child relationship, eligibility is finalized by a separate title ratification. A has a simpler procedure, while B separates family law from rights law. | Rules for biological children, adoptees, consent, marriage, and non-bloodline succession are undecided. Both alternatives preserve the pressed→unpressed child path and the non-inheritance of unpressed claims; check that automatic sharing with a spouse is prohibited. |
| T68-D09-COUNTRY-LAWS · National Institutions (Unapproved): Handling Election Vacancies, Ratification, Regency, and Acting Authority | Confirm the voters, candidates, assembly quorum, tie handling, and basis of legitimacy, and distinguish provisional authority from formal tenure | A: representatives from each state''s electoral roll confirm the assembly''s quorum requirements and elect, ties are sent back to a re-election procedure, and a registered acting officer performs only the specified custody and handover duties. B: an assembly of eligible representatives from each state nominates candidates, a separate ratifying body adjudicates tie disputes, and a registered regent performs only the specified delegated duties. A emphasizes tracing electoral responsibility, while B emphasizes separating election from ratification. Neither option adopts a replacement for each state''s existing election, hereditary, or appointment method. | Actual voter and candidate rolls, assembly quorum rules, the body and procedure for resolving ties, the registration, termination, and signing scope of regents and acting officers, and the evidence and effect of ratification and legitimacy are undecided. Do not invent quorum numbers, legitimacy thresholds, or state law. QA: check that missing voters, a failed quorum, or a tie never result in writing that a formal transfer of rights has taken place; that institutional continuity is preserved during a vacancy; that the authority check blocks a registered acting officer who requests an out-of-scope grant or war approval; and that regency ratification does not turn into an automatic transfer of personal property or titles. |
| T68-D10-RIGHTS-LIFECYCLE: Transfer and Extinction | Can explain the effect and reason for each right and the validity of the current casus belli | A: review per event of the basis''s extinction; B: renewal review per type of right and re-check upon extinction of the basis. A avoids unnecessary deadlines, while B expresses a record-renewal system. | Transferable types, inheritance exceptions, expiration periods, and ratifying bodies are undecided. If extinction overlaps with +100, check validity first; check that no common success roll or common TTL is created. |
| Duration, Cost, and Risk of Claim Construction, and Interrupted Progress | Record access and the responsible authority are linked to actual costs | A: cost per ratification stage and a successor''s re-check of existing records; B: full cost of the work contract and performance of the remaining work after approval of the takeover. A emphasizes responsibility per stage, while B emphasizes contract predictability. | Amounts, durations, detection effects, and successor takeover conditions are undecided. Check that direct manipulation by those without office is blocked, that what is already consumed or completed is preserved, and that unfinished work is not turned into a right. |
| GP-13: Casus Belli, Negotiation, Truce, and Reparations | The scope of coercion and voluntary transactions can be compared | A: an itemized price list per casus belli; B: a per-state evaluation table fixed to the casus belli version. A emphasizes comparative consistency, while B expresses institutional differences. | Casus belli registration, costs, truces, reparations, and acceptance adjustments are undecided. Check that automatic acceptance at V≤W is prohibited, that signing unregistered items is blocked, and that beneficiaries and attackers are distinguished. |
| Occupation and Objective Time Score | Control recovery and the basis of the score can be explained | A: sum of contributions per approved stronghold; B: sum of contributions weighted by approved scale. A suits comparing strongholds, while B suits expressing differences in scale. | The denominator, partial occupation, duration, and growth rate are undecided. Check that cumulative conditions are re-checked when part of an objective is recovered, and that the cap of 50 on battles between rulers is preserved. |
| Non-Bloodline Succession, Special Regimes, and Unaffiliated Armies | Only key figures under the actual institution correspond to the capture score | A: only registered institutional successors correspond to existing items; B: regimes with no correspondence get a separate effect approved. A minimizes misidentification, while B suits expressing non-hereditary institutions. | Correspondence inputs and separate effects are undecided. Election candidates are not arbitrarily made successors of a state. Check that the scope of claims on the lowest-tier title and the independent casus belli for each of the four tiers are also separately approved. |
| T68-D11-CONTRACT-RECALL: Grant, Delegation, and Recall Handover | Distinguish legal rights from on-site takeover responsibility | A: staged handover after ratification; B: contractual takeover confirmation after ratification. A emphasizes visibility of on-site disputes, while B emphasizes tracing contractual responsibility. | Obligations, consent, expiration, costs, and recall conditions are undecided. Check that the original holder is retained upon delegation, that rights are preserved after the agent''s death, and that automatic transfer of treasuries and troops is prohibited. |

The original Basic dependency judgment and the generation of personal combat in sieges and captures are not closed by these options. Organizing the approval structure so that it can be read, finalizing state-specific policies and figures, and completing implementation are different outcomes.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d11', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d11', 'D11 Grants, Delegation, Recall, and Vassals — Design Document for Readers', '', 'proposal', 118, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d11.md', '# D11 Grants, Delegation, Recall, and Vassals — Design Document for Readers

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/power-and-institutions/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

- This document explains the confirmed scope of the existing rules on grants, vassals, and title rights. Institutions, contracts, quantities, and formulas not yet decided are left in Chapter 12.

Notation rules. Each paragraph of this document carries one of the following.

- 【Approved】 — This is the existing scope exactly as already accepted. This document does not widen it by a single character.
- 【Undecided】 — An item that cannot be decided without any basis. It is handled in Chapter 12 as a pending decision item.

The Crusader Kings III (hereafter CK3) and Norland records in Chapter 11 are **comparisons against installed scripts, documents, and announcements**, not observations of engine execution. Neither side''s figures have been adopted as Seoul''s coefficients, probabilities, or formulas.

For the numerical limit on grants, the existing **limit of two directly held duke-tier titles** is kept. Costs, durations, and probabilities not yet decided are not filled in with implicit values.

## 1. What This Domain Covers

This document covers **how title and office authority is divided, entrusted, and taken back, and what contract establishes a vassal relationship**. A player continues the campaign through one main-body character, and other characters in the world are NPCs using the same action contract. Grants, appointments, delegations, and recalls can all be proposed only with the authority that main body actually holds, and one cannot switch screens to turn back time or duplicate the main body. World time is one.

This document restates the already-approved policies below so they can be used in actual play decisions. Each item is attributed to the approved decision it comes from.

- The four kinds of draft: a Grant changes the holder of a title''s rights; an Appointment confers public-office authority; a Delegation leaves the original holder in place and entrusts only the stated operation, signing, and command; a Recall is a proposal that requires an existing contract''s recall clause, evidence of breach, and ratification.
- Order of gates: the holder does not change until the recipient has consented and the state has ratified.
- Grant limit: a grantor may give out only titles lower than their own, and may directly hold no more than two ducal-rank titles. No title is created where there is no actual title identifier, domain, or rank.
- Membership requirement: to receive a grant from an existing state, the recipient must already be a member of that state.
- Separation of axes: office, title, effective control, command, and property are different axes, and office tier is only a classification for comparison.
- Principle of explicit inclusion: buildings, institutional treasuries, public stockpiles, and unit command are not transferred automatically unless their inclusion is stated. Writing "included" is merely a declaration of the needed scope, not proof of the authority to transfer. The ownership, management, use, and command rights of each target, and the valid authority to transfer them, are checked separately. Including an institutional treasury does not turn it into personal property.
- Separation of ledgers: the ledger of title holdings, rights, obligations, and superior contracts and the effective-control ledger are independent; occupation or transfer changes only on-site control.
- The substance of a vassal contract: a vassalage is established not by a state-relations number but by a contract with signatories and clauses.
- Resolution of conflicts: if two grants overlap on the same title in the same version, only one is accepted, in a deterministic acceptance order.
- Obligations of identifiers and versions: identifiers are permanent; revision conflicts and save errors are surfaced explicitly; there is no automatic renumbering, automatic transfer, or silent mixing of rules.

No values that convert policy into points, costs, or durations have been approved. There are no numbers for the cost of a grant, the duration of a delegation, the deadline for a recall, or the evaluation or obligations of a vassal. Items marked [Undecided] are treated as awaiting decision in Chapter 12.

## 2. Actors

| Actor | What They Do |
| --- | --- |
| Principal Character (Player) | Create drafts and propose approval only with a grant authority you actually hold, an office''s signing authority, or valid command authority. |
| NPCs (all other characters) | They use the same action contract as the player character. The grantor, recipient, proxy, and ratification participants all act within this contract. |
| Grantor (a member holding grant authority) | Creates the draft. May give out only titles lower than their own, and may directly hold no more than two ducal-rank titles. |
| Recipient | Receives the draft and consents or refuses. Without consent, the draft does not proceed to ratification. |
| Ratifying Body (varies by state) | Approves or declines according to that state''s ratification procedure. Who this body is has not yet been decided for each state. [Undecided] |
| Original Holder (Delegator) | Does not lose their rights through a delegation. Entrusts only the operation, signing, and command within the stated scope. |
| Proxy (the one receiving the delegation) | Acts validly only within the entrusted scope. Even if the proxy dies, the original holder''s rights remain. |
| Authorized Record, Notary, and Genealogy Officer | Acts on the orders of a ruler or office-holder to carry out title-domain claim operations (gathering/drafting evidence → constructing the claim → recognizing/rejecting its effect). A direct-operation path for a player character without an office is not adopted. |
| Acting Deputy (a person registered in the institution) | Acts as deputy only for persons and within the scope already registered in that institution. A vacancy alone does not create a deputy; the procedures for filling a vacancy, such as election, succession, or appointment, follow that state''s institutions. |
| Occupier / Disputed Holder | Actually controls the site. Occupation alone does not confer lawful inheritance, ownership of an institutional treasury, or the cooperation of residents. |

A state name shows only the name of a seat. The state names used in the cases below (e.g., the Government of the Republic of Korea, the Floodgate Bureau, the Transshipment Bureau, the Eastern Office) are seat names already listed in the per-state table of the existing rules; the grantors, recipients, and title identifiers in the cases are placeholders for reading these rules, not approved characters, titles, or quantities. Actual title identifiers, holders, and superior contracts await confirmed wiki input (12.1).

## 3. Decisions the Player Actually Makes

1. **Choosing the kind of draft** — Choose one of four, Grant, Appointment, Delegation, or Recall, and create the draft. This choice is a named decision, and the four produce different outcomes (Chapter 4).
2. **Finalizing the draft''s contents** — Specify the recipient, the target title and version, the superior contract, the rights, obligations, and duration, the ratifying body, and the conditions for handing over relics, documents, and keys. For buildings, institutional treasuries, public stockpiles, and unit command, confirm separately whether each is included and who has authority to transfer the relevant rights. Custody, holding keys, or public-office authority alone does not create ownership.
3. **Responding as the recipient** — Consent to or refuse the draft you have received. Without consent, it does not proceed to the ratification stage.
4. **Specifying the scope of a delegation** — A delegation is established only if the entrusted scope and its basis are written down. Actions outside the scope are not justified by that delegation.
5. **Choosing the basis for a recall proposal** — A recall is a separate proposal that requires an existing contract''s recall clause, evidence of breach, and a ratification procedure. There is no way to recall a contract that has no such clause unconditionally.

The player cannot arbitrarily create the ratifying body of a state where it has not been determined, nor treat its procedure as completed. However, if the player character actually holds a legitimate ratifying office and authority under the state''s institutions, they may approve or decline using that authority. An undetermined institution is not a rule that uniformly bars the player from a ratifying role. There is no path for a player character without a military office to directly alter a title-domain claim. A vacancy does not automatically cause delegation, inheritance, appointment of a deputy, or absorption of authority.

The action screen distinguishes `Ganeung` (possible), `Seonhaengjogeon Bujok` (insufficient prerequisites), `Gwonhan Eopseum` (no authority), `Eungdap Daegi` (awaiting response), and `Jedo Mijeong` (system undetermined), and displays the basis for each (Chapter 8).

## 4. Eligibility — Actual Checks by Path

Whether any action is permitted follows a fixed order of checks: **target existence and version → feasibility such as being alive or not detained → authority → actual location/delivery → resource and exclusive reservation → the other party''s will/institutional procedure**. An action that is physically impossible is never made to succeed through high trust.

**Grant (transfer of title rights)** — The grantor must be a member holding grant authority, may give out only titles lower than their own, and may directly hold no more than two ducal-rank titles. The recipient must already be a member of that state, and the target title identifier and its domain must already exist. No arbitrary title is created where there is no actual title identifier, domain, or rank. The required inputs for a draft are the grantor, the recipient, the state membership status, the target title and version, the grant authority, the superior contract, the rights, obligations, and duration, the ratifying body, and the conditions for handing over relics, documents, and keys.

**Appointment (conferring public-office authority)** — Gives the signing, command, and operation authority of a public office. It does not change the title holder. Authority arising from an appointment stops, and only that authority, when the post is lost.

**Delegation (proxy with a defined scope)** — Leaves the original holder in place and entrusts only the stated operation, signing, and command. It is established only when the entrusted scope and its basis are written down. Delegated command is valid only within that scope.

**Recall (reversal of a contract)** — Requires all of an existing contract''s recall clause, evidence of breach, and a ratification procedure. If there is only an allegation of breach and no recall clause or procedure, no automatic recall is established. Recalling only the relic without revoking the delegation opens a betrayal issue.

**Signing a vassal contract** — A vassalage is established not by a state-relations number but by a contract with signatories and clauses. A single line of the contract specifies the superior and subordinate titles, the proposer, signatory, and ratifier on each side, the distinction between attribution to the office and attribution to the person, and the clauses actually to be performed among water supply, passage, rations, escort, record ratification, and levy. The public faith is written down, and a hidden faith is kept only in the non-public ledger. Also specified are both sides'' culture keys, the ritual clauses actually to be attended, and one of automatic continuation, re-ratification, or termination for when a signatory dies or loses their post. A contract missing the public faith, culture key, or ritual calendar opens a re-ratification issue. The same goods cannot be bound to two contracts at once, nor the same relic placed in two shrines at once. A levy clause does not summon combat troops; it only determines to whom the mobilization roster, armory keys, and desertion ledger are opened. A contract that places a levy on merchant tradespeople is rejected. A station held directly by the chief is directly administered; a station given to a named vassal is delegated.

**Realm of the Central Record Culture** — In that realm, a grant is void if record ratification or relic inspection is missing. Even when it becomes void, the title is neither newly created nor passed to someone else.

The boundaries are also clear. High tier alone does not open grants, appointments, and command all at once. Holding a public office does not make an institutional treasury personal property or open command of all troops. A membership record alone does not establish a marriage, and a marriage record alone does not pass a title. Occupation or transfer changes only on-site control, not the legal holder.

## 5. States and Transitions

### 5.1 State Sequence of a Grant 【Approved】

Draft → authority/target check → recipient''s consent → ratification/approval → rights ledger change → operational takeover → completion or dispute.

- The default policy is not to change the holder before the required ratification.
- If the on-site takeover cannot be made after approval, it remains as `rights held·unrecovered/takeover incomplete`. Holding the rights and having taken possession of the place are different things.

### 5.2 Change to the Rights Ledger 【Approved】

Once ratification is recorded, that title identifier is removed from the grantor''s holdings and registered in the recipient''s list **under the same identifier**. No copy is created. Subordinate contracts, taxes/water supply/passage, and ritual obligations record the signatory and the succession policy. Buildings, institutional treasuries, public stockpiles, and unit command are not transferred automatically unless the inclusion of each is stated. Even included targets must satisfy the relevant ownership and transfer authority and contract. Taking over management, use, and command authority differs from transferring ownership, and without an actual rights input for the target, the transfer is not confirmed.

The ledger of title holdings, rights, obligations, and superior contracts and the effective-control ledger are independent. The two ledgers display unrecovered fiefs, disputes, and occupants together. A claim to a title differs from holding the title, and a claim is not converted into a grant.

### 5.3 State of a Delegation 【Approved】

A delegation has one of the states **valid, suspended, expired, or recalled**, and stores the triggering event and the scope with it. A delegation does not transfer ownership. Even if the proxy dies, the original holder''s rights do not disappear. Whether a delegation expires before arrival is rechecked by the part that handles movement and arrival, and this domain does not set an expiry deadline. [Undecided]

### 5.4 Change of Signatory in a Vassal Contract 【Approved】

A vassal contract specifies in advance what happens when a signatory dies or loses their post: **one of automatic continuation, re-ratification, or termination**. Which of these is left unwritten differs from contract to contract, and a contract missing the public faith, culture key, or ritual calendar opens a re-ratification issue. The actual wording of the clauses and the costs and deadlines have not been approved. [Undecided]

### 5.5 Conflicts and Versions 【Approved】

If two grants of the same title overlap in the same version, only one is accepted, according to a deterministic acceptance order. Simultaneous transfers of rights are evaluated against the same ledger version. Resending the same event ID produces no second result. If a death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact intervenes between approval and execution, the remaining handovers are checked again.

### 5.6 Storage and Version Obligations 【Approved】

A result ID that has already been applied is not applied again. A rule version mismatch or a corrupted save is an explicit error. Time and succession rules that differ between documents are not mixed, and new rules are not mixed in without an approved migration.

## 6. Approved Judgments and Recording Obligations

Whether a grant is permitted is determined only by the actual per-route checks in Chapter 4. A formula that converts policies into scores has not been approved. What has been approved is the recording obligation below.

- Record the draft''s required inputs (grantor, recipient, state membership status, target title and version, grant authority, superior contract, rights, obligations, and term, ratifying body, and delivery conditions for relics, documents, and keys).
- When rights change, record that the title was removed from the grantor''s holdings and registered to the recipient under the same title ID. No copies are made.
- Record subordinate contracts and tax, water-supply, passage, and ritual obligations together with their signatories and succession policy.
- Buildings, institutional vaults, public stockpiles, and unit commands whose inclusion is not explicitly stated are recorded as not transferred.
- A delegation is stored together with its status (valid, suspended, expired, revoked), the event it is based on, and its scope.
- Record the title-holding ledger and the effective-control ledger separately, and display unrecovered domains, disputes, and occupants together.
- Resending the same event ID does not produce a new result.
- If a death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact intervenes after approval, record the fact that the remaining handovers were checked again.
- IDs are permanent, and revision conflicts and storage errors are stated explicitly.

## 7. Failure and Preservation

**Even if it fails, the holder remains unchanged.**

- If the required ratification is missing, the holder remains unchanged. Because there was no ratification, the draft remains without having transferred any rights.
- In a region with a central-record culture, if record ratification or relic inspection is missing, the grant is void. Even when it is void, no new title is created and the title is not passed to anyone else.
- If a required approver dies before ratification, the accepted sentence gives that death no special consequence. Who approves in place of that vacancy has not been decided 【Undecided】.
- A draft sent to a recipient who has not joined is rejected.

**Facts that have already occurred are preserved.**

- If a death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, only the remaining handovers are checked again; materials already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released according to the contract.
- Even if the person who received a delegation dies, the original holder''s rights do not disappear.
- Seizing only the relic without revoking the delegation opens a betrayal issue. Possessing the relic alone does not revoke the delegation.
- Seizing something illegally is not a grant. It is recorded as a change in on-site control and as a rights dispute. A higher tier alone does not immediately eliminate another person''s private property or position.
- Even if a grant is approved, buildings, institutional vaults, public stockpiles, and unit commands not listed in the draft remain where they were.

**A vacancy creates nothing by itself.**

- A vacancy does not create a delegation. When a living person loses a position, only that position''s signing, command, and operation stop; that person''s private life, movement, and separately valid contracts remain under their own capacities.
- A vacancy alone does not trigger inheritance, transfer, appointment of a deputy, or absorption of authority. A deputy is permitted only for persons and scopes already registered in that institution. If there is no registration, it remains a vacancy and a succession dispute, and no unnamed leader is created.
- Even if a leader dies, the institution and the state do not disappear on that fact alone.

## 8. Boundaries of Information and Authority

- The action screen distinguishes `ganeung` (possible), `seonhaeng jogeon bujok` (prerequisites lacking), `gwonhan eopseum` (no authority), `eungdap daegi` (awaiting response), and `jedo mijeong` (institution undetermined), and displays the grounds. It does not leak hidden facts through the stated reason, and explains them as `hwagindoen geungeo bujok` (confirmed grounds lacking). A conferral in a country whose ratifying body has not been determined is shown as `jedo mijeong`.
- A vassal contract states the public faith, and a hidden faith is kept only in the private ledger. The public contract screen does not read the private ledger.
- Occupation and cession events change on-site control, but resident cooperation, operation, stabilization, integration, and ratification are separate stages. A rights transfer at the end of a war does not imply that resident cooperation or facility restoration is complete. The screen displays both ledgers together (unrecovered domains, disputes, occupants).
- A lookup does not create conferral authority. A title holder''s `ganeunghan haengdong` (possible actions) are only the contractual operation, income, and obligations of their own title, claims for restoring rights, and permitted sub-conferrals; immediate use of a facility they have not taken possession of, and arbitrary creation of equal or higher titles, are actions that require additional authority.
- Claimants and succession candidates may only submit evidence, assert eligibility for succession, request support, and act as beneficiary candidates of a designated cause; they cannot use a claim as holding rights or on-site control. A claim to a title is different from holding the title.

## 9. Cross-Domain Ownership

D11 owns the framework of grants, appointments, delegations, revocations, and vassal contracts, and each domain owns the substance of the items in the table below. D11 only reads those values and does not redefine them.

| Domain | What D11 reads | What D11 does not do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D01 Characters and Identity | Character IDs and versions, survival and capacity to express intent | Carrying out the identity check itself |
| D03 Actions and Commands | Procedure for issuing orders within a delegated command scope | Rules for transmitting, receiving, and accepting remote commands |
| D06 Movement and Contact | Actual delivery routes for relics, documents, and keys; rechecking delegation expiry before arrival | Travel time and cost formulas |
| D07 Relationships and Memory | Attribution of relationships and evaluations when a betrayal issue or re-ratification issue is opened | Relationship change functions, increments and decrements, and decay |
| D08 Houses and Marriage | Facts distinguishing membership records from marriage records | Rules for establishing marriages and their effects |
| D09 States and Legitimacy | Each state''s ratification procedures and registration of vacancy deputies (awaiting input) | Actual definitions of the institutions of the 16 states |
| D10 Titles and Casus Belli | Title-right instances, and the distinction between claims and causes | Superior-contract conditions for claims, and registered values and deadlines for causes |
| D12 Succession and Main Body | The fact of death and the existence of a succession designation | Deciding who the heir is |
| D13 Assets and Delivery | Transfer of property whose inclusion is explicitly stated, and delivery status | Detailed rules for ownership succession and delivery procedures |
| D16 War and Prisoners | The list of rights transfers at the end of a war | Settlement order, occupation denominators, and prisoner rules |
| D17 Negotiation and Contracts | Signing Procedure for Vassal Contract Clauses | Price Table and Acceptance Function |
| D18 Time and Saving | Ledger Version and When the Deterministic Acceptance Order Takes Effect | Design of time event ordering and save format |

## 10. Four Cases — State Transition Design

The four cases below are a **design output** that applies the existing rules'' list of verification cases and the transition rules from the source sheet. They are not execution proofs, and the names and identifiers that appear are placeholders from Chapter 2. Each case includes before, request, event, and after.

### 10.1 Unaffiliated Recipient Rejected — Eligibility Failure

- **Before**: Grantor A is a member of the Gatekeeper State and holds one real title ID. Recipient B has never joined the Gatekeeper State.
- **Request**: A creates a grant draft naming B as the recipient.
- **Event**: The draft passes the target-existence and version steps of the check order, but the affiliation check confirms that B is not a member of that state. Because grants within an existing state can only be received by members, the draft cannot advance to the confirmation stage and ends in rejection.
- **After**: A''s holdings list and B''s status remain unchanged. Only the fact of rejection and its basis (no affiliation) are recorded. The draft does not remain pending, and B is not automatically enrolled.

### 10.2 Same Title, Two Grants — Version Conflict and Deterministic Acceptance

- **Before**: A is the holder of one existing title ID T- and has valid sub-grant authority. B and C are eligible members of the same state. Both drafts pass the target and authority checks, and each requires the recipient''s consent and confirmation through a valid state procedure. This is the premise of the case, in which institution and authority inputs are given; it does not fix the institutions of any particular state.
- **Request**: While following the sequence of draft → authority/target check → recipient consent → confirmation/ratification → rights ledger change, two rights transitions for the same title contend at the same ledger version.
- **Event**: Concurrent rights transitions are evaluated at the same ledger version, and since two grants overlap on the same title, only one is accepted through the deterministic acceptance order. The acceptance order is not set as a separate gate prior to consent or confirmation. Resending the same event ID does not produce a second result.
- **After**: Only on the approved side is the title ID removed from A''s list and registered in the recipient''s list under the same ID. No copy is created. The other draft cannot transfer the same right again, and the basis for the contention result and the acceptance order is retained. This case does not additionally define specific internal adjudication steps or failure state names. The specific priority formula that sets the order has not been approved (12.7).

### 10.3 Confirmation Rejected or Approver Dead — Original Holding Retained

- **Before**: For this case only, assume that a valid national institution and the necessary ratifying office and approver are already given. The draft in which Gap confers two existing title identifiers T- on Eul has completed the authority/target checks and consent, and only ratification remains. This premise does not establish the ratifying body of any real country. In a real country where the institution is undetermined, no approver or rejection event is invented, and it is left as `jedo mijeong` 【Unresolved】.
- **Request**: A confirmation is about to be recorded.
- **Event**: It splits into two branches. Branch A (confirmation rejected) — if the confirming body does not approve, the holder does not change. Branch B (approver dead) — if a required approver dies before confirmation, the death itself yields no special result; since there was no confirmation, the holder remains unchanged. Who approves in place of that vacancy has not been decided, and this document does not choose one.
- **After**: In both branches, A''s holdings list remains as it was, and only the draft and the fact of rejection or non-confirmation are recorded. No title is newly created or passed to a third party. Grounds for invalidity (omitted record confirmation or relic inspection in the Central Records Culture Sphere) likewise leave the same result.

### 10.4 Grant Succeeds, Key Not Handed Over — Separation of Right and Takeover, Contrast with Illegal Seizure

- **Before**: A''s draft to grant B three title IDs T- has passed both consent and confirmation. The draft did not include the state''s treasury or command of any unit, and the key to that title is still in A''s hands. At the same time, C seizes the title''s site by force.
- **Request**: The rights ledger change and the on-site takeover proceed.
- **Event**: Because the ratification is recorded, the three title identifiers T- are removed from Gap''s list and registered under the same identifiers in Eul''s list. No copy is made. The vault and the unit command were not in the draft, so they remain with the previous ownership and the previous command. Because the key has not been handed over, Eul remains in `gwolli boyu·insu miwan` (right held, takeover incomplete). Byeong''s occupation is not a conferral, so Byeong is not registered as a holder, and it remains as a record that on-site control has changed and as a rights dispute.
- **After**: B holds the right but has not obtained the site, and the title holding ledger and the effective control ledger show different facts side by side. Materials already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed, and only the remaining takeover is rechecked. The handover procedure itself is a matter for the side that handles custody and delivery.

## 11. External References — Crusader Kings III and Norland

The content below is a description confirmed from installation scripts, documents, and announcements. The engine execution of the two games, transaction atomicity, invalidation upon death, and contention handling were not observed. It is not adopted as a Seoul rule or coefficient.

### 11.1 Crusader Kings III

The title grant script declares the gates for actor, recipient, and selected title, along with automatic acceptance, but states that the actual grant is handled in code. Therefore the script alone cannot prove a raw grant transaction. The vassal transfer script distinguishes the selected vassal''s liege change from confirmed effects, and contract obligation changes use change amounts and guards. The PAM petition is a path that changes the legal liege title through a separate gate and letter choices. Changing the owner in a relic gift is not equivalent to a title-ID carrying system.

### 11.2 Norland

In the documentation, Grant/Revoke adds or removes lord status, and Pass Crown changes king status. The investiture ritual is described as coming after the grant. These roles and events are not equivalent to Seoul''s transferable territorial title ledger or the pre-grant confirmation gate. The vassal demand acceptance, refusal, and war outcomes, as well as the rules for tribute, puppets, and hostages, are likewise read only within the scope of that document version''s description. A hostage joining a city or a seizure by force does not prove a lawful transfer of title ownership.

## 12. Still Undecided

### 12.1 Title and Confirmation Inputs

The actual title IDs, ranks, domains, holders, superior contracts, and confirming bodies, the durations and costs of acquisition, stabilization, and integration, and the claimable range of the lowest-tier titles are undecided. Without real inputs, placeholders are not used as confirmed titles, and holders are not changed by occupation alone.

### 12.2 State-Specific Institutions

Each state''s rules for joining, signing, candidacy, suffrage, examinations, and confirmation; tie-breaking, quorum, proxy, and legitimacy rules; and the detailed institutions of the guild apprentice council and dual vacancy are undecided. Who can approve is judged only once the confirmation post and its authority are verified. Undecided institutions are not invented arbitrarily, and the legitimate post authority of the principal body is not excluded across the board either.

### 12.3 Grant, Delegation, and Revocation Contracts

The actual authority, duration, tax, supply, passage, and ritual obligations, consent and confirmation, evidence of violation, settlement of death, expiry, and conflict, and the takeover and return clauses are undecided. Unconditional revocation is not created without contract clauses, evidence, and a confirmation procedure. The rights of ownership, transfer, management, and command over each of buildings, treasuries, public stockpiles, and units also await actual inputs. An explicit statement of inclusion does not substitute for that authority.

### 12.4 War and Diplomatic Settlement

The detailed inputs for casus belli registration, costs, and superior contracts; the growth rate of target occupation time; the occupation denominator, prisoner release, and re-arrest; truce, reparations, siege, and peace prices and acceptance; and organizational survival or dissolution and atomicity contracts are undecided. The transfer of rights at the end of a war is distinguished from the actual handover.

### 12.5 Superior Contract Conditions for Claims

The conditions that a superior contract requires when someone exercises a title claim on another''s behalf are undecided. A claim is neither a holding right nor control of the site.

### 12.6 Actions in Progress by the Delegate

How actions a delegate had in progress are concluded upon expiry, revocation, or death is undecided. The recheck-after-approval rule does not substitute for that conclusion policy.

### 12.7 Undecided Figures and Detailed Conflict Procedures

The grant cost, delegation duration, revocation deadline, number of vassal evaluations and obligations, acquisition, stabilization, and integration durations and costs, contract clause prices, and confirmation quorum and tie handling are undecided. The rule that contention is resolved by a deterministic acceptance order at the same ledger version is maintained, but the specific priority formula, tie-break key, and internal adjudication steps are not defined.

## 13. Summary

- **What is approved**: the distinction among the four kinds of drafts (conferral, appointment, delegation, revocation); the consent and ratification gates, and the holder remaining unchanged before ratification; the requirement that the recipient be a member of the organization; the limits of lower titles only and two at duke rank; the separation of axes (office, title, effective control, command, property); the principle of explicit inclusion; removal and registration under the same identifier with no copies; the `gwolli boyu·insu miwan` state; the four states of delegation and preservation of the original right; the signatories and substantive clauses of the vassal contract and the three branches of signatory change; the clause, evidence, and ratification requirements for revocation; the betrayal issue when only a relic is revoked; the denial of conferral for unlawful seizure; the denial of automatic creation of vacancies and the allowance of registered acting holders only; the deterministic acceptance order and the invalidity of retransmitting an event identifier; permanent identifiers and explicit collisions; and the separation of the title-holding ledger from the effective-control ledger.
- **Not yet decided**: the title and confirmation inputs, state-specific institutions, actual contracts, war and diplomatic settlement, claim superior contracts, delegated in-progress actions, and the figures and detailed conflict procedures of Chapter 12.
- This document remains a proposal and does not claim that gameplay has been implemented.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d12', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d12', 'Succession, Death, and Principal Body Transition', '', 'proposal', 119, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d12.md', '# Succession, Death, and Principal Body Transition

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/power-and-institutions/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

One person''s death changes the body, the post, and the property through different procedures. Permanent detention does not open inheritance, and orders and wars in progress at the moment of death are rechecked against the remaining facts and authority. Figures and procedures not yet decided are left as undecided.

One player continues the campaign with one principal body character, and every other character is an NPC using the same action contract. The world has a single timeline. Time is not rewound by switching screens, and a body is not duplicated in another location.

## What This Domain Covers

One person''s death does not hand over the body the player controls, the post that person held, and the property that person owned all at once. The three are separate outcomes. Permanent detention does not substitute for death either. This domain covers the rules that handle these three separated branches individually, item by item; the conditions under which a new body is determined; the temporary command of the remaining squad; the recheck of scheduled tasks at the moment of death, detention, or dismissal; and the preservation rule that loading and changing bodies do not reset the saved history.

Who is next in the order of succession, how and by whom each country fills a vacant office, and how inheritance handles shares, residuals, and creditor priority have not yet been decided.

## Actors Involved

- **Player (Primary Character).** One surviving opening character. If that body dies or is permanently detained, the designated heir or the next character in the order of succession becomes the new primary body. The player engages with the world only through one primary body''s actions and the commands that are actually valid for that body.
- **NPC Characters.** They follow the same action conditions as the player. Designated heirs, characters in the order of succession, and candidates for offices, titles, and property all fall under the same contract.
- **Candidates and Eliminated Candidates.** Each item''s institution checks eligibility. A candidate who is not chosen keeps whatever claim that institution leaves them, and does not vanish by being eliminated.
- **Remaining Squad and Acting Squad Leader.** A squad that has lost its primary body picks an acting squad leader from among its rank-and-file soldiers. The approved rule goes only as far as this: the acting squad leader issues the same kinds of orders as a full hero commander, with weaker effects. How much weaker is not yet decided, and the acting leader does not automatically receive a full hero''s class or campaign role. The existence of an acting squad leader alone does not establish remote command by the new primary body.
- **States and Institutions.** They do not disappear when their head dies. The election, nomination, examination, hereditary, and confirmation procedures for a vacant office belong to the state''s institutions.
- **Ledger of Events, Rights, and Property.** Death, detention, will changes, office transfers, ownership transfers, delivery reservations, and receipt verifications are all records with an identifier and a version. Applying the same event twice does not duplicate property.

## Decisions the player actually makes

- **Designating an heir and changing the designation.** Set or change the designated heir through the institutional means the character already has. This document defines the outcome when a designation exists. A will change is a means of the same kind, and if a will change and a death occur at the same moment, the already-accepted event order and that institution''s effective time are used.
- **Continuing with a new body.** When a body dies or is permanently detained, a new body is determined and the view moves to wherever that character actually is. The new primary character starts with the relationships and skills that were already their own. The previous primary body''s rights, skills, friends, trust toward specific people, and unaccepted orders do not carry over through the body switch alone.
- **Checking whether to take over command of the remaining squad.** After the remaining squad picks an acting squad leader, check the actual command relationship between the new primary body and the squad, a valid command contract within the same faction, and a strategic communication channel. Then check the sender''s authority and the target and order versions, and a remote order is established only if there is a record that the squad received and accepted it. A valid contract alone does not automatically permit a remote order, and if any of the relationship, contact, receipt, or acceptance is unconfirmed, the result of that order is undetermined.
- **Accepting the re-check of scheduled work.** If a death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approved work and its execution, the scheduled work is checked again. Supplies already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released under the contract.
- **Taking part in the item-by-item procedures for offices and property.** The new body takes part in a procedure only if it actually holds candidate, voter, or beneficiary eligibility under that institution. Even if the new body is not eligible, the procedures for other eligible candidates or beneficiaries continue. Only when an item has no eligible person or procedure does it remain a vacancy, a dispute, or held inheritance, and any result whose required inputs are unconfirmed is undetermined.
- **Resetting war commitments item by item.** If a leader dies during an ongoing war, the new person in charge decides for each personal commitment and each office commitment whether to maintain, re-ratify, or terminate it.

## Who is eligible

- **Body switch.** For the body to change, there must be a designated heir or an already-accepted next person in the order of succession. Who is next in the order is for the family''s institution to decide and has not yet been decided. If there is no heir at all, that character''s path ends.
- **Offices, titles, and property.** An item passes on only if it has a candidate who meets its own institution''s requirements. Having no heir for one item does not create an heir for another. If there is no eligible candidate or no procedure, the office remains vacant or in a succession dispute. A nameless leader is not invented to fill the empty seat.
- **Permanent detention.** It makes no one an heir. A detained character is alive, and that state is not treated as death to open inheritance. A living captive keeps their own property. Interviews, letters, and negotiations permitted by the detention contract are possible, but free movement and fieldwork are not. Whether the office''s signing, command, and operation are possible, and the character''s other contracts, are each re-checked for feasibility, authority, and delivery conditions, and are not considered all frozen by detention alone. What counts as permanent detention has not yet been decided. The direction of returning to a released former primary body has been chosen to be allowed only with a separate, explicit, and authorized handover of identity and control, but the specific authority who would approve that handover has not yet been decided.
- **Identity.** Issuing an identity to a character is already accepted. That issuance does not determine how many ages, skills, locations, or rights there are. Becoming a new body does not create such numbers anew.

## Keep rights and states separate

Death stops the dead person''s new actions. When a living person loses an office, only that office''s signing, command, and operation stop, while the person''s private life, movement, and separately valid contracts continue to be judged under their own qualifications. A vacancy alone does not trigger inheritance, transfer, appointment of a deputy, or absorption of authority. A deputy is permitted only as a person already registered in the institution and within an already registered scope. Even when an office is empty, the institution and the country do not disappear.

The beneficiary of personal property is determined by reading the country''s institutions, a valid will, or a marriage or joint-property contract that has already been established. If none of those three names a beneficiary, the property is held inheritance. It is neither made ownerless nor divided equally. An institution''s property stays with the institution, and only the authority needed for management passes to the new office holder. Rights of use and leases are maintained, re-approved, or ended according to the clauses stating they can be succeeded. Debts and credits are judged by whether they belong to the person, the office, or the institution, and by their succession clauses, and debts are not passed unconditionally to every heir.

A change of ownership is different from the item coming into one''s hands. The order is to fix the inheritance list, mark disputes and collateral, transfer ownership, reserve the actual delivery, and verify receipt. Even if ownership is obtained, supplies in a warehouse occupied by the enemy do not move into the inventory. Personal equipment in transit cannot be used before it arrives. Even when inheritance is divided, the total quantity stays the same, and supplies that are reserved or held as collateral are marked separately.

For survivors, the facts of being family or lovers remain, along with the event of mourning. High trust does not create command authority, property rights, consent, or right of passage that did not exist. Even if a relationship changes, existing contracts change only through their termination clauses and action events.

The right to hold a title follows that title''s succession institution. A pressed claim passes to an eligible child as a not-yet-pressed claim. A not-yet-pressed claim is not inherited in that way. A spouse does not share all claims merely by being a spouse. The qualification of a candidate for public office, the right to vote, and skill qualifications are not treated as the same as a title claim.

A delegation leaves the original holder in place and entrusts only the stated operation, signing, and command. If the delegate dies, the original holder''s rights do not disappear. A delegation is not an appointment that fills a vacant office.

The alliance, property, confirmation, and succession effects of an already established marriage are examined separately for each effect at death, and are maintained, ended, or re-confirmed. They are not all ended at once. A marriage record alone does not establish a marriage. For a case where a marriage is explicitly not established, there is no marriage effect to pass on, but for a case where the applicable institution is unknown and so whether it was established is undetermined, it is not converted into not established or no effect. In such a case, both establishment and effects remain undetermined.

## States and Already Approved Rules

These are the states this domain reads and writes: primary body designation and succession order records; per-character relationships, skills, and trust; occupancy and history of offices and titles; for property items, the owner, beneficiary, basis of quantity, collateral, reservation, delivery location, and fulfillment status; squad command state; and event identifiers and ledger versions. A hero has only one state at a time among commander, attached, independent agent, and unavailable. A strategic squad that can move needs one full hero commander.

The already approved rules are these.

- **Three-Way Separation.** Succession of the player''s primary body, succession of the head of a state or institution, and succession of individual titles and property are handled as separate events. All of the dead primary body''s authority, skills, and friends are not copied to the character who becomes the new primary body.
- **Body Switch.** When the primary body dies or is permanently detained, the designated heir becomes the new primary body, or if there is none, the character in the order of succession does. If there is no heir, that character''s path ends. The viewpoint moves to the new primary body''s actual location, and the remaining squad elects a temporary rank-and-file squad leader. The previous primary body''s rights, skills, relationships, and unaccepted orders are not transferred by the switch alone. For a remote order to the remaining unit, the actual command relationship between the new primary body and the squad, the same-faction command contract, the communication channel, and the receipt and acceptance records are all checked.
- **Order of the Vacancy Procedure.** Confirm the basis of death or vacancy → halt the dead character''s new personal actions, or, for a living character who loses an office, halt only that office''s signing, command, and operation authority → snapshot the lists of offices, titles, property, and contracts → look up the succession institution for each item → check candidate eligibility → run procedures such as election, nomination, examination, or heredity → confirmation → transfer of rights item by item → delivery and takeover → record disputes.
- **Order of Property Transfer.** Fix the inheritance list → mark disputes and collateral → transfer ownership → reserve the actual delivery → verify receipt. Re-applying the same death event does not duplicate property.
- **Detention Rule.** Even if the primary body changes because of permanent detention, the detained character is alive. This is not treated as death to open inheritance. A detained person keeps their own property. Actions and office authority during detention are re-checked against the detention contract and the actual conditions of performance, and are not frozen across the board. Only the direction has been chosen that returning to the previous primary body after release requires a separate, explicit handover of authority, and the specific authority is undetermined.
- **Claim Succession.** A pressed claim passes to an eligible child as an unpressed claim, and an unpressed claim is not separately inherited. A spouse does not automatically share all claims.
- **Preservation of delegation.** When a deputy dies, the original holder''s right does not disappear. A vacancy does not take over that delegation.
- **Per-effect marriage adjudication.** On death, detention, or loss of office, the contracts for each effect (alliance, property, confirmation, succession) are checked separately and sorted into maintained, terminated, or needing reconfirmation; they are not all terminated at once. If the applicable institution is undetermined, then formation and effects are also undetermined, not "no effect."
- **War recheck.** When the leader of an ongoing war dies, the validity of the previously registered casus belli, the legal object of the right, and the beneficiary and exercise eligibility are rechecked first, and then the new responsible party is confirmed. In a claim-backed war, the approver and the beneficiary are distinguished, and the victory right goes to the eligible beneficiary. Personal promises and office promises are adjudicated item by item for maintenance, reratification, or termination, and succession is not used to erase debts, truces, or damage records. The rule for choosing the actual war-ending type after this check has not yet been decided.
- **Names of starting successions.** The starting successions of the sixteen states are recorded with the name of their method: election among the duty houses, election at a covenant council of water-contract house representatives, primogeniture among the track houses, guild council and apprentice lineage, hereditary commander, dual commander and garrison chief, house inheritance, election by a council of investor houses, pastor father-to-son succession with elder confirmation, priesthood election and mountain assembly acclamation, a master''s nomination of a successor disciple, examination election, and direct election by union members. Who elects, how many must gather, and what happens in a tie are not contained in the name.

There are no approved numbers for this domain. The formula for squad capacity, how weak an interim squad leader''s command is, the duration or threshold of detention, the score that decides succession, and the time limit for succession are not set, and this document does not create them. Succession, election, and death figures from other games are not Seoul''s numbers.

## Event identification and storage

Identification is permanent. Reapplying the same death event does not increase rights or property, and returns the original receipt. If a will change and a death occur at the same time, the already accepted event order and the effective time of that institution are used. There is no new rule for which to consider first. Simultaneous right transfers are evaluated against the same ledger version, and conflicts are resolved with a deterministic acceptance order.

Loading and body changes do not reset the stored history of relationships, rights, or property. The history of titles and offices, right disputes, the fulfillment and breach of promises, captivity, injury, and death, marriage and kinship, educational progress, disclosed evidence, property and debts, truces and war outcomes, and witnessing, memory, and current trust all remain. Later actions reread those facts.

## Following a single death to the end

Follow it in order: before death, at the moment of death, and after death.

**Before death.** The person in the body, who has a designated successor, holds one office, one trust directed at a specific person, and one piece of personal property. The successor already has other relationships. There is no will to pass on the property, and no election result to fill that office.

**At the moment of death.** The body dies. The dead person''s new actions stop.

**After death.**

1. The designated successor becomes the new body. The screen is where that successor actually is. The successor keeps their own trust and does not receive the dead person''s trust, skills, or friends.
2. The office stays on the list and becomes vacant. No acting holder is created. The state remains as it is.
3. The property stays on the list for later inheritance of property. Since there is neither a will naming a beneficiary nor an institutional outcome, it is neither handed over nor divided.
4. The remaining squad chooses an interim squad leader from among the rank-and-file soldiers. Within the approved scope, the interim leader can issue the same kinds of orders with weaker effects. For remote orders from the new main body, the actual command relationship, the same-faction command contract, the contact route, receipt, and acceptance are all verified, and if any one of them is not confirmed, the result of the order is undetermined.
5. This is how this death ends. The player''s body is the successor. The office is vacant. Inheritance of the property is still open. The dead person''s personal trust has not been copied.

If there is no successor at all, that person''s path ends. The item-by-item procedures for office and property still open in the same order. The path ending does not make the state vanish or the property get divided equally. In the case of permanent detention, only the body changes if there is a successor. Inheritance does not open. The detained person is alive and keeps their own property.

## What remains on failure

- Outcomes that treat detention as death are not allowed. The detained person and their property remain as they are.
- Outcomes that copy the dead person''s trust, skills, and friends to the successor are not allowed. The successor remains with the relationships that were originally their own.
- Outcomes that create a nameless office holder are not allowed. If there is no procedure or no eligible person, the office is vacant or disputed.
- The lack of a successor for one item does not lead to inventing a successor. That item is held pending or stays vacant, and the other items continue with their own procedures.
- Reapplying the same death does not increase rights or property. Supplies already used and progress already completed are not reversed. Only the remaining resources are released under that contract.
- If death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between an approved task and its execution, the reserved work is rechecked. Supplies already consumed and progress already completed are preserved, and for each remaining action the surviving actor, authority, location or contact route, resources, the counterpart''s intent, and the institutional procedure are judged again. Cancellation or death alone does not reject all remaining actions wholesale or erase existing records.

## Boundaries of information and authority

- Personal trust is not copied to the successor. Trust is a judgment input in the relationship domain, not a source of authority. High trust does not create command, property rights, consent, or passage rights that did not exist.
- Authority comes from contracts and actual relationships. Remote command by the new main body requires confirming not only a valid contract but also the actual command relationship, the same faction, the contact route, and receipt and acceptance. Office authority is distinct from personal property, title rights, and other valid contracts, and a vacancy alone does not cause anything to happen automatically.
- Observation and memory belong to the one who witnessed. Witnessing, memory, and current trust are stored even after death, and later actions reread those facts.
- Stored world history is not automatically made known or disclosed to the new main body. The reports and records the successor can see are checked separately against that body''s actual knowledge and qualifications, public access rights, and delivery routes. The previous main body''s private knowledge and access rights are not copied by the body switch alone, and hidden facts are not revealed as a reason for failure.
- Legal ownership and physical custody are separate. Even after gaining ownership, supplies in an occupied warehouse do not teleport into the inventory, and equipment in transit cannot be used before it arrives.
- A name is not an identifier. People, events, and property are referred to by internal identifiers.
- The rules in this domain do not change decisions that are already locked. Appointment grants public office authority, conferral changes the holder of a title right, and delegation leaves the original holder in place. A war must have a registered legitimate casus belli.

## What other domains handle

- **Relationships, Memory, and Loyalty (D07).** How trust is used as an input to actions, and how the survivors'' mourning events are handled.
- **Houses, Kinship, Marriage, and Adoption (D08).** Whether a marriage is established, and the institutions of houses. This document examines the effects of an already established marriage effect by effect, and keeps the formation and effects undetermined for cases where the applicable institution is unknown.
- **States, Government, Institutions, and Legitimacy (D09).** How each state conducts elections, nominations, examinations, hereditary succession, and confirmation, and who fills a vacant office. The names of the starting succession methods already exist in the setting, and the voters, the number needed to convene, and the tie handling within them are open inputs of that domain.
- **Titles, Rights, Casus Belli, and Confirmation (D10).** Which rights end or transfer with death, and the institution of claims.
- **Conferral, Delegation, Revocation, and Vassals (D11).** The details of delegation status and the procedures for conferral and revocation. This document maintains the boundary at which the original right is preserved when the person who received a delegation dies.
- **Assets, Debts, Inheritance, and Transfer (D13).** How property is divided and how wills, shares, and collateral are handled. When there is no beneficiary, this document only holds the matter pending.
- **Combat, Command, Squads, and Capacity (D15).** The formula for squad capacity and the magnitude of the interim squad leader''s command effect.
- **Diplomacy, War, Siege, and Captives (D16).** How an ongoing war actually ends after the validity of the registered casus belli, the legal object, and the beneficiary and exercise eligibility are rechecked. The scope in which the victory right goes to the eligible beneficiary of a claim-backed war is approved, but the choice of ending type after a death is undetermined.
- **Time, Events, and Saving (D18).** The world clock, event order, save boundaries, and versions.

## Four worked examples

The four examples below show the rules of this document as state transitions. The characters and items are rule-verification inputs, not executed tests, and contain no approved game numbers.

**Example 1 — When the heirs to the body, office, and property differ.** The dead character has a designated successor, one office, and one piece of personal property. The registered will proposes a specific beneficiary, but that beneficiary''s legal eligibility and the confirmation of the overall asset allocation do not yet exist. Transition: the new body becomes the designated successor, and the screen moves to the successor''s actual location. The office enters its item-by-item procedure but stays vacant because there is no election result to fill it. The property awaits eligibility and confirmation while preserving the registered will''s proposal and the existing collateral. After: the body, office, and property have reached different outcomes. The successor did not receive the dead person''s rights, trust, skills, friends, or unaccepted orders, and neither the collateral nor the physical location disappeared.

**Example 2 — When there is no successor.** The dead character has neither a designated successor nor a next person in the succession order. Transition: that character''s path ends. The item-by-item procedures for office and property open in the same order. After: that character''s path has ended. This rule does not decide where a new body is obtained, the state does not vanish, and the property is not divided equally. The outcomes for the office and property depend on candidate eligibility and the procedures of the relevant institution or contract respectively. For example, if the office candidates and procedure and the property beneficiary and confirmation result are not given, the item-by-item outcomes are also undetermined. A nameless leader is not created.

**Example 3 — Detained but alive.** The main body character is permanently detained and has a designated successor. Transition: only the main body switches to the successor, and inheritance does not open. The detained character cannot move freely or work in the field, and meetings, letters, and negotiations are possible only where the detention contract allows. Signing, command, and operation of the office, as well as separate personal contracts, are each re-checked under their own conditions. After: the detained character is alive and keeps their own property. The property inheritance procedure has not opened, but whether the office is actually vacant or merely unable to be exercised, and whether existing contracts continue, are separate determinations. Returning after release requires a separate, explicit transfer of authority, and the specific authority who would grant it has not yet been decided.

**Example 4 — When the claimant dies during a war.** The claimant dies in an ongoing claim-backing war. Transition: first check whether the registered casus belli remains valid, whether a legal target remains, and whether the beneficiary has a valid claim, consent, affiliation or backing contract, and eligibility to exercise it. If the beneficiary is eligible, the victory right passes to that beneficiary and not to the attacker. Then, for the new responsible party, personal commitments and office commitments are sorted item by item into maintained, re-ratified, or terminated. After: succession does not erase debts, truces, or damage records. If the casus belli or the beneficiary is invalid under this input, it cannot be asserted that the war simply continues, and even if both are valid, the actual choice of ending type has not yet been decided.

## Source Game References

Seoul''s rules for this domain come from the owner''s decisions about Seoul. The two source games are objects of comparison, and the source-game figures and edition records below are not Seoul''s numbers.

### Crusader Kings III (Paradox Interactive)

- Edition: installed copy 1.20.0.3, build 25652598 (installation boundary of the prior audit; active branch, DLC, and mod state unconfirmed). The content below is based on preserved script and documentation fragments and is not verification of runtime behavior.
- Heir designation: the branches of designation eligibility, per-ruler cost, and designation effects (matching top-level administrative ruler, joint rule, Mandala special case, and adult adventurer and no-prestige branches) were confirmed from script declarations. They are not raw observations of succession recalculation.
- Succession order and elections: inheritance ranks, election candidate policy, highest-score selection, and the composition of bonus points were confirmed. Raw ordering for ties and equal ages is absent from the preserved source text and remains unresolved.
- Partition inheritance: succession law settings and minimum shares were confirmed. The engine''s title traversal, packing, and rounding were not observed and remain unresolved.
- Office elections and appointments: the separation between feudal title elections and administrative officer appointments, the bonus-point definitions, and the upstream accumulation variables were confirmed. Ledger storage, cap application, and tie handling remain unresolved.
- Simultaneous death: pre-death handling and support for simultaneous effects and events are documented, but the queue, ties, and atomic commit for multiple deaths remain unresolved. No ordering is inferred.
- Ongoing wars: ordinary casus belli invalidation and claimant faction switching were confirmed as separate paths. The re-evaluation timing remains unresolved, and it is not inferred that the casus belli transfers to the heir.
- Detention and survival: imprisonment is a separate survival-state event and not an ordinary death succession. Activity and movement effects are limited in scope. In the current D12 r2 review, "imprisoned heirs are universally disqualified" was classified as a hypothetical, unfounded generalization that the original consumer had explicitly excluded, and no actual consumer contradiction was confirmed. The challenge path of the Mandala special case and the separate paths of exile and abdication remain valid within their respective scopes.
- Orders right after death: only activity and travel cancellation and the ordered vassal-instruction variables were confirmed. Army order state is a separate mechanism and is not equivalent.
- No heir: in succession revision 35670 version 1.10, there is an internal conflict in the source text between the shorthand "no descendants" and the discussion of alternatives when there are no collateral relatives or valid living heirs. This is an internal counter-evidence within the source that the original consumer had already bounded, not an actual consumer contradiction. The handling of an empty candidate pool, ties, and player game-over in the current edition remains unresolved.
- **Relation to Seoul.** The CK3 script and documentation fragments examined do not establish the runtime for switching the player''s main body, the independent transitions of body, office, and property, the ban on re-application, or the ledger of command receipt and acceptance that Seoul requires. This is not a global claim that CK3 as a whole lacks such features, but a boundary stating that this preserved material cannot support Seoul''s model. Seoul''s separation and preservation rules follow the Seoul-approved basis above and do not use CK3''s succession or election figures.

### Norland (Long Jaunt / Hooded Horse)

- Edition: a documentation survey based on official community wiki oldid captures (Actions rev1318, Lords rev1314, World rev1284, Warfare rev1319) and Steam announcements (IDs 6146943657173940808, 1796631172375296, 1809869180071665, 1816849002015273, 1825093633188129, 1830797770236682, 1839676055888772, 1845383656387035), with historical capture build 25211575. It is not verification of the current build or of execution.
- Heir designation and throne succession actions (wiki rev1318): actions that designate an eligible target as heir or hand over the throne. These are merely examples of designation actions, and the preserved material has no raw rules for automatic succession selection.
- Ring rules (announcement 6146943657173940808, patch 25): when a ruler dies, the ring passes to a spouse or eldest child of the same faction (when both are on the local map). Priority, remote delivery, division, and disposal are not determined by the sentence and remain unresolved.
- Trade agreements persist after the king''s death. This is a counterexample to the generalization that "all contracts are terminated en masse by death."
- When an overlord dies, all vassals become independent (wiki rev1284), and peace may also end upon the death of the peace counterparty. Cases that differ by target are recorded separately, and there is no global event order.
- Hostages and prisoners: hostages can be assigned to actions other than military actions (wiki rev1314), and scope fixes for the order of prisoner recognition, corpse, and grave, as well as the ban on kidnapping rulers (patch 58), exist as separate scope-limited records. Regency, eligibility of a detained heir, resurrection and recovery, and body switching are not documented.
- Death threshold (wiki rev1319): the pain threshold is 50, and a warrior dies when it is exceeded. That threshold figure belongs to Norland and is not Seoul''s death determination.
- No mixing of test builds and the main release: the bishop succession transfer and free-title protection in the test-build patch, and the heir preservation in the main-release patch, are records from different branches. They are not merged into a single cumulative current rule without an edition lineage and a mapping to installed builds.
- Partial ordering of death outcomes: mourning and political removal, death during envoy delivery, rebellion intent, legion commander replacement, and cancellation due to target death are each recorded as scope-limited. The material examined does not establish the global ordering, atomicity, or exception priority that Seoul needs.
- **Relation to Seoul.** The Norland documentation is a collection of separate features and fixes, not an integrated succession lifecycle, and there is no evidence of automatic succession selection, a simultaneous-death transaction, or a body-switching ledger. Seoul''s item-by-item separation and preservation rules were not taken from Norland, and Norland''s threshold and rule figures are not used as Seoul''s numbers.

## What Remains Undecided

The items below are boundaries where the current rules cannot fix the outcome. Even where the owner has chosen a direction, anything whose specific authority or actual institution has not been decided is kept distinct.

- **Permanent detention and return to the main body.** The legal determination of what counts as permanent detention is undecided. The direction chosen is that returning to a released former main body requires a separate, explicit, authorized transfer of identity and control, but release itself is not a return, and the specific approving authority has not yet been decided.
- **The next person in the succession order.** Who is chosen as the new main body when there is no designated successor must be decided by the family system, and there is currently no ordering rule.
- **Vacancy procedures by state.** Names for the starting succession methods exist, but the electors, quorum, tie handling, and actual ratification procedure do not yet exist.
- **The post-death lifespan of rights.** Whether each right falls under expiry, transfer, or preservation has not yet been decided. Only the limited passing of an enforced claim to children and the non-passing of an unenforced claim are maintained.
- **The remaining parts of property inheritance.** A registered will proposes beneficiaries, and only a ratified allocation of the entire estate transfers title. Valid collateral, reservations, custodians, locations, and routes remain in force after title transfer, and personal debts remain with the existing debtor and the estate review. Joint property uses only contracts signed asset by asset by all current owners, and marriage alone does not change ownership. The actual governing law, the beneficiary''s legal eligibility, and shares, residuals, and creditor priority are undecided.
- **Ending an ongoing war whose claimant has died.** First check the validity of the registered casus belli, the legal target, and the beneficiary and eligibility to exercise, and the victory right of a claim-backing war is placed with the eligible beneficiary. Which ending type is chosen after that result is undecided.
- **Establishing the new main body''s remote orders.** The policy that a temporary squad leader can issue the same kind of order with a weaker effect is approved. A remote order by the new main body is established only when an actual command relationship, a same-faction command contract, a contact route, and receipt and acceptance records all exist, and only the outcome of specific cases lacking these inputs is undecided.
- **Undetermined numbers.** The formula that sets squad capacity, how much weaker a temporary squad leader''s command is, the duration or threshold of detention, the score that separates successors, and the time limit for succession are not set. The capacity formula reads inputs that vary with individual stats, and its source text has not yet been obtained or verified. This document does not fill that gap with public abstracts or numbers from other games, and only records the exact absence of the source text. The remaining figures await decisions in their respective domains.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d13', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d13', 'D13 Assets, Debts, Inheritance, and Handover — Reader''s Design Document', '', 'proposal', 120, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d13.md', '# D13 Assets, Debts, Inheritance, and Handover — Reader''s Design Document

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/economy-and-logistics/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

- Basis: the confirmed clauses of SP-1.0.0, the already-approved decision scope of the campaign, economy, and faction rules, and the records of choices the owner actually responded to. The owner-choice policy is edition draft-v1 and is neither full rule approval nor adoption as the canonical text (Chapter 12). Reading this document does not substitute for approval, and quantities and formulas that have not been approved are left undetermined.

## 1. What This Domain Covers

D13 covers **assets, debts, inheritance, and delivery**. When a person dies, when supplies set out on a road, or when an asset is put up for an enforcement item, "what belongs to whom and where it is" splits apart. This document is written so that this split can be read using only rules that have already been accepted.

The list of approved policies that serve as the basis for reading this document follows. Each item is attributed to the approval decision it belongs to.

- One body, one shared action contract, one world time: the player takes part as a single-body character, and other characters follow the same action conditions (sp1.b3, sp1.b92; scope-map `singleplayer-one-body`).
- Separation of asset types: personal supplies, equipment, and currency; institutional warehouses, vaults, and facilities; joint property and shares; leases and use permits; debts, claims, and collateral; and records, keys, and relics are all distinct things (sp1.b61; scope-map `separate-status-and-authority`).
- The nine axes of asset state: location, quantity or individual item, owner, custodian, usage right, reservation, collateral, in delivery, dispute. Logistics state and legal-rights state are kept separate, and cargo in wartime transit keeps the same asset ID and split origin (sp1.b62).
- Separation of succession events: body succession, state/institution head succession, title succession, and property succession are different events (sp1.b47; scope-map `succession-and-body-change`).
- Permanent detention does not open an inheritance: a detained person is still alive, and the property remains with that person (sp1.b49).
- Order of transfer upon death: confirm the heir list → mark disputes/collateral → transfer ownership → reserve actual delivery → verify receipt. Gaining ownership does not teleport supplies in an enemy-occupied warehouse into the inventory. Divided inheritance preserves the total quantity, and supplies under reservation or collateral are marked separately. An undetermined system means the inheritance is suspended; it is not replaced by an ownerless state or an automatic equal split (sp1.b65).
- Basis for determining beneficiaries and institutional retention: for personal property, check the relevant state''s system, any valid will, and marriage/joint-property contracts; institutional property stays with the institution, and the new office holder takes over only the management authority needed; for debts and claims, check attribution and succession clauses, and do not unconditionally pass debts to all successors (sp1.b64).
- Permanent storage fields and the ban on reapplication: record the previous owner, beneficiary, quantity, basis, collateral, reservation, delivery location, fulfillment status, and cause ID, and do not duplicate property when the same death event is reapplied (sp1.b67).
- Check order and event handling: target existence and version → ability to act while alive/detained → authority → actual location/delivery → resource and exclusive reservation → counterparty intent/institutional procedure. Resending the same event ID does not produce a new result, and simultaneous rights transfers are resolved from the same ledger version in a deterministic acceptance order (sp1.b12, sp1.b13).
- Inclusive arrival and same-time ordering: if the arrival time of verified cargo is at or before the consumption deadline, it is included in the available stock at the destination for that deadline. At the same world time, arrival verification is applied first, and then reservation, production, and consumption are processed according to the existing economic resolution order. A duplicate arrival ID is rejected, and in-transit stock is neither consumed nor counted simultaneously as available stock in two places (`economy-owner.20261003.arrival`; scope-map `T68-CARGO-INCLUSIVE-CUTOFF`).
- Negotiation per enforcement item: negotiating individual items is a Seoul-specific extension adopted with the owner''s approval. Signing and settlement are locked for items whose price version is unregistered, a single ledger asset cannot be claimed redundantly for multiple items, if V≤W it is not automatically accepted, and after signing a campaign contract to deliver remains. Mid-point negotiation is possible when both sides are under 100 in absolute value; the continuing validity of the casus belli and the eligible beneficiary are rechecked first; and if a valid casus belli reaches the ±100 boundary before acceptance, forced termination of that version takes effect first (sp1.b34, sp1.b119, sp1.b121, sp1.b124–b127; scope-map `custom-peace`).
- Boundary of marriage''s property effects: marriage applies each state''s political, property, and succession systems; marriage alone does not automatically grant new claims or territory; and upon death, detention, or loss of office, contracts for each property effect are checked to decide between maintenance, termination, and re-ratification (`claims-owner.20261003.marriage`; scope-map `T68-MARRIAGE-EFFECTS`).
- Directions chosen by the owner (version draft-v1, with the remainder distinguished in Chapter 12): recognition of registered wills, explicit allocation of all assets, retention of personal debts for estate review, maintenance of collateral and transfer of title, reservation upon acceptance (proposals are non-binding), and four directions on family law (jurisdictional law forum, direct consent of the parties, consent of the parties to an adoption, per-asset signed contracts).

State property systems, detailed will formats, prices, share ratios, and the disposition of collateral that have not been decided are not filled in as if they had been decided (Chapter 12).

## 2. Actors

| Actor | What They Do | Basis |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Principal Character (Player) | Approves and enforces transfers of ownership over their own property, reserves delivery, confirms receipt, and signs enforcement items. They are a single body, and choosing another character does not change ownership. | sp1.b3, sp1.b92 |
| NPCs (Other Characters) | Owners, custodians, recipients, and creditors who use the same action contract as the body. Even if the body dies, their ownership, custody, and claims do not change by themselves. | sp1.b3, sp1.b92 |
| Owner | The party that holds legal ownership of an asset. The source of disposal authority. | sp1.b61, sp1.b64 |
| Custodian | Actually manages the place where the asset is located. The custodian role alone does not confer ownership. The owner and custodian may be the same party. | sp1.b61 |
| Beneficiary of a Registered Will (Candidate) | The proposed beneficiary named by a registered will. Actual legal eligibility and allocation are checked separately. | Owner choice `testament-A` (12.1) |
| Institution | The owning party of warehouses, vaults, and facilities. The death or loss of office of a member does not pass assets to personal inheritance. | sp1.b64 |
| New Office Holder | Takes over a vacant office and assumes only the management authority that office requires. Does not receive ownership of the vault. | sp1.b64 |
| Acceptor (Counterparty of the Proposal) | Evaluates an enforcement proposal and accepts, rejects, or counteroffers. If the acceptance passes the recheck, an exclusive reservation on that asset takes effect. | sp1.b126, sp1.b127; owner choice `offer-reservation-B` |
| Recipient | Proves receipt of arrived cargo with a confirmation signature. | Owner response record `custody-receipt-A` (12.6) |
| Occupier | Holds effective control on site but does not gain ownership. | sp1.b61; scope-map `separate-status-and-authority` |
| Key Holder | Holds only a means of physical access, not a legal right. | sp1.b61 |
| Synthetic Being Recognized as a Person | Excluded from the list of transferable assets. | sp1.b61 |

State and institution names show only the name of a position. As the case in sp1.b66 shows, when a chairman dies, personal equipment and personal shares are separated from the company warehouse; a child''s inheritance of shares and the new chairman''s authority to order from the warehouse are different events; and even if personal equipment in transit is inherited, it cannot be used before it arrives. The names appearing in the cases below are placeholders for reading these rules, not approved characters or confirmed quantities.

## 3. Decisions the Player Actually Makes

1. **Approval and enforcement of ownership transfer** — Ownership is handed over only when the basis is a state system, a valid will, or an established marriage or joint-property contract. Joint property is not changed by marriage alone; all current owners sign separately for each asset (owner choice `joint-property-A`).
2. **Registration of a will** — Only a registered will can propose a beneficiary. The will record itself is not a delivery or an allocation, and a marriage record is not a will (`claims-owner.20261003.marriage`; owner choice `testament-A`).
3. **Reservation of actual delivery and receipt confirmation** — Even when ownership changes, the goods do not come into hand. After arrival, delivery is closed by the recipient''s confirmation signature (owner response record `custody-receipt-A`).
4. **Signing an enforcement item** — Signing is allowed only when the price version is registered and disposal authority exists. A proposal is non-binding, and an exclusive reservation on that asset takes effect only after the acceptance passes the recheck (sp1.b122–b127; owner choice `offer-reservation-B`).
5. **Handling of debts** — Personal debts attach to the recorded debtor and to the estate review, and are not automatically loaded onto heirs. Even when the debtor dies, the attribution of the debt and its succession clauses are checked separately (sp1.b64; owner choice `debt-A`).
6. **Leaving a suspended inheritance alone, or requesting an inheritance system** — If the system and will cannot determine a beneficiary, the inheritance is suspended. The player does not fill the suspension with other rules, and the only path left is to request a decision on the state system (12.1).

The action screen distinguishes `ganeung` (possible), `seonyeol yogeon bujok` (prerequisites lacking), `cheobun gwonhan eopseum` (no disposal authority), `gagyeok mideungnok` (price unregistered), and `jedo mijeong` (institution undetermined), and displays the grounds (Chapter 8).

## 4. Eligibility — Actual Checks by Asset Type

Eligibility for ownership transfer, reservation, and signing is judged by actual checks defined differently for each asset type. The check order is fixed (sp1.b12). **Target existence and version → ability to act (alive/detained, etc.) → authority → actual location/delivery → resource and exclusive reservation → counterparty intent/institutional procedure**.

- **Personal property** — The beneficiary is determined by reading the relevant state system, a valid will, and an established marriage or joint-property contract. If the state system is undecided and there is no valid will, no beneficiary is created. The inheritance is suspended (sp1.b64, sp1.b65).
- **Institutional warehouses, vaults, and facilities** — These remain with the institution. The new office holder takes over only the management authority needed for that office (sp1.b64). A vault does not pass into personal inheritance just because the deceased person''s office is vacant.
- **Usage rights and leases** — Follow the succession clause of that contract. Whether the clause says to maintain, re-approve, or terminate can be known only when such a clause exists.
- **Debts and claims** — Look at whether they attach to an individual, an office, or an institution, and at their succession clauses. Debts are not divided and loaded onto all successors (sp1.b64).
- **Eligibility for inclusion in the cargo deadline** — Applies when arrival has been verified and that time is earlier than or equal to the consumption deadline (`economy-owner.20261003.arrival`).
- **Enforcement signing eligibility** — Applies when the price version of that item is registered and the proposing party has the authority to dispose of that asset. An item with no price cannot be signed (sp1.b122, sp1.b126). A single ledger asset cannot be claimed redundantly for multiple items (sp1.b125).

Cases of ineligibility are also clear. A person who only holds something in safekeeping, a person who only has the key, the successor to a vacated post, an occupier, and a party to a marriage that has not yet taken effect do not gain ownership by that fact alone (sp1.b61). A lookup does not create ownership, and possessing a key is not itself a legal right.

## 5. States and Transitions

### 5.1 State of a Single Asset

The state recorded for a single asset consists of location, quantity or individual item, owner, custodian, usage right, reservation, collateral, in-transit status, and dispute (sp1.b62). Whether it is on the road and whose it is legally are recorded separately. Cargo transported during a war keeps the same asset ID, and if it is split, the source of the split is retained.

### 5.2 Inheritance State Sequence

Death begins → inheritance list fixed, with dispute/collateral marked → (once an institution, will, or contract designates the beneficiary) ownership transfer → delivery reservation for actual handover → receipt verification (sp1.b65).

- If there is no valid institution or will to designate a beneficiary, the process remains on hold before the ownership transfer step. The item is not recorded as ownerless and is not divided equally.
- Applying the same death again does not duplicate assets. If a will change and a death occur at the same time, the already recognized event order and the effective time of that institution are followed (sp1.b67).
- Permanent detention does not open this state sequence. A detained person is alive, and the property remains with that person (sp1.b49).
- An institutional vault is not personal property in this state sequence. It remains with the institution (sp1.b64).

### 5.3 Delivery State Sequence

Delivery reservation → in transit (same asset ID) → arrival intake and verification, and confirmation by the designated recipient → verified receipt, or settlement as awaiting acceptance/failed. Station intake alone does not complete receipt verification; the designated recipient''s confirmation signature is required (`custody-receipt-A`).

- Stock in transit is not consumed. It is not counted simultaneously as dispatched stock and arrived stock (`economy-owner.20261003.arrival`).
- If a verified arrival occurs before or at the same time as the consumption cutoff, it becomes destination stock for that cutoff. Within the same world time, arrival verification comes before reservation, production, and consumption.
- The same arrival ID is rejected. A failed acceptance is quarantined locally to block consumption, and an undelivered shipment keeps its existing reservation but is not counted as available stock at the destination. Only the seven-option contract of GP-06 has been independently confirmed; actual acceptance authentication, clock, and storage implementation have not been confirmed (12.6).
- Even when ownership passes, goods in an enemy warehouse do not teleport into the holdings list. After the ownership transfer comes the delivery reservation, and after that comes receipt verification (sp1.b65).

### 5.4 Peace-Term Signature State Sequence

Proposal (non-binding) → evaluation by both sides → rejection/counteroffer/acceptance → ledger and authority recheck → exclusive reservation of accepted assets established → single closing settlement of signed terms → delivery/performance (sp1.b124–b127; owner choice `offer-reservation-B`). Acceptance creates a reservation only after passing the ledger and authority recheck, and a signed term remains a campaign contract to be performed rather than a completed physical receipt. A signature is not placed ahead of the reservation, and the signature itself is not treated as delivery.

- The structure of the evaluation formula is V(proposal) = Σ price of demanded items − Σ price of offered items, and prices are evaluated using a state snapshot of the same war and proposal version (sp1.b126). The price function and the acceptance correction coefficient have not been approved (12.3).
- If V≤W, it is not processed as automatic acceptance. The war score W and the price score are different records (sp1.b125, sp1.b126).
- After the closing inputs are locked, the continued validity of the casus belli and the beneficiary are rechecked first (sp1.b34, sp1.b119, sp1.b121). If the casus belli is already invalid, the invalidity conditions are handled first and no right is created. If the casus belli is valid and the ±100 boundary is reached before acceptance, the forced closing specified in the casus belli version takes effect first and the unaccepted proposal is discarded (sp1.b127). The recipient of a right is the eligible beneficiary, and is not determined merely because someone is an approver, the attack''s responsible party, or a commander (sp1.reference.beneficiary). For acceptance/100/death at the same boundary, a single closing is determined by the confirmed event order and the version. The number at this boundary is neither a price nor a distribution ratio.
- It is not copied into the holdings list at the moment of signing. Delivery remains a separate campaign contract.

### 5.5 Storage and Versioning Obligations

Identifiers are permanent, and revision conflicts and storage errors are stated explicitly. Rule version SP-1.0.0 is not mixed into a mismatched save without an approved migration, and if a contract is empty it is undetermined and is not closed with a number from a draft (sp1.b107, sp1.b109). Retransmission of the same event ID does not produce a new result (sp1.b13).

## 6. Approved Judgments and Recording Obligations

The permissibility of ownership, reservation, and signature follows only the actual checks by type in Chapter 4. The approved evaluation structure is V(proposal) = Σ price of demanded items − Σ price of offered items, using a state snapshot of the same war and proposal version (sp1.b126). The inputs read by the acceptance function are also specified, but the specific coefficients of the price function and the acceptance correction coefficient are undetermined (12.3, 12.7). The recording obligations are as follows.

- Permanent storage records the previous owner, beneficiary, quantity, basis, collateral, reservation, delivery location, performance status, and cause ID (sp1.b67).
- When the beneficiary is not yet known, the case is on hold with the beneficiary field empty, and the blank is not filled by any other rule.
- When a split actually occurs, the total quantity is preserved. Goods that are reserved or pledged as collateral keep that marking (sp1.b65).
- A transfer of title for encumbered property preserves the collateral, reservation, custody, location, and route (binding wording of owner choice `encumbered-estate-A`).
- A negotiation proposal carries the war ID, proposal version, signing authority, target/beneficiary, item list, performance timing, deadline, and breach effects (sp1.b124). Each item carries a unique ID, type, quantity/scope, disposal authority, evaluation inputs and price version, score, permitted range under the casus belli, and feasibility (sp1.b125).
- Consent to and ratification of a marriage, and the attribution of its effects, are recorded in the event ledger (`claims-owner.20261003.marriage`).

## 7. Failure and Preservation

**The result of a failure is a hold in an undetermined state, not a substitute distribution.**

- If the institution and the will fail to designate a beneficiary, the inheritance is on hold. Neither equal distribution nor no ownership is a substitute result of failure (sp1.b65).
- Goods in an enemy warehouse do not appear on the heir''s list even once ownership arises. The delivery reservation and receipt verification remain separate (sp1.b65).
- If an arrival ID is duplicated, that arrival is rejected. The stock meaning of the arrival verified first is retained (`economy-owner.20261003.arrival`).
- Peace-term items with no registered price are locked from signing and settlement. Negotiation of other registered items is not blocked by this alone (sp1.b122).
- If one asset is demanded in two items, that demand is rejected (sp1.b125).
- The mere fact that a living person loses a post does not wholesale change the ownership or location of personal assets or freeze all deliveries. The institutional management and signing scope of the lost post is rechecked, while personal living, movement, and other valid contracts remain under their own qualifications. Any separate property effects of losing the post are checked contract by contract for continuation, termination, or re-ratification (sp1.b64; claims-owner.20261003.marriage).
- If death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the reserved work is rechecked. Materials already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released according to the contract (sp1.b13).
- Permanent detention does not open inheritance. The property of a detained person remains attached to that person, and there is not yet a method of management during detention (sp1.b49, 12.2).
- A marriage judged not to have taken effect has no property effect only when its circumstances are separately specified. If the applicable institution is not yet known and the marriage''s validity cannot be determined, it also cannot be determined whether there is a property effect, and it is not asserted that there is none (`claims-owner.20261003.marriage`).

## 8. Boundaries of Information and Authority

- The action screen distinguishes `ganeung` (possible), `seonyeol yogeon bujok` (prerequisites lacking), `cheobun gwonhan eopseum` (no disposal authority), `gagyeok mideungnok` (price unregistered), and `jedo mijeong` (institution undetermined), and displays the grounds. Unregistered items appear on the negotiation screen as `pyeongga mijeong` (valuation undetermined) and block signing and settlement of the final proposal (sp1.b126).
- Ownership, custody, and use are different roles, and one party may hold more than one of them. The custody role by itself does not grant ownership. A key is itself a physical asset that can be owned or assigned, but possessing it does not by itself confer legal rights over other assets (sp1.b61).
- The screen restricts asset and delivery information according to the current character''s actual knowledge, record access and disclosure authority, and the source, confirmation time, and receiving channel of reports. It does not omnisciently reveal the full asset list of all participants. Knowledge and qualifications are reapplied in the new body as well (sp1.b21, sp1.b70, sp1.b75, sp1.b94). A lookup does not create disposal authority. A marriage record is not a will, and that record alone does not mean a share, debt, or beneficiary has arisen (`claims-owner.20261003.marriage`).
- A person is not an item that can be transferred. Demands concerning persons are classified as targets of valid acts such as release or repatriation of prisoners, an interview, or voluntary assignment. Synthetic beings recognized as persons are excluded from the list of transferable assets (sp1.b61).
- A new claim or territory is not granted merely because a marriage has taken effect (`claims-owner.20261003.marriage`).

## 9. Cross-Domain Ownership

D13 covers asset identity, ownership, the marking of collateral and reservations, ownership transfer, delivery reservation, receipt verification, and the arrivals to be included in a cutoff. The facts below are determined by each domain. D13 only reads these values and does not redetermine them.

| Domain | What D13 Reads | What D13 Does Not Do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D05 Economy and Production | Production reservations after arrival, and the order of economic resolution | Reservation and production design. It states only that arrival verification comes first at the same time |
| D06 Movement, Cargo, and Map | Which route cargo actually travels | Movement design. It keeps in-transit and post-arrival as a single asset |
| D08 Houses and Marriage | Whether a marriage takes effect, and the attribution of its property effects | Marriage validity determination. If validity cannot be determined, property effects are not determined either |
| D09 State and Institutions | State property institutions, and the procedure for taking over management authority of a vacant post | Authoring of institution content. Without an institution, inheritance is on hold |
| D11 Grants, Delegation, and Contracts | Among the obligations of gifts and contracts, the transfer of custody | Types of Obligations and Recovery Conditions |
| D12 Succession and Principal Body Transition | Who the New Principal Body Is | Determining the estate beneficiary. The beneficiary may differ from the principal body. |
| D16 War and Prisoners | A captive''s detention status and the registration requirements for a casus belli | Captive rules and the declaration-of-war procedure. A person is not an item that can be transferred. |
| D17 Negotiation and Pricing | Functions for price, valuation, acceptance, rejection, and counteroffer | Price coefficients and acceptance adjustments. An unregistered price blocks signing. |
| D18 Time and Saving | Ordering of events at the same timestamp, and the save/restore contract | Time design. It follows the event order that has already been recognized. |

## 10. Four Cases — State Transition Design

### 10.1 Death Initiation and Inheritance Hold

- **Before**: One item of equipment E belonging to person P is held by carrier C and in transit, and its destination is an enemy-occupied warehouse. One institutional vault I is owned by institution K. E is listed as a demand in an unaccepted peace proposal, but because the proposal is non-binding, E has not been reserved by this proposal alone. There is neither a valid will nor a state institution that designates a beneficiary.
- **Action**: Apply event D, in which P dies. E is placed on the inheritance list and marked as encumbered and disputed, but because no beneficiary has been determined, it is held before the transfer of ownership. I is excluded from the personal estate and remains owned by K. Reapplying D does not create duplicates. If only detention had occurred, inheritance would not be opened at all (sp1.b49, sp1.b64–b67).
- **Post-action preservation check**: E''s ID, quantity of 1, recorded previous owner P, custodian C, and transit status are unchanged, and the beneficiary remains undetermined. I''s ID, quantity of 1, and owner K are also unchanged. Personal equipment 1 = held 1 + allocated 0; total physical items 2 = 2 both before and after. E is not added to the destination''s available inventory. The proposal has not been signed, and the authority to dispose of items after death must be checked again. The arrival time and the inheritance custody system are not determined by this input.

### 10.2 Full Asset Allocation and Preservation of Encumbrances under a Registered Will

- **Before**: Four actual personal assets of P are held — equipment E 1 in transit, collateralized share S 1, gold bundle M 1, and physical key K 1. Each ID is distinct. E''s custodian is carrier C, and S''s collateral is L. M and K are kept directly by P, so the owner and custodian are the same. R, a synthetic companion recognized as a person, is neither an asset nor part of the estate from the outset and is excluded from the count. A validly registered will designates E to the eldest child H and S to the spouse B, and in this case the legal eligibility of H and B and the full asset allocation have been validly approved.
- **Action**: After checking the registered will''s proposal and legal eligibility, allocate only the approved full E and S (testament-A, allocation-A). The undesignated M and K are held. The key K is also a physical asset and could be a target of valid allocation, but this will did not allocate it; possessing K does not confer legal rights to a different warehouse. Only the title of S is transferred to B, while collateral L, reservations, custodian, location, and route are preserved (encumbered-estate-A). Personal debts remain in a separate debtor and estate review record and are not automatically imposed on H and B (debt-A).
- **Post-action preservation check**: E is owned by H and S by B, while M and K remain as hold records linked to the previous owner P. The four IDs and the quantity of 1 for each are unchanged. Actual estate 4 = allocated 2 + held 2, and collateral L 1 is also 1 before and after. R is a person outside the estate both before and after. E''s custodian C and route are maintained, so no physical item appears on H''s in-hand list. Actual delivery reservation and receipt verification remain. No new share ratio or residual distribution has been created.

### 10.3 Acceptance, Reservation, Signing, and Conclusion of Peace Terms

- **Before**: In war W1 under valid casus belli version CB, the absolute value of each side''s score is below 100. The rights beneficiary H is a different person from commander C and is an eligible beneficiary of CB. Proposal P1 demands 1 unit of asset A belonging to disposition authority O. A has no reservation in ledger version r1 and is held by custodian D. B 1 in a separate proposal has no price version, so signing of the final proposal is locked. In this case, A''s price version is registered, but no numerical coefficients are presented.
- **Action**: Evaluate using the structure V = Σ demanded prices − Σ offered prices and the same war and proposal version snapshot, and decide using the acceptance function. Do not accept based solely on the comparison V ≤ W. A proposal that includes B cannot be signed or settled. If the opponent accepts a valid proposal consisting of A only, re-check the ledger and authority, then reserve A exclusively (offer-reservation-B). A competing acceptance for the same A re-checks this reservation and the ledger version and cannot be reserved together. For a signed item, record the target, beneficiary, quantity, performance time, deadline, and breach effect. In a single concluding settlement, after the inputs are locked, first confirm the continued validity of CB and H''s eligibility, and then finalize the list of rights transfers (sp1.b34, sp1.b119, sp1.b121, sp1.b124–b127).
- **Post-action preservation check**: In the valid signing and settlement branch, A''s ID and quantity of 1 are maintained, and the transfer of ownership to the contractual beneficiary H is recorded, but custodian D and the current location are unchanged, so a campaign contract for delivery remains. Commander C does not acquire the rights in H''s place. B''s ID, quantity of 1, and original ownership are unchanged, and it was not signed. Total quantity 2 = 2 before and after, and there is one reservation on A. In the contrasting branch where ±100 is reached before acceptance, the defined conclusion effect of the valid CB is processed first and the unaccepted proposal is discarded. In the branch where CB is already invalid, the invalidity check is performed first even at a score of 100, so no unfounded rights are created from this casus belli. Acceptance, 100, and death at the same boundary are settled only once, according to the confirmed event order and versions.

### 10.4 Common Deadline, Failure to Take Delivery, and Late Arrival after Non-delivery

- **Before**: Cargo F, H, and J owned by institution O each have a unique ID and a quantity of 1. F and J are in transit in the custody of carrier C. H has arrived at the destination dock but failed designated-recipient verification. J has a reservation for job Q and no arrival confirmation. At the common daily deadline T, the designated-recipient confirmation and arrival verification for F are established, while J is still undelivered. In this case there is no blockade, and the times and quantities are case inputs.
- **Action**: Since F''s verified arrival time = T, after arrival verification, reflect F 1 in the destination''s available inventory at T in the order of reservation, production, and consumption. A retransmission of F with the same arrival ID is rejected as a retransmission of the F event; no separate cargo G is created. H cannot be consumed because of local quarantine, retains its ownership, custody, physical location, and evidence, and materials already consumed are not refunded. J does not enter consumption or available inventory at T, keeps the Q reservation, and records the shortfall. After T and before the next common deadline U, apply a separate event in which J''s designated recipient confirms and arrival verification is established. J then becomes available inventory at the destination, but the settlement at T is not retroactively changed. At U, J, if not yet consumed, is read as eligible inventory (GP-06 optional contract; economy-owner.20261003.arrival).
- **Post-action preservation check**: F 1 is reflected as arrived only once, H 1 is quarantined on the dock, and J 1 is at the destination after late verification. This case calculates only up to arrival availability and sets actual consumption to 0, so total physical items before and after 3 = F 1 + H 1 + J 1. Each ID and owner O are unchanged, and only the verified receipt custody of F/J is taken over at the eligible destination. The available portion at T (F 1), quarantined H 1, and undelivered J 1 at T are kept distinct, and J does not remain late inventory forever. Subsequent use or release of reservation Q follows that job''s contract, and no automatic reservation release is invented. If a blockade had existed at the same timestamp, independent inputs would be checked in order of receipt and causal inputs in the order of their accepted causes, and an eligible receipt that has already been completed is not erased by a subsequent blockade or failed retry.

## 11. External References — Crusader Kings III and Norland

The observations from the two games are **the behavior of those games** and not Seoul rules. Neither game''s observations have been adopted as Seoul''s coefficients, probabilities, or formulas. The citations are references for reading the gate structure and ordering.

### 11.1 Crusader Kings III

- Version: installed copy **1.20.0.3, build 25652598**.
- Authority read: the **installed developer script** (developer-installed-script-not-native). This is not an observation of the running game, but a comparison of the designated line ranges in the files below.
- Ownership and transfer gates: `game/common/character_interactions/00_artifact_interactions.txt` lines 1–127, 129–245, and 4385–4503, and `game/common/scripted_triggers/00_artifact_triggers.txt` lines 53–143. The blocking variable for artifact gifting is nested inside a conditional, so it cannot be read as an unconditional ownership = custody = use rule (counterexample D13-C01).
- Repair and cash conversion: `game/common/script_values/00_artifact_values.txt` lines 1–38 (rarity multipliers 4/3/2/1) and `game/events/artifacts/artifact_events.txt` lines 1740–1753 and 1772–1859 (durability added after payment). A counterexample remains that the wiki''s repair bracket table is only an informational figure and not the actual internal calculation (D13-C02).
- Dismantling and remainder: `game/common/scripted_effects/00_ep1_artifact_effects.txt` lines 516–610. The order is documented in which the payout value, with rounding (`round=yes`) and a minimum (`min=1`) applied inside `add_gold`, is calculated first, paid out, and then the artifact is destroyed; however, the primitive handling of split shares and residual allocation has not been confirmed (D13-C03).
- Ransom: `game/common/scripted_effects/00_prison_effects.txt` lines 1–234, `game/common/character_interactions/00_prison_interactions.txt` lines 1886–2051, and `game/common/script_values/00_interaction_values.txt` lines 171–371. The order in which payment precedes release can be read, but the wording of the lower-bound condition in the full-payment branch is ambiguous, so promotion remains on hold (ESCALATE D13-C04). This fragment is not cited as a Seoul rule.
- Borrowing and inheritance: `game/common/scripted_effects/00_scripted_effects.txt` lines 20–79 (loan variable setup), `game/events/death_events/death_management_events.txt` lines 95–105 and 141–291 (reassigning creditors on death), `game/events/relations_events/vassal_events.txt` lines 1033–1071, 1205–1215 and 1275–1290 (repayment), `game/common/on_action/death.txt` lines 2417–2425. The observation that the death event rebinds the debtor list to `player_heir` shows a **different** design from Seoul''s "debt is not piled onto every heir" (sp1.b64) (D13-C05, D13-C06).
- Actual delivery: `game/common/task_contracts/laamp_transport_contracts.txt` lines 367–445, 455–554 and 557–664, `game/events/dlc/ep3/ep3_contract_events.txt` lines 78–94, 393–418, 514–536, 2363–2368 and 2446–2475, `game/common/on_action/travel_on_actions.txt` lines 186–222 and 1110–1150. The temporary change of the owner variable during contract travel was read as an expression of transport custody, not as a separate legal transfer of ownership (D13-C07).
- Title-bound inheritance: `game/common/on_action/title_on_actions.txt` lines 1067–1129 and 1387–1437. Among the previous holder''s artifacts, those matching the title-designated variable are passed to the new holder (D13-C08).
- Financial relief and debt tiers: `game/common/character_interactions/10_tgp_interactions.txt` lines 11626–11782, `game/common/defines/00_defines.txt` lines 295–302 (array of debt tiers based on months of income).
- Wiki: The official hosted community documents newly cross-checked this time are five: Succession laws oldid=36702, Prisoner oldid=36542, Subjects oldid=36525, Authority laws oldid=36448, and Resources oldid=36512. Separately, the three documents **Government oldid=36619, Laws oldid=36572, Power sharing oldid=36540** retain only their existing past citations. Because the exact past index paths for these three no longer exist, they are not newly read evidence this time, and no replacement source text was adopted. The sibling-split ratio and cap of the gold exception on succession remain only as observations and are not Seoul values (D13-C11).
- Reading limits: This is a cross-check of installed scripts and wiki documents; the raw engine internals (fixed-point arithmetic, allocation atomicity, storage format) have not been verified. Some claims are classified as refuted or held back from promotion.

### 11.2 Norland

- Reading authority: **official hosted wiki documents and developer announcements** (documentary only). There is no installed-build badge, and nothing has been verified by execution.
- Ring inheritance: developer announcement **Patch #25** (announcement ID `6146943657173940808`) — when a ruler dies, the ring passes to the spouse or eldest child of the same faction, with the condition that both are on the regional map. This is an observation of conditional transfer.
- Debt of honor: developer announcement **Patch #36 (MAIN)** (ID `1797820624494526`) — it remains valid while both rulers are alive and does not need to be claimed immediately. The same announcement also includes a bug fix for when a king inherits a ring.
- Delivery-order bug fix: developer announcement **Patch #41 (MAIN)** (ID `1813041031246909`) — a fix for a bug where the ransom quest appeared before the prisoner was actually delivered to the city. This is an after-the-fact repair observation pointing in the same direction as Seoul''s ordering rule that "arrival and delivery come first" (`economy-owner.20261003.arrival`, sp1.b65).
- Storage and funds: developer announcement **Patch #42 (MAIN)** (ID `1815580768299345`) — fixes for vagrants stealing from storage, for confiscated goods going to the player''s storage when the thief is caught, and for the self-quests for depositing and withdrawing funds.
- Borrowing: developer announcement **Patches 46–48 (MAIN)** (ID `1825093633188129`) — non-repayment only provokes the creditor''s anger, and the loan limit is adjusted to 500 coins. Seoul does not adopt this amount (12.7).
- Wiki: Trade oldid=1275 (trade contract cost and duration, world-map transport by peasants), Lords oldid=1314 (price ±3% per trade technology point), Construction oldid=1385 (State Gold storage), Warfare oldid=1319 (prisoners escorted to settlements by peasants, equipment used from the inventory). These figures are merely observations, not Seoul prices.
- Reading limits: This is a cross-check of documents and announcements; the raw processing (queues, concurrency, storage) is unknown. The generalized ownership/custody/use triple structure of the state-custody record is not exposed (source boundary axis `ownership/custody/use` = documented_analogues).

## 12. Not Yet Decided: Alternatives Ready for Decision

The items below are not blanks. Each is in a decision-pending state, equipped with a question, actual consumer use, ideal outcome, current gap, rationale, and verification method (QA). No value is filled in implicitly.

### 12.1 GP-09·T68-D09-COUNTRY-LAWS — Actual National Laws and Inheritance Systems

- Question: What are each country''s marital property and inheritance eligibility; who holds a deferred inheritance when there is no national property system; and which institutional procedure takes over the administrative authority to run the institution''s treasury after a post becomes vacant? The owner chose the five directions of registered will, whole-estate allocation, collateral maintenance, estate review, and acceptance reservation, and the four of jurisdictional law forum, party consent, adoption consent, and per-asset signature contract (version draft-v1), but the selection contract explicitly excluded share ratios, residual property, creditor priority, and the content of actual national laws.
- Actual consumer use: owner (review of institution approval), future implementer (beneficiary determination and custody authority mapping), verifier (ledger reconciliation of the pending state), player (display of `Jedo mijeong` (institution undecided) on the inheritance screen), evidence reader (reporting of unresolved state).
- Ideal outcome: each country''s system registers its beneficiary determination rules and the custodian of deferred inheritances, and for countries without one, the inheritance remains deferred.
- Current gap: there is no per-country system content, and shares, residuals, and creditor priority are undecided. It is not possible to arbitrarily invent one system and determine beneficiaries.
- Reason: the per-country legal systems of the Seoul world are the owner''s world-design decisions, not engineering defaults.
- QA: a before/event/after cross-check of whether the inheritance remains deferred in a death case with no system, whether allocation occurs only at the whole-estate level in a registered-will case, and whether shares and residuals are preserved without a new ratio.

### 12.2 T68-D12-PERMANENT-DETENTION — Property Management During Detention

- Question: The property of a living prisoner remains with that person. Who manages the custody of and access to that property during detention, and how? Whether ownership remains and whether inheritance opens have already been decided (closed).
- Actual consumer use: the property display on the detention status screen, and the custodian designation flow.
- Ideal outcome: it is chosen whether the fact of detention produces no change of custodian or creates explicit administrative authority, and inheritance does not open.
- Current gap: there is not yet a management method. This document says only that "detention does not open inheritance."
- Reason: the details of the detention system are decided together with the owner in D12 succession and body transition.
- QA: a cross-check of which one of the nine axes of asset state changes in a detention-start event, and that no state column opens.

### 12.3 T68-D17-PRICE-ACCEPTANCE — Price Function and Acceptance Adjustment

- Question: Under the approved snapshot of the same war and offer version, what are the specific price function and acceptance adjustment, and the detailed inputs for refusal, counteroffer, expiry, and violation?
- Actual consumer use: item prices on the enhancement negotiation screen and the `Pyeongga mijeong` (valuation pending) display, acceptance determination.
- Ideal outcome: under the structure V=Σdemands−Σofferings (sp1.b126), the price functions for all categories and the acceptance adjustment coefficients are registered.
- Current gap: no price coefficient or acceptance adjustment has been approved. This document speaks only of the structure of V and the unregistered lock.
- Reason: general pricing and acceptance coefficients are on the list of "things not to be created from what has not been decided" (12.7).
- QA: a cross-check of three cases: signature lock on unregistered items, negotiation permitted on registered items, and non-automatic acceptance when V≤W.

### 12.4 GP-13 — Reparations and Prisoner Delivery Outside Registered Prices

- Question: What kind of performance are reparations and prisoner delivery outside the registered price? A person is not a good to be transferred.
- Actual consumer use: the display of non-price items within a peace treaty, and performance tracking.
- Ideal outcome: a feasibility check is registered for each kind of non-price performance (release, repatriation, interview, voluntary assignment).
- Current gap: the performance method is undecided. This document says only that a demand for a person is not a demand for goods.
- Reason: the detailed performance of war termination is decided together with D16 war and prisoners.
- QA: a cross-check that prisoner items are not recorded as assets in the asset ledger, and that performance state is distinguished from the delivery state column.

### 12.5 T68-D11-CONTRACT-RECALL — Custody Transfer in Gifts and Contract Obligations

- Question: Which obligations in a gift or contract also transfer custodial responsibility?
- Actual consumer use: the display of custodian changes on the gift and delegation screens.
- Ideal outcome: for each kind of obligation, what is transferred among ownership, custody, and access is registered.
- Current gap: D13 reads only whether gifts and contracts include obligations, and leaves the kinds of obligations to the domain of contracts (D11).
- Reason: the kinds of contract obligations and their recall belong to D11 grants and delegation.
- QA: a cross-check that, in a gift event, the transfer of ownership and the change of custodial responsibility are recorded independently.

### 12.6 GP-06 Current Selection Policy and Remaining Logistics Inputs

- Currently selected policy: a split parent remains as an immutable history record, and the sum of the two children''s active quantities equals the original quantity. A handover requires the confirming signature of the designated recipient. On a reservation conflict, the existing reservation is kept, the reason for the conflict and an explicit re-proposal are shown, and no automatic queue is created. Daily closing uses a common phase and does not halt movement. For independent blockades and arrivals at the same time, input receipt order applies; for causally linked inputs, the order of the accepted causes applies. A receipt completed by an eligible arrival is not deleted by a later blockade or failure retry. A failed handover is quarantined locally to block consumption, and for non-delivery the existing reservation is kept and the shortfall is recorded.
- Actual consumer use: the logistics screen distinguishes dispatched, in transit, awaiting handover, verified receipt, quarantined, and undelivered, and cargo verified late is read at the first eligible closing after that. Player information display follows the knowledge and authority limits of Chapter 8.
- Open questions and gaps: the receipt identification protocol, the authentication method, the clock and calendar origin, the exact cycle and phase values, the supply rate, the shortage coefficient, and durable storage are undecided. The selected policy does not mean the full logistics rules or their implementation are complete.
- QA: distinguish simultaneous arrival at closing, duplicate events for the same cargo, local quarantine, and a verified arrival after non-delivery but before the next closing, and check that ID, ownership, total quantity, reservations, and completed-receipt history are preserved. The actual handover authentication and storage implementation cannot be said to pass merely because a policy was selected.

### 12.7 List of Unapproved Numbers

General price, acceptance coefficient, equal-distribution ratio, interest, length of the closing cycle, and ransom amount. These values are **undecided**, not 0 or an implicit default. The only numbers that already exist are the boundary that mid-negotiation is possible when both sides are under 100 in absolute value, and the boundary that if 100 or its opposite boundary is reached before acceptance, forced just-cause termination takes precedence (sp1.b122, sp1.b127). Those numbers are neither a price nor a distribution ratio. Numbers from external references (rarity multiplier, ring inheritance conditions, a loan of 500 coins, trade cost of 150) are observations, not Seoul values (Chapter 11).

### 12.8 Evidence Status

- **Basic original**: the 2004 original text (the 2004 GURPS Basic Set, Fourth Edition) is still not obtained. The basic dependency calculation gap in D13 is empty, so it does not block independent writing of parts of this document. Partially disclosed material is not adopted as the original.

## 13. Summary

- **Approved**: the separation of asset type from the nine state axes, the separation of ownership from actual delivery, the separation of succession events, no initiation of permanent detention, the order and total conservation of death transfers, retention of institutional assets, suspension of inheritance when the beneficiary is unconfirmed, permanent storage fields, the inspection order and the ban on resending event IDs, inclusion of verified arrivals up to the closing and their concurrent ordering, the gates of enhanced item-by-item negotiation (locking of unregistered prices, no duplicate demands, V≤W is non-automatic, ±100 forced termination takes precedence after rechecking the continuing just cause and eligible beneficiary, and signed-then-delivered contracts), and the boundary on the property effects of marriage.
- **Directions chosen by the owner (draft-v1)**: registered wills, allocation of all assets, holding of estate-review debts, transfer of title with collateral maintained, reservation upon acceptance, a jurisdictional law forum, direct consent of the parties, consent of the parties to adoption, and per-asset signed contracts. Of the seven choices in GP-06, only the selection contract was confirmed in the latest independent receipt (12.6).
- **Undecided**: the national law and inheritance system of Chapter 12, management during detention, the price function, performance outside registered prices, transfer of custody of contractual obligations, the remaining inputs of GP-06 such as actual authentication, clock, supply rate, and storage, and the list of numbers in 12.7.
- This document remains a proposal and does not claim that gameplay has been implemented.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d14', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d14', 'D14 Knowledge, Education, Technology, Restoration', '', 'proposal', 121, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d14.md', '# D14 Knowledge, Education, Technology, Restoration

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/faith-culture-and-knowledge/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

Obtaining a document, understanding its contents, actually practicing, performing a skill, and gaining institutional qualification are all different things. The player finds a teacher, arranges access rights and practice conditions, and carries out actual instruction. Discovering a record or naming a successor does not immediately unlock a skill. This document distinguishes confirmed action and preservation boundaries from policies not yet chosen. The duration, cost, and outcomes of education, and the numbers for individual proficiency, are undecided.

## 1. Actors and Player Decisions

The player continues the campaign with a single main-body character. NPCs also follow the same movement, education, authority, and resource conditions. A teacher accepts or refuses a request based on their actual availability and authority, and does not accept automatically just because the player chose them. An institution exercises authority over the records and educational procedures it manages. A sender reports only what they actually know, within their own disclosure authority and delivery channels. Switching screens is neither duplicating the main body nor rewinding time. There is one world time, and there is no battle pause, speed control, or command queue. Education likewise creates no new time control. [S1: sp1.b3, b12, b75, b92]

What the player decides is which skill to learn, whom to ask, which records to access, whether to secure tools and a place, and, after an interruption, whether to ask a new teacher to recognize existing progress. Skill-specific requirements and the other party''s acceptance limit the choices that are actually possible. Naming an apprentice is a choice about eligibility as a candidate for a position, and does not substitute for completing skill proficiency. The approved identity rule is to distinguish persons by stable ID and to verify the ledger version and current state. A feature for finding persons or records by name or search term is a separate, unadopted proposal, and a name or an old roster alone does not confirm current survival, location, or authority. [S1: sp1.b12, b73; S0: identity-version-permission; latest instruction]

## 2. Qualifications and Pre-Start Information

Each skill separately specifies which of teacher proficiency, records, tools, practice site, and learner conditions are required. The same five items are not forced on every skill. If an input listed as required for a skill is missing, that education does not proceed. High trust does not create nonexistent tools, unreachable places, or authority to teach. A job title or the existence of a record does not substitute for an actual skilled practitioner. Qualification to maintain and qualification to manufacture are also separate. The specific list of those skills and the numeric boundaries have not yet been chosen. [S1: sp1.b12, b72, b76; S2]

Before an action, checks are made in this order: target and ledger version, feasibility such as survival and detention, authority, actual location and delivery, resources and exclusive reservations, then the other party''s intent and institutional procedures. A report known to the player carries its source and the time of confirmation. Even if the teacher has actually died in the world, a report that has not yet arrived is not automatically copied into a character''s knowledge. Conversely, even if an old report states that the teacher is alive, it does not pass the actual performance conditions for execution. [S1: sp1.b12, b75]

A knowledge object has content and version, source, authenticity and verification, confidentiality, and ownership or access rights. A learner separately has records encountered, understanding, practice progress, performed proficiency, and qualification. Ownership of a medium and access rights are also not the same. A successor without access rights to an institution''s secrets does not receive the secrets merely by being the successor. [S1: sp1.b70, b76]

## 3. From Request to Outcome

Education proceeds as request → acceptance by teacher or institution → skill-specific requirement check → reservation of schedule, tools, and place → instruction, reading, and practice → assessment → recording of actual progress and proficiency. Request, acceptance, reservation, actual education, and assessment are not merged into a single unlock. In place of an assessment formula not yet decided, no arbitrary success rate or progress amount is applied. [S1: sp1.b72; S2]

Conditions are checked again after acceptance and before actual execution. If there is a death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact, it is checked whether the reserved work is still possible as is. Even if canceled or interrupted, materials already consumed are not automatically refunded, and completed progress and existing proficiency are preserved. Only unused reserved resources are released under the relevant contract. Refund amounts, penalties, or periods not in that contract are not newly invented. [S1: sp1.b13, b73]

If a record was read but required practice could not be done, only access and understanding change, and proficiency is not completed. When moving to another teacher, progress already earned is not erased, and the recognizable progress for that skill is checked. Automatic assignment of a replacement teacher or unconditional recognition is not a confirmed rule. Transmission and recovery are actual skill-specific education, and differ from copying documents, positions, or property. [S1: sp1.b70, b73; S2]

Re-delivering the same causal event does not create a new result. Reapplying inheritance of the same document or the death of the same teacher does not raise proficiency twice. Saving and loading are not in-world death or departure, and restore ongoing education and reservations at their current stage. An incompatible rules version is treated as an explicit error, and new rules are not mixed in without an approved transition. [S1: sp1.b13, b76, b93, b107, b109]

## 4. Failure and Remaining State

Teacher death, tool breakage, learner detention, and loss of the venue are grounds for interruption or suspension. Teacher departure, record loss, facility destruction, and parts shortage are each recorded as their actual causes. The death of a teacher is not treated as though documents and facilities also vanish with them. A failure of acceptance is not completion of education, nor is it a loss of proficiency already held. [S1: sp1.b73; S2]

Forgery, exposure, and refutation do not erase the original but leave competing evidence and events. A confidentiality leak separately leaves the exposed information and the responsibility of the person who leaked it. None of trust, authenticity, ownership, or disclosure authority automatically resolves the others. A report does not give the whole of current world facts, and acquiring information is not made into a fixed repeating resource reward. Delivery speed, information staleness, and exploration success probability are undecided. [S1: sp1.b75; S3]

## 5. Before, During, and After Action Cases

The following quantities are case inputs that show state preservation, and are not education costs or outcome coefficients. Completed progress is not converted into a personal proficiency score.

### Case A: Teacher Death and Education Cancellation

Before: the learner has 2 completed practice stages, existing performed proficiency, and a document already read. Of 5 reserved materials, 2 have already been consumed and 3 are unused. The contract in this case releases unused reservations upon cancellation. The teacher was alive and the next practice session had been accepted.

Action: the teacher dies, and the required teacher condition disappears. The next practice session is rechecked and the reason for interruption is recorded.

After: The 2 completed stages remain as they are, and the existing proficiency is kept. The 2 consumed slots stay consumed, and only the 3 unused ones are released (5 − 2 = 3). The death creates no new progress and does not add the 2 already-consumed slots back to inventory. Receiving the same death event again gives the same result. A request can be made to a new teacher, but acceptance and recognition of progress are separate matters. The teacher''s death in itself is neither an inheritance of documents nor a destruction of facilities. [S1: sp1.b13, b70, b73, b76]

### Case B: Document Inheritance and Lack of Practice

Before: The successor has no performance proficiency and is eligible to inherit the document media. The example skill requires actual practice. Access to the institution''s secrets is separate, and the successor does not have it.

Action: Inherits the document media and reads the permitted content. Does not gain a practice site or access to the relevant secrets.

After: Owning the media and having access to and understanding of what was read may differ, but proficiency is not complete. Institutional secrets are not acquired automatically. Applying the same inheritance event again does not copy proficiency. This example does not force practice on every skill; it shows the boundary of one skill authored as requiring practice. [S1: sp1.b70, b72, b73, b76]

### Case C: Report vs. Actual Arrival

Before: The learner knows only a report that the teacher was at the training site at an earlier time. The actual teacher has already moved to another location. The cargo of teaching tools arrives after the settlement cutoff.

Action: Requests instruction based on that report, but at execution checks the actual teacher''s availability, contact, and tool arrival.

After: The old report remains as knowledge with a source and a confirmation time, and does not guarantee the current location. The lesson is not carried out with tools that have not yet arrived. Arrivals after the cutoff are excluded from that cutoff''s settlement, but they are not in transit forever once they actually arrive. Arrival and inventory updates are handled by the logistics domain, and D14 refers to actual availability when instruction is executed. [S1: sp1.b12, b75, b76; S3: remaining-owner.20261003.summary]

## 6. Ownership Boundaries with Other Domains

D14 is responsible for knowledge objects, each learner''s separate knowledge, progress, and proficiency states, education requests, acceptance, and interruption, and the sourced knowledge created by arrival reports. Person identity, survival, and location belong to the Person and World Ledger; successors and positions belong to Succession; media ownership and delivery belong to Assets; actual cargo arrival and inventory belong to Logistics; and report movement belongs to the Movement and Information domain. The historical roster does not replace the current World Ledger. [S1: sp1.b12, b70, b75; D14 manuscript: source.b26–b28]

Combat is Total War–style unit command and applies to all squads assigned to a commander. Immediate squad-leader autonomy and evaluation belong to the combat domain, and any unit that actually arrives during an ongoing battle is subject to the reinforcement conditions. D14 does not cut off reinforcements with an additional cutoff or capacity limit. Immediately before executing a forced effect from war score ±100 within the target scope, a war rechecks whether its casus belli is still valid. If the casus belli is invalid, that forced effect is not executed even if ±100 has been reached. The fixed eligible beneficiary is kept separate from the attacker and is not automatically replaced by the attacker. Territory, effective control, rights, and settlement are distinct. In item-by-item negotiations below an absolute value of 100, the sum of demanded and offered values is not automatic acceptance; registered prices, actual authority, and rechecks are required. Reservation, signing, and handover are also separate. This boundary is not brought into D14''s education and proficiency formulas. [Latest instruction; D14 manuscript: source.b28]

## 7. What Was Confirmed in the Original Game and the Scope Used in Seoul

### Crusader Kings III

In the installation scripts of CK3 1.20.0.3, build 25652598, child education points, language learning progress, lifestyle XP, and cultural innovations have different owners, units, and procedures. Child education is initialized only when no existing variable is present. Language learning progress is stored when the learner is alive, the target has died or gone out of range, and the language is not yet a native or known language. A replacement target must satisfy the same language and starting eligibility, and restoration is not generalized to every interruption or abandonment. [C-CHILD, C-LANGUAGE, C-CULTURE]

The learning lifestyle default in the same version is 25 XP per month. Combined with the top-level `monthly_learning_lifestyle_xp_gain_mult = 0.4` of the `education_learning_4` trait, the arithmetic is 25 × 1.4 = 35. This is not an observed education rate that includes other modifiers or engine rounding, and it is not adopted as a Seoul figure. The weights 100 and 75 of map events 121 and 122 are relative weights, and the 5-year reuse interval is not a sustained growth rate. [C-XP, C-TEACH]

In Seoul, the use is a comparison that distinguishes access, educational process, and proficiency outcome and makes the interrupted state explicit. Seoul''s confirmed basis is separately in S1 and S2. The ideal application is to expose per-skill requirements and the preservation of actual stages, and the difference is that Seoul''s actual tools, locations, public authority, and institutional secrets cannot be replaced by CK3''s single XP. This script comparison does not prove actual engine execution or a ruling on an individual''s proficiency in Seoul.

### Norland

Norland Knowledge wiki revision 1293 lists 3 sheets of paper for copying a learned book. The Patch #27 announcement of 2024-11-22 states 3/6/9 sheets by knowledge stage, and the release Patch #28 of 2024-12-13 states that compressed notes reduce paper use. Because the point at which the wiki revision corresponds to the installed build has not been confirmed, the historical cost and the modifier without a stated reduction amount are not combined to compute the current cost. [N-01, N-02]

Actions wiki revision 1318 describes the transmission of Wise Conversation as probabilistic. The 2025-06-25 beta announcement describes guaranteed transmission when the recipient''s intelligence condition is met, two transmissions by the elderly, and the Enlightener''s exception for ineligible listeners, and 50% is the cultural conversion probability. The 2026-06-26 beta Patch #54 and the 2026-07-31 release Patches 54–56 respectively announced, on the world map, the condition of a relationship of 25 or more between the ruler and the player ruler and the exception for Thirst for Knowledge. It is not extended to a relationship condition for every on-site conversation. [N-03–N-06]

In Construction wiki revision 1385, repairing a breakdown costs 2 tools per building stage. The announcements correcting the brewery cost from 3 iron to 2 tools appear in the 2026-04-24 beta and the 2026-05-11 release respectively. Wood repair of burned buildings and stage-independent costs are a different damage state, announced in the 2025-06-20 beta and the 2025-07-14 release. [N-07–N-10]

The announcement that economic knowledge becomes shared knowledge 10 days after it becomes known in a province appears in the 2025-11-21 beta Patch #43 and the 2025-12-12 release Patches 43–45 respectively. It is not extended to all knowledge. The notification merging in the 2025-11-27 beta is a display change and does not prove that learning timers and eligibility are unchanged. The explanation of city knowledge generation in the 2025-04-02 beta states an intent that already-learned knowledge should not appear and that culture-specific knowledge is mainly chosen; it is not a definite exclusion or a selection of culture-specific knowledge only. [N-11, N-12, N-22, N-58]

In Seoul, the use is a comparison that separates recording media, transmission, recovery, and version conditions. The ideal application is to trace which knowledge was conveyed to whom and under what conditions. The difference is that these sources do not prove the preservation of a disciple''s partial XP after the teacher''s death, the exact education duration and XP formula, the order of rejection, waiting, consumption, and refund for requests without inventory, or Seoul''s confidentiality and eligibility policy. The beta and release announcements each have their own source scope and are not proof of execution in installed build 25211575. Those gaps are not filled with Seoul''s rules.

## 8. Concrete Alternatives for Undecided Policies

The following are design proposals with options. None of the options has been adopted automatically. In common, they separate knowledge access from proficiency and preserve actual completed progress and consumption.

| Part, State, Basis | Where Consumers Use It, Ideal | Gap, Reason | Concrete Alternatives and Behavior After Selection | QA |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Access rules: GP-10 not approved; S0, S1.b70·b75·b76 | Learners and institutions judge whether records can be viewed; clarify ownership versus viewing rights | No per-institution grant or revocation conditions; inheritance alone cannot create public authority | A: explicit viewing rights per person, B: viewing rights attached to institutional positions. After selection, author the basis of the right, the version, and a recheck upon retirement | A successor or owner without access rights, but unable to view secrets; execution after retirement |
| Progress recognition: GP-10 not approved; S1.b73 | Review of a substitute teacher''s education request; distinguish preserving existing achievements from a new assessment | No recognition criteria; preservation is not automatic recognition | A: recognize completed stages that have evidence, B: the new teacher recognizes them after reassessment. After selection, author per-skill recognition inputs; neither option permits deleting existing records | Prior completion records are retained despite the teacher''s death, lost evidence, or refusal to reassess |
| Reservation and interruption details: GP-10 not approved; S1.b13·b72 | Institution and asset managers release unused reservations; prevent double reservation | No release conditions or cancellation cost | A: release the unused reservation for that education immediately, B: release at the end of the contractual joint practice. After selection, specify the reservation owner and release event; neither option refunds consumption | Distinguishing consumed from unused before cancellation, event retransmission, tool-sharing conflicts |
| Maintenance, manufacturing, and recovery conditions: GP-10 not approved; S1.b76, S2 | Skill performers judge actually available actions; separate requirements by qualification | No per-skill conditions or numeric boundaries | A: separate skill entries and qualifications, B: separate performance requirements within one skill. After selection, author it so that maintenance success does not imply manufacturing permission | Able to maintain but not manufacture, facility relocation, parts shortage, capture of a skilled person |
| Duration, cost, outcome, and proficiency conversion: Basic dependency not approved; S0, S2 | Education scheduling, resources, and individual proficiency rulings; unit consistency between actual progress and figures | The relevant clause and version of the original Basic that would determine individual proficiency, and Seoul''s conversion rule, are undecided | A: directly adopt the source''s learning and proficiency units, B: a separate Seoul conversion that distinguishes them from the source''s units. Neither option is the current rule, and figures are undecided | Review of source units, per-skill requirements, lack of practice, and duplicate application of the same result |
| State and institutional certification procedures: GP-10 not approved; S0 | State, teacher, and learner separate qualification from track record; a position does not copy proficiency | No procedures or per-skill requirements; no state policy has been selected | A: Each institution accepts and evaluates separately; B: A shared national evidence procedure plus institution-specific practical review. After selection, author the responsibilities and authority; numbers and technical requirements are approved separately. | Characters with only an occupation, succession designations, institutional secrets |
| Information aging and delivery: GP-02 not approved; S3, S1.b75 | The recipient interprets the report; the actual world and knowledge are kept distinct | No speed, aging, or exploration probability | A: Show the report time as is and let the player judge; B: Add a needs-confirmation state per version. After selection, specify what the state means; no arbitrary times or probabilities. | Reports of old locations, refutations, late-arriving reports, preservation of originals |

Although the actual performance of education and the preservation of achievements are confirmed, the per-skill numbers and policies remain undecided until the decisions in this table. Name, person, and record search is an unadopted proposal separate from stable-ID approval. Even if search is adopted, it does not mean a name match alone would establish the same person, access rights, or current status. Completing this document is not approval of the whole of D14, its numbers, its runtime, or the Basic source.

## Basis Notation

S0 is the scope of D14 decisions, S1 is the marked blocks of the single-player design SP-1.0.0, S2 is the per-skill actual education clauses, and S3 is the movement, information, and settlement clauses of campaign progression. C-CHILD, C-LANGUAGE, C-XP, C-CULTURE, and C-TEACH are the marked script mechanisms of CK3 1.20.0.3/build 25652598. N-01–N-12, N-22, and N-58 are Norland documents whose edition, release date, and beta or release version are marked above. The source work''s documents and script descriptions are separate from Seoul''s confirmed rules.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.mechanism-design-index-d15', 'system-design', 'mechanism-design-index-d15', 'D15. Combat, Command, Squads, Capacity', '', 'proposal', 122, 'system-design/mechanism-design-index-d15.md', '# D15. Combat, Command, Squads, Capacity

## Game Logic Overhaul Alignment Boundary

This document retains its existing proposal status. The formulas, tables, and blueprints are neither implementation facts of Seoul nor approved figures. The Seoul-fact / CK3-reference / product-authority distinctions in the linked chapters below and SP-1.0.0 take precedence, and undecided values are not interpreted as 0 or any arbitrary default. The deep-survey discrepancy totals and clan formulas are not adopted.

[Corresponding Chapters and Confirmed Grounds](/gdd/rules/war-and-personal-resolution/)

Status: Proposed. This distinguishes approved rules, selected policies, unadopted alternatives, and undecided values. It does not mean implementation is complete.

[Design Guide](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index/) · [Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/) · [Sheets and Calculations](/gdd/system-design/character-sheet-formulas/)

## 1. What the Player Decides

The player continues a single campaign through the body of one character, not the state itself. NPCs also follow the same qualification, authority, and resource conditions for movement, labor, education, politics, and combat. Combat controls are Total War–style unit selection and unit orders. They are not action inputs that continuously control a hero''s personal attacks and dodges. Individual resolution and unit aggregation are separate responsibilities that calculate the actual results of those orders.

If the main body is a battle commander, they directly select **all attached squads** within the command range registered for that battle and direct their movement, stance, and attacks. The existence of a separate squad leader does not block higher command authority. If the main body is a squad leader, they receive a superior commander''s orders within the battle as an immediate signal and choose among following, partially carrying out, or acting independently. Receiving an order is neither a control lock nor forced obedience. A superior main body''s valid command authority and a subordinate NPC''s execution judgment go through the same execution check. Autonomous judgment does not eliminate the superior''s attachment range.

The battlefield has no pause, slow-down or speed-up, or queue for stacking orders. The current state and change history of an order remain, but the history is not executed as a queue of future actions. The battlefield screen is shown only for battles in which the main body actually participates. In other battles, the local commander commands automatically using pre-set objectives, stance, and authority, along with the information, abilities, and targets they actually observed. Automatic and manual command use the same execution and result-application contract, and the utility formula, tie-breaking, and re-evaluation interval of automatic command are undecided.

Basis: SP-1.0.0 sp1.b92, b108, b111–115; combat rules combat-formula.20261003.pipeline/status; D15 source.b4–6, b20.

## 2. Qualifications for Orders and Participation

| Action | Preconditions | Changes After the Action | On Failure or When Undecided |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Commanding an attached squad | Target exists and is the current version, feasibility, registered command range and attachment, actual presence on site and delivery, resource and exclusive reservations, check of the other party''s intent/procedure | The receipt, execution, and cause of change of a valid order are recorded | Enemies, unattached independent forces, and allies outside the range cannot be controlled merely because they are visible |
| In-battle order delivery | Valid sender, target, and in-battle order relationship | Distinguishes the squad leader''s choice and actual execution after immediate receipt | During melee engagement, a new order is rejected until that engagement ends. The rejection is not recorded as carrying out the order or as deliberate disobedience |
| Ordering a distant unit | Valid command authority and an actual communication channel | Reservation/execution after delivery, receipt, and acceptance | An in-battle immediate signal is not extended into strategic communication or teleportation |
| Reinforcing an ongoing battle | The battle is still in progress and the unit has actually arrived | The arriving unit may participate; its current attachment and authority are checked separately | Eligibility to participate is not closed off by origin, rank, initial roster, or an arbitrary time window. Participation and the transfer of command authority are separate |
| Unit organization | A person''s acceptance, equipment and supplies, a commander, non-overlapping time reservations | Assigns an existing person to the actual organization | Merely viewing a roster or armory does not create people, equipment, or units |
| Withdrawing from the entire battle | A valid line of retreat and confirmation, and a valid order applying to the entire battle | Settles the established withdrawal result | A squad''s rearward movement is movement within the battlefield and is neither leaving participation nor the end of the battle |

A unit that arrives after a battle has ended cannot participate in that ended battle. However, this does not mean the fact of its arrival is erased and it is forever in transit. The movement domain updates the actual arrival and current location. Supplies that arrive after the consumption cutoff are excluded only from that cutoff. The logistics domain decides whether they can be used for the next eligible consumption, and their reservation. The logistics consumption cutoff is not a new participation cutoff for reinforcing an ongoing battle.

Basis: sp1.b11–14, b111–115; D15 source.b9–12, b24; OWNER-REINFORCEMENT-20261004; population rules actor-authority-labor.20261003.transitions. The concrete total ordering when arrival, capture of the main body, and termination occur at the same moment is an undecided policy in §8.

## 3. Compliance, Performance, World Facts, and Characters'' Information

At receipt, the world time, order identifier, original issuing commander, target squad, purpose, permitted scope, and version are recorded. After execution, the order''s purpose, time of receipt, feasibility, squad state, actual actions, contribution to objectives, losses, civilian harm, and any change or cancellation by a superior order are linked as evaluation data. **Compliance and performance are two separate records.** Contributing to an objective through independent action is not converted into order compliance. Nor is the failure of an impossible order, or of an order voided by the commander''s death, converted into arbitrary disobedience.

Evaluation results are attributed to the commander character and become the basis for memories of that main body and for later attachment and trust decisions. Even after a commander is replaced, the original sender and history are retained and re-checked against the new authority. The same evaluation result is not applied twice. Promotion and disciplinary conditions, the detailed linkage of responsibility allocation, the evaluation function, and trust change amounts have not yet been chosen.

Actions that actually occurred in the world and actions each character learned of through observation or reports are kept separate. A distant commander does not immediately know that a hidden action exists in the battle result ledger. The relationship evaluation reads whose report they learned it from and when. The display also distinguishes insufficient authority, unmet preconditions, awaiting a response, and an undecided system, and explains the lack of confirmed grounds instead of the hidden fact. Character and squad names should be searchable, but the identity behind namesakes, name changes, and commander replacements is confirmed by permanent identifiers. A historical roster is merely the basis for identity issuance at the time and not the basis for current survival, location, rights, or abilities.

Trust T[A,B] and T[B,A] are separate. The existing thresholds are retained: friend at 25 or above, enemy at -25 or below, and nemesis at -50 or below. Loyalty to a ruler on a 0–100 scale is a separate state. Kinship, contracts, oaths, and command are relationship facts, not trust scores themselves. High trust does not create command authority or consent that does not exist. A fixed trust reduction is not automatically assigned for disobedience, and relationship changes and decay require observed grounds and approved rules. A deceased person''s personal trust is not copied to their successor.

Basis: sp1.b13–14, b79–89, b111–115; D15 source.b14, b17, b24.

## 4. People, Organization, Capacity, and Responsibility for Results

Writing an aggregate resident as an individual is a change of representation that links an identity to an existing member. It is not the creation of a new person. The same person is not counted twice as aggregate available labor and as individual labor, nor reserved overlappingly at the same time for facility, transport, treatment, or unit roles. Birth, death, migration, and capture are separate population/recruit events. A return neither creates a new person nor revives a dead person''s reservations.

Capacity varies with the commander''s personal ability, the state''s level, and the settlement''s level. There is no common cap for all units. The former maximum of 20 per rank-and-file squad is not the current cap. Heroes are separate from rank-and-file troops and are not counted in the rank-and-file number. If the commander is absent, the temporary rank-and-file squad leader rule is kept, but no numbers are created for its effects. Even if capacity shrinks, excess personnel already in a unit are preserved. The definitions of ability and level, the capacity formula, the reorganization location for excess personnel, and the policy for handling new replacements are undecided. Preserving excess personnel is not approval of a new cap or of a restriction on arrival eligibility.

The approved scope of combat calculation is situational modifiers, individual resolution, unit aggregation, and a single result ledger. The situation includes the basis of command, morale, fatigue, supply, terrain, formation, and scale; the individual includes ability, skills, weapons, armor, physical condition, and mood; and the aggregate includes organization, participating personnel, equipment, and actual actions. The channels of individual hit, defense, DR, damage, shock, injury, consciousness, and death are not mixed with the channels of aggregate ability, attrition, morale, and operational capacity. If the aggregate is a means of inflicting damage, it must first be determined to which person''s action and target it is attributed. Each action and target has exactly one application responsibility; aggregate damage is not added after individual damage, and aggregate losses are not deducted twice from both the total and the individual. A ResultId is settled only once per campaign.

The applicable version, errata, and individual calculation inputs of the original Basic, as well as Seoul''s new functions, coefficients, upper and lower bounds, channel selection, casualty allocation, and time conversion, have not been closed. Approving the scope alone does not produce hit or loss numbers. Another game''s toughness or defense weighting is not converted into individual DR. Combat, encounter, world event, and character random numbers are separated by purpose, and the same world and events must be reproducible from the same seed and choice record, but no new random number algorithm has been chosen.

Basis: Intent Decision 15; D15 source.b12, b15–18, b24, b31; population rules transitions; combat-formula.20261003.pipeline/status; save rules source.b4/b8.

## 5. What Remains After Cancellation, Death, and Shutdown

If an in-world cancellation, death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the remaining execution, authority, and reservations are rechecked. Supplies already consumed and progress already completed are not rolled back. Only the remaining unused resources are released under the relevant contract. Canceling an invalid order does not erase actions already carried out, losses already incurred, or witness and evaluation history.

Changing the screen''s subject because the main body dies or is permanently detained, a unit''s temporary command, item-by-item succession of rights, and the detention of a living character are separate events. If the new main body is far away, it exists there and is not duplicated onto the battlefield. Previous command authority is not copied automatically. Shutdown/loading differs from in-world death or withdrawal; it restores the work in progress in the current phase and the results already settled. Resending the same event does not duplicate people, equipment, damage, or progress. A version mismatch or corrupted save is an explicit error, and gaps are not filled with draft numbers or POC values.

Basis: sp1.b13, b51–52, b88–94, b107–109, b115; D15 source.b24.

## 6. Scenes Read Before and After a State

The numbers below are hypothetical state inputs used to explain preservation and boundaries. They are not game values for combat ability, price, capacity, or time intervals.

### Scene A: Higher Command Authority and Independent Action

Before the action, two squads, Cheongmun and Seokgyo, are attached within the registered range of the main-body commander, and each has its own separate squad leader. The allied Munru squad is outside the range. The main body can issue valid orders to both Cheongmun and Seokgyo, while Munru can only be observed. In another scene where the main body is a squad leader, it immediately receives a higher commander''s order to defend the bridge, but it may choose to take a detour to carry out a rescue. After the action, compliance is recorded as independent action, and the result is recorded separately according to the actual rescue and defensive contribution. Observer Gap knows about the rescue, but Eul, who has not yet received the report, does not. Eul''s trust is not updated automatically. No numbers for damage or trust changes are produced.

### Scene B: Late Reinforcements and Separate Command Authority

Before the action, the battle is in progress and Dasol''s unit is on the move. It was not on the initial roster. If the confirmed sequence is that it actually arrived at world time 12 and the battle ended at 13, Dasol gains eligibility to take part in the ongoing battle. Without a registered attachment, the main body gains no authority for direct control. Conversely, in the independent input where the battle ends at 11 and arrival is at 12, it cannot join the finished battle, but the completed movement and the place of arrival remain. If the end and arrival occur at the same time and their order is not confirmed, participation is not decided arbitrarily. This scene does not create a 1-unit reinforcement deadline.

### Scene C: Preservation of Personalization, Reservation, and Consumption

Before the action, the total population is 500, with 500 aggregate and 0 individual. Personalizing 5 gives 495 aggregate + 5 individual = 500. Assigning 3 of them to a unit gives 495 unassigned aggregate + 2 unassigned individual + 3 in the unit = 500. The 3 in the unit are not also added to facility labor at the same time. Of 10 prepared supplies, 4 are actually consumed and 6 are unused; after 2 stages of preparation progress are completed, the commander dies and the remaining work is canceled. After the action, the 4 consumption records and 2 completed progress stages are preserved, and the remaining 6 supplies are released under the contract. There is no full refund of all 10 and no rewinding progress to 0. Death is a separate population event, so the 500-person preservation of personalization itself must not be confused with the result of the death.

### Scene D: Excess Personnel and Re-receiving the Same Result

Before the action, assume the capacity of a unit with 8 soldiers is validly lowered externally to 6. The excess 8−6=2 are not deleted, so after the action the existing soldiers number 8. This does not approve a formula that yields 6, nor a policy for reassigning the 2 excess. If result R, already applied once, is received again, the application count stays at 1. Even if the same attack is issued again through the individual and aggregate channels, the same damage is not added twice. If the damage distribution formula is undecided, the example is not closed by inventing new casualty numbers.

## 7. Confirmed Content from the Two Source Games and Its Use in Seoul

### Crusader Kings 3

The combat phase description in CK3 Community Warfare document version 36436 is 2 days. The installation declaration in the preserved CK3 1.20.0.3, build25652598, checksum8e45 is 3 days. These two sources are different authorities and are not merged into an execution result of the same version. Native execution of casualty distribution, rounding, individual identity linking, final capacity selection, and excess handling has not been confirmed, and the execution truth of the current build is not settled by this comparison alone. Neither time value is Seoul''s combat cycle.

What is worth referencing is the structure that reads individual injuries, aggregate casualties, duels, commander growth, and morale, attack, and defense separately. Aggregate toughness is not a DR subtracted from a single blow. A commander''s result/terrain experience is not a per-order evaluation of feasibility, compliance, and outcome. Seoul uses its own approval contract that separates compliance from outcome, and does not substitute CK3''s experience or relative weights. The reinforcement replenishment rate and actual arrival and participation on the battlefield are also different channels. Seoul''s caps, arrival windows, and queues are not set from unresolved width, replenishment cycles, native RNG, or retreat-order handling.

### Norland

The Norland Community Warfare version 1319 example of dagger 10 / light armor 10 / heavy armor 30 and the preserved build25211575 values of sword cut 130, sword total 273, light armor total 430, and heavy armor total 1460 are different labels/authorities. No correspondence between the preserved build25211575 and the patch notes below has been confirmed. The heavy armor total is 60+400+1000=1460, and the old 1490 is not used. This total is not interpreted as an actual probability or scalar DR.

Patch38''s technical 20-person squad limit is that patch''s own rule. It is not brought into Seoul''s variable capacity. Patch36''s 60-person Unholy Horde limit after capture is a modification for a specific faction and condition, not a universal capacity or a people-conservation formula. Patch54''s victory loyalty of (10+Command)% differs from the order-compliance evaluation. Patch57 BETA''s description of preventing flight/surrender for 10 minutes after entering combat is not Seoul''s melee lock, withdrawal, or reinforcement restriction. Patch40 BETA''s descriptions of gate/archer and tower destruction do not prove a gate-before-tower execution order. Warfare1319''s death at pain>50 and States1262''s 5-hour unconsciousness after exceeding a threshold conflict with each other, and neither is chosen as the current native truth. What is referenced is the distinction between the channels of individual state, morale, command, and capacity, not a wholesale transplant of the numbers.

The documents and declarations in these sources do not signify confirmation of native execution in the source games or adoption of rules by Seoul. Time, capacity, and probability are not used beyond the scope of application of each source.

## 8. Concrete Alternatives Not Yet Chosen

Every row in the table below is **unapproved** and is not the current default. Each alternative is a different game behavior for the same gap. Values that depend on Basic require a source basis and a separate decision.

| Item · Status | Affected Area and Required Outcome | Current Gap and Reason | Concrete Game Behavior Alternatives |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GP-08 Muster and Reorganization Policy | Organization/Economy: acceptance, equipment source, reservations, and the actual location of excess personnel | Only preservation of the excess is approved; reorganization procedure undecided | A Keep the excess personnel in the existing organization / B Move existing people to a reserve organization. Both alternatives preserve the history of people, equipment, and reservations, and do not restrict the participation eligibility of reinforcements in ongoing battles |
| GP-08 and Individual Values for Variable Capacity | Organization: capacity and replenishment feasibility | Definitions of individual ability, state, and settlement and the Basic inputs are not finalized | A Sum of the three inputs as independent terms / B Combination of the three inputs by bottleneck terms. Values and functions are undecided in both alternatives, and there is no common cap |
| GP-11 Order Evaluation Linkage | Commander/Relationships: responsibility for the original order, action, and outcome | Separation of compliance and outcome is approved; details of the responsibility linkage and utility undecided | A Evaluation bundle of the original sender per order / B Link evaluations by modified-order interval to the original order. In either alternative, impossibility, refusal, and arbitrary disobedience are recorded as different things |
| GP-11 Individual, Situational, and Aggregate Calculation | Combat Results: single settlement of per-person damage, attrition, and operational capability | Basic source and Seoul''s coefficients/channels/distribution/time conversion undecided | A Aggregate from individual results / B Aggregate adjudication only for designated actions, then attribute to individuals. Responsibility for applying damage for one action and target is single, and new damage numbers are undecided |
| GP-11 Automatic Command Policy | Combat Without the Main Body: judgment based on local observation | Utility, tie-breaking, and re-judgment cycle undecided | A Re-judge when the observed state changes / B Re-judge at approved scheduled intervals and on events. The local information version is used, and melee lock and authority checks are maintained |
| GP-12 Reinforcement Location, Authority, and Simultaneous Events | Movement/Battlefield: participation location and eligible command range of units that actually arrive | Participation itself is approved; location, posture, authority transfer, and capture/end simultaneous ordering undecided | Location: A actual approach route entrance / B safety-verified entrance; authority: A recheck the existing contract / B recheck after a valid attachment-change event; simultaneous ordering: A confirmed acceptance order / B acceptance order after cause dependency. Safety verification is a location choice and not a new condition prohibiting participation. Neither alternative creates a new participation window |
| T68-D07-EVALUATION-DECAY Relationship Change | Character Relationships: separation of memory, trust, loyalty, and mood after observation | Change amounts, decay, and promotion/discipline unapproved | A Preserve per-event memory and end only duration effects / B Preserve memory and apply separately approved trust decay. No fixed disobedience penalty, instant omniscience, or copying of trust to a successor |
| T68-PF-04 Character Ability Corpus | Individual Adjudication/Education: ability and skill inputs for real figures | The historical gurps-cast proposal of Basic2004 CP/skill/derived values is not approved for everyone | A Confirmed ability and skill inputs per person / B Per-person evidence-based inputs built on confirmed role prototypes. Source, version, and individual evidence are required, and access to a document is not proficiency |
| T68-PF-06 Unit Identity and Numeric Correspondence | Organization/Saving: identity of people and units after renaming, splitting, and merging | Roster unit and new recruitment policy unapproved; old max_size/base_size deactivated | Identity: A parent squad ID and change history / B new squad ID and lineage upon an actual split. Numbers: A individually confirmed equipment/skill / B confirmed per-role inputs and individual-person evidence. Name search and permanent identity linkage are retained; CP prices, ability matching, and capacity numbers are undecided |
| T68-PF-07 Resident Personalization / Ability Generation | Residents/Organization: actual population and training evidence, and role acceptance | Generation rate, age/candidate policy, and ST/DX/IQ/HT/CP/skill assignment are not approved | Policy: A personalize existing members upon role acceptance / B personalize upon confirmation of existing resident records; Ability: A input based on individual training records / B input based on role templates and individual training evidence. Headcount does not increase, and affiliation or occupation alone does not grant equipment or skill |

An undecided state is not a value for actual damage, price, capacity, or relationship change. The existence of an alternative does not mean that behavior is executed under the current rules.

## 9. Contracts with and Handoffs to Other Domains

What D15 owns is command of units within the registered scope, squad leader orders, autonomous choices and evaluation data, actual organization and prevention of duplicate reservations, participation by arrival in an ongoing battle, and the input direction of variable capacity. Results from other domains are not automatically generated here.

| Owning Domain | Facts D15 Consumes/Provides | Meanings Not Handed Over |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Orders and Schedules D03 | Remote delivery, receipt, and acceptance; authority version / immediate in-battle orders and actual execution history | A battle signal does not replace remote delivery |
| Residents, Economy, and Logistics D04–06 | Actual people, equipment, supplies, reservations, and movement completion / organization, consumption, losses, and arrival participation | A consumption deadline is not a reinforcement deadline, and cancellation is not a consumption refund |
| Relationships and Evaluation D07 | Observation, reports, receipt, compliance, performance, and sender / commander memory data | Undecided trust coefficients and disciplinary actions are not automatically approved |
| Recruits and Succession D12–13 | Actual death, detention, injury, and succession of rights / interim command and re-check of remaining orders | Capture is not death, and a change of the on-screen subject is not a copy of battlefield authority |
| Knowledge and Education D14 | Actual per-skill education requirements, progress, and proficiency / reservation of people and roles | Document access or inheritance is not a copy of proficiency or instant unlocking; completed progress is preserved even if the teacher dies |
| War D16 | Battle results and prisoner/occupation evidence / re-check of casus belli, fixed beneficiary, and objective scope | Tactical victory or occupation is not a legal right or a completed takeover of a settlement. After continuously checking for a valid casus belli, D16 settles the objective-limited ±100 forced effect |
| Negotiation D17 | Actual authority, registered prices, per-item conditions, and re-check upon acceptance | A custom negotiation at abs(war score)<100 and the V-sum structure are not automatic acceptance. Reservation, signing, rights settlement, and actual delivery are kept separate |
| Save and World Time D18 | Single time, version, event/result IDs, and purpose-specific random numbers / consistent results and progress state | Document completion is not approval for automatic conversion of runtimes or earlier saves |

## 10. Boundaries of the Current Design

The main character''s battle command authority applies to all attached squads within the registered scope, and units outside the scope are not controllable merely because they are visible. Squad leader orders are received immediately, but following, partially carrying out, or acting independently are separate choices, and compliance and performance are also separate records. Participation of an actually arrived unit in an ongoing battle and command of that unit are not the same event.

Facts that occurred in the world and information a character has learned are distinguished. Even after cancellation, death, capacity reduction, or duplicate results, supplies already consumed, completed progress, personnel to be preserved, and the original sender''s history do not disappear. While the policy alternatives in §8 and the Basic-dependent numbers remain undecided, no new combat formulas, capacities, or trust change amounts are fixed, and the original work''s times, capacities, and probabilities are not used as Seoul''s defaults.');
