- [D17 Negotiation, Trade, Reinforcement, Contracts](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index-d17/) · [Situations](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/). Calculation links: SEOUL-NEGOTIATION-V, SEOUL-W-ENDPOINT, SEOUL-SINGLE-RESULT.
- [D18 Time, Events, Campaigns, Saving](/gdd/system-design/mechanism-design-index-d18/) · [Situations](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/). Calculation links: SEOUL-CUTOFF, SEOUL-SINGLE-RESULT.

## Basis and Adoption Boundary

The only references to the original work are the GURPS Fourth Edition preview that was read directly and edition-specific comparisons with each preserved document. The Seoul approval boundary is maintained only up to that boundary. The owner''s choice is a selected partial policy, not a full contract or implementation certification. Data types and the additional current-custodian check are unadopted proposals. Missing figures, systems, and original rules remain undecided.

## Situation Report Deliverables

[Situation Report](https://evidence.linalab.io/evidence/seoul-gdd-sheets-situations-20261007-f2bbda37-c30b-4c23-8684-b51724045d48/situations.html) · [Domain Design Proposal Reader](https://evidence.linalab.io/evidence/seoul-gdd-sheets-situations-20261007-f2bbda37-c30b-4c23-8684-b51724045d48/index.html). The reader is a design document that distinguishes the approved scope from unadopted proposals. The situations for each domain can be read in [Domain Situations and Cases](/gdd/system-design/domain-situations/).

## Canonical Navigation and Public Paths

[Design Home](/gdd/design/home/) · [Mechanism Reference Index](/gdd/references/ref-mechanism-index/). The supported publishing surface for GDD design documents is /design-store/. Browse each document and domain through the design table of contents.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Original-TRPG-Core', 'system-design', 'original-trpg-core', 'Original TRPG Core Rule System', '', 'accepted', 127, 'system-design/Original-TRPG-Core.md', '# Original TRPG Core Rule System

*Seoul: Nationwide* designs its own character system without operationally borrowing from external rule tables. The owner''s decision of 2026-10-12 sets the eight competencies (Leadership, Strategy, Negotiation, Administration, Insight, Learning, Skill, and Stamina) at a base integer of 0–20, a final integer of 0–100, and a separate action proficiency of 0–99.9. A base competency of 10 means an expert in that field, and no effect can push the final value above 100. The single-Skill 0–12 resolution profile of 2026-10-08 is preserved as a past design and is not automatically applied to the new character system. The new resolution formula, effect composition, and growth formula are undecided.

## Past Opposed d10 Resolution Profile

The 2026-10-08 profile used a fair d10 for each of the acting side and the resisting side to calculate margin = (rA − rB) + (aA − aB) + c. Positive, zero, and negative results were classified as success, deadlock, and resisting-side success. The distributions of 45·10·45 for equal conditions and 64·8·28 for a 2-point Skill advantage were confirmed by full enumeration at the time. This verification is limited to the old profile and does not prove the resolution formula or probabilities of the new eight-competency and proficiency system.

The Skill 0–12 and the modifier −4 to +4 of the past profile seoul-opposed-d10-0-12-c4-v1 are records of that edition. They are neither converted to the new base competency 0–20, final 0–100, and proficiency 0–99.9, nor simply expanded in range. Resolution does not automatically generate facts of consent, ownership, arrival, treatment, or death; authority and actual fulfillment follow separate contracts.

## Preservation of Existing Figures

The 54,467 character figures of the existing 1,022 characters have been sealed without change. Ability scores, skills, CP, and auxiliary figures are preserved read-only as historical setting values and are not automatically converted into the new Skill. Both historical editions (parent pin ce173686, working copy 5f34d92d) are maintained, and issuance, screens, and APIs require an explicit edition selection. There is no automatic latest selection.

## Traits, Abilities, and Icons

Traits affect performance, action choices, stress, and relationships, and are acquired through events and actions. Core dispositions do not change easily, but some traits and states change through experience. The trait list and numerical effects will be designed separately, and the past nine logic and eight perspective categories are not automatically adopted. Authority is not granted by ability scores. Icons are produced in Seoul''s own grammar, and traits for which there is no observation authority are not displayed.

## Calculation Sheets and Contracts

The past preserved ledger, edition selection, opposed d10, and eighteen-domain sheets are records verified by spreadsheet recalculation at the time. The existing modules in TOOL/tools/mechanisms/original-trpg are also contracts of that edition and do not mean the new system has been implemented.

The assignment of eight competencies, proficiencies, and traits to individual characters, the formulas for physiology, damage, growth, and effects, and the national systems are subject to separate authoring. The degree of damage and functional state of both arms, both legs, head, and torso are distinguished from disease progression. This document records the confirmed scope and unresolved rules, not issuance to all characters or completed implementation.');
INSERT INTO gdd_document (id, category_id, slug, title, summary, status, sort, source_path, body_md) VALUES ('doc.Singleplayer-Design-SP-1-0-0', 'system-design', 'singleplayer-design-sp-1-0-0', 'Seoul: Nationwide Single-Player Detailed Design Document', '', 'accepted', 128, 'system-design/Singleplayer-Design-SP-1-0-0.md', '# Seoul: Nationwide Single-Player Detailed Design Document

Edition: SP-1.0.0 · 2026-10-03 · Canonical baseline GDD main eae8951b

This design document defines a campaign that one player continues through one main-body character. Other characters in the world are NPCs that use the same action contract. Here, a game session is a single campaign continued through saving and loading. Network connections, hosts, occupancy between players, and synchronization are not included in the design scope.

The confirmed rules of the existing GDD are maintained. Following the owner''s instructions, the mechanisms of state, action, succession, transmission, relationships, saving, combat orders, justification, and original reinforcement are defined as contracts of this edition. Separate figures, empty national data, and calculation coefficients are undecided. Calculation functions, new costs, and new durations are not invented as numbers. If there is no input in the system ledger, the relevant action is marked "System Undecided" and is not given arbitrary success or free processing.

## 1. The Center of Play and the Common Execution Contract

The player''s goal is to maintain and expand the main-body character''s survival, livelihood, relationships, skills, and rights. Becoming a ruler is not the only path of progression. Workers, agents, officers on duty, commanders, fief holders, and institutional heads all act on the basis of their own qualifications and contracts. Livelihood achievements become evidence of contract fulfillment, and that evidence affects cooperation, appointment, support, and confirmation. Successfully completing a repair does not automatically grant a post.

### 1.1 Status Is Not Merged into a Single Rank Slot

| State Axis | Facts to Store | Meaning Read from Actions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Character | Permanent ID, survival, consciousness/injury, detention, ability to express intent | Can the character perform actions on their own |
| Location | Actual whereabouts, movement route, posture, progress | On-site access, interviews, work, combat participation |
| Affiliation and citizenship | Joining a state or organization, recognizing party and edition | Rights and duties of the joined party |
| Office | Institution, title, tier, term/succession method, jurisdiction, signing authority | Which institutional acts can be approved |
| Title and Holding | Peerage ID, holder, target domain, superior contract, recognition status | What rights and duties the character holds |
| Effective control | On-site controller, stationing, occupation, dispute | Can it currently be enforced on site |
| Command | Target unit, command trunk line, scope of delegation, expiration | Which orders can be given to whom |
| Skill and Labor | Personal proficiency, qualifications, schedule, reservations | Can the character actually do the work |
| Property and access | Ownership, custody, use, collateral, lease, and vault authority | Can the character spend, transfer, or deliver the property |
| Relationships and memory | Directional trust, kinship, marriage, debt, oaths, event memories | Basis for acceptance, refusal, support, retaliation |

Post tier is a comparative classification. Even at Tier 1, the post differs by country, such as president, monarch, station master, commander, or chairman. Peerage and post, and occupation and command authority, are separate. Holding public office does not turn an institutional vault into personal property or give command over all troops.

### 1.2 Execution Order of All Actions

Common states: Proposal → Qualification check → Delivery/Receipt → Counterparty acceptance or institutional approval → Resource and schedule reservation → Execution → Result application → Receipt. The branch states are refusal, hold, cancellation, suspension, and expiration.

The inputs are the actor, target, action type, basis of authority, target ledger edition, location/contact route, conditions, resources, and cause event ID. The check order is target existence and edition → feasibility such as survival/detention → authority → actual location/delivery → resources and exclusive reservations → counterparty intent/institutional procedure. High trust does not make a physically impossible action succeed.

If death, dismissal, occupation, or loss of contact occurs between approval and execution, the reserved work is checked again. Supplies already consumed and progress already completed are not reversed, and only the remaining resources are released according to the contract. Resending the same event ID does not create a new result. Simultaneous transfers of rights are evaluated on the same ledger edition, and conflicts are resolved by a deterministic acceptance order.

The action UI distinguishes "Possible," "Prerequisites Lacking," "No Authority," "Awaiting Response," and "System Undecided" and displays the basis. It does not leak hidden facts through the reason display and instead explains with "Insufficient confirmed basis."

## 2. Actual Actions by Character and Peerage State

### 2.1 Common Actions and Restrictions

Every able person can perform their own movement, observation, conversation requests, personal purchase/transfer requests, job applications, education requests, treatment requests, and relationship actions. Location, passage rights, skills, resources, and the other party''s consent are checked separately. A detained person can only hold the interviews, letters, and negotiations that the detention contract permits, and cannot move freely or do fieldwork. Injury does not act as a single entry on a prohibition list; it changes the conditions for performing the relevant action.

| Status/Rights State | Available Actions | Actions Requiring Additional Authority |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unaffiliated·Random | Employment applications, trade, expeditions, escort contracts, membership requests, requests for arbitration of one''s own rights | Joining before receiving a state fief grant, commanding state troops, granting titles to others |
| State Member·No Office | Services under civic/organizational institutions, labor/education/service contracts, petitions for grants | Declaring war in the state''s name, disposing of public assets, ratifying records |
| Tier 5 Staff | Assigned counter, duty, and signing work; submitting evidence and reporting | Appointments, grants, or musters beyond one''s own signing scope; a position without land is not treated as a fief |
| Tier 4~2 Institutional Officials | Orders, budgets, and duty assignments for delegated facilities, living areas, and tasks | Tier alone does not open up military command, inheritance, or full state diplomacy |
| Title Holder | Contractual operations, income, and duties of one''s own title; claims to restore rights; permitted sub-grants | Immediate use of facilities not yet occupied; arbitrary creation of titles of equal or higher rank |
| Appointed Commander | Posture, movement, and operation orders for the designated unit; remote orders are subject to the communication contract | Disposing of institution-owned property, forcing civilian labor, incorporating other units |
| Head of State | Appointments, grant proposals, diplomacy, war approval, and succession procedures under state institutions | Another state''s ratification authority, granting titles higher than one''s own, unconditional revocation without a contract violation |
| Claimant / Succession Candidate | Submitting evidence, asserting succession eligibility, requesting support, being a candidate beneficiary of a designated casus belli | Using a claim as ownership or on-site control |
| Occupier / Disputed Holder | Guarding, supplying, and negotiating under actual control; enforcing occupation rules | Occupation alone does not secure legitimate inheritance, ownership of institutional vaults, or residents'' cooperation |

Each action is checked across multiple status axes. For example, a landholder without command authority can make a "troop muster request" but cannot move another commander''s unit by their own order. A records officer carries out claim-building work under a qualified head''s order but does not personally gain anyone else''s territory.

### 2.2 Actual Action Menu

- Daily Life: Apply for rations, buy water/food, request lodging/treatment, rest, attend appointments. Entitlement, actual supplies, and schedule are checked.
- Economy: Propose orders, reserve labor, approve repair/construction stages, commission escorts, deliver cargo, set up leases/collateral. The budget owner and the signer are distinguished.
- Politics: Join, support/recommend appointments, petition for title grants, propose contract amendments, apply for ratification, submit grounds for supporting/disqualifying a successor, negotiate marriage/adoption.
- Military: Authorized recruitment, assignment, and reorganization; movement/garrisoning/ambush; submitting war plans; declaring war; attacking objectives; requesting withdrawal/surrender. Actual command authority is distinguished from access to the mobilization roster.
- Intelligence: Scouting, receiving reports, requesting record access, refuting/exposing rumors, creating authorized records. Known facts are distinguished from the authority to disclose them.

## 3. Starting, Conducting, and Ending a War

### 3.1 Casus Belli Contract Before Declaring War

War inputs are the war ID, approver and authority, attack/defense leads, casus belli ID, target title/territory, beneficiary, demands, approval cost, objectives, and each ending effect. A "hostile relationship" alone does not open a declaration of war. A main character without office may make plans, give support, and propose beneficiaries, but cannot approve a state war. A person whose independent state has been established uses that state''s head procedures.

Registered casus belli categories: restoring recognized rights, supporting an eligible third party''s claim, and redress for breach of obligations under an approved superior contract. Whether each category is registered, its target scope, cost, truce, and reparations are pending confirmation. Personal debts, passage disputes, and floodgate water-supply cutoffs are not automatically converted into grounds for territorial conquest. An unregistered casus belli does not create a war.

### 3.2 War State Transitions

Preparation → validation → declaration record → in progress → peace request/demand fulfilled/surrender/casus belli void → ending effects applied → post-war contract. If preparation fails, no war is created. During the war, battle, occupation, and prisoner events reference the relevant war and objective, and each event changes the score only once.

The existing confirmed score is an attack/defense value of -100 to +100 in opposite directions, and it changes through battles, occupation, key prisoners, and objective control. Battle score between rulers is capped at 50, capturing the head gives +100, capturing the designated first/second/third successor gives +50/+25/+10, and occupying the capital gives +10. These are used only when they correspond to an actual institutional head, successor, or capital. Candidates in an electoral state are not arbitrarily designated "first successor" to award points. The duration of objective occupation and the rate of increase are undecided.

+30 qualifies one to request a white peace, but acceptance is not guaranteed. +100 enforces the demands fixed in the casus belli, -100 is surrender by the losing side, and voluntary surrender is also possible. Separately from the score, if the grounds for the casus belli disappear, the void rules for that casus belli are checked. The death of a head alone does not automatically end every war.

### 3.3 Separating Military Actions from War

Viewing the mobilization roster or armory is not unit creation. Actual formations are created only after personnel acceptance, equipment, supplies, commander, and reservation checks. Automatic battles are decided by the local commander based on objective, posture, observation, and authority, and use the same adjudication/result ledger as manual ones. Only battles the main character actually joined provide the field screen. Late joiners must satisfy the permitted reinforcement conditions, entry route, and actual arrival.

Winning a battle is not the same as winning the war. Occupying a stronghold changes on-site control, but facility recovery, residents'' cooperation, and the transfer of legal rights are separate. Occupation outside the objectives does not widen the scope of demands. Withdrawal requires a valid retreat route and is recorded differently from a rout, an accepted surrender, or capture.

### 3.4 Order of Ending Procedures

Lock ending inputs → recheck casus belli and beneficiary → finalize the rights transfer list → occupation retain/return list → prisoner handling → reparations/truce/superior contract → cancel or recheck ongoing orders → event receipt. If two different wars try to transfer the same title at once, a ledger version conflict occurs and the second settlement is reviewed again. If a battle opens during a peace proposal, it is rechecked against the facts at the time of acceptance.

Example: If A fights to restore B''s claim and wins, B becomes the new holder of the target right. A does not get all the occupied lands. The superior relationship between B and A is checked against the conditions stated in the casus belli, and actual floodgate control and operator cooperation remain separate matters.

## 4. Granting, Delegating, and Revoking Titles

### 4.1 Grant Contract

Inputs: grantor, recipient, state membership status, target title and version, grant authority, superior contract, rights·duties·term, ratifying body, and delivery conditions for relics/documents/keys. To receive a grant from an existing state, one must already belong to it. A head may only distribute titles lower than their own, and the existing worldbuilding limit of two directly held duke-rank titles is maintained. No arbitrary title is created where there is no actual title ID, territory, or rank.

States: draft → authority/target check → recipient consent → ratification/approval → rights ledger change → operational takeover → completed or disputed. The default policy is not to change the holder before the required ratification. If on-site takeover fails after approval, it remains as "rights held·not recovered/takeover incomplete." In regions with a central records culture, omitting record ratification or relic inspection is grounds for invalidity.

When a right changes hands, the target right is removed from the grantor''s holdings list and the same title ID is registered to the recipient. For subordinate contracts and tax/water-supply/passage and ritual duties, the signer and succession policy are recorded. Buildings, institutional vaults, public stockpiles, and unit command are not transferred automatically unless each one''s inclusion is explicitly stated.

### 4.2 Distinguish Appointment, Grant, and Delegation

Appointment gives official authority. Grant changes the holder of a title right. Delegation leaves the original holder in place and entrusts only specified operations/signing/command. Delegation status is valid, suspended, expired, or revoked, and the basis event and scope are stored. If the proxy dies, the original holder''s rights do not disappear.

A revocation proposal requires the contract''s revocation clause, evidence of violation, and the ratification procedure. If a relic is reclaimed without withdrawing the delegation, a betrayal issue opens as under the existing rules. Others'' personal property or positions are not immediately removed just because of a higher tier. Illegal seizure is not marked as a normal grant, and is recorded as a change in on-site control and a rights dispute.

Validation cases: rejection of an unaffiliated recipient; version conflict when the same title is granted to two people simultaneously; original holder retained when ratification is refused; takeover incomplete when the grant succeeds but the keys are not delivered; distinguishing drafts from confirmed rights before and after the grantor''s death.

## 5. Succession and Title Transfer

### 5.1 Three Kinds of Succession

Player main-character succession, state/institution head succession, and succession to individual titles and property are handled as separate events. All of the dead main character''s authority, skills, and friends are not copied to the person who becomes the new main character.

Previously confirmed: when the main body dies or is permanently detained, the designated successor becomes the new main body; if there is none, the person next in the line of succession does. If there is no successor, that character''s path ends. The viewpoint moves to the new main body''s actual location, and the remaining squad elects a temporary private-rank squad leader. Check the contract scope to confirm that command of the remaining unit passes validly to the new main body.

Even when the main body changes because of permanent detention, the detained person is still alive. Do not treat this as death or open an inheritance. The specific criteria for judging permanent detention and whether re-manipulation is allowed are pending confirmation.

### 5.2 Processing Order for Succession Events

Confirm the basis for death/vacancy → suspend the deceased''s new personal actions, or, if a living person loses a post, suspend only the signing, command, and operating authority of the vacated post → snapshot the lists of posts, titles, property, and contracts → look up each item''s succession system → check candidate eligibility → run procedures such as election/nomination/examination/hereditary succession → ratification → transfer rights item by item → handover/takeover → record disputes. The personal life and movement of a living person, and actions under a separate valid contract, are judged by their own eligibility conditions and are not suspended merely because a post is vacant. Post authority is distinguished from personal property, title rights, and other valid contracts, and a vacancy alone does not trigger automatic inheritance, transfer, appointment of a proxy, or absorption of authority. The claims of candidates who were not chosen remain according to the system; a person is not deleted for having been eliminated.

Even if a post is vacant after a leader''s death, the institution and the state do not automatically cease to exist. Only persons and scopes registered in the system are permitted to act as proxy. If there is no eligible person or procedure, it remains a vacancy/succession dispute, and no anonymous leader is automatically generated.

### 5.3 Basis of Each State''s Leader Procedure and Remaining Inputs

| State/Type | Current Worldbuilding Approach | Inputs Needed for Detailed Design |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Government of the Republic of Korea | Duty-Family Election | Voting family rosters, candidate eligibility, attribution of votes/party posts, vacancy proxies |
| Floodgate State | Election by the Water-Contract Family Representatives'' Contract Council | Valid contracts, representative eligibility, quorum of meetings, ratification |
| Transshipment State | Hereditary Succession by the Eldest Child of the Rail Family | Order of eligible children, exceptions for wills and adoption, handover of posts and keys |
| Standards League | Guild council, apprenticeship lineage | Targets to which candidate skill, guild support, and apprenticeship succession apply |
| Eastern Command | Hereditary Commander | Distinction between family eligibility and military command takeover/subjugation registration |
| Russian Legation Command | Dual structure of commander and garrison chief | Which seat is vacant, and the rules for joint authority and proxies |
| Taeuk Group, Seongha Group, Sinnae Transport | Family Inheritance | Distinction between personal shares/licenses and the leader''s post, and between appointment and ratification |
| Yeouido Investment Federation | Council Election by the Investing Families | Separation of share inheritance, representative selection, and chair election |
| Registry Church | Hereditary father-to-son pastorate, ratification by elders | Candidate lineage and ordination/ratification conditions |
| Myeongdong Archdiocese, Anguk Great Monastery | Election by the priesthood, acclamation by the mountain-wide assembly | Eligibility and procedure for election/acclamation, takeover of the corps/commander post |
| White Cross Order | Master''s Designation of a Disciple as Successor | Valid nomination, training/proficiency, confirming authority |
| Central Technology Preservation Institute | Examination-Based Selection | Examination eligibility, results, and takeover of record-viewing rights |
| Jeongdong Labor Federation | Direct Election by Union Members | Voting union members, candidate eligibility, authority over the strike roster |

Differences in detailed wording between canonical sources are not unified arbitrarily here. In particular, the link between the guild''s apprenticeship lineage and council elections, and vacancies under the dual structure, are open questions to be confirmed.

### 5.4 Inheritance of Claims and Rights

Title-holding rights follow the succession system of that title. Enforced claims keep the existing path by which they pass to eligible children as unenforced claims, and unenforced claims are not separately inherited. A spouse does not automatically share all claims merely by being a spouse. Public-office candidacy, voting rights, and technical qualifications are not handled as the same data type as title claims.

When the leader of an ongoing war dies, the new responsible party, beneficiary, and casus belli are rechecked. Whether personal promises and post promises are maintained, re-ratified, or terminated is judged item by item. Succession must not be used to erase debts, ceasefires, or damage records.

## 6. Ownership, Transfer, and Handover of Assets

### 6.1 Classifying Assets

Personally owned supplies, equipment, and currency; institution-owned warehouses/vaults/facilities; joint/share property; leases and usage permits; debts, claims, and collateral; and records, keys, and relics are all separate. A custodian is not an owner, and holding a key is not itself a legal right. Synthetic beings recognized as persons are excluded from the list of transferable assets.

Asset state has location, quantity/unit, owner, custodian, usage rights, reservations, collateral, in-transit status, and disputes. Logistics state and legal-rights state are kept separate. Cargo in transit during war also keeps the same asset ID/split origin.

### 6.2 Contract for Transfer upon Death

Personal property determines its beneficiary by checking the state''s system, any valid will, and marriage/joint-property contracts. Institutional property stays with the institution, and the new post holder takes over only the management authority they need. Usage rights and leases are maintained, re-approved, or terminated according to their succession clauses. Debts and claims are checked for whether they belong to the individual, the post, or the institution, and for succession clauses; debt is not unconditionally pushed onto every successor.

The order is: finalize the inheritance list → mark disputes/collateral → transfer ownership → reserve actual handover → verify receipt. Even after gaining ownership, supplies in an enemy-occupied warehouse do not teleport into the inventory. Divided inheritance preserves the total quantity, and supplies that are reserved or pledged as collateral are marked separately. An undetermined system means inheritance is put on hold; it is not replaced by an ownerless state or automatic equal distribution.

Example: when a chairman dies, personal equipment and personal shares are divided separately from the company warehouse. A child''s inheritance of shares and the new chairman''s authority to place warehouse orders are different events. Even if personal equipment in transit is inherited, it cannot be used before the cargo arrives.

Permanent storage: previous owner, beneficiary, quantity, basis, collateral, reservation, handover location, fulfillment status, cause ID. Reapplying the same death event must not duplicate property. If a will change and a death occur at the same time, use the recognized event order and the institutionally valid time.

## 7. Transfer, Education, and Loss of Knowledge

### 7.1 Separating Records from People''s Proficiency

A knowledge object has content/edition, source, authenticity/verification, confidentiality, and ownership/viewing rights. A person has the records they have encountered, understanding, practice progress, performed proficiency, and qualifications. Inheriting a document transfers ownership or viewing rights of the paper/medium; it is not a copy of proficiency. When a master dies, the disciple''s existing proficiency remains, but the available teachers and verification routes for new education change.

### 7.2 Education State Contract

Education request → teacher/institution acceptance → check each skill''s requirements → reserve schedule, tools, and location → instruction/reading/practice → assessment → record progress/proficiency. For each skill, author whether teacher, records, tools, practice location, and learner conditions are required. The same items are not enforced across all skills.

If a teacher dies, tools are damaged, someone is detained, or a location is lost, record the reason for suspending/holding the education and preserve the progress already gained. When transferring to another teacher, check the recognizable progress for that skill. If someone has read the records but cannot practice, only the record access/understanding state changes. Nominating an apprentice is a post-candidacy event and is separate from completing skill proficiency.

### 7.3 Disclosure, Exchange, and Concealment

Information transmission checks the sender''s actual knowledge, disclosure authority, and the receiving route. When a report is delivered, the recipient gains knowledge with a source and a time of verification, not the entirety of the world''s current facts. Forgery, exposure, and refutation leave competing evidence and events rather than erasing the original. When a secret is leaked, the exposed information and the person''s responsibility are recorded separately.

Verification cases: no duplicate proficiency gains from inheriting the same document; learning progress is kept after a teacher''s death; maintenance ability and manufacturing ability are separate; a successor without access rights does not automatically obtain institutional secrets; an old report does not guarantee the current location.

## 8. Changes in Character Relationships and Permanent Effects

### 8.1 The Roles of Values and Facts

The previously confirmed relationship value is directional trust T[A,B]. No separate affection value is added. The thresholds of T 25 or above for friend, -25 or below for enemy, and -50 or below for nemesis are kept. Family, marriage, romance, master/disciple, contracts, debts, oaths, and command are real relationship facts and are not the same as trust. Just because A trusts B does not mean B trusts A at the same value.

Loyalty to the ruler is a separate existing state from 0 to 100. It reads the daily mood and trust in the ruler, and the ruler themselves is at 100. If loyalty stays below 25, paths open for a successor''s rebellion or the defection of other heroes, but the duration, amount of change, and risky-action thresholds are undecided. Do not interpret this as a rebellion that must occur every day.

### 8.2 Processing Relationship Events

Spending time together, treatment and rescue, fulfilling/violating contracts, gifts and trades, public support and insults, exposure, detention and release, and marriage, adoption, and death are the sources of relationship events. For each event, record the actor, target, harm/benefit, witness/report, secrecy scope, and basis. Modifier amounts use confirmed rules; if undecided, no arbitrary numbers are inserted.

The evaluating party updates its own trust and memory at the point when it learns of the event through observation or report. A method in which hidden betrayals are known instantly from anywhere in the world is not used. Direct harm is distinguished from public reputation, and no fixed personality or betrayal probability is assigned to a state.

### 8.3 Actual Behavior Changed by Relationships

Relationships are inputs for judging whether to accept an interview, accept risky work, support a successor, accept a teacher, testify, and whom to protect, conceal, defect from, or plot against. High trust does not create command authority, property rights, consent, or passage rights that do not exist. Even when a relationship changes, existing contracts change through termination clauses and action events. When a friend becomes an enemy, all trades and unit organizations do not automatically vanish at that moment.

When someone dies, the survivors'' family/romantic relationships and mourning events remain. The deceased''s T[A,B] is not copied to the successor. The new main body starts with the personal relationships it already had, and the previous person''s contracts, institutional obligations, and public history are each carried over by succession policy.

### 8.4 What Remains Permanently in the Campaign

Store title/post history, rights disputes, fulfillment/violation of promises, prisoners, injuries and deaths, marriage and kinship, education progress, disclosed evidence, property and debts, ceasefire and war outcomes, and witnessed events/memories along with current trust. Later actions reread these facts. Loading a save and changing the main body do not reset them.

Permanent storage does not mean every emotion is fixed forever. For effects with recovery, reconciliation, mourning, change, or expiry periods, the rule and the end point are stored together. The forgetting/decay function is pending confirmation. The world record of hidden facts and the facts each character knows are stored separately.

Example: If A breaches a escort contract with B, trust, memory, and the contract breach are recorded according to the grounds B learned of. Even if A''s successor C becomes the Principal, B''s personal trust in C is separate. If an office-attached reparation obligation has been inherited, it remains with C''s institution, and personal promises are handled according to the maintain/re-ratify/terminate policy.

## 9. Single-Player Time, Saving, and Principal Succession

World time is singular. The player engages with the world through one Principal''s actions and valid command authority. NPCs follow the same movement, labor, education, political, and combat conditions. Switching screens does not rewind time or duplicate the Principal to a remote location. As previously approved, combat has no pause, speed control, or command queue. This document does not add new campaign time controls.

Save boundary: A snapshot is made at a consistent simulation boundary where accepted events and result application are complete, and subsequent events are recorded after it. Quitting/loading is not an in-world death or departure event. Ongoing wars, orders, education, cargo, handovers, succession procedures, and reservations are restored from their current stage. An already applied ResultId is not applied again. A rules-version mismatch or corrupted save is an explicit error and is not silently replaced with a new campaign.

Principal succession performs the step of changing only the screen subject and the transfer of rights as separate steps. Even if a new Principal is selected at the moment of death, incomplete inheritance procedures, disputes, and handovers remain. Reports/records that become inaccessible because the Principal changed are displayed in a restricted way according to the new character''s knowledge and qualifications.

## 10. Detail Screens the Player Checks

- Character: survival, injury, detention, and actual location; affiliation/position/title/command authority; schedule and reservations; skills; property; directional relationships; currently available actions and reasons for unavailability.
- Title: the four domains of holding, claim, effective control, and ratification; superior contract; method of conferral/succession; income/obligations; assets being handed over; change history.
- War: justification, objectives, beneficiaries, and termination effects; the event-by-event basis for the score; actual participation/supply/occupation; peace proposal status; prisoners; termination settlement.
- Succession: grounds for death/vacancy; differences between Principal candidates and candidates by position/title; qualifications, ratification procedure, and disputes; list of transmissions/handovers; contracts to maintain/re-ratify/terminate.
- Assets/Knowledge: who owns, stores, and uses them; reservations/collateral/access restrictions; current handover location; difference between records and mastery; education stage and reason for interruption.
- Relationships: distinction between character A→B and B→A; disclosable causal events; contract status; kinship/oath/command facts; recoverable effects and permanent history.

Each screen is a surface that reads ledger facts. Pressing a button sends a request to the common action contract, and unconfirmed actions are shown as pending. The report diagram does not replace this detailed design document.

## 11. Confirmed Scope of This Version and Pending Data

Confirmed: cross-qualification checks, no rights change before ratification, item-by-item succession, institutional assets remaining in place, actual handover, separation of permanent detention from death, separation of records from mastery, preservation of education progress, relationship updates after observation, no copying of personal relationships to successors, and single-campaign saves.

Pending data: actual title decrees and holders, candidate/election/exam/ratification inputs by state, costs/truces/reparations by justification list, adjudication functions and correction/decay coefficients, and price lists for individual negotiation items. Pending data is not automatically generated as approval of canonical mechanics.

## 12. State Verification Scenarios

Refusal of conferral to an unaffiliated character; head''s death just before conferral approval; simultaneous conferral of the same title; successful rights inheritance with failed handover of a warehouse occupied by the enemy; no property inheritance after the Principal''s permanent detention; different beneficiaries of a public-office election and personal property inheritance; valid/invalid branching of a war justification during a change of successor; non-attribution of occupation outside the objective; restoration of existing progress after a teacher''s death; per-observer updates of a relationship secret; separation of contract succession from personal trust; rejection of re-settlement of the same ResultId after a save restore. In each case, compare the state before and after, the reason for failure, totals, IDs, and event order.

## 13. Grounds and Approval Boundary

Confirmed grounds: GP-01 to 14 of the GDD Campaign-Progression, the Principal succession and command in Hero-Squads-and-Simultaneous-Turns, RWARFARE-16/37/38/41/42/43 of Rules-Factions-Warfare and the 2026-10-03 claim/war clauses, and the economy, saving, and combat round-trip contracts. Worldbuilding grounds: WIKI Sixteen-States, Offices-and-Ranks, Chaebol-Houses-and-Century-Factions, People-and-Machines.

This design document is the SP-1.0.0 single-player detailed design contract and is not a report of completed product implementation. The shared-world/host-session portions of existing reports are not execution premises of this design. The consistency of the canonical and derived documents of this version is verified, and product implementation is carried out separately.

## 14. SP-1.0.0 Precedence and Replacement of Existing Mechanics

The version ID is SP-1.0.0, and it applies to the new single-player design and to campaigns created with this version thereafter. The document storage format schemaVersion 1 differs from the game rules version. The confirmed clauses of this version take precedence over existing designs in the same scope. The existing designs for shared world, maximum participants, host, disconnection, proxy play, and occupation/branching sessions are excluded from the execution premises of the current product design and preserved as history. It is not claimed that existing implementations and saves were automatically converted. An incompatible save is an explicit error, and new rules are not mixed in without an approved migration.

Replaced scope: the multiplayer premise in the campaign/user journey/architecture/save documents → one Principal, NPCs, and a single-campaign save. Physical delivery delay in combat/monopoly on local command → direct orders from the Principal battle commander and immediate system relay to squad leaders. The presumption that unofficed characters hold no rights → every character may hold justification and rights instances. Waiting for a response/collecting additional damage at ±100 → forced termination per justification. Scope not replaced: actual strategic movement, on-site participation, no pausing in combat, the single result ledger, separation of facility operation from legal rights, and state-specific qualifications, consent, personality, and world facts. Design confirmation does not mean the implementation code is complete.

Design changes require a revised version such as SP-1.0.1, and incompatible changes in action/rights semantics require a separate new version and a succession contract. Different time or succession rules are not mixed across documents. A contract gap is a pending state and is not closed with draft figures or POC execution values.

## 15. Operation Contract for Battle Commanders and Squad Leaders

A battle commander has a command scope registered in the combat session and a list of assigned squads. If the Principal is a battle commander, they directly coordinate all battle-map squads in that scope through valid orders such as select, move, stance, and attack. Even if each unit/squad has a separate squad leader, this higher command authority is not blocked. Enemy forces, unassigned independent forces, and allied forces outside the command scope cannot be controlled merely because they are visible on the battle map. No authority arises over another Principal or another site. A squad''s rearward movement is a movement order within the battlefield and is not a participant departure or termination result. An order to withdraw that ends the battle applies to the entire battle under existing rules, and a valid retreat route and confirmation procedure are checked. Order-execution restrictions such as melee engagement lock remain in place.

If the Principal is a squad leader, orders from the higher commander are delivered as an immediate system signal rather than by messenger or radio travel. Receipt records the world time, higher order ID, issuing commander, target squad, purpose, permitted scope, and version. This immediate delivery applies only to battle relay and does not make remote contact on the strategic map or character movement instantaneous.

A Principal squad leader may choose to follow the relayed order, carry it out in part, or act independently. Receiving an order does not lock input or force execution of the order. The link between the orders of a higher-commander Principal carrying out the owner''s instructions and the judgments of subordinate NPCs uses the same execution check. No new combat order queue is created; only the current relay/execution status and update history are kept.

Evaluation inputs: the order''s purpose, time of receipt, feasibility, squad status, the Principal''s actual actions, contribution to the objective, losses, civilian harm, and changes/cancellations of the higher order. The evaluation result is attributed to the commander character and becomes the basis for memory of that Principal and for later assignment/trust decisions. The relationship-change function, figures, and promotion/disciplinary conditions are pending, and a fixed trust penalty is not automatically imposed for disobedience. For impossible orders and invalid relays after the commander''s death, execution failure is distinguished from arbitrary disobedience. Even if independent action produced a good outcome, compliance and results are recorded separately.

Relay → immediate receipt → follow/independent choice → actual execution → per-order evaluation → reflection in the commander''s memory/relationship. The same order evaluation is not applied twice under a result ID. When a commander is replaced, existing orders/evaluations preserve the original issuer and history and are rechecked under the new commander''s authority.

## 16. Justifications and Title Rights Held by Characters

Every character may hold justifications and title rights like a list of instances. Each has a character ID, right ID, type, target, grounds, issuance/succession event, effect, ratifying party, version, and inheritance/extinction conditions. The holding list is an interface to rights, not a rule that every right can be freely traded or duplicated like an object. Whether it can be transferred, used, or inherited follows the contract of the right''s type. Title-holding rights, latent/enforced/unenforced claims, and war justifications are displayed as different types.

Characters without office or affiliation may also hold them if they have qualifying grounds. Holding a justification does not automatically grant authority to declare war, troops, or command. A character may ask an authorized head to support war for their own claim, and the beneficiary is fixed to the justification. If there is no valid justification instance when a war begins, creation is refused.

A justification specifies the target domain, beneficiary, war objectives, effects of attacker victory/defender victory/white peace/nullification, and validity conditions. An ongoing war references the justification version at its start and explicitly checks only for nullification when the grounds change. Even at a score of 100, territory, assets, or characters beyond the scope required by that version are not forcibly acquired.

## 17. Forced Termination and Interim Negotiation

The war score W ranges from -100 to 100, and the opponent''s is -W. If W=100, the opponent''s forced acceptance at -100 is recorded, and the victory/defeat effects defined in the justification proceed immediately to termination settlement. Additional captures, occupations, and property demands do not keep accumulating due to NPC response delays. Forced acceptance does not mean owning every target but accepting the termination effects per justification. If the justification is already invalid, the invalidity conditions are checked first, and rights without grounds are not created.

Either side may negotiate an interim settlement while the absolute value is below 100. The source war system''s ordinary endings are demand fulfillment, white peace, surrender, and casus belli invalidation; a peace that sums scores item by item across territory, assets, and persons is not confirmed. Item-by-item negotiation is adopted as an Seoul:nationwide original extension with owner approval. The per-item numeric tables do not invent or fill in values absent from the source. Signing and settlement are locked for negotiated items whose price version is unregistered, but negotiation of validly registered items is permitted. Demands concerning persons are treated not as goods to be handed over but as the object of valid actions such as prisoner release/repatriation, an audience, or voluntary assignment.

## 18. Item-by-Item Evaluation Contract for Original Peace

When the value is below 100, each side writes both its demands and its offers into the proposal. A proposal carries the war ID, proposal version, signing authority, target/beneficiary, item list, performance timing, deadline, and breach effect. Items are fiefdom/right transfers, asset payments/returns, prisoner release/repatriation, and diplomatic commitments such as passage/water supply/rationing/escort/recognition/superior contract/truce. The structure does not permit the sale of persons, forced marriage, or ordinary assignment without consent.

Each item has a unique ID, type, quantity/scope, disposal authority, evaluation inputs and price version, score, casus-belli-permitted range, and feasibility. Fiefdoms read the evaluation unit of the registered title/territory; supplies read actual quantity and the registered economic unit; prisoners read their detention status and the political effect of release; diplomacy reads scope, duration, obligations, and actual routes. A single ledger asset may not be demanded redundantly across multiple items. War score and price score are separate records, and negotiation does not consume or manipulate combat score.

The structure of the evaluation formula is V(proposal) = Σ price of demanded items − Σ price of offered items. Prices are evaluated against a state snapshot of the same war/proposal version. Approval, rejection, or counteroffer is decided by an acceptance function that reads war score W, objective control, supply, succession, key prisoners, current obligations, relations, memory, and feasibility. It is not handled as automatic acceptance simply because V≤W. Price functions for every category and acceptance adjustment coefficients are subject to data design and do not invent or fill in values absent from the source. Unregistered items appear in the negotiation UI as ''Evaluation Pending'' and block signing/settlement of a finalized proposal.

The flow is: proposal → evaluation by both sides → rejection/counteroffer/acceptance → ledger and authority recheck → single closing settlement → handover/performance. Time and combat continue while proposals are exchanged. If ±100 is reached before acceptance, the forced casus belli ending takes effect first and any unaccepted proposal is discarded. For acceptance/100/death at the same boundary, the single ending is determined by the confirmed event order and version.

The casus belli is the maximum scope of forced demands. An interim negotiation may reduce the demands within it or replace them with mutually agreed substitute payments/obligations, but it may not include territory, assets, or persons the other party cannot dispose of, nor third-party rights. Additional forced cession of fiefdoms outside the objective is not allowed. A separate voluntary transaction is not disguised as a casus belli enforcement effect. After signing, asset handover, prisoner repatriation, and follow-up obligations remain as campaign contracts and are not instantly copied before performance is complete.

## 19. Detailed Handling of Outcomes by Casus Belli and Rights Succession

In a claim-backing war, the approver and the beneficiary are separated. The beneficiary''s consent, affiliation/backing contract, claim validity, target title, and institutional eligibility to exercise are checked. The victory right transfers to the beneficiary, and the attacker does not hold it personally in their place. The superior relationship is rechecked against the actual rank of the title the beneficiary gains and any superior contract. A beneficiary who gains a title of equal or higher rank is not unconditionally bound as a lower vassal. Office tier is not used directly as title rank.

| Termination | Target Right | Claim Effect | Additional Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Attacker''s Demand Fulfilled | Transfers the title designated in the casus belli to the eligible beneficiary | Records the successful right and the cleanup of remaining claims per casus belli | Subordinate contracts, on-site operations, and handover are checked separately |
| White Peace | Holding right to the target is retained | The unfulfilled claim used in this war changes to a fulfilled state | Does not alter any of the beneficiary''s other claims |
| Attacker''s Defeat/Surrender | The defender retains the holding right to the target | Removes the claim used in this casus belli | The defender does not automatically acquire all of the attacker''s territory |
| Casus Belli Nullification | Checks the return/remainder rules per casus belli | Differs from white peace, so there is no automatic claim promotion | Cancellation costs, prisoners, and truce are specified in the casus belli policy |

Personal claims, superior rights, subjugation release, and institutional asset recovery are different types of casus belli. The defeat reparations, person detention, and superior-contract effects of another casus belli are not copied into an ordinary claim war. Exceptions in which white peace or prisoner score differs for some casus belli are permitted only under registered policies and do not apply to all wars. Occupation is an actual change of control, and a normal enemy occupation does not automatically invalidate a rights-recovery war. Changes to the legal target, beneficiary, or eligibility to exercise are the inputs for the invalidation check.

Inheriting an existing holder''s title and children inheriting, as unfulfilled claims, the fulfilled claims the holder possessed are separate paths. Whether an eligible child gains a claim to a parent''s title they did not actually receive, and which candidate retains it under partitioned inheritance, requires per-state institutions. If a party dies during a war, the attack leader, defense leader, and claim beneficiary are each rechecked, and if there are multiple candidates it is not replaced with an arbitrary first child. If there is no valid successor, the invalid/pending-succession handling per casus belli applies.

A truce stores the ending type, casus belli, signatory/office attribution, start/end times, exceptions, and breach effect. It is not presumed that a change of leader automatically deletes a truce. Durations in Seoul world time and breach costs per institution are separate inputs. Acceptance evaluation includes not only war score and item value but also ongoing losses, feasibility of holding the objective, detention, relations, contracts, and supply; however, nonexistent routes, property, or authority are not passed through by score adjustment.');
